low=1, high=0 -> then low>high)\n            # so actually, if a<=b and b=1, then a must be 1, but a!=b -> contradiction.\n            # because a and b are consecutive in tour, and a!=b.\n            # if b=1, then a cannot be 1.\n            # and if a<=b and b=1, then a<=1, so a=1, but a!=b -> impossible.\n            # so when b=1, we must have a>1, so a> b, so we are in the else branch.\n\n        So in the clockwise case, if a<=b, then b>=a+1, so b-1>=a>=1, so high = b-1 >= a >=1, so high>=1.\n\n        If a>b, then high = b-1, which could be 0.\n\n        So in the a>b case:\n            if b-1 >= 1:\n                two segments: [a, N] and [1, b-1]\n            else: # b-1==0, i.e., b==1\n                only [a, N]\n\n   Similarly, in the counterclockwise case, when we set low=b, high=a-1, then if a==1, high=0, and if b>1, then low>high, and we do:\n        if high>=1: add to [1,high] and [low,N]\n        else: only [low, N]\n\n   So we can write:\n\n   For a given segment and extra cost:\n\n   if d_cw < d_ccw:\n        l = a\n        r = b - 1\n        if r == 0:\n            r = N  # no, we don't want to do that. We want to handle b==1 separately.\n        # Instead, we do:\n        if a <= b:\n            # then r = b-1 >= a (since b>a) and r>=1\n            diff[l] += extra\n            diff[r+1] -= extra\n        else: # a > b\n            # r = b-1\n            diff[a] += extra\n            if b-1 >= 1:\n                diff[1] += extra\n                diff[b] -= extra   # because r+1 = (b-1)+1 = b\n            # else b-1==0, i.e., b==1, then only [a, N]\n            # so we don't add to diff[1], and no subtract\n            # but we need to subtract at N+1? no, because [a, N] is from a to N, so we do:\n            #   diff[a] += extra\n            #   diff[N+1] -= extra\n            # But we are not doing that.\n\n        We are not handling the [a, N] part with a subtract at N+1.\n\n   We must do:\n\n   For a non-wrap range [l, r] (l<=r): \n      