s.\n\n   But note: in the counterclockwise case, we set l=b, r=a-1, and if b<=a, then we do [b, a-1] — but if b<=a, then a-1>=b-1, but we need a-1>=b? only if a-1>=b, i.e., a>=b+1.\n\n   But if b<=a, then we can have b==a, but a!=b? no, a and b are different.\n\n   But in the tour, a and b are consecutive, a!=b.\n\n   But in the counterclockwise case, we have d_ccw < d_cw, which means the counterclockwise distance is shorter.\n\n   And we are in the else branch of d_cw>=d_ccw, but we already did d_cw<d_ccw, so this is d_ccw<d_cw.\n\n   And we set l=b, r=a-1.\n\n   If b<=a, then we require that the clockwise path from b to a is valid, which uses bridges [b, a-1] — but only if a-1>=b.\n\n   But if b==a, then a-1 = b-1 < b, so b<=a but a-1 < b.\n\n   But b<=a and a-1 < b means a-1 < b <= a → so b = a.\n\n   But a!=b — contradiction.\n\n   So if b<=a, then b < a, so a>=b+1, so a-1>=b.\n\n   So [b, a-1] is valid.\n\n   Therefore, the code is safe.\n\n   But note: when a==1, then in counterclockwise case, r = a-1 = 0.\n\n   Then in the b>a branch: b>a means b>1, then we do:\n        diff[b] += extra\n        diff[N+1] -= extra\n        if 0>=1? no, so skip.\n\n   So only [b, N] — correct.\n\n   And in the b<=a branch: if b<=a and a==1, then b<=1, so b=1, but a=1, a!=b — contradiction.\n\n   So when a==1, we must have b>1, so b>a, so we are in the else branch.\n\n   So it's safe.\n\n   Summary:\n\n   Steps:\n\n   - Read N, M, list X of M integers.\n   - base_length = 0\n   - diff = [0]*(N+2)  # 1-indexed, indices 1 to N+1\n   - For k in range(M-1):\n        a = X[k], b = X[k+1]\n        d_cw = (b - a) % N\n        if d_cw == 0:\n            d_cw = N   # but a!=b, so d_cw in [1, N-1], so not needed\n        # Actually, (b-a)%N is in [0, N-1], and since a!=b, it's not 0 only if b>a? \n        # But (b-a)%N = 0 only if b==a mod N, i.e., a==b, which is not the case.\n        # So d_cw = (b - a) % N\n        # But if negative, % makes it positive.\n        d_cw = (b - a) % N\n        if d_cw == 0:\n            d_cw = N   # should not happen\n        d_ccw = N - d_cw\n        d = min(d_cw, d_ccw)\n\n        base_length += d\n\n        if d_cw == d_ccw:\n            # both paths same length, so no matter which bridge we remove, we can avoid it\n            continue\n\n        extra = N - 2*d   # extra cost if we remove a bridge on the short path\n\n        if d_cw < d_ccw:\n            # short path: clockwise from a to b\n            if a <= b:\n                l = a\n                r = b - 1\n                diff[l] += extra\n                diff[r+1] -= extra\n            else: # a > b\n                # [a, N] and [1, b-1] if b-1>=1\n                diff[a] += extra\n                diff[N+1] -= extra\n                if b - 1 >= 1:\n                    diff[1] += extra\n                    diff[b] -= extra   # r+1 = (b-1)+1 = b\n        else: # d_ccw < d_cw\n            # short path: counterclockwise from a to b = clockwise from b to a\n            if b <= a:\n                l = b\n                r = a - 1\n                diff[l] += extra\n                diff[r+1] -= extra\n            else: # b > a\n                diff[b] += extra\n                diff[N+1] -= extra\n                if a - 1 >= 1:\n                    diff[1] += extra\n                    diff[a] -= extra\n\n   - Now, compute penalty array:\n        penalty = 0\n        min_penalty = a big number\n        for i in range(1, N+1):\n            penalty += diff[i]\n            if penalty < min_penalty:\n                min_penalty = penalty\n\n   - Answer = base_length + min_penalty\n\n   But note: min_penalty could be negative? \n        extra = N - 2*d, and d < N/2, so 2*d < N, so extra > 0.\n        So we are adding positive values, so penalty >=0.\n\n   But we are doing range add of positive values, so min_penalty is at least 0.\n\n   But is it possible that no bridge is used by any short path? then min_penalty=0.\n\n   Example: if all segments have d_cw==d_ccw, then we skip, so diff all 0.\n\n   But also if the short paths avoid a particular bridge.\n\n   But we are taking min_penalty over bridges.\n\n   So answer = base_length + min_penalty\n\n   Let's test with sample 1: N=3, M=3, X=[1,3,2]\n\n   base_length = 0\n   Segment 1: 1->3\n        d_cw = (3-1)%3 = 2\n        d_ccw = 3-2=1\n        d = 1\n        d_ccw < d_cw → counterclockwise is shorter.\n        extra = 3 - 2*1 = 1\n        b=3, a=1 → in the segment a=1, b=3\n        so in the else branch: d_ccw < d_cw\n            b=3, a=1\n            b>a? 3>1 → yes\n            so: \n                diff[3] += 1\n                diff[4] (N+1=4) -= 1\n                a-1=0, so skip the if\n        So diff[3] +=1, diff[4]-=1\n\n   Segment 2: 3->2\n        a=3, b=2\n        d_cw = (2-3)%3 = (-1)%3 = 2\n        d_ccw = 1\n        d=1, d_ccw<d_cw\n        so counterclockwise: use clockwise from b=2 to a=3\n        b=2, a=3\n        b<=a? 2<=3 → yes\n        l=2, r=3-1=2\n        so diff[2] += extra, diff[3] -= extra\n        extra = 3-2=1\n        so diff[2] +=1, diff[3] -=1\n\n   Now diff: index 1..4\n        diff[2] = 1\n        diff[3] = 1 (from first) -1 (from second) = 0? \n        But we do:\n            first: diff[3] +=1 → diff[3]=1; diff[4]-=1\n            second: diff[2] +=1 → diff[2]=1; diff[3] -=1 → diff[3]=0\n        diff[4] = -1\n\n   Now compute penalty for bridges 1,2,3:\n        i=1: penalty = diff[1] = 0\n        i=2: penalty = 0 + diff[2] = 1\n        i=3: penalty = 1 + diff[3] = 1+0=1\n\n   min_penalty = 0\n\n   base_length = 1 (for 1->3) + 1 (for 3->2) = 2\n\n   answer = 2 + 0 = 2 → matches.\n\n   But which bridge has penalty 0? bridge 1.\n\n   So we can remove bridge 1 with no extra cost.\n\n   Why? \n        Segment 1: 1->3: short path is counterclockwise: 1→3, which uses bridge 3 (since 1 and 3 are connected by bridge 3). \n            So bridge 1 is not used.\n        Segment 2: 3->2: short path is counterclockwise: 3→2? But counterclockwise from 3 to 2