    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_344",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nAtCoder Inc. sells T-shirts with its logo.\nYou are given Takahashi's schedule for N days as a string S of length N consisting of 0, 1, and 2.\nSpecifically, for an integer i satisfying 1\\leq i\\leq N,\n\n- if the i-th character of S is 0, he has no plan scheduled for the i-th day;\n- if the i-th character of S is 1, he plans to go out for a meal on the i-th day;\n- if the i-th character of S is 2, he plans to attend a competitive programming event on the i-th day.\n\nTakahashi has M plain T-shirts, all washed and ready to wear just before the first day.\nIn addition, to be able to satisfy the following conditions, he will buy several AtCoder logo T-shirts.\n\n- On days he goes out for a meal, he will wear a plain or logo T-shirt.\n- On days he attends a competitive programming event, he will wear a logo T-shirt.\n- On days with no plans, he will not wear any T-shirts. Also, he will wash all T-shirts worn at that point. He can wear them again from the next day onwards.\n- Once he wears a T-shirt, he cannot wear it again until he washes it.\n\nDetermine the minimum number of T-shirts he needs to buy to be able to wear appropriate T-shirts on all scheduled days during the N days. If he does not need to buy new T-shirts, print 0.\nAssume that the purchased T-shirts are also washed and ready to use just before the first day.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN M\nS\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the minimum number of T-shirts Takahashi needs to buy to be able to satisfy the conditions in the problem statement.\nIf he does not need to buy new T-shirts, print 0.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1\\leq M\\leq N\\leq 1000\n- S is a string of length N consisting of 0, 1, and 2.\n- N and M are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n6 1\n112022\n\nSample Output 1\n\n2\n\nIf Takahashi buys two logo T-shirts, he can wear T-shirts as follows:\n\n- On the first day, he wears a logo T-shirt to go out for a meal.\n- On the second day, he wears a plain T-shirt to go out for a meal.\n- On the third day, he wears a logo T-shirt to attend a competitive programming event.\n- On the fourth day, he has no plans, so he washes all the worn T-shirts. This allows him to reuse the T-shirts worn on the first, second, and third days.\n- On the fifth day, he wears a logo T-shirt to attend a competitive programming event.\n- On the sixth day, he wears a logo T-shirt to attend a competitive programming event.\n\nIf he buys one or fewer logo T-shirts, he cannot use T-shirts to meet the conditions no matter what. Hence, print 2.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n3 1\n222\n\nSample Output 2\n\n3\n\nSample Input 3\n\n2 1\n01\n\nSample Output 3\n\n0\n\nHe does not need to buy new T-shirts.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's break down the problem step by step.\n\n---\n\n### **Understanding the Problem**\n\nWe are given:\n- `N`: number of days\n- `M`: number of plain T-shirts available (all clean at the start)\n- `S`: a string of length `N`, where each character is:\n  - `'0'`: no plan → no T-shirt worn, **and all worn T-shirts so far are washed** (available again from next day)\n  - `'1'`: go out for a meal → can wear **plain or logo** T-shirt\n  - `'2'`: attend competitive programming event → must wear a **logo** T-shirt\n\nRules:\n- Once a T-shirt is worn, it cannot be reused until washed.\n- Washing happens **only on days with `'0'`**, and it washes **all T-shirts worn so far** (not just that day’s).\n- All T-shirts (original plain and any bought logo ones) are clean before day 1.\n- We can buy **only logo T-shirts**.\n- We want to find the **minimum number of logo T-shirts to buy** so that Takahashi can follow his schedule.\n\n---\n\n### **Key Observations**\n\n1. **Only `'2'` days require logo T-shirts.**\n   - `'1'` days can use either plain or logo.\n   - `'0'` days involve no wearing, but **trigger washing** of all previously worn T-shirts.\n\n2. **Washing resets availability.**\n   - After a `'0'`, all worn T-shirts (plain and logo) become available again the next day.\n   - So we can **reuse** T-shirts across segments separated by `'0'`.\n\n3. **We can model the problem in segments between `'0'` days.**\n   - Each segment is a substring between two `'0'`s (or from start to first `'0'`, or last `'0'` to end).\n   - Within a segment, T-shirts **cannot be reused** unless washed.\n   - But after a `'0'`, all are washed → fresh start.\n\n4. **We want to minimize number of logo T-shirts bought.**\n\n---\n\n### **Strategy**\n\nWe can process the string in **segments** separated by `'0'`. Each segment is a maximal substring without `'0'`.\n\nFor each segment:\n- We need to assign T-shirts to days.\n- `'2'` days **must** use logo T-shirts.\n- `'1'` days can use plain or logo.\n- We have `M` plain T-shirts available **at the start of the segment** (since they were washed before).\n- We want to **minimize logo T-shirt usage** in the segment by using plain ones when possible.\n\nBut: **We are allowed to buy logo T-shirts**, and they are available from the start (clean), and we want the **minimum total number of logo T-shirts** such that **across all segments**, the usage never exceeds the total available (bought + original M? No — only plain T-shirts are M; logo T-shirts we buy are separate).\n\nWait: Important clarification:\n- We start with `M` **plain** T-shirts.\n- We can buy **logo** T-shirts.\n- So total **plain = M**, **logo = X (to be determined)**.\n\nOur goal: **find minimum X** (number of logo T-shirts to buy) such that the schedule can be followed.\n\n---\n\n### **Approach**\n\nWe can simulate day-by-day, keeping track of:\n- How many plain T-shirts are currently **available** (not worn since last wash)\n- How many logo T-shirts are currently **available**\n- But we don't know how many logo T-shirts we have — we are trying to find the **minimum required**.\n\nSo instead, we can **simulate the worst-case demand for logo T-shirts across any segment**, because:\n- Between `'0'`s, no washing occurs → T-shirts worn are \"used up\" for that segment.\n- After `'0'`, all are washed → reset.\n\nSo we can process **each segment** (group of non-zero days between zeros), and for each segment:\n- Count how many **logo T-shirts** are **required** in that segment, given that we have `M` plain T-shirts to help.\n\nBut note: `'1'` days can use plain or logo, so we should **use plain first** to save logo T-shirts.\n\nSo in a segment:\n- Let `required_logo = 0`\n- We have `M` plain T-shirts available at the start of the segment.\n- We will use plain T-shirts for as many `'1'` days as possible.\n- Any `'1'` day beyond `M` plain T-shirts must use logo.\n- All `'2'` days must use logo.\n\nBut wait: the T-shirts are worn **sequentially**, and cannot be reused within the segment.\n\nSo in a segment of consecutive non-zero days:\n- Each day uses one T-shirt.\n- We can use at most `M` **plain** T-shirts in the segment (since only `M` exist, and no washing until end).\n- So:\n  - All `'2'` days must be covered by logo T-shirts.\n  - `'1'` days: up to `M` of them can be covered by plain; the rest must be covered by logo.\n\nSo:\n- Let `ones = count of '1' in segment`\n- Let `twos = count of '2' in segment`\n- Then, number of logo T-shirts **needed in this segment** is:\n  ```\n  logo_needed = twos + max(0, ones - M)\n  ```\n\nWhy? Because we can cover `min(ones, M)` `'1'` days with plain, and the rest must use logo.\n\nThen, across all segments, we need enough logo T-shirts to cover the **maximum** logo demand in any **single segment**, because:\n- After a `'0'`, all T-shirts are washed, so we can reuse the same logo T-shirts in the next segment.\n- So the **same set of logo T-shirts** can be used in multiple segments, as long as within each segment, we don't exceed the number available.\n\nBut **within a segment**, we cannot reuse T-shirts, so we need **at least `logo_needed` distinct logo T-shirts** for that segment.\n\nTherefore, the **minimum number of logo T-shirts to buy** is the **maximum** of `logo_needed` over all segments.\n\n> Because the same logo T-shirts can be reused across segments (thanks to washing on `'0'`), we only need enough to cover the **worst-case segment**.\n\n---\n\n### **Algorithm**\n\n1. Split the string `S` into segments separated by `'0'`.\n   - Note: `'0'` itself is not part of any segment; it's a washing day.\n2. For each segment:\n   - Count number of `'1'` → `ones`\n   - Count number of `'2'` → `twos`\n   - Compute `logo_needed = twos + max(0, ones - M)`\n3. Answer = `max(logo_needed over all segments)`\n4. If no segment, answer is 0.\n\nBut note: what if the string starts or ends with `'0'`? Then we may have empty segments — skip them.\n\n---\n\n### **Example Walkthrough**\n\n#### Sample Input 1: `N=6, M=1, S=\"112022\"`\n\nSplit by `'0'`: `\"112\"`, `\"22\"`\n\nSegment 1: `\"112\"`\n- ones = 2, twos = 1\n- logo_needed = 1 + max(0, 2 - 1) = 1 + 1 = 2\n\nSegment 2: `\"22\"`\n- ones = 0, twos = 2\n- logo_needed = 2 + max(0, 0 - 1) = 2\n\n→ max = 2 → output 2 ✅\n\n#### Sample Input 2: `N=3, M=1, S=\"222\"`\n\nSegment: `\"222\"`\n- ones = 0, twos = 3\n- logo_needed = 3 + 0 = 3 → output 3 ✅\n\n#### Sample Input 3: `N=2, M=1, S=\"01\"`\n\nSplit: `\"\"`, `\"1\"` → only non-empty: `\"1\"`\n- ones = 1, twos = 0\n- logo_needed = 0 + max(0, 1 - 1) = 0 → output 0 ✅\n\n---\n\n### **Edge Cases**\n\n- All `'0'` → no segments → answer 0\n- No `'0'` → one segment: entire string\n\n---\n\n### **Implementation**\n\n```python\ndef solve():\n    import sys\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().splitlines()\n    \n    N, M = map(int, data[0].split())\n    S = data[1].strip()\n    \n    # Split by '0' to get segments\n    segments = S.split('0')\n    \n    max_logo_needed = 0\n    \n    for segment in segments:\n        if not segment:  # empty segment\n            continue\n        ones = segment.count('1')\n        twos = segment.count('2')\n        logo_needed = twos + max(0, ones - M)\n        if logo_needed > max_logo_needed:\n            max_logo_needed = logo_needed\n    \n    print(max_logo_needed)\n\nsolve()\n```\n\nLet’s test with the samples.\n\n- `\"112022\"` → split → `['112', '22']`\n  - `112`: 2 ones, 1 two → 1 + max(0,1) = 2\n  - `22`: 0 ones, 2 twos → 2 → max = 2 → output 2\n- `\"222\"` → `['222']` → 0 + 3 = 3\n- `\"01\"` → `['', '1']` → only `'1'`: 1 one, 0 twos → 0 + max(0, 1-1)=0\n\nPerfect.\n\n---\n\n### **Final Code**\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().splitlines()\n    N, M = map(int, data[0].split())\n    S = data[1].strip()\n    \n    segments = S.split('0')\n    max_logo = 0\n    \n    for seg in segments:\n        if not seg:\n            continue\n        ones = seg.count('1')\n        twos = seg.count('2')\n        logo_needed = twos + max(0, ones - M)\n        if logo_needed > max_logo:\n            max_logo = logo_needed\n    \n    print(max_logo)\n\nsolve()\n```",
