      "generated_answer": "I need to analyze this problem step by step.\n\nThe key points are:\n1. A \"change of key\" occurs when a different key is pressed compared to the last used key\n2. Case changes (like 'a' to 'A') don't count as key changes because they can be achieved with shift or caps lock\n3. I need to count how many times the user had to change to a different physical key\n\nLet me trace through the examples:\n- Example 1: \"aAbBcC\"\n  - 'a' to 'A': same key (just case change), no change\n  - 'A' to 'b': different key, count = 1\n  - 'b' to 'B': same key (just case change), no change  \n  - 'B' to 'c': different key, count = 2\n  - 'c' to 'C': same key (just case change), no change\n  - Result: 2\n\n- Example 2: \"AaAaAaaA\"\n  - All characters are 'a' or 'A', so same physical key throughout\n  - Result: 0\n\nThe algorithm:\n1. Convert each character to lowercase (or uppercase) to ignore case differences\n2. Compare each character with the previous one\n3. If they're different after case normalization, increment the counter\n4. Return the total count\n\n```python\ndef countKeyChanges(s):\n    if len(s) <= 1:\n        return 0\n    \n    changes = 0\n    prev_char = s[0].lower()\n    \n    for i in range(1, len(s)):\n        current_char = s[i].lower()\n        if current_char != prev_char:\n            changes += 1\n        prev_char = current_char\n    \n    return changes\n```",
