      "generated_answer": "Let's solve this step by step.\n\n---\n\n### **Problem Understanding**\n\nWe are given **N rectangular cuboids** in 3D space. Each cuboid is defined by two diagonal points:  \n$(X_{i,1}, Y_{i,1}, Z_{i,1})$ and $(X_{i,2}, Y_{i,2}, Z_{i,2})$,  \nwith all edges **parallel to coordinate axes**.\n\nSo, the cuboid spans:\n- $x \\in [X_{i,1}, X_{i,2}]$\n- $y \\in [Y_{i,1}, Y_{i,2}]$\n- $z \\in [Z_{i,1}, Z_{i,2}]$\n\nWe are told that **no two cuboids have overlapping volume** (intersection has volume 0), but they **may touch**.\n\nWe are to compute, **for each cuboid**, the number of **other cuboids that share a face with it** — that is, the **surfaces** (boundaries) of two cuboids intersect in a region with **positive area** (not just a line or point).\n\n---\n\n### **Key Observations**\n\n- Two cuboids **share a face** if their **surfaces intersect in a 2D region of positive area**.\n- Since all cuboids are axis-aligned, any shared face must be a **rectangle** (or square) lying on a plane perpendicular to one of the axes.\n- For two cuboids to share a face:\n  - They must be adjacent along one dimension.\n  - Their projections on the other two dimensions must **overlap in area** (i.e., intervals overlap in both dimensions).\n  - They must **touch** exactly at a face — meaning, for example, the max x of one equals the min x of the other, and their y and z ranges overlap in 2D.\n\n---\n\n### **Approach**\n\nFor each pair of cuboids $i$ and $j$, we want to check if they **share a face**.\n\nBut since $N$ can be up to $10^5$, we **cannot** check all $O(N^2)$ pairs directly.\n\nBut note: coordinates are **integers**, and bounded:  \n$0 \\leq X_{i,1} < X_{i,2} \\leq 100$, same for Y and Z.\n\nSo the **coordinates are small** (only 0 to 100), but number of cuboids is large.\n\nWe can exploit this.\n\nIdea:\n- Since coordinates are small, we can **group cuboids by their faces**.\n- Each cuboid has 6 faces:\n  - $x = x_{min}$\n  - $x = x_{max}$\n  - $y =