n\nBut then we might count the same cuboid multiple times, so we need a set.\n\nAnd the area of the query rectangle might be large.\n\nBut the number of unique cuboids that overlap is at most the number of cells, but we can break early.\n\nBut worst-case, we might do area of Q work.\n\nAnd sum over all cuboids of the surface area might be large.\n\nBut each cuboid has 6 faces, each face has area at most 10201, so total work 6 * 10201 * N = 6e9, same as before.\n\nSo no improvement.\n\nGiven the constraints, and that 6e9 is the worst-case, but in practice it might be faster, and perhaps the intended solution in C++ is O(N * constant) with constant 1e4, in Python we might need to hope or optimize.\n\nBut let's try to implement the first method and see if we can optimize with PyPy or hope that test cases are not worst-case.\n\nOr perhaps the bound is not tight because the cuboids are not on the same plane.\n\nBut anyway, let's implement the straightforward method.\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Read N.\n2. Read N lines, each with 6 integers.\n3. Store each cuboid as (x1, x2, y1, y2, z1, z2)\n4. Create dictionaries:\n   - x_plus = defaultdict(list)  # x -> list of (y1,y2,z1,z2, idx)\n   - x_minus = defaultdict(list)\n   - y_plus = defaultdict(list)  # y -> (x1,x2,z1,z2, idx)\n   - y_minus = defaultdict(list)\n   - z_plus = defaultdict(list)  # z -> (x1,x2,y1,y2, idx)\n   - z_minus = defaultdict(list)\n\n5. For i in range(N):\n   - x1, y1, z1, x2, y2, z2 = coords\n   - x_min, x_max = min(x1,x2), max(x1,x2)  # but given x1<x2, etc.\n   - similarly for y,z.\n   - x_plus[x_max].append( (y1,y2,z1,z2, i) )\n   - x_minus[x_min].append( (y1,y2,z1,z2, i) )\n   - y_plus[y_max].append( (x1,x2,z1,z2, i) )\n   - y_minus[y_min].append( (x1,x2,z1,z2, i) )\n   - z_plus[z_max].append( (x1,x2,y1,y2, i) )\n   - z_minus[z_min].append( (x1,x2,y1,y2, i) )\n\n6. For each cuboid i with (x1,x2,y1,y2,z1,z2):\n     count = 0\n     # x-faces\n     # right face: x2, check x_minus[x2]\n     for (y1j, y2j, z1j, z2j, j) in x_minus.get(x2, []):\n         if j == i: continue\n         if max(y1, y1j) < min(y2, y2j) and max(z1, z1j) < min(z2, z2j):\n             count += 1\n     # left face: x1, check x_plus[x1]\n     for (y1j, y2j, z1j, z2j, j) in x_plus.get(x1, []):\n         if j == i: continue\n         if max(y1, y1j) < min(y2, y2j) and max(z1, z1j) < min(z2, z2j):\n             count += 1\n\n     # y-faces\n     # front face: y2, check y_minus[y2]  # y2 is y_max, so top face, check cuboids that have y_min = y2\n     for (x1j, x2j, z1j, z2j, j) in y_minus.get(y2, []):\n         if j == i: continue\n         if max(x1, x1j) < min(x2, x2j) and max(z1, z1j) < min(z2, z2j):\n             count += 1\n     # back face: y1, check y_plus[y1]  # y1 is y_min, so bottom face, check cuboids with y_max = y1\n     for (x1j, x2j, z1j, z2j, j) in y_plus.get(y1, []):\n         if j == i: continue\n         if max(x1, x1j) < min(x2, x2j) and max(z1, z1j) < min(z2, z2j):\n             count += 1\n\n     # z-faces\n     # top face: z2, check z_minus[z2]  # z_max, so top, check z_min = z2\n     for (x1j, x2j, y1j, y2j, j) in z_minus.get(z2, []):\n         if j == i: continue\n         if max(x1, x1j) < min(x2, x2j) and max(y1, y1j) < min(y2, y2j):\n             count += 1\n     # bottom face: z1, check z_plus[z1]  # z_min, check z_max = z1\n     for (x1j, x2j, y1j, y2j, j) in z_plus.get(z1, []):\n         if j == i: continue\n         if max(x1, x1j) < min(x2, x2j) and max(y1, y1j) < min(y2, y2j):\n             count += 1\n\n     print(count)\n\n7. Print count for each i.\n\nBut note: in the sample, cuboid 1 and 2 share a face at z=1? Let's see.\n\nSample 1:\n- cuboid0: 0 0 0 1 1 1 -> x[0,1], y[0,1], z[0,1]\n- cuboid1: 0 0 1 1 1 2 -> x[0,1], y[0,1], z[1,2]\n- so they touch at z=1: cuboid0 has z_max=1, cuboid1 has z_min=1.\n- and x and y overlap: [0,1] and [0,1] -> overlap.\n- so in z-faces: for cuboid0, z_max=1, so check z_minus.get(1) -> cuboid1 has z_min=1, so in z_minus[1]\n  - then check x: max(0,0)=0 < min(1,1)=1 -> true\n  - y: max(0,0)=0 < min(1,1)=1 -> true\n  - so count +=1\n\nSimilarly for cuboid1, z_min=1, check z_plus.get(1) -> cuboid0 has z_max=1, so in z_plus[1], and overlap, so count +=1.\n\nAlso, do they touch in other ways? x and y are the same, but x_min=0 for both, so not touch in x or y.\n\nSo cuboid0: count=1, cuboid1: count=1.\n\ncuboid2: 1 1 1 2 2 2 -> x[1,2], y[1,2], z[1,2]\n- with cuboid0: x: 0-1 and 1-2 -> touch at x=1? cuboid0 x_max=1, cuboid2 x_min=1.\n  - y: [0,1] and [1,2] -> max(0,1)=1, min(1,2)=1 -> 1<1? False.\n  - so no overlap in y.\n  - similarly z: [0,1] and [1,2] -> overlap at z=1, but only a line.\n  - so no face share.\n\nSo only the z-face for 0 and 1.\n\ncuboid3: isolated.\n\nSo output: 1,1,0,0.\n\nMatches.\n\nNow, let's code accordingly.\n\nWe'll use the above.\n\nGiven the potential performance issue, but hoping that in practice the lists are short, or that PyPy is fast, but in Python we might need to optimize.\n\nBut for now, let's implement.\n\nNote: we are using the condition for overlap: max(a1,b1) < min(a2,b2) for intervals [a1,a2], [b1,b2].\n\nThis is correct for open or closed? Since they are closed intervals, but the intersection has positive length if the overlap is more than a point.\n\nFor example, [0,1] and [1,2] -> max(0,1)=1, min(1,2)=1, 1<1 is false, so no overlap, good.\n\n[0,2] and [1,3] -> max(0,1)=1, min(2,3)=2, 1<2 -> true.\n\nSo correct.\n\nNow, let's write the code.\n\nWe'll use sys.stdin for speed.\n\n```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not data:\n        return\n    \n    n = int(data[0])\n    cuboids = []\n    index = 1\n    for i in range(n):\n        coords = list(map(int, data[index:index+6]))\n        index += 6\n        x1, y1, z1, x2, y2, z2 = coords\n        # ensure min/max\n        x_min, x_max = min(x1, x2), max(x1, x2)\n        y_min, y_max = min(y1, y2), max(y1, y2)\n        z_min, z_max = min(z1, z2), max(z1, z2)\n        cuboids.append((x_min, x_max, y_min, y_max, z_min, z_max))\n    \n    # Dictionaries for faces\n    x_plus = defaultdict(list)  # x_max -> list of (y_min, y_max, z_min, z_max, idx)\n    x_minus = defaultdict(list) # x_min -> list of (y_min, y_max, z_min, z_max, idx)\n    y_plus = defaultdict(list)  # y_max -> list of (x_min, x_max, z_min, z_max, idx)\n    y_minus = defaultdict(list) # y_min -> list of (x_min, x_max, z_min, z_max, idx)\n    z_plus = defaultdict(list)  # z_max -> list of (x_min, x_max, y_min, y_max, idx)\n    z_minus = defaultdict(list) # z_min -> list of (x_min, x_max, y_min, y_max, idx)\n    \n    for i, (x1, x2, y1, y2, z1, z2) in enumerate(cuboids):\n        x_plus[x2].append((y1, y2, z1, z2, i))\n        x_minus[x1].append((y1, y2, z1, z2, i))\n        y_plus[y2].append((x1, x2, z1, z2, i))\n        y_minus[y1].append((x1, x2, z1, z2, i))\n        z_plus[z2].append((x1, x2, y1, y2, i))\n        z_minus[z1].append((x1, x2, y1, y2, i))\n    \n    # For each cuboid, count neighbors\n    results = []\n    for i, (x1, x2, y1, y2, z1, z2) in enumerate(cuboids):\n        count = 0\n        \n        # Check x-faces\n        # Right face: x2, check cuboids that start at x2 (x_min = x2)\n        if x2 in x_minus:\n            for (y1j, y2j, z1j, z2j, j) in x_minus[x2]:\n                if i == j:\n                    continue\n                if max(y1, y1j) < min(y2, y2j) and max(z1, z1j) < min(z2, z2j):\n                    count += 1\n        \n        # Left face: x1, check cuboids that end at x1 (x_max = x1)\n        if x1 in x_plus:\n            for (y1j, y2j, z1j, z2j, j) in x_plus[x1]:\n                if i == j:\n                    continue\n                if max(y1, y1j) < min(y2, y2j) and max(z1, z1j) < min(z2, z2j):\n                    count += 1\n        \n        # Check y-faces\n        # Top face: y2, check cuboids that start at y2 (y_min = y2)\n        if y2 in y_minus:\n            for (x1j, x2j, z1j, z2j, j) in y_minus[y2]:\n                if i == j:\n                    continue\n                if max(x1, x1j) < min(x2, x2j) and max(z1, z1j) < min(z2, z2j):\n                    count += 1\n        \n        # Bottom face: y1, check cuboids that end at y1 (y_max = y1)\n        if y1 in y_plus:\n            for (x1j, x2j, z1j, z2j, j) in y_plus[y1]:\n                if i == j:\n                    continue\n                if max(x1, x1j) < min(x2, x2j) and max(z1, z1j) < min(z2, z2j):\n                    count += 1\n        \n        # Check z-faces\n        # Top face: z2, check cuboids that start at z2 (z_min = z2)\n        if z2 in z_minus:\n            for (x1j, x2j, y1j, y2j, j) in z_minus[z2]:\n                if i == j:\n                    continue\n                if max(x1, x1j) < min(x2, x2j) and max(y1, y1j) < min(y2, y2j):\n                    count += 1\n        \n        # Bottom face: z1, check cuboids that end at z1 (z_max = z1)\n        if z1 in z_plus:\n            for (x1j, x2j, y1j, y2j, j) in z_plus[z1]:\n                if i == j:\n                    continue\n                if max(x1, x1j) < min(x2, x2j) and max(y1, y1j) < min(y2, y2j):\n                    count += 1\n        \n        results.append(count)\n    \n    for res in results:\n        print(res)\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n```\n\nLet's hope it works.\n\nWe'll run with the samples.\n\nSample 1: should give 1,1,0,0.\n\nSample 2: 3 cuboids.\n\nWe can test.\n\nBut since we are to output the code, we output as requested.\n\nNote: This might be slow for worst-case, but given the constraints and the bound on the number of cuboids per plane, it should be acceptable in many cases.\n\nWe'll output the code.",
