      "generated_answer": "**Solution Explanation**\n\nFor every empty cell we have to know how many empty cells can be reached by the\nfollowing rule  \n\n* from the current cell we may move to a 4‑neighbouring empty cell **only if**\n  none of the four neighbours of the current cell contains a magnet (`#`);\n* otherwise we cannot move any more (the current cell is a dead end).\n\nThe reachable cells form a *directed* graph:\n\n* an empty cell that has a neighbouring magnet has **no outgoing edges**,\n  but it can have incoming edges;\n* an empty cell without a neighbouring magnet (we call it **safe**) has an\n  outgoing edge to each of its empty neighbours (both safe and unsafe).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 1.   Observations  \n\n* A **unsafe** cell (has a neighbouring magnet) can never leave it, therefore\n  its degree of freedom is always `1`.\n* Between two safe cells the edges exist in both directions, i.e. they are\n  *mutually reachable*.\n  Consequently all safe cells that are connected by 4‑neighbour moves form an\n  **undirected component** of safe cells.\n* From any safe component we can additionally step once into every unsafe cell\n  that touches the component, but we stop there.\n  Hence for a whole component  \n\n```\nreachable cells = (number of safe cells in the component)\n                + (number of distinct unsafe empty cells that are neighbours\n                   of at least one safe cell of the component)\n```\n\nThe answer is the maximum of the above value over all safe components\n(and at least `1` for the unsafe cells).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 2.   Algorithm  \n\n```\n1. read the grid\n2. for every empty cell decide whether it is safe\n       safe = true  ⇔  none of the four neighbours is a magnet\n3. find all connected components consisting only of safe cells\n   (ordinary BFS/DFS on the undirected graph of safe cells)\n   while exploring one component\n        – count safe cells\n        – for each neighbour that is an unsafe empty cell,\n          count it only once inside this component\n4. answer = max( 1 ,   max size obtained in step 3 )\n```\n\nThe only delicate point is counting the unsafe neighbours **once per component**.\nWe keep an integer array `unsafe_mark[H][W]`, initially `0`.\nFor the *k*-th component we use the marker value `k`:\n\n```\nif unsafe_mark[x][y] != k:\n        unsafe_mark[x][y] = k          # first time we see this unsafe cell\n        unsafe_cnt += 1\n```\n\nNo extra clearing of the array is necessary.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.   Correctness Proof  \n\nWe prove that the algorithm outputs the required maximum degree of freedom.\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 1  \nIf a cell is unsafe, its degree of freedom equals `1`.\n\n**Proof.**  \nBy definition an unsafe cell has at least one neighbouring magnet, therefore the\nmovement rule forbids any move from this cell. The only reachable cell is the\nstarting cell itself. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 2  \nAll safe cells belonging to the same connected component (using 4‑neighbour\nadjacency) can reach each other.\n\n**Proof.**  \nInside a component every edge connects two safe cells.  \nFor a safe cell the movement rule permits moving to *any* empty neighbour,\ntherefore also to another safe neighbour.\nThus from a safe cell we can move to each adjacent safe cell, and by repeating\nthis we can walk along any path inside the component.\nConsequently the whole component is mutually reachable. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 3  \nFrom any safe cell `v` the set of reachable cells is exactly  \n\n```\nC(v) = (safe component containing v)  ∪\n       (unsafe empty cells adjacent to that component)\n```\n\n**Proof.**  \n\n*Subset*  \nTake a cell reachable from `v`.  \nIf it is safe, every step used a safe source, therefore the whole path stays\ninside the safe component of `v`; thus the cell belongs to that component.  \nIf the reachable cell is unsafe, the last step of the path must come from a\nsafe cell (otherwise we could not have moved). That safe cell lies in the same\ncomponent, so the unsafe cell is adjacent to the component.\n\n*Superset*  \nAll safe cells of the component are mutually reachable by Lemma&nbsp;2.  \nFor an unsafe cell `u` adjacent to the component pick a neighbour `s` that is\nsafe and belongs to the component. Because `s` has no neighbouring magnet,\nthe movement rule allows the step `s → u`. Hence `u` is reachable from any\nstarting safe cell of the component. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 4  \nFor a safe component `K` the algorithm computes  \n\n```\nsize(K) = |K|  +  (number of distinct unsafe neighbours of K)\n```\n\n**Proof.**  \nDuring the BFS that explores exactly the cells of `K`\n\n* each visited safe cell increments `safe_cnt`, therefore after the BFS\n  `safe_cnt = |K|`;\n* each time a neighbour is an unsafe empty cell, the algorithm checks the\n  marker array. The first encounter of this unsafe cell inside the current\n  component raises the marker to the current component id and increments\n  `unsafe_cnt`. Later encounters keep the same marker, thus the cell is counted\n  only once. Consequently `unsafe_cnt` equals the number of distinct unsafe\n  neighbours of `K`. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 5  \nFor a safe component `K` the value `size(K)` computed by the algorithm equals\nthe degree of freedom of every safe cell inside `K`.\n\n**Proof.**  \nBy Lemma&nbsp;3 the reachable set from any safe cell of `K` is exactly the union\ndescribed there, whose cardinality is `|K|` plus the number of distinct unsafe\nneighbours of `K`. By Lemma&nbsp;4 the algorithm computes the same number,\nhence `size(K)` equals the degree of freedom. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 6  \nThe algorithm’s variable `answer` equals the maximum degree of freedom over\nall empty cells.\n\n**Proof.**  \n\n*Unsafe cells*: by Lemma&nbsp;1 each contributes `1`. `answer` is initialised with\n`1`, therefore it is at least their degree.\n*Safe cells*: they belong to some component `K`. By Lemma&nbsp;5 their degree\nequals `size(K)`, and the algorithm updates `answer` with the maximum of all\nsuch `size(K)`. Hence after processing all components `answer` is the maximum\nover all safe cells as well. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Theorem  \nThe program prints the maximum degree of freedom among all cells without\nmagnets.\n\n**Proof.**  \nFrom Lemma&nbsp;6 `answer` is exactly the maximum degree of freedom.\nThe program outputs `answer`. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.   Complexity Analysis  \n\n*Let `N = H·W` ( ≤ 10⁶).*\n\n* Determining safety of each empty cell: `O(N)`.\n* BFS over all safe cells visits each safe cell once and inspects its four\n  neighbours: `O(N)`.\n* All auxiliary arrays have size `O(N)`.\n\nTherefore  \n\n```\nTime   : O(H·W)\nMemory : O(H·W)\n```\n\nBoth satisfy the limits (`H,W ≤ 1000`).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.   Reference Implementation  (Python 3)\n\n```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import deque\n\ndef solve() -> None:\n    sys.setrecursionlimit(2_000_000)\n    data = sys.stdin.read().splitlines()\n    H, W = map(int, data[0].split())\n    grid = data[1:]\n\n    # directions\n    DIRS = [(1, 0), (-1, 0), (0, 1), (0, -1)]\n\n    # 1) which cells contain a magnet\n    magnet = [[c == '#' for c in row] for row in grid]\n\n    # 2) safe = empty and no adjacent magnet\n    safe = [[False] * W for _ in range(H)]\n    for i in range(H):\n        row = grid[i]\n        for j in range(W):\n            if row[j] == '.':\n                ok = True\n                for di, dj in DIRS:\n                    ni, nj = i + di, j + dj\n                    if 0 <= ni < H and 0 <= nj < W and magnet[ni][nj]:\n                        ok = False\n                        break\n                safe[i][j] = ok\n\n    visited_safe = [[False] * W for _ in range(H)]\n    unsafe_mark = [[0] * W for _ in range(H)]   # component‑wise marker\n    cur_mark = 0\n    answer = 1          # at least one empty cell exists (degree 1)\n\n    for i in range(H):\n        for j in range(W):\n            if grid[i][j] == '.' and safe[i][j] and not visited_safe[i][j]:\n                cur_mark += 1\n                q = deque()\n                q.append((i, j))\n                visited_safe[i][j] = True\n\n                safe_cnt = 0\n                unsafe_cnt = 0\n\n                while q:\n                    x, y = q.popleft()\n                    safe_cnt += 1\n                    for dx, dy in DIRS:\n                        nx, ny = x + dx, y + dy\n                        if 0 <= nx < H and 0 <= ny < W and grid[nx][ny] == '.':\n                            if safe[nx][ny]:\n                                if not visited_safe[nx][ny]:\n                                    visited_safe[nx][ny] = True\n                                    q.append((nx, ny))\n                            else:          # unsafe empty neighbour\n                                if unsafe_mark[nx][ny] != cur_mark:\n                                    unsafe_mark[nx][ny] = cur_mark\n                                    unsafe_cnt += 1\n\n                comp_size = safe_cnt + unsafe_cnt\n                if comp_size > answer:\n                    answer = comp_size\n\n    print(answer)\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```\n\nThe program follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above and conforms\nto the required input‑output format.",
