unter\n    \n    freq = Counter(s)\n    T = max(freq.values())\n    k = T - 1\n    \n    char_indices = defaultdict(list)\n    for idx, char in enumerate(s):\n        char_indices[char].append(idx)\n    \n    surviving = set()\n    for char, indices_list in char_indices.items():\n        r = max(0, freq[char] - k)\n        surviving.update(indices_list[-r:])\n    \n    return ''.join(char for idx, char in enumerate(s) if idx in surviving)\n```\n\nBut note: `indices_list[-r:]` gives the last `r` elements, which are the largest indices — correct.\n\nWe can optimize by not using `Counter` separately, but it's fine.\n\nLet me test with `s = \"abcd\"`:\n\n- freq: each 1, T=1, k=0\n- r=1 for each\n- each char's indices: one element, last 1 → include\n- all indices survive → \"abcd\"\n\nCorrect.\n\nWe are good.\n\nNow, return only the function body as requested.\n\nNote: The problem says \"Return the function body without invoking it\".\n\nSo final answer:\n\n```python\ndef lastStringBeforeEmpty(s):\n    from collections import defaultdict, Counter\n    \n    freq = Counter(s)\n    T = max(freq.values())\n    k = T - 1\n    \n    char_indices = defaultdict(list)\n    for idx, char in enumerate(s):\n        char_indices[char].append(idx)\n    \n    surviving = set()\n    for char, indices_list in char_indices.items():\n        r = max(0, freq[char] - k)\n        surviving.update(indices_list[-r:])\n    \n    return ''.join(char for idx, char in enumerate(s) if idx in surviving)\n```",
