      "generated_answer": "We are given a graph $ G $ with $ N $ vertices and $ M $ edges. The graph is **good**, meaning that for $ K $ forbidden pairs $ (x_i, y_i) $, there is **no path** between $ x_i $ and $ y_i $ in $ G $.\n\nThen we are given $ Q $ queries. Each query adds an edge $ (p_i, q_i) $ to the graph and asks: **Is the new graph still good?**\n\nThat is, after adding the edge $ (p_i, q_i) $, is it still true that **none** of the $ K $ forbidden pairs $ (x_i, y_i) $ are connected by a path?\n\n---\n\n### Key Observations\n\n- The original graph is good: for each $ (x_i, y_i) $, $ x_i $ and $ y_i $ are in **different connected components**.\n- Adding an edge may **merge** two connected components.\n- The new graph is **not good** if, after adding the edge, **any** forbidden pair $ (x, y) $ ends up in the **same** connected component.\n\nSo the problem reduces to:\n\n> For each query $ (p, q) $, if we merge the components of $ p $ and $ q $, do **any** of the $ K $ forbidden pairs end up in the same component?\n\nBut doing this naively (simulating union and checking all $ K $ pairs for each query) would be too slow ($ Q \\times K $ up to $ 4 \\times 10^{10} $).\n\nWe need an efficient solution.\n\n---\n\n### Strategy\n\nWe can use **Union-Find (Disjoint Set Union, DSU)** to manage connected components.\n\n#### Step 1: Preprocessing\n- Build the initial graph using DSU from the $ M $ edges.\n- After building, each vertex belongs to a connected component (represented by its root in DSU).\n\nNote: The graph may have self-loops and multi-edges. DSU naturally handles these (self-loops can be ignored, multi-edges don't change connectivity).\n\n#### Step 2: Forbidden Pairs\n- We are told that in the original graph, for each $ (x_i, y_i) $, $ x_i $ and $ y_i $ are in **different components**.\n- So for each such pair, we record that **these two components must never be connected**.\n\nBut we don’t want to store per-component constraints directly.\n\nInstead, we can think:\n\n> When we add an edge $ (p, q) $, we are considering merging the components $ C_p $ and $ C_q $.  \n> The new graph is **not good** if **any** forbidden pair $ (x, y) $ has $ x \\in C_p \\cup C_q $ and $ y \\in C_p \\cup C_q $, and after merging, $ x $ and $ y $ become connected.\n\nBut even that is too vague.\n\nBetter idea:\n\nWe can **precompute** for each connected component, the set of **forbidden partner components** — but storing sets explicitly is too expensive.\n\nAlternative idea: use **union-find with rollback or virtual merging**? But $ Q $ is large.\n\nEven better idea: **reverse thinking**.\n\nBut note: we are not modifying the original graph. We are only **testing** what would happen **if** we added $ (p, q) $.\n\nSo for each query:\n\n1. Find the components of $ p $ and $ q $: $ r_p = \\text{find}(p), r_q = \\text{find}(q) $\n2. If $ r_p = r_q $: adding the edge doesn't change connectivity → graph remains good → answer **Yes**\n3. Else: we are merging components $ r_p $ and $ r_q $. The new graph is good **iff** no forbidden pair $ (x_i, y_i) $ has one endpoint in $ r_p $'s component and the other in $ r_q $'s component.\n\nWait: not quite. A forbidden pair $ (x, y) $ might already have both in $ r_p $, or both in $ r_q $? But originally, they are in different components, so they can't both be in $ r_p $ unless $ r_p $ contains both — but that would mean they are connected, contradiction.\n\nSo: each forbidden pair $ (x_i, y_i) $ has $ \\text{find}(x_i) \\ne \\text{find}(y_i) $\n\nNow, when we merge components $ A $ and $ B $, a forbidden pair $ (x, y) $ becomes connected **only if** $ x $ is in $ A $ and $ y $ is in $ B $, or vice versa.\n\nSo: the merge of $ A $ and $ B $ is **bad** if there exists a forbidden pair $ (x, y) $ such that:\n- $ \\text{find}(x) = A $ and $ \\text{find}(y) = B $, or\n- $ \\text{find}(x) = B $ and $ \\text{find}(y) = A $\n\nSo if we **precompute** all such \"forbidden merges\", we can answer each query quickly.\n\n---\n\n### Plan\n\n1. Use DSU to compute connected components of the initial graph.\n2. For each forbidden pair $ (x_i, y_i) $, get their roots: $ r_x = \\text{find}(x_i), r_y = \\text{find}(y_i) $\n   - Since $ x_i $ and $ y_i $ are not connected, $ r_x \\ne r_y $\n   - We can normalize the pair: $ (min(r_x, r_y), max(r_x, r_y)) $\n   - Store that this component pair is **forbidden to merge**\n3. But: multiple forbidden pairs may involve the same component pair? Yes.\n\nBut note: if **any** forbidden pair would become connected by merging components $ A $ and $ B $, then the merge is invalid.\n\nSo we just need to know: **is the component pair $ (A, B) $ (normalized) in the set of forbidden merges?**\n\nBut wait: a single merge of $ A $ and $ B $ may connect **multiple** forbidden pairs — but we only care whether **at least one** becomes connected.\n\nSo we can:\n\n- Create a set `forbidden_merges` of tuples $ (min(r_x, r_y), max(r_x, r_y)) $ for each forbidden pair.\n\nThen for each query $ (p, q) $:\n\n- $ r_p = \\text{find}(p), r_q = \\text{find}(q) $\n- If $ r_p == r_q $: output \"Yes\"\n- Else:\n  - $ key = (min(r_p, r_q), max(r_p, r_q)) $\n  - If $ key \\in \\text{forbidden\\_merges} $: output \"No\"\n  - Else: output \"Yes\"\n\nBUT: is that correct?\n\nWait: suppose a forbidden pair $ (x, y) $ has $ x \\in A $, $ y \\in C $, and another has $ y \\in B $, $ z \\in C $. Now if we merge $ A $ and $ B $, then $ x $ and $ y $ are still not connected (unless $ A $ and $ C $ merge), so no problem.\n\nBut if a forbidden pair $ (x, y) $ has $ x \\in A $, $ y \\in B $, then merging $ A $ and $ B $ connects them → bad.\n\nSo yes: **only the component pairs that are directly linked by a forbidden pair are dangerous**.\n\nTherefore, we only need to check whether the component merge $ (A, B) $ would connect **some** forbidden pair — which happens **iff** there exists a forbidden pair $ (x, y) $ such that $ x $ is in $ A $, $ y $ in $ B $ (or vice versa).\n\nAnd that is equivalent to: the component pair $ (A, B) $ appears as the component pair of some forbidden pair.\n\nSo we can precompute a set:\n\n```python\nforbidden_component_pairs = set()\nfor each (x, y) in forbidden_pairs:\n    rx = find(x), ry = find(y)\n    if rx > ry: rx, ry = ry, rx\n    forbidden_component_pairs.add( (rx, ry) )\n```\n\nThen for each query:\n\n```python\nrp = find(p), rq = find(q)\nif rp == rq:\n    print(\"Yes\")\nelse:\n    if rp > rq: rp, rq = rq, rp\n    if (rp, rq) in forbidden_component_pairs:\n        print(\"No\")\n    else:\n        print(\"Yes\")\n```\n\nBut wait: what if the merge of $ A $ and $ B $ causes **indirect** connection of a forbidden pair?\n\nExample: \n- Forbidden pair: $ (x, z) $\n- $ x \\in A $, $ z \\in C $\n- $ y \\in B $\n- We have $ (x, y) $ not forbidden, but $ (y, z) $ not forbidden\n- But if we merge $ A $ and $ B $, and $ B $ and $ C $ are already connected? No — components are disjoint.\n\nBut in our DSU, components are maximal. So $ A $, $ B $, $ C $ are distinct.\n\nSo merging $ A $ and $ B $ only connects vertices in $ A \\cup B $. A forbidden pair $ (x, z) $ with $ x \\in A $, $ z \\in C $ remains disconnected unless $ B $ and $ C $ are connected — but they are not.\n\nSo the **only** forbidden pairs that become connected by merging $ A $ and $ B $ are those with one endpoint in $ A $ and the other in $ B $.\n\nTherefore, the above logic is **correct**.\n\n---\n\n### Implementation Details\n\n- We'll use DSU with path compression and union by size/rank.\n- We don't actually perform unions during queries — just simulate.\n- We assume vertices are 1-indexed.\n\n---\n\n### Steps in Code\n\n1. Read $ N, M $\n2. Initialize DSU with $ N $\n3. Read $ M $ edges and union them\n4. Read $ K $, then $ K $ forbidden pairs\n   - For each, find roots of $ x_i, y_i $\n   - Store the normalized component pair in a set `bad_merges`\n5. Read $ Q $\n6. For each query $ (p, q) $:\n   - Find roots of $ p, q $\n   - If same → \"Yes\"\n   - Else, normalize the pair and check if in `bad_merges`\n     - If yes → \"No\"\n     - Else → \"Yes\"\n\n---\n\n### Edge Cases\n\n- Self-loops: DSU ignores self-loops? Actually, we can skip them, but even if we process, `union(u, u)` does nothing.\n- Multi-edges: DSU handles them naturally.\n- $ M = 0 $: no edges initially\n- $ K = 0 $: no forbidden pairs → any merge is safe\n- $ Q = 0 $: no queries\n\nBut constraints say $ K \\ge 1 $, $ Q \\ge 1 $? No: $ K \\ge 1 $, but $ Q \\ge 1 $? Constraints say $ Q \\ge 1 $. So we don't need to handle 0.\n\nWait: constraints say $ 1 \\le K \\le 2\\times10^5 $, $ 1 \\le Q \\le 2\\times10^5 $\n\nSo no zero cases.\n\n---\n\n### Let's Test on Sample\n\nSample Input:\n\n```\n6 6\n1 2\n2 3\n2 3\n3 1\n5 4\n5 5\n3\n1 5\n2 6\n4 3\n4\n2 5\n2 6\n5 6\n5 4\n```\n\nStep 1: DSU with 6 nodes\n\nEdges:\n- 1-2 → union(1,2)\n- 2-3 → union(2,3) → now 1,2,3 connected\n- 2-3 → duplicate → no change\n- 3-1 → already connected\n- 5-4 → union(5,4) → 4,5 connected\n- 5-5 → self-loop → skip\n\nSo components:\n- C1: {1,2,3}\n- C2: {4,5}\n- C3: {6}\n\nLet’s assign roots:\n- find(1)=find(2)=find(3)=1 (say)\n- find(4)=find(5)=4\n- find(6)=6\n\nForbidden pairs:\n\n1. (1,5): find(1)=1, find(5)=4 → component pair (1,4)\n2. (2,6): find(2)=1, find(6)=6 → (1,6)\n3. (4,3): find(4)=4, find(3)=1 → (1,4) again\n\nSo `bad_merges = {(1,4), (1,6)}`\n\nNow queries:\n\n1. (2,5): find(2)=1, find(5)=4 → merge (1,4) → (1,4) in bad_merges → **No**\n2. (2,6): find(2)=1, find(6)=6 → merge (1,6) → in bad_merges → **No**\n3. (5,6): find(5)=4, find(6)=6 → merge (4,6) → (4,6) not in bad_merges → **Yes**\n4. (5,4): find(5)=4, find(4)=4 → same component → **Yes**\n\nMatches expected output.\n\nPerfect.\n\n---\n\n### Code\n\nWe'll implement DSU with path compression and union by size.\n\nNote: We don't need to store the graph, just use DSU.\n\n```python\nimport sys\nsys.setrecursionlimit(300000)\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    it = iter(data)\n    n = int(next(it)); m = int(next(it))\n    \n    parent = list(range(n+1))\n    size = [1] * (n+1)\n    \n    def find(x):\n        if parent[x] != x:\n            parent[x] = find(parent[x])\n        return parent[x]\n    \n    def union(x, y):\n        rx = find(x)\n        ry = find(y)\n        if rx == ry:\n            return\n        if size[rx] < size[ry]:\n            rx, ry = ry, rx\n        parent[ry] = rx\n        size[rx] += size[ry]\n    \n    # Read M edges\n    for _ in range(m):\n        u = int(next(it)); v = int(next(it))\n        if u != v:  # skip self-loop? but union handles it\n            union(u, v)\n    \n    # Now compress all paths\n    for i in range(1, n+1):\n        find(i)\n    \n    k = int(next(it))\n    bad_merges = set()\n    \n    for _ in range(k):\n        x = int(next(it)); y = int(next(it))\n        rx = find(x)\n        ry = find(y)\n        if rx > ry:\n            rx, ry = ry, rx\n        bad_merges.add((rx, ry))\n    \n    q = int(next(it))\n    out_lines = []\n    for _ in range(q):\n        p = int(next(it)); q_val = int(next(it))\n        rp = find(p)\n        rq = find(q_val)\n        if rp == rq:\n            out_lines.append(\"Yes\")\n        else:\n            if rp > rq:\n                rp, rq = rq, rp\n            if (rp, rq) in bad_merges:\n                out_lines.append(\"No\")\n            else:\n                out_lines.append(\"Yes\")\n    \n    print(\"\\n\".join(out_lines))\n\nmain()\n```\n\nLet me run through the sample again to ensure no bug.\n\nIn DSU, after unions, we do `find(i)` for all to compress.\n\nYes.\n\nNote: when we do `union(u, v)` for self-loop `u==v`, we skip? We don't have to, because `find(u)==find(v)` already. But in code, we do `if u != v` — actually, we can remove that condition because union handles it. But it's harmless.\n\nBut in the sample, we have `5 5` — self-loop. We skip it? In code: `if u != v` → skip. But even if we don't skip, `union(5,5)` does nothing. So either way.\n\nBut to