      "generated_answer": "We are asked to compute, for each player, the **expected number of times the team containing that player wins a match** over the course of a tournament.\n\n---\n\n### 🔍 Problem Understanding\n\n- Initially, each player is in their own team.\n- There are $ N-1 $ matches.\n- In each match $ i $:\n  - Teams containing players $ p_i $ and $ q_i $ (from different teams) compete.\n  - The probability that the first team (with $ p_i $) wins is $ \\frac{a}{a+b} $, where $ a $ and $ b $ are the sizes of the two teams.\n  - The losing team is merged into the winning one (but since both teams merge regardless, it's just union).\n- We are to compute, **for each player**, the **expected number of times** that the **team containing that player wins a match** during the tournament.\n\nNote: Each match contributes **1 win** to the winning team, so each match adds 1 to the total count of wins. But each player's expected number of wins is the sum over matches of the probability that their team wins that match.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Key Insight\n\nLet $ E_i $ be the expected number of times the team containing player $ i $ wins a match.\n\nWe can think of this as:\n\n$$\nE_i = \\sum_{\\text{match } j} \\Pr[\\text{team containing player } i \\text{ wins match } j]\n$$\n\nSo we need to compute, for each player $ i $, the sum over all matches of the probability that the team containing $ i $ wins that match.\n\n---\n\n### 🧠 Strategy\n\nWe simulate the tournament using a **Union-Find (Disjoint Set Union)** data structure to track:\n- Which team (component) a player belongs to.\n- The size of each team.\n\nBut we also need to **track the probability** that a given team wins a match.\n\nHowever, **expectations are linear**, so we can compute the contribution of each match independently.\n\nFor each match $ j $, involving teams $ A $ (containing $ p_j $) and $ B $ (containing $ q_j $), with sizes $ a $ and $ b $:\n\n- The probability that team $ A $ wins is $ \\frac{a}{a+b} $\n- The probability that team $ B $ wins is $ \\frac{b}{a+b} $\n\nThen, **every player in team $ A $** gets an expected contribution of $ \\frac{a}{a+b} $ to their total expected win count from this match.\n\nSimilarly, every player in team $ B $ gets $ \\frac{b}{a+b} $.\n\nSo we can:\n- Process each match in order.\n- For each match, find the teams (roots) of $ p_j $ and $ q_j $, get their sizes.\n- Add $ \\frac{a}{a+b} $ to every player in team $ A $\n- Add $ \\frac{b}{a+b} $ to every player in team $ B $\n- Then merge the two teams.\n\nBut: $ N \\leq 2 \\times 10^5 $, and we can't update all players in a team for each match (that would be $ O(N^2) $).\n\n---\n\n### 🚀 Efficient Solution Idea\n\nWe **cannot update all players** in a team every time. Instead, we use **lazy propagation on DSU trees** or **contribution technique**.\n\nBut here's a better idea:\n\nWe maintain for each component (team) a **value array** or **additive lazy tag**, but DSU doesn't naturally support that.\n\nAlternative: Instead of updating every player, we can use **union by size** and **propagate contributions** via a tree structure.\n\nWait — another key idea:\n\nLet’s maintain an array `ans[i]` for each player, initially 0.\n\nFor each match:\n- Find roots of $ p_i $ and $ q_i $\n- Get sizes $ a $, $ b $\n- Compute $ \\frac{a}{a+b} $, $ \\frac{b}{a+b} $\n- Then we want to **add $ \\frac{a}{a+b} $ to all players in team of $ p_i $**\n- And **add $ \\frac{b}{a+b} $ to all players in team of $ q_i $**\n\nBut again, updating all players is expensive.\n\nInstead, we can use **DSU with lazy values stored per component**, and only finalize the values at the end.\n\nBut we can't easily distribute values to all members unless we track them.\n\nAlternatively, we can use **small-to-large merging** and maintain a list of players in each component.\n\nSince total number of merges is $ N-1 $, and using small-to-large, the total cost of merging lists is $ O(N \\log N) $, which is acceptable for $ N = 2 \\times 10^5 $.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Plan\n\nWe will:\n1. Use DSU with **small-to-large merging**.\n2. For each component, maintain:\n   - `root`: representative\n   - `size`: number of players\n   - `members`: list of players in the component (for updating answer later)\n3. Maintain an array `E[i]` for expected wins of player `i`, initially 0.\n4. For each match:\n   - Find components of $ p_i $ and $ q_i $\n   - Let $ a = \\text{size}_A $, $ b = \\text{size}_B $\n   - Add $ \\frac{a}{a+b} $ to **every player in component A**\n   - Add $ \\frac{b}{a+b} $ to **every player in component B**\n   - Merge the two components (using small-to-large to minimize cost)\n\nBut updating every player in the component each time would be expensive unless we do it smartly.\n\nBut note: we are doing $ N-1 $ matches, and with small-to-large merging, the total number of player updates is $ O(N \\log N) $, which is acceptable.\n\nSo we can do:\n\n```python\ncomp[i] = [i]  # initially\nsize[i] = 1\n```\n\nThen for each match:\n- Find the components of $ p_i $ and $ q_i $\n- Let $ A $, $ B $ be the two components\n- For each player in $ A $: add $ \\frac{a}{a+b} $\n- For each player in $ B $: add $ \\frac{b}{a+b} $\n- Merge $ A $ and $ B $ into one component\n\nWe must use **modular arithmetic**, since we are to output modulo $ 998244353 $.\n\n---\n\n### 🔢 Modular Arithmetic\n\nWe need to compute fractions modulo $ 998244353 $.\n\nFor a fraction $ \\frac{a}{a+b} \\mod 998244353 $, we compute:\n\n$$\na \\cdot \\text{inv}(a+b) \\mod 998244353\n$$\n\nwhere `inv(x)` is the modular inverse modulo $ 998244353 $.\n\nWe can use `pow(x, 998244353-2, 998244353)` for inverse.\n\n---\n\n### 🧪 Steps\n\n1. Read $ N $\n2. Initialize:\n   - `ans = [0]*(N+1)`  # 1-indexed\n   - `comp = [ [] for _ in range(N+1) ]`\n   - `parent = [i for i in range(N+1)]` for DSU find\n   - For i from 1 to N: `comp[i] = [i]`\n3. For each match:\n   - Read $ p, q $\n   - Find root of $ p $: `rp`, root of $ q $: `rq`\n   -