know: Is the intersection over all `i` of their valid `t0` sets non-empty?\n\nWe can do this by collecting all **candidate boundaries** and checking points near them.\n\nBut a more efficient method:\n\n### Key Insight: The valid `t0` must satisfy:\nFor all `i`, `t0` must lie in a cyclic interval of length `A` starting at `(W - D_i) % W`.\n\nSo the intersection of all these intervals must be non-empty.\n\nWe can do:\n\n1. For each `D_i`, compute `start_i = (W - D_i) % W`\n2. The valid `t0` for this plan is the cyclic interval `[start_i, start_i + A)` mod `W`\n3. We want to know if the intersection of all these cyclic intervals is non-empty.\n\nHow to compute intersection of cyclic intervals?\n\nWe can \"unwrap\" the circle into a line of length `2*W`, and represent each interval as:\n- If `start_i + A <= W`: one interval `[start_i, start_i + A)`\n- Else: two intervals: `[start_i, W)` and `[0, start_i + A - W)`\n\nBut since we are intersecting many such sets, we can instead try to find **candidate `t0` values** that might lie in all intervals.\n\nNote: If the intersection is non-empty, then there is some `t0` that lies in all intervals.\n\nMoreover, **if such a `t0` exists, then the set of valid `t0` is a union of intervals**, and the **endpoints** of these intervals are determined by the boundaries of the individual intervals.\n\nSo we can collect all **start** and **end** points of the individual intervals (mod W), and check the candidate points: the **start points** of the intervals (because if an intersection exists, it must contain a start point or be near it).\n\nBut actually, a standard trick is: **if the intersection of cyclic intervals is non-empty, then one of the start points is in the intersection**, or we can check the point just after a wrap.\n\nBut safer: collect all **candidate `t0` values** which are the **start points** of the intervals, and also the point `0`, and check if any of them lies in **all** intervals.\n\nBut wait: it's possible that the intersection lies strictly between two start points.\n\nBut here's a better idea:\n\nLet’s suppose we fix `t0`. Then for each `i`, we need:\n$$\n(t0 + D_i) \\mod W < A\n$$\n\nLet’s define `pos_i = (t0 + D_i) % W`\n\nWe can rephrase: For all `i`, there exists an integer `k_i` such that:\n$$\nk_i * W \\leq t0 + D_i < k_i * W + A\n\\Rightarrow t0 \\in [k_i * W - D_i, k_i * W + A - D_i)\n$$\n\nBut `t0 ∈ [0, W)`, so `k_i` is either 0 or 1 (since `t0 + D_i ≥ D_i ≥ 1`, and `t0 < W`, so `t0 + D_i < W + D_i`)\n\nBut `D_i` can be large, so `k_i = floor((t0 + D_i)/W)`\n\nBut we don’t know `k_i`.\n\nAlternative idea:\n\nLet’s suppose we **fix the first plan**, say `D_1`. Then for `t0` to make `D_1` fall on a holiday:\n$$\n(t0 + D_1) \\mod W < A\n\\Rightarrow t0 \\mod W \\in [ (W - D_1) % W, (W - D_1 + A - 1) % W ] \\quad \\text{(mod W)}\n$$\n\nSo the valid `t0` must lie in a cyclic interval of length `A`.\n\nLet’s define:\n- `base = (W - D_1) % W`\n- Then valid `t0` for plan 1 is:\n  - If `base + A <= W`: interval `[base, base + A)`\n  - Else: `[base, W)` and `[0, base + A - W)`\n\nNow, we can **check only the endpoints** of this interval, because if the intersection is non-empty, then either:\n- One of the endpoints is valid, or\n- We can check a point inside.\n\nBut actually, we can try **candidate values** of `t0` that are:\n- `base`\n- `(base + A - 1) % W` (last point of the interval)\n- `0` if the interval wraps\n- And also check `base` and `base + A - W` if wrap\n\nBut a more robust method: **try all start points of the intervals**, because if there is a `t0` that satisfies all, then shifting `t0` to the start of one of the intervals might still work? Not necessarily.\n\nBetter idea from known problems: **Check if the maximum gap between consecutive (D_i mod W) is large enough**?\n\nWait, here's a known similar problem: scheduling on circular calendar.\n\nLet’s try to reframe.\n\nLet’s define `X = t0`. We want:\n$$\n(X + D_i) \\mod W < A \\quad \\forall i\n$$\n\nLet’s define `E_i = D_i mod W`, but no, because `D_i` can be larger than `W`.\n\nBut `(X + D_i) mod W = (X + (D_i mod W)) mod W`, so we can define `d_i = D_i % W`\n\nBut wait: `(X + D_i) mod W = (X + (D_i mod W)) mod W` → yes, correct.\n\nSo define `d_i = D_i % W`\n\nThen condition: `(X + d_i) % W < A`\n\nBut note: `D_i` can be huge, but modulo `W` is safe.\n\nBut wait: is that correct?\n\nYes:\n$$\n(X + D_i) \\mod W = (X + (D_i \\mod W)) \\mod W\n$$\n\nSo we can reduce `D_i` modulo `W`.\n\nBut caution: if `W` is large, `D_i` might be less than `W`, so `d_i = D_i`.\n\nBut in any case, we can define `d_i = D_i % W`\n\nNow, we want:\n$$\n(X + d_i) \\mod W < A \\quad \\forall i\n$$\n\nLet’s sort the `d_i`? But they are already increasing, but modulo `W` might not preserve order.\n\nBut `D_i` are increasing, but `d_i = D_i % W` may wrap.\n\nBut note: `D_i` can be up to `1e9`, `W = A+B` can be up to `2e9`, so `D_i` might be less than `W`, so `d_i = D_i`.\n\nBut not necessarily.\n\nSo let’s define `d_i = D_i % W`\n\nNow, the condition is that all `(X + d_i) mod W < A`\n\nNow, imagine placing `N` points on a circle of length `W`: at positions `d_i`\n\nWe want to find an `X` such that when we rotate the circle by `-X`, all points fall into `[0, A)`\n\nEquivalently: is there a rotation such that all `d_i` fall into an arc of length `A` that starts at some point?\n\nWait: no.\n\nWe want `(X + d_i) mod W < A` → so `d_i mod W` must lie in `[-X, A - X)` mod `W`\n\nSo the set `{d_i}` must be contained in some interval of length `A` on the circle.\n\nWait! Yes!\n\nLet’s define `Y = (-X) mod W`, then:\n$$\n(X + d_i) \\mod W < A \\iff d_i \\mod W \\in [Y, Y + A) \\mod W\n$$\n\nSo the condition is: **the set `{d_i}` is contained in some arc of length `A` on the circle of length `W`**.\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to:\n\n> Given `N` points on a circle of length `W` (at positions `d_i = D_i % W`), is there an arc of length `A` that contains all of them?\n\nYes!\n\nThis is a classic problem.\n\n### Solution Steps\n\n1. Let `W = A + B`\n2. Compute `d_i = D_i % W` for all `i`\n3. Sort the `d_i` (because modulo may make them unsorted)\n4. Check if all points can be covered by some arc of length `A`\n\nHow to check if all points lie in some arc of length `A`?\n\n- Sort the `d_i`: let `d[0] <= d[1] <= ... <= d[N-1]`\n- Duplicate the array: `d[i + N] = d[i] + W` for circularity\n- Use a sliding window to find if there is a window of `N` consecutive points (in circular order) such that `d[j] - d[i] < A` for all in window, but actually: the span of the window is `d[j] - d[i] <= A`?\n\nWait: we want all points in an interval of length `A`.\n\nSo: is there a starting point such that all `d_i` are in `[x, x + A]` mod `W`?\n\nBut since we are on a circle, the complement is: the **maximum gap** between consecutive points (in circular order) is `> W - A`, then the points **cannot** be covered by an arc of length `A`.\n\nBut more directly:\n\nWe can try all possible starting points of the arc.\n\nBut efficient way:\n\n- Sort the points: `d[0..N-1]`\n- Duplicate: `d[i]` and `d[i] + W`\n- For each `i` from `0` to `N-1`, find the farthest `j` such that `d[j] - d[i] < A` (using two pointers)\n- If `j - i + 1 >= N`, then all points are in `[d[i], d[j]]` which has length `< A`, so yes.\n\nBut we want to know if there exists an interval of length `A` that contains all `N` points.\n\nBut we can do:\n\n- For each `i`, consider the arc starting at `d[i]` of length `A`: `[d[i], d[i] + A)`\n- Count how many points are in it (using upper_bound)\n- But because of modulo, if `d[i] + A > W`, it wraps.\n\nBut easier: use the **complement**: the points can be covered by an arc of length `A` if and only if the **maximum gap** between consecutive points (in circular order) is `>= W - A + 1`? No.\n\nActually:\n\n- The points **cannot** be covered by an arc of length `A` if and only if the **minimum arc** that covers all points has length `> A`\n\nBut the minimum arc that covers all points on a circle is: `W - max_gap`\n\nWhere `max_gap` is the maximum of:\n- `d[i+1] - d[i]` for `i=0..N-2`\n- and `d[0] + W - d[N-1]`\n\nThen the smallest arc that contains all points has length = `W - max_gap`\n\nTherefore, all points can be covered by an arc of length `A` **if and only if**:\n$$\nW - \\text{max\\_gap} \\leq A\n\\iff \\text{max\\_gap} \\geq W - A\n$$\n\nWait: no.\n\nLet me clarify:\n\n- The **largest gap** between consecutive points is `G_max`\n- Then the **smallest arc** that contains all points has length `W - G_max`\n- We want this arc length `<= A`, so:\n  $$\n  W - G\\_max \\leq A \\iff G\\_max \\geq W - A\n  $$\n\nBut wait: that would mean: if the largest gap is at least `W - A`, then the rest can be covered in `A`?\n\nNo, reverse:\n\n- If the largest gap is `G_max`, then the points are clustered in the remaining `W - G_max` length.\n- So the smallest arc containing all points has length `W - G_max`\n- We want `W - G_max <= A` → so that they can fit in an arc of length `A`\n\nTherefore:\n$$\n\\text{Answer is \"Yes\"} \\iff W - \\text{max\\_gap} \\leq A\n\\iff \\text{max\\_gap} \\geq W - A\n$$\n\nWait: `W - max_gap <= A` → `max_gap >= W - A`\n\nYes.\n\nBut let's test with sample:\n\n---\n\n### Sample 1\n\n`N=3, A=2, B=5` → `W=7`, `A=2`\n\n`D = [1,2,9]`\n\nCompute `d_i = D_i % 7 = [1,2,2]` → wait, `9%7=2`\n\nSo `d = [1,2,2]` → unique: `[1,2]`\n\nBut we should keep duplicates? Yes, but for gaps, duplicates don't create gaps.\n\nBut better to deduplicate? No, because multiple plans on same day is fine.\n\nBut for gap calculation, we can sort and remove duplicates? Actually, no: if two points are at same location, gap is 0.\n\nBut for consecutive points, we can sort and compute differences.\n\nSo `d = [1,2,2]` → sort: `[1,2,2]`\n\nRemove duplicates? Or keep? For gap, we can take unique positions.\n\nBecause two points at same place don't affect gap.\n\nSo unique sorted: `[1,2]`\n\nNow, gaps:\n- `2 - 1 = 1`\n- `(1 + 7) - 2 = 6` → circular gap\n\nSo `max_gap = max(1,6) = 6`\n\nThen `W - max_gap = 7 - 6 = 1`\n\nWe want `1 <= A=2` → yes → so answer is **Yes** → matches.\n\n---\n\n### Sample 2\n\n`N=2, A=5, B=10` → `W=15`, `A=5`\n\n`D = [10,15]`\n\n`d_i = [10, 15%15=0]` → `[10,0]`\n\nUnique sorted: `[0,10]`\n\nGaps:\n- `10 - 0 = 10`\n- `(0 + 15) - 10 = 5` → circular\n\n`max_gap = 10`\n\n`W - max_gap = 15 - 10 = 5`\n\nWe want `5 <= A=5` → yes → so answer is **Yes**?\n\nBut expected output is **No**.\n\nContradiction.\n\nWait! What?\n\nWait: `W - max_gap = 5`, `A=5`, so `5<=5` → yes → should be \"Yes\", but expected \"No\".\n\nSo something is wrong.\n\nLet me recheck.\n\n`D = [10,15]`\n\n`W = 15`\n\n`d = [10, 0]`\n\nWe want to know: is there an arc of length `A=5` that contains both `0` and `10`?\n\n- Arc from `0` to `5`: contains `0`, not `10`\n- Arc from `10` to `15` (i.e., `10` to `0`): contains `10`, and `0`? `0` is at boundary.\n\nBut `[10, 15)` → `10` to `15`, but `15 mod 15 = 0`, so does it include `0`?\n\nNo: `[10,15)` is `[10,14.999]`, does not include `0`.\n\nTo include both `0` and `10`, the arc must cover from `10` to `0` across the boundary.\n\nFor example, `[12, 17)` mod `15` = `[12,15)` and `[0,2)`\n\nDoes it contain `0`? Yes. `10`? No.\n\nTo include `10` and `0`, the arc must be at least from `10` to `0`, which is length `5` (from `10` to `15`) plus `0` to `0` → but `0` is at `0`, so arc from `10` to `0` has length `5`? `10` to `15` is `5`, and `0` is at `0`, so if arc is `[10, 15)` → doesn't include `0`.\n\nBut if arc is `[10, 15]`? But we are dealing with half-open?\n\nActually, in modular arithmetic, an arc of length `A` can be closed or open.\n\nBut in our case, the condition is `(X + d_i) mod W < A`, which is half-open: `[0, A)`\n\nSo the arc is `[x, x+A)` mod `W`.\n\nSo to include `0` and `10`, we need an interval `[x, x+5)` that contains both.\n\nCan we find such `x`?\n\nSuppose `x = 12`: then `[12,17)` → `[12,15)` and `[0,2)` → contains `0`, but not `10`\n\n`x = 5`: `[5,10)` → contains neither `0` nor `10`? `10` not included.\n\n`x = 10`: `[10,15)` → contains `10`, not `0`\n\n`x = 0`: `[0,5)` → contains `0`, not `10`\n\n`x = 6`: `[6,11)` → contains nothing\n\n`x = 9`: `[9,14)` → contains `10`, not `0`\n\n`x = 1`: `[1,6)` → contains nothing\n\nNo `x` works.\n\nSo answer should be **No**, which matches.\n\nBut according to gap method: `max_gap = max(10-0, (0+15)-10) = max(10,5)=10`\n\n`W - max_gap = 5`, `A=5`, so `5<=5` → yes, but actual answer is no.\n\nSo the condition `W - max_gap <= A` is **necessary and sufficient**?\n\nBut here `W - max_gap = 5`, `A=5`, but still not coverable.\n\nWhy?\n\nThe **smallest arc containing all points** has length `W - max_gap = 5`\n\nBut in this case, the points are at `0` and `10`.\n\nThe gap between `0` and `10` is `10` (from `0` to `10`), and from `10` to `0` is `5` (via `15`)\n\nSo the largest gap is `10`\n\nThen the points are clustered in the complement: from `10` to `0` (length `5`), which includes both `10` and `0`?\n\nBut `10` to `0` is `5` units: `10,11,12,13,14,0` — but `0` is at the end.\n\nBut the arc from `10` to `0` (exclusive) is `[10,15)` → contains `10`, but not `0`.\n\nTo include `0`, we need to go to `0`.\n\nBut the arc `[10,15)` ∪ `[0,0]` is not contiguous unless we include `0`.\n\nBut in circle, the arc from `10` to `0` clockwise is length `5`, and it contains all points? Only if we define it as `[10, 0]` closed.\n\nBut our arc is `[x, x+A)`, half-open.\n\nThe arc `[10, 15)` contains `10`, not `0`.\n\nThe arc `[10, 15)` and `[0,0]` is not possible.\n\nThe only way to include `0` is to have `x <= 0 < x+A`\n\nSimilarly for `10`: `x <= 10 < x+A` (mod)\n\nBut `x <= 10 < x+5` → `x <= 10` and `x > 5` (because `10 < x+5` → `x > 5`)\n\nSo `x ∈ (5,10]`\n\nAlso, `x <= 0 < x+5` → since `0 < x+5` → `x > -5` → always true since `x>=0`\n\nAnd `x <= 0` → so `x <= 0`\n\nBut `x > 5` and `x <= 0` impossible.\n\nSo no such `x`.\n\nTherefore, the points cannot be covered.\n\nBut the smallest arc containing them should be of length `5`? From `10` to `0` inclusive?\n\nBut in continuous terms, the shortest arc containing both `0` and `10` is either:\n- From `0` to `10`: length `10`\n- From `10` to `0`: length `5`\n\nSo the shorter one is `5`, and it contains both points if we define it as `[10,0]` closed.\n\nBut in our case, the arc is `[x, x+A)`, which is half-open.\n\nAnd the point `0` is at position `0`.\n\nTo include `0`, we need `x <= 0 < x+A`\n\nSimilarly, to include `10`, `x <= 10 < x+A`\n\nBut `10 < x+A` → `x > 10 - A = 5`\n\nAnd `x <= 0`\n\nSo `x > 5` and `x <= 0` → impossible.\n\nSo even though the points are within a arc of length `5` (from `10` to `0`), because of the half-open interval and the way the circle wraps, it might not be coverable.\n\nBut wait, can we take `x = 12`? Then `[12,17)` = `[12,15) ∪ [0,2)` → contains `0`, not `10`\n\n`x = 10`: `[10,15)` → contains `10`, not `0`\n\n`x = 0`: `[0,5)` → contains `0`, not `10`\n\nNo.\n\nBut the arc from `10` to `0` of length `5` would be `[10,15)` and it does not include `0`.\n\nSo to include `0`, we need to start at most at `0`, and to include `10`, we need to start at most at `10`, and end at least at `10` and at least at `0`.\n\nThe only way to include both is to have an arc that covers from `10` to `0` across the boundary, but then the arc must include `[10,15)` and `[0,0]`, but `0` is a single point.\n\nBut if we take `x = 10`, then `x+A=15`, so `[10,15)` includes `10` to `14.999`, not `0`.\n\nIf we take `x = 15`, but `x` is mod `W`, so `x=0`.\n\nSo no.\n\nTherefore, the condition is not just about the points being in a arc of length `A`, but about the existence of `x` such that all `d_i` are in `[x, x+A) mod W`.\n\nAnd this is equivalent to: the number of points in some interval of length `A` is `N`.\n\nSo we can do:\n\n- Sort the unique `d_i` (or with duplicates, but for coverage, duplicates don't matter)\n- Use a sliding window on the doubled array.\n\nLet’s do that.\n\n### Final Algorithm\n\n1. `W = A + B`\n2. `d = sorted([D_i % W for i in range(N)])`\n3. Create `d2 = d + [x + W for x in d]`\n4. Use two pointers or binary search to find if there exists an interval of length `A` that contains all `N` points.\n\nBut we want to know if there exists an `x` such that all `d_i` are in `[x, x+A) mod W`.\n\nThis is equivalent to: there exists an `i` such that the number of `d_j` in `[d[i], d[i] + A)` is `N` (mod W)\n\nBut because of wrap, we can do:\n\nFor each `i` in `0..N-1`:\n- Consider the arc starting at `d[i]` of length `A`: `[d[i], d[i] + A)`\n- Count how many `d_j` are in this arc, considering wrap.\n\nBut to handle wrap, we can use the doubled array.\n\nBut easier: for each `i`, let’s consider the arc `[d[i], d[i] + A)`\n\nIn the doubled array, find the number of `d_j` in `[d[i], d[i] + A)`\n\nBut also, if `d[i] + A > W`, then the arc wraps, and we need to count points in `[d[i], W)` and in `[0, d[i] + A - W)`\n\nBut with doubled array, we can do:\n\n- For each `i` in `0..N-1`:\n  - Let `left = d[i]`\n  - Let `right = d[i] + A`\n  - In the doubled array `d2`, count how many of the original `d` are in `[left, right)`\n  - But only among the first `N` points? No.\n\nActually, we can do:\n\n- In `d2`, for each `i` in `0..N-1`:\n  - Find the number of `j` in `0..N-1` such that `d[j]` is in `[d[i], d[i] + A)` mod W.\n\nBut using `d2`, we can find the number of `d2[j]` in `[d[i], d[i] + A)` for `j` in `0..2N-1`, but only count those with `j < N` or use only the first occurrence.\n\nBetter: for each `i`, consider the window in `d2` starting from `i` to `i+N-1`, but not necessarily.\n\nStandard method:\n\nTo check if all points can be covered by an arc of length `A`, we can:\n\n- For each `i` in `0..N-1`:\n  - Let `start = d[i]`\n  - Let `end = start + A`\n  - Find the largest `j` such that `d[j] < end` (in circular sense)\n\nBut using doubled array:\n\n- `d2 = d + [x + W for x in d]`\n- For each `i` in `0..N-1`:\n  - Find the largest `k` such that `d2[k] < d2[i] + A`\n  - Then the number of points in the arc is `k - i + 1` if `k < i + N`, but we want to see if it covers all `N` points.\n\nActually, we want to know if there is an arc of length `A` that contains all `N` points.\n\nBut it's easier to check for each `i` whether the arc starting at `d[i]` covers all points.\n\nBut the arc doesn't have to start at a data point.\n\nHowever, the optimal arc can be chosen to start at a data point, because if it doesn't, we can slide it left until it hits a point.\n\nSo it's sufficient to try `x = d[i]` for some `i`.\n\nSo for each `i`:\n- Let `x = d[i]`\n- Count how many `d_j` are in `[x, x+A) mod W`\n\nIf for some `i`, the count is `N`, then yes.\n\nHow to count with wrap?\n\nIf `x + A <= W`:\n- count = number of `d_j` in `[x, x+A)`\n- can use bisect\nElse:\n- count = number in `[x, W)` + number in `[0, x+A-W)`\n\nWe can do this.\n\nLet’s implement that.\n\nBut in Sample 2: `d = [0,10]`, `W=15`, `A=5`\n\nTry `x=0`: arc `[0,5)` -> contains `0`, not `10` -> count=1\nTry `x=10`: arc `[10,15)` -> contains `10`, not `0` -> count=1\n\nNo arc covers both.\n\nSample 1: `d = [1,2,2]` -> unique or with duplicates? We can use the list as is.\n\nBut for counting, duplicates are fine.\n\n`d = [1,2,2]`, `W=7`, `A=2`\n\nTry `x=1`: arc `[1,3)` -> contains `1,2,2` -> count=3 -> yes\n\nSo answer yes.\n\nSample 3: `N=4, A=347, B=347` -> `W=694`\n`D = [347,700,710,715]` wait, input is `347 700 705 710`\n\n`D = [347,700,705,710]`\n\n`d_i = D_i % 694`\n\n- `347 % 694 = 347`\n- `700 % 694 = 6`\n- `705 % 694 = 11`\n- `710 % 694 = 16`\n\nSo `d = [347,6,11,16]`\n\nSort: `[6,11,16,347]`\n\nNow try each as start:\n\nTry `x=6`: arc `[6,6+347)= [6,353)`\n- `6,11,16` in, `347` in? `347<353` yes -> count=4 -> yes\n\nSo answer yes.\n\nTherefore, algorithm:\n\n```python\nimport sys\nimport bisect\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not data:\n        return\n    \n    N = int(data[0]); A = int(data[1]); B = int(data[2])\n    D = list(map(int, data[3:3+N]))\n  