rges from any starting point.\n\nWe'll keep it.\n\nFinal code:\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    it = iter(data)\n    N = int(next(it))\n    M = int(next(it))\n    \n    wheels = []\n    for _ in range(N):\n        C = int(next(it))\n        P = int(next(it))\n        S = [int(next(it)) for _ in range(P)]\n        wheels.append((C, P, S))\n    \n    # dp[s] = expected minimum cost from state s\n    dp = [0.0] * (M + 1)  # dp[M] = 0\n    # Initialize states below M to a large value\n    for s in range(M):\n        dp[s] = 10**15\n    \n    eps = 1e-12\n    max_iter = 100000\n    for _ in range(max_iter):\n        max_diff = 0.0\n        # Update from high s to low s: M-1 down to 0\n        for s in range(M-1, -1, -1):\n            best_expected = float('inf')\n            for (C, P, S) in wheels:\n                total = 0.0\n                for point in S:\n                    next_state = s + point\n                    if next_state >= M:\n                        next_state = M\n                    total += dp[next_state]\n                expected_cost = C + total / P\n                if expected_cost < best_expected:\n                    best_expected = expected_cost\n            diff = abs(best_expected - dp[s])\n            if diff > max_diff:\n                max_diff = diff\n            dp[s] = best_expected\n        if max_diff < eps:\n            break\n    \n    print(dp[0])\n\nmain()\n```\n\nLet’s hope it passes.\n\nBut note: in some cases, the expected cost might be very high, but our initialization 1e15 should be safe since max cost per step is 10^4, and worst-case expected steps might be high, but M is only 100.\n\nBut for example, if a wheel gives 0 with high probability, expected cost could be high, but still finite.\n\nOur iteration should converge.\n\nWe output dp[0].\n\nNow run on sample 1:\n\nInput:\n```\n3 14\n100 2 5 9\n50 4 1 2 4 8\n70 5 2 4 2 8 8\n```\n\nExpected output: ~215.91\n\nOur code shoul