tion in sorted order:\n   - Let $ S_i $ be the list of vertices.\n   - Let $ \\text{root} = S_i[0] $\n   - For each $ v $ in $ S_i[1:] $:\n     - If $ \\text{find(root)} \\neq \\text{find(v)} $:\n       - Union them\n       - Add $ C_i $ to MST cost\n5. At the end, check if all vertices are in the same component (i.e., only one component in DSU).\n6. If yes, print MST cost; else, print -1.\n\nBut wait: is this correct?\n\n### Counterexample?\n\nSuppose $ S_i = \\{a, b, c\\} $, and initially:\n- a and b are connected,\n- c is separate.\n\nOur method:\n- root = a\n- check b: same component as a → skip\n- check c: different → union b and c (or a and c), add cost.\n\nThis connects all — correct.\n\nBut what if $ S_i = \\{a, b, c\\} $, and:\n- a is alone\n- b and c are connected\n\nThen:\n- root = a\n- check b: not connected to a → union, add cost\n- check c: now, after union(a,b), a and b are connected, c is connected to b → so find(a) == find(c)? Yes, if union was done.\n\nSo we skip.\n\nTotal: one edge added — correct.\n\nBut what if:\n- a and b: separate\n- b and c: separate\n- a and c: separate\n\nThen:\n- union(a,b): cost += C_i\n- then check c: find(a) != find(c)? Yes → union(a,c): cost += C_i\n\nTotal cost += 2 * C_i — correct.\n\nBut in reality, we could connect them with two edges — yes.\n\nBut note: **we are not required to use only edges from this operation** — DSU already has previous connections.\n\nSo this procedure **simulates adding a complete graph of weight $ C_i $** by connecting all vertices in $ S_i $ that are not already connected, using edges of weight $ C_i $, and we add one such edge per union.\n\nBut is this sufficient?\n\nYes — because in MST, when you have a complete graph of uniform weight, the minimum way to connect the vertices is to connect the components using $ (k - 1) $ edges if there are $ k $ components, but we don't need to add more.\n\nAnd by processing in increasing order of $ C_i $, we ensure that we use the cheapest available co