ind(c)? Yes → union a and c → cost += 1\n\nTotal: 2 — correct.\n\nBut what if we could connect b and c first? But weight is same — doesn't matter.\n\nSo the total cost added is $ (k - 1) \\times C_i $, where $ k $ is the number of DSU components among the vertices in $ S_i $ **before** this operation.\n\nBut we don’t need to compute $ k $ — the loop naturally adds $ k-1 $ edges.\n\nBut wait: is it always $ k-1 $? Yes, because we start with $ k $ components, and each union reduces component count by 1, and we stop when all are connected.\n\nBut our loop may not add exactly $ k-1 $ edges? Let's see:\n\n- We fix root.\n- Each vertex that is not connected to root causes a union.\n- But if two vertices are in the same component but not connected to root, we only need one edge to connect the whole component.\n\nOur method: when we process the first vertex in a component (other than root's), we union it with root → now whole component is connected.\nThen other vertices in same component are skipped.\n\nSo number of unions = number of components in $ S_i $ minus 1 (if root's component is one of them).\n\nYes — so it adds exactly $ (k - 1) $ edges, where $ k $ = number of distinct components among vertices in $ S_i $.\n\nPerfect.\n\nSo the algorithm:\n\n1. Read N, M.\n2. Store operations: each is (K, C, list of vertices)\n3. Sort operations by C (ascending)\n4. DSU with N+1 vertices (1-indexed)\n5. mst_cost = 0\n6. For each operation in sorted order:\n   - Let S = list of vertices\n   - If S is empty, skip\n   - Let root = S[0]\n   - For v in S[1:]:\n     - if find(root) != find(v):\n         - union(root, v)\n         - mst_cost += C\n7. After all operations, check if entire graph is connected: i.e., number of connected components is 1.\n   - We can do: for i from 2 to N, if find(1) != find(i), then not connected.\n8. If connected, print mst_cost; else, print -1.\n\nBut wait: what if N=1? But N>=2.\n\nLet’s test with **Sample Input 1**:\n\n```\n4 3\n3 3\n1 2 3\n2 2\n1 2\n3 