      "generated_answer": "Let's solve this step by step.\n\n---\n\n### **Problem Understanding**\n\nWe are given a grid of size $ H \\times W $, where each cell is either:\n- `.` → red\n- `#` → green\n\nWe define **green connected components** as the number of connected components in the subgraph of green cells, where two green cells are connected if they are adjacent (up/down/left/right).\n\nWe are to:\n1. Choose **one red cell uniformly at random**.\n2. Repaint it **green**.\n3. Compute the **expected number of green connected components** after this change.\n4. Output the result **modulo 998244353**.\n\nIt's guaranteed that there is **at least one red cell**.\n\n---\n\n### **Key Observations**\n\nLet:\n- $ R $: number of red cells (i.e., `.`)\n- For each red cell $ c $, let $ f(c) $: number of green connected components after repainting $ c $ to green.\n- Then the expected value is:  \n  $$\n  \\mathbb{E} = \\frac{1}{R} \\sum_{c \\text{ is red}} f(c)\n  $$\n\nSo we need to:\n1. Count the number of green connected components **in the original grid**.\n2. For each red cell, simulate repainting it green and compute how the number of connected components changes.\n3. Sum all $ f(c) $, divide by $ R $, and output modulo 998244353.\n\n---\n\n### **How Does Repainting a Red Cell Affect Components?**\n\nWhen we repaint a red cell $ (i,j) $ to green:\n- It becomes a new green cell.\n- It may **connect** to its **adjacent green cells** (up, down, left, right).\n- The number of new connected components introduced depends on how many **existing green components** it connects to.\n\nLet’s define:\n- Let $ k $: number of **adjacent green cells** that belong to **distinct connected components**.\nThen:\n- The new cell can **merge** up to $ k $ components into one.\n- So the change in number of components is:\n  $$\n  \\Delta = 1 - k\n  $$\n  Why?\n  - We add 1 new component (the cell itself),\n  - But if it connects $ k $ previously disconnected components, we reduce the total