would be a problem.\n\nAh! This is a **critical issue**.\n\nWe cannot use a shortest path from `i` to `j` that goes through a vertex `k` that we haven’t visited yet, **if we are relying on visiting k later**, because we are visiting k **during** the path from `i` to `j`, so it's okay — we are allowed to visit k early.\n\nBut in our permutation model, we are visiting k **only when we explicitly go to k**.\n\nBut if the shortest path from `i` to `j` goes through `k`, then when we go from `i` to `j`, we **do visit k**, so we can mark k as visited.\n\nBut in our DP, we are assuming that we only visit a vertex when we explicitly \"visit\" it in the permutation.\n\nSo if we go from `i` to `j` via `k`, and `k` is not yet in the mask, then we **visit k during the transition**, so we could potentially **add k to the visited set**.\n\nBut then the model becomes more complex: moving from `i` to `j` may visit multiple new vertices.\n\nSo the **simple TSP on shortest path metric** is **not valid** if shortest paths go through unvisited vertices.\n\nBut wait: we **want to visit all vertices**, so it's okay to visit them early.\n\nBut in the **DP over subsets**, we are assuming that we visit vertices **one at a time**.\n\nSo if a shortest path from `i` to `j` goes through several unvisited vertices, we **do visit them**, so we could update the mask accordingly.\n\nBut then the transition is not just from `i` to `j`, but from `i` to `j` and possibly adding many vertices.\n\nThis complicates the DP.\n\nAlternatively, we can assume that **we only visit a vertex when we explicitly \"arrive\" at it in our high-level path**, and the intermediate vertices on shortest paths are \"free\" visits.\n\nBut that is acceptable: visiting a vertex on the way is still visiting it.\n\nSo if we go from `i` to `j` via `k`, and `k` was not visited, then after this move, `k` is visited.\n\nBut in our DP, we are only adding `j` to the mask, not `k`.\n\nSo we would **miss** that `k` was visited.\n\nTherefore, the **TSP on shortest path metric with only direct transitions** is **not sufficient**.\n\n---\n\n### 🚨 Better Insight\n\nThere is a known result: in a directed graph with no negative cycles, the **minimum walk covering all vertices** can be found by:\n\n1. Compute all-pairs shortest paths.\n2. Then, the answer is the minimum, over all permutations of vertices, of the sum of shortest paths between consecutive vertices in the permutation.\n\nWhy is this valid?\n\nBecause even if the shortest path from `p[i]` to `p[i+1]` goes through some `p[j]` (j > i+1), that’s fine — we are allowed to visit vertices early. And since we are going to visit them eventually, it's okay.\n\nBut the key is: **we must ensure that every vertex appears in the permutation**, so we will \"officially\" visit it at some point.\n\nBut if we visit it early via a shortest path, do we still need to \"visit\" it again when we reach it in the permutation?\n\nNo! We only need to visit it **at least once**.\n\nSo if we go from A to B and pass through C, then C is visited. So when we later \"arrive\" at C in the permutation, we don’t need to do anything — but in our model, we are still going to go from B to C, which may be redundant.\n\nAh, here’s the flaw.\n\nBut in our permutation, we are visiting the vertices in a specific order. If we go from A to B via C, then C is visited. But if the next step is B to C, that’s unnecessary.\n\nBut we are not forced to follow the permutation strictly as direct moves.\n\nActually, the correct interpretation is:\n\nWe are **designing a walk** that visits all vertices. We can choose the order in which we **first** visit each vertex.\n\nLet `v1, v2, ..., vN` be the order of **first visits** to the vertices.\n\nThen:\n- From start (v1) to v2: we go via shortest path from v1 to v2. This path may go through other vertices, but their first visit would then be **before** v2, contradicting the order.\n\nSo the **first visit order** must be such that the shortest path from v1 to v2 does **not** go through any vertex that hasn't been visited yet — but it might.\n\nActually, it can: suppose we go from v1 to v2 and pass through v3. Then the first visit to v3 is during this path, so in the first-visit order, v3 comes **before** v2.\n\nTherefore, the **first-visit order** is constrained: we cannot have v2 before v3 if the shortest path from v1 to v2 goes through v3.\n\nSo the first-visit order must be a **topological order** of the dependencies.\n\nBut this is too complex.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Known Solution Approach\n\nAfter research and known problems (e.g., Hamiltonian Path in directed graphs with shortest paths), the standard solution for \"minimum walk covering all vertices\" in a directed graph with no negative cycles is:\n\n1. Run Floyd-Warshall to get all-pairs shortest paths.\n2. If the graph is not **strongly connected**, then there might be no walk that visits all vertices? Not necessarily — we don't need to return, just visit.\n\nBut we need that **all vertices are reachable from some starting vertex?** No — we can start anywhere, but we need to connect them.\n\nActually, the necessary and sufficient condition is that the **underlying directed graph has a path that covers all vertices**, i.e., there exists a vertex from which all others are reachable, or we can chain them.\n\nBut the safest way is: after Floyd-Warshall, check that for **some permutation** `p[0..N-1]`, every `dist[p[i]][p[i+1]]` is finite.\n\nBut we can't check all permutations.\n\nAlternative: the entire graph must be **strongly connected**? No — Sample 1: \n```\n1->2, 2->1, 2->3, 3->1\n```\nIs it strongly connected? \n- 1->2->3, 3->1, so yes.\nSample 2: \n```\n1<->2, no 3\n```\n3 is not present? Wait, N=3, but only vertices 1,2 have edges. Vertex 3 exists but has no edges in or out? In sample input 2:\n```\n3 2\n1 2 0\n2 1 0\n```\nSo vertices 1,2,3. But no edge to or from 3. So `dist[i][3] = inf` for all i, and `dist[3][j] = inf` for all j. So vertex 3 is isolated.\n\nSo in Floyd-Warshall, `dist[i][3]` and `dist[3][i]` are inf for i≠3.\n\nSo we can check: for vertex 3, can we reach it from any other? No. Can we go from it? No.\n\nSo no walk can include vertex 3 unless we start there, but then we can't go anywhere else? We can start at 3, but then we need to go to 1 or 2, but no edge.\n\nSo indeed, no walk can visit all three.\n\nSo the condition is: for the walk to exist, **there must be a sequence of vertices covering all N vertices such that each consecutive pair is connected by a finite shortest path**.\n\nBut we can use the following:\n\n> The walk exists if and only if the **condensation of the graph** (SCC) forms a **DAG that has a path covering all SCCs**, and within each SCC, we can travel.\n\nBut simpler: after Floyd-Warshall, we have `dist[i][j]`.\n\nNow, we want to know: is there a walk that visits all vertices?\n\nThis is equivalent to: is the graph **weakly connected** and has a **directed walk covering all vertices**?\n\nBut the known solution for small N is:\n\nUse **DP over subsets** with state `(mask, i)` = minimum cost to have visited the set of vertices in `mask`, ending at vertex `i`, and the cost is the sum of edge weights in the walk.\n\nBut we are allowed to go through vertices not in `mask`? Yes, but we only care about covering.\n\nBut how do we compute the cost to go from state `(mask, i)` to `(mask', j)`?\n\nWe need the shortest path from `i` to `j`, and along the way, we may visit new vertices.\n\nSo when we go from `i` to `j` via shortest path, we can **add all vertices on the path** to the mask.\n\nBut we don't know which vertices are on the shortest path.\n\nBut we can precompute the shortest path distances, and also, during Floyd-Warshall, we can reconstruct paths, but that's heavy.\n\nAlternative idea from known problems (e.g., ICPC): \n\n> The minimum walk that visits every vertex at least once is the minimum, over all permutations of the vertices, of the sum of shortest path distances between consecutive vertices in the permutation.\n\nAnd this is valid because:\n\n- You can visit vertices early.\n- The only requirement is that each vertex appears in the permutation (so it is visited at that point, or earlier).\n- But if it is visited earlier, then when you reach it in the permutation, you are revisiting, which is allowed.\n\nBut the cost is still the sum of shortest paths.\n\nAnd since you must go from one vertex to the next in the sequence, the cheapest way is the shortest path.\n\nAnd because you can choose the order, you minimize over all permutations.\n\nAnd crucially, **you do not need to visit vertices only once** — you can visit them multiple times, but the high-level journey is defined by the order in which you \"target\" each vertex.\n\nBut if you visit a vertex early, you might not need to \"target\" it later, but in this model, you still have a step for it.\n\nAh, but if you've already visited a vertex, you don't need to go to it again.\n\nSo the optimal strategy is to visit each vertex **exactly once**, in some order, and go from one to the next via shortest path.\n\nBut you might visit other vertices on the way.\n\nBut the key is: you only need to \"include\" a vertex once in the permutation.\n\nSo the walk is: start at p[0], go to p[1] via shortest path, then to p[2] via shortest path, ..., to p[N-1].\n\nThe total cost is sum_{i=0}^{N-2} dist[p[i]][p[i+1]].\n\nAnd the vertices visited include all on the paths, so if the shortest path from p[i] to p[i+1] goes through some p[j], that's fine.\n\nBut we must ensure that **all vertices are either in the permutation or on some shortest path between consecutive vertices**.\n\nBut the permutation includes **all vertices**, so we are good.\n\nTherefore, the walk visits all vertices (at least when we reach them in the permutation, or earlier).\n\nSo the only requirement is that we have a permutation of all N vertices.\n\nThen, the cost is sum of dist[p[i]][p[i+1]].\n\nAnd we minimize this over all permutations.\n\nBut is this optimal?\n\nYes, because any walk that visits all vertices can be seen as a sequence of moves from one vertex to another, and we can extract the order of first visits, and then the cost to go from first-visited to second-visited is at least dist[v1][v2], etc.\n\nSo the minimum over permutations gives a lower bound, and it's achievable.\n\nTherefore, the algorithm is:\n\n1. Read input.\n2. Initialize a dist[N+1][N+1] with inf, 0 on diagonal.\n3. For each edge, set dist[u][v] = min(dist[u][v], w)  (but only one edge per pair)\n4. Run Floyd-Warshall.\n5. Check for any vertex i, if it is completely isolated in terms of finite distances? Actually, we will check in the next step.\n6. Generate all permutations of vertices (1 to N). For each permutation, check if dist[p[i]][p[i+1]] is inf for any i. If not, compute total = sum of dist[p[i]][p[i+1]] for i=0 to N-2.\n7. Take the minimum over all such totals.\n8. If no permutation is valid, output \"No\", else output the minimum total.\n\nBut N=20, and 20! is about 2.4e18, which is too many.\n\nWe cannot iterate over all permutations.\n\nWe need a better way.\n\nBut wait: 20 is the maximum, but 20! is astronomical.\n\nBut is there a way with DP over subsets?\n\nYes, but with a twist.\n\nLet `dp[mask][i]` = minimum cost to have visited all vertices in `mask`, and currently at vertex `i`, where the cost is the sum of edge weights in the walk.\n\nBut how do we transition?\n\nWe can go from `i` to `j` by taking the shortest path from `i` to `j`.\n\nBut when we go from `i` to `j` via shortest path, we may visit new vertices.\n\nSo the new mask should be `mask OR mask_of_vertices_on_path_from_i_to_j`.\n\nBut we don't know which vertices are on the shortest path.\n\nAnd there might be multiple shortest paths.\n\nSo we would need to know the set of vertices on some shortest path.\n\nThis is complex.\n\nAlternatively, we can assume that we only visit one new vertex at a time.\n\nBut that may not be optimal.\n\nHowever, in the literature, this problem is known to be solved by:\n\n> \"Since the graph has no negative cycles, we can use the shortest path between any two vertices. Then the minimum cost to visit all vertices is the minimum over all permutations of the sum of shortest path distances between consecutive vertices.\"\n\nAnd to handle N=20, we must use **DP over subsets** with state `(mask, i)` where we have visited exactly the vertices in `mask`, and are at vertex `i`, and the cost is the sum of shortest path distances used to go from one vertex to the next.\n\nBut in this DP, we assume that we visit vertices one by one, and to go from `i` to `j`, we use `dist[i][j]`, and we only add `j` to the mask if it's not already there.\n\nBut wait, if we go from `i` to `j`, and `j` is not in `mask`, then we can add it.\n\nBut the cost is `dist[i][j]`, which may go through other vertices not in `mask`, and we would visit them, so we could add them too.\n\nBut again, we don't know.\n\nGiven the complexity, and that N ≤ 20, but 20! is too big, yet 2^20 is 1e6, we can try the following:\n\nUse DP with `dp[mask][i]` = minimum cost to have a walk that has visited at least the vertices in `mask`, and ends at vertex `i`.\n\nHow to transition?\n\nFrom `dp[mask][i]`, we can go to any vertex `j` by taking the shortest path from `i` to `j`.\n\nThe cost increase is `dist[i][j]`.\n\nThe new mask is `mask OR mask_j`, where `mask_j` is the set of vertices visited on the shortest path from i to j.\n\nBut we don't know `mask_j`.\n\nSo this is not feasible.\n\nAnother idea: since the graph is small, and we are allowed to use shortest paths, and because the shortest path from i to j is fixed, we can precompute not just the distance but also the set of vertices on some shortest path.\n\nBut there might be multiple shortest paths.\n\nWe would need the union over all shortest paths.\n\nThis is complicated.\n\n### 🚀 Practical Approach for N ≤ 20\n\nEven though 20! is large, the worst-case might not be reached if we prune.\n\nBut 12! = 479001600, 13! = 6e9, so for N>12, it's too slow.\n\nBut the constraints say N>=2 and N<=20.\n\nSo we need a better method.\n\nWait, there is a known solution: use DP over subsets, but assume that we visit vertices one by one, and the cost to go from i to j is dist[i][j], and we only add j to the mask.\n\nThis is the standard TSP DP, and it is an **approximation** if shortest paths go through unvisited vertices, but in this case, it might be valid because:\n\n> Even if the shortest path from i to j goes through k not in mask, when we go from i to j, we do visit k, so we should add k to the mask.\n\nBut in this DP, we only add j.\n\nSo we would have to later visit k, but k is already visited.\n\nSo we can modify: when we go from i to j, we can add **all vertices k for which dist[i][k] + dist[k][j] == dist[i][j]** and k is on a shortest path.\n\nBut which ones to include?\n\nAlternatively, after Floyd-Warshall, we can reconstruct one shortest path between i and j, and get the set of vertices on it.\n\nThen, when we go from i to j, we can add all vertices on that shortest path to the mask.\n\nBut there might be multiple shortest paths; we want to choose one that maximizes the number of new vertices added.\n\nBut for minimum cost, the cost is fixed: dist[i][j].\n\nSo we can precompute for each (i,j) a shortest path and the set of vertices on it.\n\nThen in DP, when we go from state `(mask, i)` to `j`, we:\n- cost = dp[mask][i] + dist[i][j]\n- new_mask = mask OR vertices_on_path[i][j]\n\nThen update dp[new_mask][j].\n\nWe initialize dp[1<<i][i] = 0 for all i.\n\nThen we iterate until mask is full.\n\nThis is feasible because:\n- Number of states: 2^N * N = 2^20 * 20 ≈ 20e6, which is acceptable in C++ but in Python might be slow, but we can try.\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Floyd-Warshall to get dist[i][j] and also next[i][j] for path reconstruction.\n2. For each (i,j), if dist[i][j] < inf, reconstruct a shortest path and get the set of vertices on it.\n3. Use DP over subsets: dp[mask][i] = minimum cost to achieve mask and end at i.\n4. For each state, for each j, if we haven't visited all, try to go from i to j:\n   - new_cost = dp[mask][i] + dist[i][j]\n   - new_mask = mask | vertices_on_path[i][j]\n   - update dp[new_mask][j]\n5. Answer is min over i of dp[(1<<N)-1][i]\n\nBut is this correct?\n\nLet's test on sample 1.\n\nSample 1:\n```\n3 4\n1 2 5\n2 1 -3\n2 3 -4\n3 1 100\n```\n\nFloyd-Warshall:\n\nInitialize:\n- dist[1][2]=5, dist[2][1]=-3, dist[2][3]=-4, dist[3][1]=100\n- dist[i][i]=0\n\nAfter Floyd-Warshall:\n\nConsider dist[1][3]: 1->2->3 = 5 + (-4) = 1\ndist[3][2]: 3->1->2 = 100+5=105, or 3->1->2->3->2? But no, 3->1->2 = 105\ndist[1][1]: 1->2->1 = 5-3=2, but also 1->2->3->1 = 5-4+100=101, so not better.\ndist[2][2]: 2->1->2 = -3+5=2\ndist[3][3]: 3->1->2->3 = 100+5-4=101\n\nBut also, dist[1][3] = 1, as above.\n\nNow, shortest path from 1 to 3: 1->2->3, vertices {1,2,3}\n\nFrom 2 to 1: 2->1, vertices {2,1}\n\nFrom 2 to 3: 2->3, vertices {2,3}\n\nFrom 3 to 1: 3->1, vertices {3,1}\n\nFrom 1 to 2: 1->2, vertices {1,2}\n\nFrom 3 to 2: 3->1->2, vertices {3,1,2}\n\nNow, DP:\n\nStart with mask=1<<0 (vertex 1), at 1, cost=0\n- go to 2: cost=0+5=5, new_mask = {1} | {1,2} = {1,2} = mask 3\n- go to 3: cost=0+1=1, new_mask = {1} | {1,2,3} = 7\n\nSimilarly, start with mask=2 (vertex 2), cost=0\n- go to 1: cost=0+(-3)= -3, new_mask = {2} | {2,1} = {1,2} = 3\n- go to 3: cost=0+(-4)= -4, new_mask = {2} | {2,3} = {2,3} = 6\n\nStart with mask=4 (vertex 3), cost=0\n- go to 1: cost=0+100=100, new_mask = {3} | {3,1} = {1,3} = 5\n- go to 2: cost=0+105=105, new_mask = {3} | {3,1,2} = 7\n\nNow from state (mask=3, at 1): cost=-3 (from 2->1)\n- go to 3: cost=-3+1= -2, new_mask = 3 | {1,2,3} = 7\n\nFrom (mask=3, at 1): also can go to 2: cost=-3+5=2, new_mask=3|{1,2}=3, no new\n\nFrom (mask=3, at 1): go to 3: cost=-2, new_mask=7\n\nSimilarly, from (mask=6, at 3): cost=-4\n- go to 1: cost=-4+100=96, new_mask=6|{3,1}=7\n- go