where i ≤ j, I want to count the contribution of groups where nums[i] is the minimum and nums[j] is the maximum.\n\nBut this might be complex. Let me go back to the sorted approach.\n\nAfter sorting, for each j, consider nums[j] as the maximum. Then:\n- Any subset of {nums[0], ..., nums[j]} that includes nums[j] will have nums[j] as maximum\n- The minimum of such a subset depends on which elements are chosen\n\nFor a subset that includes nums[j] and some elements from {nums[0], ..., nums[j-1]}, the minimum is the smallest element in the chosen subset.\n\nSo for fixed j, the total contribution when nums[j] is maximum is:\nnums[j]^2 * sum(min(subset) for all subsets of {nums[0],...,nums[j-1]} that we can combine with nums[j])\n\nThis includes:\n- The subset with just nums[j]: min = nums[j], so contribution nums[j]^3\n- Subsets with nums[j] and some elements from {nums[0],...,nums[j-1]}: min is the minimum of the chosen elements from {nums[0],...,nums[j-1]}\n\nSo total contribution for maximum = nums[j] is:\nnums[j]^2 * (nums[j] + sum(min(S) for all non-empty subsets S of {nums[0],...,nums[j-1]}))\n\nNow I need to compute sum(min(S) for all non-empty subsets S of {nums[0],...,nums[j-1]}).\n\nLet me denote A = {nums[0], ..., nums[j-1]}.\n\nFor a sorted array, the sum of minimums of all non-empty subsets can be computed efficiently.\n\nFor each element nums[i] where i < j, how many times does nums[i] appear as the minimum in a non-empty subset of {nums[0],...,nums[j-1]}?\n\nIf nums[i] is the minimum, then we must include nums[i], and we can include any subset of {nums[i+1], ..., nums[j-1]}.\n\nThere are 2^(j-1-i) such subsets.\n\nSo sum of minimums of all non-empty subsets of first j elements (excluding the empty set) is:\nsum_{i=0}^{j-1} nums[i] * 2^(j-1-i)\n\nWait, this is for subsets of {nums[0],...,nums[j-1]}.\n\nSo for fixed j, the sum of minimums of all non-empty subsets of {nums[0],...,nums[j-1]} is:\nsum_{i=0}^{j-1} nums[i] * 2^(j-1-i)\n\nBut we also have the case where the subset is just {nums[j]}, which contributes nums[j] as the minimum.\n\nActually, let me organize:\n\nWhen nums[j] is the maximum, the groups are:\n1. {nums[j]}: minimum = nums[j], contribution to sum of minimums = nums[j]\n2. {nums[j]} ∪ S where S is non-empty subset of {nums[0],...,nums[j-1]}: minimum = min(S)\n\nSo total sum of minimums for all groups with maximum = nums[j] is:\nnums[j] + sum(min(S) for all non-empty subsets S of {nums[0],...,nums[j-1]})\n\nAnd sum(min(S) for all non-empty subsets S of {nums[0],...,nums[j-1]}) = sum_{i=0}^{j-1} nums[i] * 2^(j-1-i)\n\nTherefore, total contribution when nums[j] is maximum is:\nnums[j]^2 * (nums[j] + sum_{i=0}^{j-1} nums[i] * 2^(j-1-i))\n\nLet me verify with the example:\nnums = [2,1,4] -> sorted: [1,2,4]\n\nFor j=0 (nums[0]=1):\ncontribution = 1^2 * (1 + 0) = 1\n\nFor j=1 (nums[1]=2):\nsum of minimums from non-empty subsets of {1} = 1 * 2^(1-1-0) = 1 * 2^0 = 1\ncontribution = 2^2 * (2 + 1) = 4 * 3 = 12\n\nWait, that's not right. Let me recalculate.\n\nFor j=1, we have:\n- Group {2}: min = 2\n- Groups with 2 and subsets of {1}: only {1,2}, min = 1\nSo sum of minimums = 2 + 1 = 3\ncontribution = 2^2 * 3 = 4 * 3 = 12\n\nFor j=2 (nums[2]=4):\nsum of minimums from non-empty subsets of {1,2} = \n- {1}: min=1, count=1\n- {2}: min=2, count=1  \n- {1,2}: min=1, count=1\nsum = 1+2+1 = 4\n\nUsing formula: sum_{i=0}^{1} nums[i] * 2^(2-1-i) = nums[0]*2^(1-0) + nums[1]*2^(1-1) = 1*2^1 + 2*2^0 = 2 + 2 = 4 ✓\n\nSum of minimums for groups with max=4:\n- {4}: min=4\n- {4} + non-empty subsets of {1,2}: min = min of subset\nSo sum of minimums = 4 + 4 = 8\ncontribution = 4^2 * 8 = 16 * 8 = 128\n\nTotal = 1 + 12 + 128 = 141 ✓\n\nPerfect!\n\nSo the algorithm:\n1. Sort nums\n2. For each j from 0 to n-1:\n   - If j == 0: sum_min = nums[0]\n   - Else: sum_min = nums[j] + sum_{i=0}^{j-1} nums[i] * 2^(j-1-i)\n   - Add nums[j]^2 * sum_min to result\n3. Return result mod (10^9 + 7)\n\nI can compute the sum_{i=0}^{j-1} nums[i] * 2^(j-1-i) iteratively to avoid recalculating.\n\nLet me define prefix_sum[j] = sum_{i=0}^{j-1} nums[i] * 2^(j-1-i)\n\nThen prefix_sum[0] = 0\nprefix_sum[1] = nums[0] * 2^0 = nums[0]\nprefix_sum[2] = nums[0] * 2^1 + nums[1] * 2^0 = 2*nums[0] + nums[1]\n\nI notice that prefix_sum[j] = 2 * prefix_sum[j-1] + nums[j-1]\n\nLet me check:\nprefix_sum[j] = sum_{i=0}^{j-1} nums[i] * 2^(j-1-i)\nprefix_sum[j-1] = sum_{i=0}^{j-2} nums[i] * 2^(j-2-i)\n\nSo 2 * prefix_sum[j-1] = sum_{i=0}^{j-2} nums[i] * 2^(j-1-i)\nThen 2 * prefix_sum[j-1] + nums[j-1] = sum_{i=0}^{j-2} nums[i] * 2^(j-1-i) + nums[j-1] * 2^0 = prefix_sum[j] ✓\n\nGreat! So I can compute this iteratively.\n\n```python\ndef sumOfPower(nums):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    nums.sort()\n    n = len(nums)\n    \n    result = 0\n    prefix_sum = 0  # This will store sum_{i=0}^{j-1} nums[i] * 2^(j-1-i)\n    \n    for j in range(n):\n        # Sum of minimums for all groups where nums[j] is maximum\n        sum_min = (nums[j] + prefix_sum) % MOD\n        \n        # Add contribution: nums[j]^2 * sum_min\n        contribution = (nums[j] * nums[j] % MOD) * sum_min % MOD\n        result = (result + contribution) % MOD\n        \n        # Update prefix_sum for next iteration\n        # prefix_sum[j+1] = 2 * prefix_sum[j] + nums[j]\n        prefix_sum = (2 * prefix_sum + nums[j]) % MOD\n    \n    return result\n```",
