      "generated_answer": "We are given a problem where:\n\n- There are $ N $ songs, each with duration $ T_i $ seconds.\n- At time 0, a random playlist starts: each song is chosen independently and uniformly at random from the $ N $ songs.\n- Songs are played back-to-back, with no gaps.\n- We are to compute the **probability that song 1 is being played at time $ X + 0.5 $** seconds.\n- Output this probability **modulo 998244353**.\n\n---\n\n### Key Observations\n\n1. **Time $ X + 0.5 $** means we're in the middle of a second — specifically, halfway through second $ X $. So, we're interested in whether **song 1 is being played during the time interval $[X, X+1)$**.\n\n2. Since songs are played continuously and each song takes an integer number of seconds, a song that starts at time $ s $ will be playing during $[s, s+T_i)$.\n\n   So, **song 1 is playing at time $ X+0.5 $** iff there exists some sequence of songs such that:\n   - The total time before the current (possibly ongoing) song is $ \\leq X $\n   - The current song is **song 1**\n   - And the current song started at time $ s $, such that $ s \\leq X < s + T_1 $\n\n   That is, the current song (song 1) must have started at time $ s \\in [X - T_1 + 1, X] $, and the total time before it must be exactly $ s $.\n\n3. We are to compute the total probability over all such finite sequences of songs (of any length) that end with song 1 being played during $[X, X+1)$.\n\n4. Since the number of possible sequences is infinite (the same song can repeat), we need to model this with **dynamic programming**, but note that $ X \\leq 10^4 $, so we can do DP up to time $ X $.\n\n---\n\n### Plan\n\nLet’s define:\n\n- $ dp[t] $: the **total probability (scaled appropriately)** that the total playback time is exactly $ t $ seconds **and the last song played is not necessarily song 1**, but we are tracking all ways to reach time $ t $.\n\nBut we need to know **which song is playing at time $ X+0.5 $**.\n\nSo instead, let's define:\n\n- $ dp[t] $: the total **unnormalized** probability mass (i.e., multiplied by $ N^k $ for sequences of $ k $ songs) of all sequences that end at time $ t $.\n\nBut since we care about **which song is currently playing**, we need to track when a song starts and how long it plays.\n\nAlternative idea:\n\nWe can use DP to compute the **probability that at time $ t $, a new song starts**.\n\nLet $ f[t] $ = probability that a new song starts at time $ t $.\n\nThen, for any $ t \\in [X - T_1 + 1, X] $, if a song starts at $ t $, and it is **song 1**, then it will still be playing at $ X+0.5 $ (since $ t + T_1 > X $).\n\nSo:\n\n> The event “song 1 is playing at $ X+0.5 $” is equivalent to:  \n> “At some time $ t \\in [\\max(0, X - T_1 + 1), X] $, a song starts, and that song is song 1, and $ t + T_1 > X $.”\n\nBut since $ t \\geq X - T_1 + 1 \\Rightarrow t + T_1 \\geq X + 1 > X $, so the condition $ t + T_1 > X $ is automatically satisfied.\n\nSo we only need $ t \\in [\\max(0, X - T_1 + 1), X] $ such that a song starts at $ t $, and the chosen song is song 1.\n\nNow, the probability that a song starts at time $ t $ is $ f[t] $, and the probability that the song chosen is song 1 is $ \\frac{1}{N} $, so the contribution is $ f[t] \\cdot \\frac{1}{N} $.\n\nHence, the total probability is:\n\n$$\nP = \\frac{1}{N} \\sum_{t = \\max(0, X - T_1 + 1)}^{X} f[t]\n$$\n\nNow, how to compute $ f[t] $?\n\nWe can define $ f[0] = 1 $: a song starts at time 0.\n\nFor $ t > 0 $, a song starts at $ t $ if the previous song ended at $ t $, and that previous song could have started at $ t - T_i $ for some $ i $, and was played from there.\n\nSo:\n\n$$\nf[t] = \\sum_{i=1}^{N} \\frac{1}{N} \\cdot f[t - T_i] \\quad \\text{for } t > 0\n$$\n\nBut wait — this recurrence assumes that at time $ t - T_i $, a song (any song) started, and it was song $ i $, which takes $ T_i $ seconds, so the next song starts at $ t $.\n\nBut actually, **the song that ends at $ t $** could be any song $ i $, and it s