multiply by $ invN $ again.\n\nYes, because:\n- $ f[t] $ includes $ \\frac{1}{N} $ per transition\n- But the final choice of song 1 at time $ s $ requires another $ \\frac{1}{N} $\n\nSo two factors of $ \\frac{1}{N} $ in some paths.\n\nBut in the recurrence, $ f[t] $ is computed with $ \\frac{1}{N} $ for the **previous** song choice.\n\nThe current choice (song 1) is separate.\n\nSo yes, we need to multiply the sum by $ \\frac{1}{N} $.\n\nSo code:\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Read N, X, list T of N integers\n2. MOD = 998244353\n3. invN = pow(N, MOD-2, MOD)\n4. f = [0]*(X+1)\n5. f[0] = 1\n6. For t from 1 to X:\n   s = 0\n   for each T_i in T:\n      if t >= T_i:\n          s = (s + f[t - T_i]) % MOD\n   f[t] = s * invN % MOD\n7. low = max(0, X - T[0] + 1)   # T[0] is T_1\n8. total = sum(f[low:X+1]) % MOD\n9. ans = total * invN % MOD\n10. print(ans)\n\nBut wait — in sample 1:\n\nWe computed:\n- f[0]=1\n- f[1]=0\n- f[2]=0\n- f[3] = (f[0]) * invN = 1 * inv3 mod MOD\n- f[4] = 0\n- f[5] = (f[0]) * invN = 1 * inv3\n- f[6] = (f[3]+f[1]+f[0]) * invN = (inv3 + 0 + 1) * inv3 = (1 + inv3) * inv3\n\nBut we need to do modular arithmetic.\n\nLet’s compute $ \\frac{7}{27} \\mod MOD $\n\nWe know answer is 369720131\n\nSo $ 369720131 \\times 27 \\mod 998244353 = 7 $\n\nYes.\n\nNow, in code, we need to compute:\n\ninv3 = pow(3, MOD-2, MOD)\n\nThen f[3] = inv3\nf[5] = inv3\nf[6] = (f[3] + f[0]) * inv3 = (inv3 + 1) * inv3\n\nThen sum = f[4]+f[5]+f[6] = 0 + inv3 + (1 + inv3)*inv3\n\nThen ans = sum * inv3\n\nLet’s compute symbolically:\n\nLet $ a = \\frac{1}{3} $\n\nf[3] = a  \nf[5] = a  \nf[6] = (a + 1) * a = a^2 + a  \n\nsum = a + a^2 + a = a^2 + 2a  \nans = (a^2 + 2a) * a = a^3 + 2a^2\n\nBut $ a = 1/3 $, so $ a^3 + 2a^2 = \\frac{1}{27} + \\frac{2}{9} = \\frac{1+6}{27} = \\frac{7}{27} $\n\nYes, correct.\n\nSo the code is correct.\n\nBut note: in the recurrence, we sum over all songs, including song 1,2,3.\n\nIn code, T[0] is T_1.\n\nLet’s test sample 2.\n\n### Sample 2: N=5, X=0, T=[1,2,1,2,1]\n\nSo T_1 = 1\n\nWe want song 1 to be playing at 0.5 seconds.\n\nThat means the first song must be song 1.\n\nProbability = 1/5\n\nOutput: 598946612\n\nCheck: 598946612 * 5 % MOD = 2994733060 % 998244353\n\nCompute: 998244353 * 3 = 2994733059\n\n2994733060 - 2994733059 = 1 → so 598946612 * 5 ≡ 1 mod MOD → so it is 1/5.\n\nNow compute with our method:\n\nX=0, T_1=1\n\nlow = max(0, 0 - 1 + 1) = max(0,0) = 0\n\nSo sum f[0] = 1\n\nans = 1 * inv5 mod MOD\n\ninv5 = pow(5, MOD-2, MOD)\n\nSo ans = inv5\n\nBut 1/5 mod MOD is exactly what we want.\n\nSo ans = inv5\n\nAnd 598946612 is indeed the modular inverse of 5 mod 998244353.\n\nYes.\n\nSo code works.\n\nNow sample 3: large, but we trust.\n\nEdge: if X=0 and T_1=1, then low=0, include f[0]\n\nBut if T_1 > X+1? For example, T_1=3, X=1\n\nThen low = max(0,1-3+1)=max(0,-1)=0\n\nBut song 1 lasts 3 seconds, so if it starts at 0, it plays [0,3), which includes 1.5? But X=1, so time is 1.5? No, X+0.5 = 1.5\n\nYes, so if song 1 starts at 0, it plays at 1.5.\n\nBut our condition: s in [X - T_1 + 1, X] = [1-3+1,1] = [-1,1] → s=0,1\n\nBut can a song start at 1? Only if some song of length 1 was played first.\n\nBut if T_i >=1, possible.\n\nBut s must be such that s <= X=1 and s >= X - T_1 + 1 = -1\n\nSo s=0 or 1.\n\nBut if song 1 starts at s=1, then it plays [1,1+T_1)= [1,4), which includes 1.5.\n\nYes.\n\nBut if T_1=3, X=1, then s in [max(0,-1),1] = [0,1]\n\nSo include s=0 and s=1.\n\nBut if s=0: song 1 plays [0,3) → includes 1.5 → yes\ns=1: [1,4) → includes 1.5 → yes\n\nSo condition is correct.\n\nBut note: s must be <= X, and s >= X - T_1 + 1\n\nBecause s + T_1 > X  <=> s > X - T_1, so s >= X - T_1 + 1 since integer.\n\nYes.\n\nSo code:\n\nLet’s write it.\n\nNote: X can be 0, so we need f[0] defined.\n\nWe only need f[0..X]\n\nIf X=0, loop from 1 to X does nothing.\n\nSo safe.\n\nLet’s code accordingly.\n\nOne more thing: in the recurrence, for each t, we sum f[t - T_i] for all i with T_i <= t.\n\nWe can do:\n\nfor t in range(1, X+1):\n    total_prev = 0\n    for duration in T:\n        if t >= duration:\n            total_prev = (total_prev + f[t - duration]) % MOD\n    f[t] = total_prev * invN % MOD\n\nThen sum f[low:X+1]\n\nThen multiply by invN.\n\nPrint.\n\nLet’s run sample 1 in code logic:\n\nN=3, X=6, T=[3,5,6]\n\ninvN = pow(3, MOD-2, MOD)\n\nf = [0]*7\nf[0]=1\n\nt=1: no T_i<=1 → f[1]=0\nt=2: same →0\nt=3: T_i=3 → f[0]=1 → total_prev=1 → f[3]=1 * inv3\nt=4: T_i=3 → f[1]=0 → total_prev=0 → f[4]=0\nt=5: T_i=3,5 → f[2]=0, f[0]=1 → total_prev=1 → f[5]=inv3\nt=6: T_i=3,5,6 → f[3]=inv3, f[1]=0, f[0]=1 → total_prev = inv3 + 1 → f[6] = (1 + inv3) * inv3\n\nNow low = max(0,6-3+1)=4\n\nsum = f[4]+f[5]+f[6] = 0 + inv3 + (1+inv3)*inv3\n\nans = sum * inv3\n\nWe know this should be 7/27 mod MOD = 369720131\n\nSo we trust.\n\nNow code.\n\nNote: MOD = 998244353\n\nWe'll use pow(N, MOD-2, MOD) for modular inverse.\n\nLet’s write the code.\n\nEdge: if X=0, then f[0] is only one.\n\nWe'll code accordingly.\n\nOne more corner: if T_1 is very large, say T_1 > X+1, then X - T_1 + 1 < 0, so low=0.\n\nBut if T_1 > X+1, then a song starting at s=0 will play [0, T_1), which includes X+0.5 only if T_1 > X, which it is.\n\ns + T_1 > X → since s>=0, T_1 > X → yes.\n\nBut our condition s >= X - T_1 + 1 is always true if T_1 > X, because X - T_1 + 1 <= 0, so s>=0 covers.\n\nSo it's correct.\n\nBut if T_1 <= X, then we require s >= X - T_1 + 1.\n\nYes.\n\nSo final code:\n\n```python\nMOD = 998244353\n\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    N = int(data[0])\n    X = int(data[1])\n    T = list(map(int, data[2:2+N]))\n    \n    invN = pow(N, MOD-2, MOD)\n    \n    # f[t] = probability that a song starts at time t\n    f = [0] * (X+1)\n    f[0] = 1  # song starts at time 0\n    \n    # Fill f[1] to f[X]\n    for t in range(1, X+1):\n        total_prev = 0\n        for duration in T:\n            if t >= duration:\n                total_prev = (total_prev + f[t - duration]) % MOD\n        f[t] = total_prev * invN % MOD\n    \n    # Find the range of start times s such that s <= X < s + T[0]\n    # i.e., s >= X - T[0] + 1 and s <= X\n    low = max(0, X - T[0] + 1)\n    total_sum = 0\n    for s in range(low, X+1):\n        total_sum = (total_sum + f[s]) % MOD\n    \n    # Multiply by 1/N for choosing song 1 at that start time\n    ans = total_sum * invN % MOD\n    print(ans)\n\nmain()\n```\n\nLet’s test with sample 2:\n\nN=5, X=0, T=[1,2,1,2,1]\n\nf[0]=1\n\nlow = max(0,0-1+1)=0\n\ntotal_sum = f[0]=1\n\nans = 1 * inv5 mod MOD = inv5\n\ninv5 = pow(5,998244353-2,998244353)\n\nWe know it should be 598946612\n\nYes.\n\nSo we output the code.\n\nNote: We assume that all operations are mod 998244353.\n\nWe do mod at each addition and multiplication to prevent overflow.\n\nNow, run.\n\nIt should pass.\n\nNote: Constraints: N<=1000, X<=10000, so O(N*X) = 10^3 * 10^4 = 10^7, acceptable in Python.\n\nLet’s write the final code.",
