      "generated_answer": "Let's break down the problem step by step.\n\n### Problem Understanding\n\nWe have:\n- An attraction that can hold **K** people.\n- **N** groups in a queue, where the i-th group has **A_i** people.\n- Each group must be admitted **as a whole** — we can't split a group.\n- We start with **K empty seats**.\n\n### Procedure:\n1. If the queue is empty, **start the attraction once** and end.\n2. Otherwise:\n   - Let `current_empty` be the current number of empty seats.\n   - Let `front_group = A_i` be the size of the first group in the queue.\n   - If `front_group > current_empty`:\n     - **Start the attraction** (so the ride runs, seats reset to K).\n     - Do **not** admit this group yet.\n   - Else:\n     - Admit the entire front group.\n     - Remove the group from the queue.\n     - Reduce `current_empty` by `front_group`.\n3. Repeat until the queue is empty.\n\nWe are to **count how many times the attraction is started**.\n\n> Note: Even when the queue becomes empty, we must **start the attraction one last time** if there are people already inside (i.e., if not all seats are empty).\n\nBut actually, from the logic:\n- Every time we **start** the attraction, we reset the empty seats to K.\n- The attraction is started **only when** the next group doesn't fit.\n- And importantly, **after all groups are processed**, we must still start the attraction **one final time** if there are people in it.\n\nWait — but according to the procedure:\n\n> \"If there are no groups in the queue, start the attraction and end the guidance.\"\n\nSo **even if there are leftover people**, when the queue is empty, we **start the attraction** (and that counts).\n\nBut actually, during the process, we **only start the attraction** when the next group doesn't fit. Then we reset and continue.\n\nSo the **number of starts** is:\n- Each time we **can't fit** the next group → start the ride (count += 1).\n- And finally, when the queue is empty, we **start the ride** (if there are any people inside).\n\nBut note: the procedure says:\n> \"If there are no groups in the queue, start the attraction and end the guidance.\"\n\nSo that final start **always happens** when the queue is empty.\n\nBut wait — is that **in addition** to previous starts?\n\nYes. So the total number of starts is equal to:\n- The number of times we **start the attraction during** the process (because group doesn't fit)\n- **Plus one** for the final start when the queue is empty.\n\nBut let's reexamine:\n\nActually, the procedure is:\n\n1. If queue is empty → start attraction → end.\n2. Else:\n   - If front group size > current_empty → start attraction (ride runs), reset empty seats to K, **but don't remove the group**.\n   - Else → admit group, remove it, reduce empty seats.\n\nSo the **start** happens **before** processing the group that doesn't fit.\n\nAnd the **final start** happens when the queue becomes empty.\n\nBut here's the key: **Every time we start the attraction, it counts as one run.**\n\nSo we need to simulate:\n\n- Initialize:\n  - `empty_seats = K`\n  - `starts = 0`\n  - Queue = list of A_i\n\n- While queue is not empty:\n  - If front group > empty_seats:\n    - Start the attraction → `starts += 1`\n    - Reset `empty_seats = K`\n    - (Do not remove the group)\n  - Else:\n    - Remove front group\n    - `empty_seats -= group_size`\n\n- After the loop (queue is empty), we do:\n  - Start the attraction → `starts += 1`\n\nBut wait — the first step of the loop is:\n> \"If there are no groups in the queue, start the attraction and end.\"\n\nSo we **only** enter the loop body if there **are** groups.\n\nSo algorithm:\n\n```python\nstarts = 0\nempty = K\n\nwhile there are groups:\n    if front group > empty:\n        starts += 1\n        empty = K   # ride starts, resets\n        # don't remove group\n    else:\n        remove front group\n        empty -= group_size\n\n# Now queue is empty\nstarts += 1   ????\n\nWait — but the procedure says:\n> \"If there are no groups in the queue, start the attraction and end.\"\n\nSo that **final start** is part of the procedure.\n\nBut in our simulation, when the queue becomes empty, we break the loop. But according to the procedure, we must **start the attraction** at that point.\n\nBut actually, the attraction is started **only once per \"reset\"**, and the **last batch** of people must be started.\n\nSo yes — when the queue becomes empty, we **must start the attraction** one last time.\n\nBut is that already counted?\n\nLet’s simulate **Sample 1**:\n\nInput:  \nN=7, K=6  \nA = [2, 5, 1, 4, 1, 2, 3]\n\nSimulation:\n\n- empty = 6, starts = 0\n- Queue: [2,5,1,4,1,2,3]\n\n1. 2 <= 6 → admit → empty = 4\n2. 5 > 4 → **start attraction** → starts=1, empty=6 (group [5,...] still in queue)\n3. 5 <= 6 → admit → empty = 1\n4. 1 <= 1 → admit → empty = 0\n5. 4 > 0 → **start attraction** → starts=2, empty=6\n6. 4 <= 6 → admit → empty = 2\n7. 1 <= 2 → admit → empty = 1\n8. 2 > 1 → **start attraction** → starts=3, empty=6\n9. 2 <= 6 → admit → empty = 4\n10. 3 <= 4 → admit → empty = 1\n11. Queue empty → **start attraction** → starts=4\n\nSo final answer = 4.\n\nThus, we **must count the final start** when the queue becomes empty.\n\nBut in our loop, we only count the starts **during** the process when a group doesn't fit.\n\nSo after the loop, we do one more start.\n\nBut wait — is that correct?\n\nThe procedure says:\n> - If there are no groups in the queue, start the attraction and end the guidance.\n\nSo yes — **one final start**.\n\nBut is that always the case?\n\nYes — because even if the attraction is empty (which only happens if last group exactly filled it), we still need to **start the attraction** to run it.\n\nSo every batch ends with a start.\n\nTherefore, **the number of starts is equal to the number of times we start during the process (when group doesn't fit) plus 1 (for the final run)**.\n\nBut wait — actually, every time we **start**, we reset and continue. The **final start** is when the queue is empty.\n\nSo we can simulate as:\n\n```python\nstarts = 0\nempty = K\ngroups = list of A_i\n\nwhile groups:\n    if groups[0] > empty:\n        starts += 1\n        empty = K\n    else:\n        g = groups.pop(0)\n        empty -= g\n\n# Now groups is empty\nstarts += 1  # final start\n```\n\nLet’s test with sample 1:\n\n- groups = [2,5,1,4,1,2,3], empty=6, starts=0\n- 2 <= 6 → empty=4\n- 5 > 4 → starts=1, empty=6\n- 5 <= 6 → empty=1\n- 1 <= 1 → empty=0\n- 4 > 0 → starts=2, empty=6\n- 4 <= 6 → empty=2\n- 1 <= 2 → empty=1\n- 2 > 1 → starts=3, empty=6\n- 2 <= 6 → empty=4\n- 3 <= 4 → empty=1\n- groups empty → starts += 1 → starts=4 → output 4 ✅\n\nSample 2:\n\nN=7, K=10  \nA = [1,10,1,10,1,10,1]\n\nSimulate:\n\n- empty=10\n- 1 <= 10 → empty=9\n- 10 > 9 → start (starts=1), empty=10\n- 10 <= 10 → empty=0\n- 1 > 0 → start (starts=2), empty=10\n- 10 <= 10 → empty=0\n- 1 > 0 → start (starts=3), empty=10\n- 10 <= 10 → empty=0\n- 1 > 0 → start (starts=4), empty=10\n- 1 <= 10 → empty=9\n- groups empty → starts += 1 → starts=5\n\nBut expected output is **7**.\n\nWait! This gives 5, but expected is 7.\n\nSo something is wrong.\n\nWait — let's re-simulate carefully.\n\nSample Input 2:  \n7 10  \n1 10 1 10 1 10 1\n\nLet’s follow the procedure step by step.\n\nInitial: empty=10, queue: [1,10,1,10,1,10,1]\n\n1. Queue not empty.\n   - Front group = 1, empty=10 → 1 <= 10 → admit group.\n   - Remove 1 → empty = 9\n   - Queue: [10,1,10,1,10,1]\n\n2. Front=10, empty=9 → 10 > 9 → **start attraction** (count=1), reset empty=10. Queue still [10,1,10,1,10,1]\n\n3. Front=10 <= 10 → admit → empty=0. Queue: [1,10,1,10,1]\n\n4. Front=1, empty=0 → 1 > 0 → **start attraction** (count=2), reset empty=10. Queue: [1,10,1,10,1]\n\n5. Front=1 <= 10 → admit → empty=9. Queue: [10,1,10,1]\n\n6. Front=10 > 9 → **start attraction** (count=3), reset empty=10. Queue: [10,1,10,1]\n\n7. Front=10 <= 10 → admit → empty=0. Queue: [1,10,1]\n\n8. Front=1 > 0 → **start attraction** (count=4),