egree 2 could be:\n     - a center of a level-2 star (so degree 2), or\n     - a merged leaf (also degree 2)\n\n   So how to distinguish?\n\n   Let’s consider:\n\n   - In a level-2 star: 3 vertices — one center (degree 2), two leaves.\n   - The two leaves may or may not be merged later.\n\n   But the **center remains degree 2**.\n\n   So both centers of level-2 stars and merged leaves have degree 2.\n\n   So we cannot distinguish them just by degree.\n\n---\n\n### 🔍 Better Insight: Reconstruction via \"Smallest Subtrees\"\n\nAlternative idea from known similar problems (e.g., CodeForces \"Recover the Tree\" or AtCoder problems):\n\nWe know:\n- The merging process connects **degree-1 vertices** from different components.\n- So the entire process is similar to building a tree by gluing leaves of stars.\n- The **centers** are the **only vertices that never had their degree changed**.\n- But more importantly, **each star becomes a subtree** in the final tree, and the merging edges connect leaves of different stars.\n\nWait — when two leaves are connected, that edge **joins two stars**.\n\nSo the final tree has:\n- Original star edges (center to leaf),\n- Plus $ M - 1 $ extra edges that connect former leaves.\n\nSo total edges: $ \\sum L_i + (M - 1) = (N - M) + (M - 1) = N - 1 $, which matches.\n\nSo:\n- The final tree has two types of edges:\n  1. **Internal edges** of stars: from center to leaf.\n  2. **Bridge edges**: added during merging, between two leaves.\n\nSo if we can **identify the bridge edges**, then removing them gives us the original stars.\n\nBut we don’t know which edges are bridge edges.\n\nBut note:\n- A bridge edge connects two vertices that were originally in different components.\n- And both endpoints had degree 1 **before** the edge was added.\n- But in the final tree, their degree is at least 2.\n\nBut we don’t know the order.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 Key Insight: Degree-1 vertices in the final tree are **unused leaves**\n\nLet:\n- $ \\ell = $ number of degree-1 vertices in $ T $\n- These were **never used** in any merge → they are original leaves that remained.\n\nThen:\n- Total original leaves = $ N - M $\n- Used leaves = $ (N - M) - \\ell $\n- But used leaves = $ 2(M - 1) $, since each merge uses 2\n\nSo:\n$$\n(N - M) - \\ell = 2(M - 1)\n\\Rightarrow N - M - \\ell = 2M - 2\n\\Rightarrow N - \\ell + 2 = 3M\n\\Rightarrow M = \\frac{N - \\ell + 2}{3}\n$$\n\nWhich matches earlier.\n\nSo we can compute $ M $ from $ \\ell $.\n\nNow, to get the levels $ L_i $, we need to find the degrees of the **centers**.\n\nBut how?\n\nAnother idea:\n\n- Each star has one center.\n- So there are $ M $ centers.\n- The centers are among the vertices.\n- Their degrees in the final tree are equal to their original degrees (unchanged).\n- So if we can **identify the M vertices that are centers**, then their degrees are the $ L_i $.\n\nSo can we identify the centers?\n\nLet’s suppose:\n- A center has degree $ d $ → it had $ d $ leaves.\n- Some of those leaves may have been merged (connected to other components), but the edge from center to leaf remains.\n- So in the final tree, the center still has $ d $ edges to its leaves.\n\nSo the **entire star structure remains**, but some of the leaves may now have extra edges (to other components).\n\nBut in the final tree, the **only thing that changed for a leaf is that it may have an extra edge** (if it was used in a merge).\n\nSo:\n- A leaf that was used in a merge has degree 2: one edge to its center, one to the merged vertex.\n- A leaf not used has degree 1.\n\nSo for a center of degree $ d $, it has $ d $ neighbors, each of which is:\n- either a degree-1 vertex (unused leaf),\n- or a degree-2 vertex (used leaf).\n\nBut a degree-2 vertex could also be a center (of a level-2 star)!\n\nSo again, ambiguity.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Known Solution Approach (from similar problems)\n\nAfter research and known contests (e.g., AtCoder), this problem is known.\n\nThe correct approach:\n\n> The **levels of the st