ices 1,3,4,6,11,13,16,17,18,20 → 10 vertices\n- deg 2: 5,7,9,10,12,15,19 → 7 vertices\n- deg 3: 14 → 1\n- deg 4: 8 → 1\n- deg 7: 2 → 1\n\nSo:\n- Leaves: 10 → $ \\ell = 10 $\n- $ M = (20 - 10 + 2) / 3 = 12 / 3 = 4 $\n\nNow, vertices with degree ≥3:\n- 2: deg 7\n- 8: deg 4\n- 14: deg 3\n- others: deg ≤2\n\nSo $ C_{\\geq3} = 3 $ (vertices 2,8,14) — wait: 2,8,14 → yes, three.\n\nWait: vertex 2 has deg 7, 8 has deg 4, 14 has deg 3 → all ≥3 → 3 vertices.\n\nSo $ C_{\\geq3} = 3 $\n\nNeed $ k = M - 3 = 4 - 3 = 1 $ more center from degree-2 vertices.\n\nSum of degrees of ≥3 vertices: 7 + 4 + 3 = 14\n\nAdd 1 center of degree 2 → total center degree sum = 14 + 2 = 16\n\n$ N - M = 20 - 4 = 16 $ → matches.\n\nSo levels are: [7,4,3,2] → sort: [2,3,4,7] → \"2 3 4 7\" → correct.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Final Algorithm\n\n```python\nRead N\nBuild graph, compute degree of each vertex\nCount leaves (degree == 1) → ℓ\nM = (N - ℓ + 2) // 3\nFind all vertices with degree >= 3 → collect their degrees\nLet count_high = number of such vertices\nLet sum_high = sum of their degrees\nNeed k = M - count_high centers from degree-2 vertices\nEach contributes degree 2, so total center degree sum = sum_high + 2*k\nThis must equal N - M (for consistency)\nThen the levels are:\n   [degrees of high-degree vertices] + [2] * k\nSort and output\n```\n\nNote: We don't need to know which degree-2 vertex is a center — we just need the multiset of levels.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Code\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    n = int(data[0])\n    edges = []\n    index = 1\n    degree = [0] * (n + 1)\n    \n    for _ in range(n - 1):\n        u = int(data[index])\n        v = int(data[index + 1])\n        index += 2\n        edges.append((u, v))\n        degree[u] += 1\n        degree[v] += 1\n    \n    # Count leaves (degree 1)\n    leaf_count = 0\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        if degree[i] == 1:\n            leaf_count += 1\n    \n    # Number of initial stars\n    m = (n - 