  - Similarly, `j = bisect.bisect_left(b, q)`\n   - Then increment `piece_count[(i,j)]`\n6. Let `counts = list of all values in piece_count.values()`\n7. Let `non_empty = len(counts)`\n8. `total_pieces = (A+1) * (B+1)`\n9. If `non_empty < total_pieces`:\n     min_val = 0\n   Else:\n     min_val = min(counts)\n10. max_val = max(counts) if counts else 0  # if no strawberries, but N>=1 so safe\n11. Print min_val, max_val\n\nBut what if N=0? But constraint says N>=1.\n\nLet’s test with sample 1:\n\n### Sample Input 1:\n```\n7 6\n5\n6 1\n3 1\n4 2\n1 5\n6 2\n2\n2 5\n2\n3 4\n```\n\nVertical cuts: [2,5] → A=2 → 3 vertical strips:\n- [0,2), [2,5), [5,7] → but cuts at x=2 and x=5\n- So strips:\n  - strip 0: x < 2\n  - strip 1: 2 < x < 5\n  - strip 2: x > 5\n\nSimilarly horizontal cuts: [3,4] → B=2 → 3 horizontal strips:\n- strip 0: y < 3\n- strip 1: 3 < y < 4\n- strip 2: y > 4\n\nNow assign strawberries:\n\n1. (6,1): \n   - x=6 → bisect_left([2,5],6) → index=2 (since 6>2,6>5)\n   - y=1 → bisect_left([3,4],1) → index=0\n   - piece (2,0)\n\n2. (3,1):\n   - x=3 → bisect_left([2,5],3) → 3>2, 3<5 → index=1\n   - y=1 → index=0\n   - (1,0)\n\n3. (4,2):\n   - x=4 → index=1 (since 2<4<5)\n   - y=2 → index=0\n   - (1,0)\n\n4. (1,5):\n   - x=1 → index=0\n   - y=5 → bisect_left([3,4],5)=2 (5>3,5>4)\n   - (0,2)\n\n5. (6,2):\n   - x=6 → index=2\n   - y=2 → index=0\n   - (2,0)\n\nSo counts:\n- (2,0): 2\n- (1,0): 2\n- (0,2): 1\n\nSo values: [2,2,1] → min among non-empty = 1, max=2\n\nBut total_pieces = (2+1)*(2+1)=9\nnon_empty pieces = 3 < 9 → so min = 0\n\nThus output: 0 2 → matches sample.\n\nSample Input 2:\n```\n4 4\n4\n1 1\n3 1\n3 3\n1 3\n1\n2\n1\n2\n```\n\nW=4, H=4\nN=4\nStrawberries: (1,1), (3,1), (3,3), (1,3)\nA=1, a=[2]\nB=1, b=[2]\n\nVertical strips:\n- strip 0: x < 2 → x in (0,2)\n- strip 1: x > 2 → x in (2,4)\n\nHorizontal strips:\n- strip 0: y < 2\n- strip 1: y > 2\n\nAssign:\n- (1,1): (0,0)\n- (3,1): (1,0)\n- (3,3): (1,1)\n- (1,3): (0,1)\n\nEach piece has one strawberry.\n\nCou