-empty)\n- Print min_val, max_val\n\nLet's write the code accordingly.\n\nNote: We must import bisect.\n\nWe'll do:\n\n```python\nimport bisect\n\ndef main():\n    import sys\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().split()\n    \n    # Parse first values\n    idx = 0\n    W = int(data[idx]); H = int(data[idx+1]); idx += 2\n    N = int(data[idx]); idx += 1\n    \n    strawberries = []\n    for _ in range(N):\n        p = int(data[idx]); q = int(data[idx+1]); idx += 2\n        strawberries.append((p, q))\n    \n    A = int(data[idx]); idx += 1\n    a_cuts = list(map(int, data[idx:idx+A])); idx += A\n    \n    B = int(data[idx]); idx += 1\n    b_cuts = list(map(int, data[idx:idx+B])); idx += B\n    \n    from collections import defaultdict\n    count = defaultdict(int)\n    \n    # Assign each strawberry to a piece\n    for p, q in strawberries:\n        i = bisect.bisect_left(a_cuts, p)  # vertical strip index\n        j = bisect.bisect_left(b_cuts, q)  # horizontal strip index\n        count[(i, j)] += 1\n    \n    total_pieces = (A + 1) * (B + 1)\n    non_empty_pieces = len(count)\n    \n    if non_empty_pieces < total_pieces:\n        min_val = 0\n    else:\n        min_val = min(count.values())\n    \n    max_val = max(count.values())\n    \n    print(min_val, max_val)\n\nmain()\n```\n\nLet’s test with the samples.\n\nSample 1:\n- a_cuts = [2,5], b_cuts=[3,4]\n- strawberries:\n  - (6,1): i = bisect_left([2,5],6)=2, j = bisect_left([3,4],1)=0 → (2,0)\n  - (3,1): i = bisect_left([2,5],3)=1 (since 3>2, 3<5 → index=1), j=0 → (1,0)\n  - (4,2): i=1, j=0 → (1,0)\n  - (1,5): i=0, j=2 → (0,2)\n  - (6,2): i=2, j=0 → (2,0)\n- count: (2,0):2, (1,0):2, (0,2):1 → values: [2,2,1] → min=1, max=2\n- total_pieces = 3*3=9, non_empty=3 <9 → min_val=0\n- output: \"0 2\" → correct.\n\nSample 2:\n- a_cuts=[2], b_cuts=[2]\n- (1,1): i = bisect_left([2],1)=0, j=0 → (0,0)\n- (3,1): i = bisect_left([2],3)=1, j=0 → (1,0)\n- (3,3): i=1, j=1 → (1,1)\n- (1,3): i=0, j=1 → (0,1)\n- count: 4 pieces, each count=1\n- total_pieces= (1+1)*(1+1)=4, non_empty=4 → min_val = min([1,1,1,1])=1\n- max=1 → \"1 1\" → correct.\n\nEdge: what if all strawberries in one piece? Then non_empty=1, total_pieces could be large → min=0.\n\nYes.\n\nSo code is correct.\n\nWe'll write accordingly.\n\nNote: We are reading all stdin at once with `sys.stdin.read().split()` for efficiency, since input can be large.\n\nFinal code:\n\n\n```python\nimport bisect\nimport sys\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not data:\n        return\n    \n    idx = 0\n    W = int(data[idx]); H = int(data[idx+1]); idx += 2\n    N = int(data[idx]); idx += 1\n    \n    strawberries = []\n    for _ in range(N):\n        p = int(data[idx]); q = int(data[idx+1]); idx += 2\n        strawberries.append((p, q))\n    \n    A = int(data[idx]); idx += 1\n    a_cuts = list(map(int, data[idx:idx+A])); idx += A\n    \n    B = int(data[idx]); idx += 1\n    b_cuts = list(map(int, data[idx:idx+B])); idx += B\n    \n    count = defaultdict(int)\n    \n    for p, q in strawberries:\n        i = bisect.bisect_left(a_cuts, p)\n        j = bisect.bisect_left(b_cuts, q)\n        count[(i, j)] += 1\n    \n    total_pieces = (A + 1) * (B + 1)\n    non_empty_pieces = len(count)\n    \n    min_val = 0 if non_empty_pieces < total_pieces else min(count.values())\n    max_val = max(count.values())\n    \n    print(min_val, max_val)\n\nmain()\n```",
