heap:\n          neg_t, u = heapq.heappop(heap)\n          t = -neg_t\n          if t < dp[u]:  # outdated\n              continue\n          for (l, d, k, c, v) in incoming[u]:\n              # train from v to u\n              latest_arrival_allowed = dp[u] - c\n              if latest_arrival_allowed < l:\n                  continue\n              # j from 0 to k-1: t_j = l + j*d <= latest_arrival_allowed\n              j = (latest_arrival_allowed - l) // d\n              if j < 0:\n                  continue\n              j = min(j, k-1)\n              candidate = l + j * d\n              if candidate > dp[v]:\n                  dp[v] = candidate\n                  heapq.heappush(heap, (-dp[v], v))\n\n      for i in range(1, N):\n          if dp[i] < 0:\n              print(\"Unreachable\")\n          else:\n              print(dp[i])\n\n   Let's test with sample 1.\n\n   But note: in sample 1, station 5 has a train to 6: (7,10,2,3,5,6) -> so stored in incoming[6] as (7,10,2,3,5)\n\n   Then when processing u=6, we compute for v=5: \n        latest_arrival_allowed = INF - 3 ~ INF\n        j = (INF - 7) // 10 -> huge, but min with 1 -> j=1\n        candidate = 7+10=17 -> dp[5]=17\n\n   Then when processing u=5, we look at incoming[5]: \n        which has (3,10,2,4,2) -> from 2 to 5\n        latest_arrival_allowed = 17 - 4 = 13\n        j = (13-3)//10 = 1 -> candidate = 3+10=13\n        then update dp[2] = max(dp[2], 13) -> but later we get 56 from station 3, so it will be updated.\n\n   So it's correct.\n\n   We'll run the code on the samples.\n\n   However, note: the train from 2 to 3: (5,3,18,2,2,3) -> stored in incoming[3] as (5,3,18,2,2)\n\n   When processing u=3 (dp[3]=58), then for this train:\n        latest_arrival_allowed = 58 - 2 = 56\n        j = (56-5)//3 = 51//3 = 17 -> min(17,17)=17\n        candidate = 5+3*17 = 56 -> update dp[2]=56\n\n   Correct.\n\n   So we write the code accordingly.\n\n   Let me run sample 2:\n\n   Train1: (1000000000,1000000000