    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_507",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nGiven a string s, you need to partition it into one or more balanced substrings. For example, if s == \"ababcc\" then (\"abab\", \"c\", \"c\"), (\"ab\", \"abc\", \"c\"), and (\"ababcc\") are all valid partitions, but (\"a\", \"bab\", \"cc\"), (\"aba\", \"bc\", \"c\"), and (\"ab\", \"abcc\") are not. The unbalanced substrings are bolded.\nReturn the minimum number of substrings that you can partition s into.\nNote: A balanced string is a string where each character in the string occurs the same number of times.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: s = \"fabccddg\"\nOutput: 3\nExplanation:\nWe can partition the string s into 3 substrings in one of the following ways: (\"fab, \"ccdd\", \"g\"), or (\"fabc\", \"cd\", \"dg\").\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: s = \"abababaccddb\"\nOutput: 2\nExplanation:\nWe can partition the string s into 2 substrings like so: (\"abab\", \"abaccddb\").\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= s.length <= 1000\ns consists only of English lowercase letters.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef minPartitions(s: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a string s, partitions it into the minimum number of balanced substrings.\n    A balanced substring is one where each character in the string occurs the same number of times.\n    \n    Uses dynamic programming where dp[i] is the minimum number of balanced \n    partitions for the prefix s[0:i].\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(s)\n    # dp[i] stores the minimum number of partitions for the prefix of length i\n    dp = [float('inf')] * (n + 1)\n    dp[0] = 0\n    \n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        # We check every possible substring ending at i\n        counts = {}\n        distinct_chars = 0\n        max_freq = 0\n        \n        # Iterating backwards from i to 1 to update character counts incrementally\n        for j in range(i - 1, -1, -1):\n            char = s[j]\n            counts[char] = counts.get(char, 0) + 1\n            \n            # Update distinct character count and the maximum frequency found so far\n            if counts[char] == 1:\n                distinct_chars += 1\n            max_freq = max(max_freq, counts[char])\n            \n            # A substring is balanced if all characters in it appear exactly max_freq times.\n            # This is equivalent to checking if the sum of all frequencies equals \n            # (distinct_chars * max_freq).\n            total_chars = i - j\n            if total_chars == (distinct_chars * max_freq):\n                dp[i] = min(dp[i], dp[j] + 1)\n                \n    return int(dp[n])\n```",
