ctual_print_time\n```\n\nThis is a known greedy strategy for \"scheduling jobs with intervals and cooldown\" — similar to \"maximum jobs on a machine with cooldown\", and sorting by deadline (exit time) is optimal.\n\n---\n\n### Why sort by exit time?\n\nBecause if a product leaves early, we must print on it early. If we delay it, it may expire. So we handle the ones with **earliest deadline** first.\n\nThis is analogous to the classic \"minimize maximum lateness\" or \"maximize number of jobs\" scheduling — where sorting by deadline is optimal.\n\n---\n\n### Let's Test on Sample 1\n\nInput:\n```\n5\n1 1  → [1,2]\n1 1  → [1,2]\n2 1  → [2,3]\n1 2  → [1,3]\n1 4  → [1,5]\n```\n\nCompute exit times:\n- (1,1): 2\n- (1,1): 2\n- (2,1): 3\n- (1,2): 3\n- (1,4): 5\n\nSort by exit time:\n1. (1,1): [1,2]\n2. (1,1): [1,2]\n3. (2,1): [2,3]\n4. (1,2): [1,3]\n5. (1,4): [1,5]\n\nNow simulate:\n\n- last = -1\n- count = 0\n\n1. (1,1): earliest print = -1 + 1 = 0; actual = max(1,0) = 1; 1 <= 2 → print at 1 → last = 1, count = 1\n2. (1,1): earliest = 2; actual = max(1,2) = 2; 2 <= 2 → print at 2 → last = 2, count = 2\n3. (2,1): earliest = 3; actual = max(2,3) = 3; 3 <= 3 → print at 3 → last = 3, count = 3\n4. (1,2): earliest = 4; actual = max(1,4) = 4; 4 <= 3? No → skip\n5. (1,4): earliest = 4; actual = max(1,4) = 4; 4 <= 5 → print at 4 → last = 4, count = 4\n\nTotal: 4 → matches.\n\nBut wait: product (1,2) is available until 3, but we try to print at 4 → too late.\n\nBut why is (1,2) after (2,1)? Both have exit time 3. But we sorted by exit time only. Should we break ties?\n\nLet’s see: (2,1) ends at 3, (1,2) ends at 3 → same.\n\nBut (1,2) is available from 1 to 3, so we could have printed at 3? But we printed at 3 on (2,1), and (2,1) enters at 2 — so at time 3, both are still in range? (1,2) leaves at 3, so can print at 3.\n\nBut we printed (2,1) at 3 — can we print (1,2) at 3? No — same time, but printer needs 1 microsecond cooldown.\n\nWe printed at time 2, then next at 3 → allowed 