4,1): [4,5]\n- (4,1): [4,5]\n\nExit 6:\n- (5,1): [5,6]\n- (5,1): [5,6]\n- (2,4): [2,6]\n\nNow simulate:\n\nlast = -1, count = 0\n\n1. (1,2): earliest = 0, actual = max(1,0)=1 ≤ 3 → print at 1 → last=1, count=1\n2. (2,1): earliest = 2, actual = max(2,2)=2 ≤ 3 → print at 2 → last=2, count=2\n\nNow exit 5 group — sort arbitrarily? Let's try in order given:\n\n3. (4,1): earliest = 3, actual = max(4,3)=4 ≤ 5 → print at 4 → last=4, count=3\n4. (1,4): earliest = 5, actual = max(1,5)=5 ≤ 5 → print at 5 → last=5, count=4\n5. (3,2): earliest = 6, actual = max(3,6)=6 > 5 → skip\n6. (4,1): earliest = 6 > 5 → skip\n7. (4,1): skip\n\nNow exit 6:\n\n8. (5,1): earliest = 6, actual = max(5,6)=6 ≤ 6 → print at 6 → last=6, count=5\n9. (5,1): earliest = 7 > 6 → skip\n10. (2,4): earliest = 7, actual = max(2,7)=7 > 6 → skip\n\nTotal: 5\n\nBut expected is 6.\n\nSo we only got 5, but expected 6.\n\nSo something is wrong.\n\nWait — maybe the order within same exit time matters.\n\nWe did (4,1), (1,4), (3,2), etc.\n\nBut (1,4) has a long interval [1,5] — we printed at 5.\n\nBut (3,2): [3,5] — we tried at 6 → too late.\n\nBut if we had printed (3,2) earlier?\n\nBut we printed (4,1) at 4, (1,4) at 5.\n\nBut what if we do (3,2) before (1,4)?\n\nAfter (2,1) at time 2:\n\nlast = 2\n\nNow consider (3,2): [3,5] → earliest = 3, actual = max(3,3)=3 ≤ 5 → print at 3 → last=3, count=3\n\nThen (4,1): earliest=4, actual=max(4,4)=4 ≤5 → print at 4 → last=4, count=4\n\nThen (1,4): earliest=5, actual=5 ≤5 → print at 5 → count=5\n\nThen (5,1): earliest=6, print at 6 → count=6\n\nThen others: skip\n\nSo we get 6.\n\nAh! So the **order within same exit time matters**.\n\nWe should **sort by exit time, and within same exit time, by start time? or by something else?**\n\nIn this case, (3,2) starts at 3, (1,4) starts at 1 — but we want to do (3,2) first because it **starts later**? No.\n\nBut (3,2) has **tighter window**? It can only be printed from 3 to 5.\n\nBut (1,4) can be printed from 1 to 5 — more flexible.\n\nSo we should **prioritize less flexible intervals first**.\n\nThat is: among same deadline, do the one with **later start time** first? Because it has less room to be scheduled late.\n\nAlternatively: sort by exit time, and then by start time descending? Or by length?\n\nBut the known optimal strategy for this problem (which is known as \"interval scheduling with minimum separation\") is:\n\n> Sort by exit time (T_i + D_i). If equal, sort by T_i descending (i.e., later start first), because they have less flexibility.\n\nWhy? A product that starts late has fewer chances to be printed — we must print it in [start, exit], and if start is high, we cannot delay it.\n\nWhereas a product that starts early can be printed later.\n\nSo in our case:\n\nFor exit time 5:\n- (1,4): [1,5] — very flexible\n- (3,2): [3,5] — less flexible\n- (4,1): [4,5] — even less\n\nSo we should sort by **exit time ascending, then by start time descending** (i.e., later start first).\n\nLet's try that.\n\nSame exit time group (5): sort by T_i descending:\n\n- (4,1): T=4\n- (4,1): T=4\n- (4,1): T=4\n- (3,2): T=3\n- (1,4): T=1\n\nSo order: all T=4 first, then T=3, then T=1.\n\nBut we have three (4,1): all T=4.\n\nNow simulate:\n\nAfter printing (1,2) at 1, (2,1) at 2 → last=2, count=2\n\nNow:\n\n1. (4,1): earliest=3, actual=max(4,3)=4 ≤5 → print at 4 → last=4, count=3\n2. (4,1): earliest=5, actual=max(4,5)=5 ≤5 → print at 5 → last=5, count=4\n3. (4,1): earliest=6 >5 → skip\n4. (3,2): earliest=6 >5 → skip\n5. (1,4): earliest=6 >5 → skip\n\nThen exit 6:\n- (5,1): earliest=6, actual=max(5,6)=6 ≤6 → print at 6 → count=5\n- (5,1): skip\n- (2,4): earliest=7>6 → skip\n\nTotal: 5 → still not 6.\n\nBut we want to print (3,2) at 3.\n\nSo we need to do (3,2) before the (4,1)s?\n\nBut (3,2) has T=3 < 4.\n\nSo if we sort by **start time ascending**? No.\n\nWait — we want to do jobs that **must be done earlier** first.\n\nBut (3,2) can be done at 3, but (4,1) cannot be done before 4.\n\nSo (4,1) must be done