gth 1.\n\nBut we want to do shorter intervals first? Because less flexible.\n\nYes — shorter availability window means less flexibility.\n\nSo sort by exit time, then by D_i ascending (duration).\n\nLet's try:\n\nExit time 5:\n- (4,1): D=1\n- (4,1): D=1\n- (4,1): D=1\n- (3,2): D=2\n- (1,4): D=4\n\nSo do (4,1) first.\n\nBut we saw that doing (4,1) first at 4, then (3,2) at 5, then (1,4) at 6 — fails.\n\nBut if we do (3,2) at 3, then (4,1) at 4, then (1,4) at 5 — works.\n\nSo we need to do (3,2) before (4,1)? But (3,2) has longer duration.\n\nSo not duration.\n\nAnother idea: sort by **start time descending** — so that later-starting ones go first.\n\n(4,1): T=4\n(4,1): T=4\n(4,1): T=4\n(3,2): T=3\n(1,4): T=1\n\nThen:\n- (4,1): earliest=3, actual=4 → print at 4\n- (4,1): earliest=5, actual=5 → print at 5\n- (4,1): skip\n- (3,2): earliest=6 >5 → skip\n- (1,4): skip\n\nThen (5,1) at 6 → count=4 (from earlier) +1 =5\n\nNot 6.\n\nBut if we do (3,2) before the (4,1)s, we can do it at 3.\n\nSo how about sort by **start time ascending**?\n\nThen order:\n- (1,4): T=1\n- (3,2): T=3\n- (4,1): T=4\n- (4,1): T=4\n- (4,1): T=4\n\nNow:\n- (1,4): earliest=3, actual=max(1,3)=3 ≤5 → print at 3 → last=3\n- (3,2): earliest=4, actual=max(3,4)=4 ≤5 → print at 4 → last=4\n- (4,1): earliest=5, actual=5 → print at 5 → last=5\n- (4,1): earliest=6>5 → skip\n- (4,1): skip\n\nThen (5,1): at 6 → count=2 (from first two) +3 =5, then +1 =6\n\nYes! count=6.\n\nAnd the products printed:\n- (1,2) at 1\n- (2,1) at 2\n- (1,4) at 3\n- (3,2) at 4\n- (4,1) at 5\n- (5,1) at 6\n\nAll valid.\n\nBut we printed (1,4) at 3 — which is fine.\n\nAnd (3,2) at 4 — within [3,5]\n\nAnd (4,1) at 5 — within [4,5]\n\nAnd (5,1) at 6 — within [5,6]\n\nPerfect.\n\nBut why did sorting by start time ascending work?\n\nBecause (1,4) has early start, so it can be printed early, freeing up later times for others.\n\nBut (4,1) cannot be printed before 4, so they are less flexible in start, but we can delay them.\n\nThe known solution for this exact problem is:\n\n> Sort by exit time (T_i + D_i), and if tie, sort by T_i (start time) ascending.\n\nBut is that always optimal?\n\nAlternatively, there is a known problem: \"maximum number of intervals that can be hit with points at least 1 apart\" — and the solution is:\n\n- Sort by exit time.\n- For ties in exit time, it doesn't matter much, but to be safe, we can sort by start time ascending.\n\nBut in our case, sorting by exit time then start time ascending gave us 6.\n\nBut in sample 1, does it matter?\n\nSample 1 exit time 2: two products with T_i=1 — same, so order doesn't matter.\n\nExit time 3: (2,1) and (1,2) — T_i=2 and 1.\n\nIf we sort by T_i ascending: (1,2) then (2,1)\n\nAfter printing two at 1 and 2:\n\n- (1,2): earliest=3, actual=max(1,3)=3 <=3 → print at 3\n- (2,1): earliest=4, actual=4 >3 → skip\n\nThen (1,4) at 4.\n\nCount=4.\n\nIf reverse: (2,1) then (1,2)\n\n- (2,1): earliest=3, actual=3 <=3 → print at 3\n- (1,2): earliest=4, actual=4 >3 → skip\n\nSame.\n\nSo both give 4.\n\nSo sorting by exit time, then by start time ascending is fine.\n\nBut is there a case where it fails?\n\nAlternatively, we can try a different approach: sort only by exit time, and within same exit time, it doesn't matter — but our simulation showed that it does matter.\n\nBut in competitive programming, a known solution for this exact problem is to sort by exit time, and then greedily assign.\n\nBut to be safe, we'll sort by exit time, and then by start time ascending.\n\nBut wait — in sample 3, we have three (4,1) with T_i=4 — they are identical.\n\nBut we only printed one of them? No, we printed one at 5.\n\nBut there are three (4,1): we only need to print on one product per label? No — the input has multiple products with same T_i, D_i — they are distinct products.\n\nIn sample input 3:\n\n```\n4 1\n...\n4 1\n...\n4 1\n```\n\nThree separate products with [4,5].\n\nIn our simulation with sort by exit time then start time ascending, we did:\n\n- (1,4) at 3\n- (3,2) at 4\n- then first (4,1): earliest=5, actual=5 → print at 5\n- next (4,1): earliest=6>5 → skip\n- next (4,1): skip\n\nSo only one (4,1) printed.\n\nBut could we print two (4,1)? Only if we print one at 4 and one at 5.\n\nBut at time 4, we printed (3,2).\n\nIf we had printed a (4,1) at 4, then (3,2) at 5, then (1,4) at 6 — too late.\n\nBut (1,4) could have been printed at 3.\n\nBut we did.\n\nBut if we print (4,1) at 4, (3,2) at 5, (1,4) at 3 — same as before, but swap (3,2) and (4,1).\n\nBut (4,1) can be printed at 4, (3,2) at 5.\n\nThen (1,4) at 3.\n\nThen another (4,1) at 6? But 6>5 — no.\n\nSo still only one (4,1).\n\nBut there are three (4,1) — can we print on two of them?\n\nOnly if we print at 4 and 5.\n\nBut at 4, we could print one (4,1), at 5 another (4,1).\n\nBut then (3,2) would need to be at 3 or 5.\n\nIf at 3: then (4,1) at 4, (4,1) at 5, (3,2) at 3.\n\nAnd (1,4) at 6? too late.\n\nBut (1,4) could be at 3? conflict with (3,2).\n\nOr at 4? conflict with (4,1).\n\nOr at 5? conflict with (4,1).\n\nSo (1,4) cannot be printed.\n\nBut previously we printed (1,4), (3,2), and one (4,1) — same count.\n\nSo maximum is 6.\n\nSo our simulation with sort by exit time then start time ascending works.\n\nBut in the simulation, we did not print on a (4,1) at 4 — we printed (3,2) at 4.\n\nBut we could have printed a (4,1) at 4.\n\nBut it doesn't increase count.\n\nSo the algorithm is:\n\n- Read N\n- Read N lines of T_i, D_i\n- Create list of (T_i, D_i, exit_i = T_i + D_i)\n- Sort by exit_i, then by T_i (start time) ascending\n- last_print = -1\n- count = 0\n- For each product in sorted order:\n    earliest = last_print + 1\n    actual = max(T_i, earliest)\n    if actual <= T_i + D_i:\n        count += 1\n        last_print = actual\n\nPrint count.\n\nLet's run sample 3 with this:\n\nProducts:\n\n1. (1,2) -> exit=3, T=1\n2. (2,1) -> exit=3, T=2\n3. (4,1) -> exit=5, T=4\n4. (1,4) -> exit=5, T=1\n5. (3,2) -> exit=5, T=3\n6. (5,1) -> exit=6, T=5\n7. (5,1) -> exit=6, T=5\n8. (4,1) -> exit=5, T=4\n9. (4,1) -> exit=5, T=4\n10. (2,4) -> exit=6, T=2\n\nSort by exit, then T:\n\nExit 3:\n- (1,2): T=1\n- (2,1): T=2\n\nExit 5:\n- (1,4): T=1\n- (3,2): T=3\n- (4,1): T=4\n- (4,1): T=4\n- (4,1): T=4\n\nExit 6:\n- (2,4): T=2\n- (5,1): T=5\n- (5,1): T=5\n\nNow simulate:\n\nlast = -1\n\n1. (1,2): actual = max(1,0)=1 <=3 -> print at 1, last=1, count=1\n2. (2,1): actual = max(2,2)=2 <=3 -> print at 2, last=2, count=2\n3. (1,4): actual = max(1,3)=3 <=5 -> print at 3, last=3, count=3\n4. (3,2): actual = max(3,4)=4 <=5 -> print at 4, last=4, count=4\n5. (4,1): actual = max(4,5)=5 <=5 -> print at 5, last=5, count=5\n6. (4,1): actual = max(4,6)=6 >5 -> skip\n7. (4,1): skip\n8. (2,4): actual = max(2,6)=6 <=6 -> print at 6, last=6, count=6\n9. (5,1): actual = max(5,7)=7 >6 -> skip\n10. (5,1): skip\n\nCount=6 -> matches.\n\nNote: we printed (2,4) at 6, not (5,1). But (5,1) requires to be printed between 5 and 6, and at 6 it is still in range (since leaves at 6), so can print at 6.\n\nBut we printed (2,4) at 6 — is that allowed? (2,4) is available until 2+4=6, so yes, can print at 6.\n\nAnd (5,1) could also be printed at 6, but we can only do one.\n\nBut both are valid.\n\nSo count=6.\n\nPerfect.\n\nSo the algorithm is correct.\n\n### Final Code\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    import sys\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    n = int(data[0])\n    products = []\n    index = 1\n    for i in range(n):\n        t = int(data[index]); d = int(data[index+1]); index += 2\n        products.append((t, d, t + d))\n    \n    # Sort by exit time (t+d), then by start time (t) ascending\n    products.sort(key=lambda x: (x[2], x[0]))\n    \n    last_print = -1\n    count = 0\n    for t, d, exit_time in products:\n        earliest = last_print + 1\n        actual = max(t, earliest)\n        if actual <= exit_time:\n            count += 1\n            last_print = actual\n    \n    print(count)\n\nmain()\n```\n\nLet's run with sample 1 to be sure.\n\nSample 1:\n\nProducts:\n(1,1,2), (1,1,2), (2,1,3), (1,2,3), (1,4,5)\n\nSort by exit, then t:\nexit 2: (1,1,2), (1,1,2) — both t=1\nexit 3: (1,2,3) t=1, (2,1,3) t=2\nexit 5: (1,4,5) t=1\n\nWait, (1,2,3) has t=1, (2,1,3) has t=2 — so (1,2) before (2,1)\n\nBut in sample, (1,2) is available [1,3], (2,1) [2,3]\n\nNow simulate:\n\nlast=-1\n\n1. (1,1): actual=max(1,0)=1<=2 -> print at 1, last=1, count=1\n2. (1,1): actual=max(1,2)=2<=2 -> print at 2, last=2, count=2\n3. (1,2): actual=max(1,3)=3<=3 -> print at 3, last=3, count=3\n4. (2,1): actual=max(2,4)=4>3 -> skip\n5. (1,4): actual=max(1,4)=4<=5 -> print at 4, count=4\n\nOutput 4 — correct.\n\nNote: we printed (1,2) at 3, not (2,1). But both are valid.\n\nSo it works.\n\nFinal code below.",
