      "generated_answer": "We are given a string `S` of length `N` consisting of `'0'` and `'1'`. This represents a binary sequence $ A = (A_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_N) $, where $ A_i = 0 $ if the i-th character is `'0'`, and 1 otherwise.\n\nWe need to compute:\n$$\n\\sum_{1 \\leq i \\leq j \\leq N} f(i,j)\n$$\nwhere $ f(i,j) $ is defined recursively as:\n- $ f(i,i) = A_i $\n- $ f(i,j) = f(i,j-1) \\barwedge A_j $ for $ i < j $\n\nThe **NAND** operator $ \\barwedge $ is defined as:\n- $ 0 \\barwedge 0 = 1 $\n- $ 0 \\barwedge 1 = 1 $\n- $ 1 \\barwedge 0 = 1 $\n- $ 1 \\barwedge 1 = 0 $\n\nThat is:\n$$\nx \\barwedge y = \n\\begin{cases}\n1 & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ or } y = 0 \\\\\n0 & \\text{if } x = 1 \\text{ and } y = 1\n\\end{cases}\n\\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x \\barwedge y = \\text{not}(x \\text{ and } y)\n$$\n\nSo we can define:\n```python\ndef nand(x, y):\n    return 1 if not (x and y) else 0\n```\n\nBut note: the function $ f(i,j) $ is the **left-associative NAND** of the subarray $ A[i:j+1] $. That is:\n$$\nf(i,j) = (((A_i \\barwedge A_{i+1}) \\barwedge A_{i+2}) \\barwedge \\cdots \\barwedge A_j)\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Naive Approach (Too Slow)\n\nA direct double loop over all $ i, j $ with $ i \\leq j $, and for each $ i $, compute $ f(i,j) $ incrementally, would be $ O(N^2) $. But $ N \\leq 10^6 $, so $ O(N^2) $ is too slow.\n\nWe **cannot** do $ O(N^2) $.\n\nWe need a **linear or near-linear** solution.\n\n---\n\n### Key Observations\n\nLet’s understand how the NAND operation behaves when applied repeatedly.\n\nLet’s define:\n- $ f(i,j) $: the NAND of $ A_i, A_{i+1}, \\ldots, A_j $, left-associated.\n\nLet’s analyze the behavior of a sequence of NANDs.\n\nLet $ x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_k $ be a binary sequence.\n\nDefine:\n- $ v_1 = x_1 $\n- $ v_2 = x_1 \\barwedge x_2 $\n- $ v_3 = (x_1 \\barwedge x_2) \\barwedge x_3 $\n- $ v_k = v_{k-1} \\barwedge x_k $\n\nWe can simulate this easily for a single starting index $ i $, but doing it for all $ i $ would be $ O(N^2) $.\n\nWe need to **optimize the summation** over all $ i \\leq j $.\n\nLet’s try to find **patterns** or **state transitions**.\n\n---\n\n### Insight: Behavior of Left-Associative NAND\n\nLet’s simulate some sequences.\n\n#### Case 1: Starting with 0\n- $ 0 $ → 0\n- $ 0 \\barwedge x = 1 $ for any $ x $ (since 0 NAND x = 1)\n- $ 1 \\barwedge x = \\text{not } x $\n- $ (1 \\barwedge x) \\barwedge y = \\text{not}( (\\text{not } x) \\text{ and } y ) $\n\nLet’s try:\n- `0` → 0\n- `0,0` → 0 NAND 0 = 1\n- `0,0,1` → (0 NAND 0) NAND 1 = 1 NAND 1 = 0\n- `0,0,1,1` → 0 NAND 1 = 1\n- `0,0,1,1,0` → 1 NAND 0 = 1\n\nSo: `00110` → [0,1,0,1,1] for f(1,j)\n\nWait, this matches the example.\n\nBut can we find a pattern?\n\nLet’s define for a fixed starting index $ i $, the value $ f(i,j) $ as we extend $ j $.\n\nLet’s denote $ v_j = f(i,j) $. Then:\n- $ v_i = A_i $\n- $ v_{j} = v_{j-1} \\barwedge A_j = \\text{not}(v_{j-1} \\text{ and } A_j) $\n\nSo the recurrence is:\n$$\nv_j = \n\\begin{cases}\n1 & \\text{if } v_{j-1} = 0 \\text{ or } A_j = 0 \\\\\n0 & \\text{if } v_{j-1} = 1 \\text{ and } A_j = 1\n\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThis is deterministic.\n\nNow, can we simulate this for all starting positions efficiently?\n\nStill, doing it naively is $ O(N^2) $, which is too slow.\n\nBut note: **the values are binary**, and the recurrence depends only on the previous value and current bit.\n\nSo for a fixed start $ i $, the sequence $ v_j $ evolves deterministically.\n\nBut we need the sum over all $ i \\leq j $ of $ f(i,j) $.\n\nLet’s denote:\n$$\n\\text{Answer} = \\sum_{i=1}^N \\sum_{j=i}^N f(i,j)\n$$\n\nWe can swap the order:\n$$\n= \\sum_{j=1}^N \\sum_{i=1}^j f(i,j)\n$$\n\nSo for each $ j $, we want to compute the sum of $ f(i,j) $ over all $ i \\leq j $.\n\nLet’s define $ F(j) = \\sum_{i=1}^j f(i,j) $\n\nThen answer = $ \\sum_{j=1}^N F(j) $\n\nSo if we can compute $ F(j) $ efficiently (say in $ O(1) $ amortized), we can get $ O(N) $.\n\nSo the idea: **maintain, for each j, the values of f(i,j) for all i ≤ j**, and update them as j increases.\n\nBut storing all $ f(i,j) $ is $ O(N) $ per j → $ O(N^2) $.\n\nBut note: $ f(i,j) = f(i,j-1) \\barwedge A_j $\n\nSo:\n$$\nf(i,j) = \\text{nand}(f(i,j-1), A_j)\n$$\n\nSo we can think of maintaining an array $ dp[i] = f(i,j) $ at current $ j $, and update it to $ dp[i] = \\text{nand}(dp[i], A_j) $ for all $ i \\leq j $.\n\nBut updating all $ i $ from 1 to j is $ O(j) $, so total $ O(N^2) $ — too slow.\n\nBut maybe we can **compress** the state.\n\nObservation: $ f(i,j) $ is binary. So for fixed $ j $, the values $ f(i,j) $ for $ i=1..j $ are 0s and 1s.\n\nSuppose we group starting indices $ i $ by the value of $ f(i,j) $, and also by **state** that determines future evolution.\n\nBut the recurrence depends only on the current $ f(i,j-1) $ and $ A_j $.\n\nSo if we know $ f(i,j-1) $, then:\n- If $ A_j = 0 $: $ f(i,j) = \\text{nand}(f(i,j-1), 0) = 1 $ (since anything NAND 0 is 1)\n- If $ A_j = 1 $: $ f(i,j) = \\text{nand}(f(i,j-1), 1) = \\text{not } f(i,j-1) $\n\nSo:\n$$\nf(i,j) = \n\\begin{cases}\n1 & \\text{if } A_j = 0 \\\\\n1 - f(i,j-1) & \\text{if } A_j = 1\n\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThis is **crucial**.\n\nSo we can write:\n- When $ A_j = 0 $: all $ f(i,j) = 1 $ for $ i < j $, and $ f(j,j) = A_j = 0 $\n- When $ A_j = 1 $: $ f(i,j) = 1 - f(i,j-1) $ for $ i < j $, and $ f(j,j) = 1 $\n\nWait: $ f(j,j) = A_j $, so:\n- if $ A_j = 0 $, $ f(j,j) = 0 $\n- if $ A_j = 1 $, $ f(j,j) = 1 $\n\nAnd for $ i < j $:\n- $ f(i,j) = \\text{nand}(f(i,j-1), A_j) $\n\nSo:\n- If $ A_j = 0 $: $ f(i,j) = 1 $ for all $ i < j $\n- If $ A_j = 1 $: $ f(i,j) = 1 - f(i,j-1) $ for $ i < j $\n\nThis is a **huge simplification**.\n\nSo we can simulate the evolution of $ f(i,j) $ values across $ j $, using only the previous values and the current bit.\n\nLet’s define:\n- Let $ V_j $ be a multiset or count of how many $ i \\leq j $ have $ f(i,j) = 1 $\n\nBut we don’t need the full multiset — we only need the **sum** $ F(j) = \\sum_{i=1}^j f(i,j) $\n\nLet $ s_j = F(j) = \\sum_{i=1}^j f(i,j) $\n\nWe want $ \\sum_{j=1}^N s_j $\n\nNow, we can compute $ s_j $ from $ s_{j-1} $ and $ A_j $.\n\nLet’s define:\n- $ c_{j-1}^{(1)} = \\sum_{i=1}^{j-1} f(i,j-1) = s_{j-1} $\n- $ c_{j-1}^{(0)} = (j-1) - s_{j-1} $\n\nNow, for $ j $:\n\nCase 1: $ A_j = 0 $\n- For $ i = 1 $ to $ j-1 $: $ f(i,j) = 1 $\n  - So sum over $ i < j $: $ j-1 $\n- $ f(j,j) = A_j = 0 $\n- So $ s_j = (j-1) + 0 = j-1 $\n\nCase 2: $ A_j = 1 $\n- For $ i = 1 $ to $ j-1 $: $ f(i,j) = 1 - f(i,j-1) $\n  - So sum over $ i < j $: $ \\sum (1 - f(i,j-1)) = (j-1) - s_{j-1} $\n- $ f(j,j) = 1 $\n- So $ s_j = (j-1 - s_{j-1}) + 1 = j - s_{j-1} $\n\nAnd base case:\n- $ j = 1 $:\n  - $ s_1 = f(1,1) = A_1 $\n  - So if $ A_1 = '0' $, $ s_1 = 0 $; if $ A_1 = '1' $, $ s_1 = 1 $\n\nSo we can compute $ s_j $ iteratively in $ O(1) $ per $ j $, and accumulate the total sum.\n\n---\n\n### Algorithm\n\nLet:\n- `total_sum = 0`\n- `prev_s = 0`  # will store s_{j-1}\n\nFor j from 1 to N:\n- Let `bit = S[j-1]` (since S is 0-indexed)\n- If j == 1:\n  - s = 0 if bit == '0' else 1\n- Else:\n  - If bit == '0':\n    - s = j - 1\n  - Else:  # bit == '1'\n    - s = j - prev_s\n- Add s to total_sum\n- Set prev_s = s\n\nAfter loop, print total_sum.\n\nLet’s test with sample: `N=5`, `S=\"00110\"`\n\nj=1: bit='0' → s1 = 0 → total=0\nj=2: bit='0' → s2 = 2-1 = 1 → total=0+1=1\nj=3: bit='1' → s3 = 3 - s2 = 3 - 1 = 2 → total=1+2=3\nj=4: bit='1' → s4 = 4 - s3 = 4 - 2 = 2 → total=3+2=5\nj=5: bit='0' → s5 = 5-1 = 4 → total=5+4=9\n\n✅ Output: 9 — matches.\n\nLet’s verify what s_j means:\n- s_j = sum_{i=1}^j f(i,j)\n\nj=1: f(1,1)=0 → s1=0\nj=2: f(1,2)=1, f(2,2)=0 → sum=1 → s2=1\nj=3: f(1,3)=0, f(2,3)=1, f(3,3)=1 → sum=0+1+1=2 → s3=2\nj=4: f(1,4)=1, f(2,4)=0, f(3,4)=0, f(4,4)=1 → sum=1+0+0+1=2 → s4=2\nj=5: f(1,5)=1, f(2,5)=1, f(3,5)=1, f(4,5)=1, f(5,5)=0 → sum=1+1+1+1+0=4 → s5=4\n\nYes, matches.\n\nSo algorithm is correct.\n\nNow code accordingly.\n\n---\n\n### Final Code\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().splitlines()\n    N = int(data[0].strip())\n    S = data[1].strip()\n    \n    total_sum = 0\n    prev_s = 0  # will hold s_{j-1}\n    \n    for j in range(1, N+1):\n        bit = S[j-1]\n        if j == 1:\n            s = 0 if bit == '0' else 1\n        else:\n            if bit == '0':\n                s = j - 1\n            else:\n                s = j - prev_s\n        total_sum += s\n        prev_s = s\n    \n    print(total_sum)\n\nmain()\n```",
