[0.9**i for i in range(N)]` to avoid recomputation.\n\nBut in inner loop, we need `0.9**(k-1)` for each k.\n\nAlternatively, we can compute it on the fly.\n\nBut for performance, precompute.\n\nBut N=5000, so we can do:\n\n```python\npow09 = [1.0] * N\nfor i in range(1, N):\n    pow09[i] = pow09[i-1] * 0.9\n```\n\nThen `pow09[k-1]` is `(0.9)**(k-1)`\n\nSimilarly, denominator for k: `10 * (1 - pow09[k])` because `pow09[k] = 0.9**k`\n\nWait: `pow09[i] = 0.9**i`\n\nSo for length k:\n- weight for the j-th selected (0-indexed in reversed order) is `pow09[j]`\n- so for adding an element as the k-th (last in reversed, so weight `pow09[k-1]`)\n- denominator = sum_{j=0}^{k-1} 0.9^j = (1 - 0.9^k)/(1-0.9) = 10*(1 - 0.9^k) = 10*(1 - pow09[k])\n\nYes.\n\n---\n\n### **Code**\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    n = int(data[0])\n    P = list(map(int, data[1:1+n]))\n    \n    # Reverse the performances\n    rev_P = P[::-1]\n    \n    # Precompute powers of 0.9 up to n\n    pow09 = [1.0] * (n + 1)\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        pow09[i] = pow09[i-1] * 0.9\n    \n    # dp[k] = maximum weighted sum for subsequences of length k (in reversed order)\n    # We only need 1D, initialize with -inf, except dp[0]=0\n    dp = [-10**18] * (n + 1)\n    dp[0] = 0.0\n    \n    # Process each performance in reversed order\n    for p in rev_P:\n        # Update dp backwards from n down to 1\n        for k in range(n, 0, -1):\n            # Option: take p as the k-th element in the subsequence (in reversed order)\n            # Its weight is 0.9^(k-1) = pow09[k-1]\n            prev = dp[k-1]\n            if prev == -10**18:\n                continue\n            new_val = prev + p * pow09[k-1]\n            if new_val > dp[k]:\n                dp[k] = new_val\n    \n    # Now compute the best rating over k from 1 to n\n    best_rating = -10**18\n    \n    for k in range(1, n + 1):\n        if dp[k] == -10**18:\n            continue\n        # Denom