      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef furthestDistanceFromOrigin(moves: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    To maximize the distance from the origin, we should either move in the \n    positive direction as much as possible or in the negative direction as \n    much as possible.\n    \n    Let:\n    c_R be the number of 'R' moves\n    c_L be the number of 'L' moves\n    c_u be the number of '_' moves\n    \n    If we treat every '_' as +1, the final position is:\n    pos_max = c_R + c_u - c_L\n    \n    If we treat every '_' as -1, the final position is:\n    pos_min = c_R - (c_L + c_u)\n    \n    The maximum distance from the origin is the maximum of the absolute values\n    of these two extremes.\n    \"\"\"\n    c_L = moves.count('L')\n    c_R = moves.count('R')\n    c_u = moves.count('_')\n    \n    # Maximize distance by moving right whenever possible\n    dist_max = abs(c_R + c_u - c_L)\n    # Maximize distance by moving left whenever possible\n    dist_min = abs(c_R - (c_L + c_u))\n    \n    return max(dist_max, dist_min)\n```",
