      "generated_answer": "I need to create a function that counts symmetric integers in a given range.\n\nLet me break down the problem:\n1. A symmetric integer has 2*n digits (even number of digits)\n2. The sum of the first n digits equals the sum of the last n digits\n3. Numbers with odd number of digits are never symmetric\n4. I need to count how many symmetric integers exist in the range [low, high]\n\nLet me trace through the examples:\n- Example 1: low=1, high=100\n  - Numbers 1-9: 1 digit (odd) → not symmetric\n  - Numbers 10-99: 2 digits (even) → check if first digit equals second digit\n  - 11, 22, 33, 44, 55, 66, 77, 88, 99 are symmetric\n  - 100: 3 digits (odd) → not symmetric\n  - Total: 9\n\n- Example 2: low=1200, high=1230\n  - All numbers in this range have 4 digits (even)\n  - For each number, split into first 2 digits and last 2 digits, check if sums are equal\n  - 1203: first half \"12\" sum = 1+2 = 3, second half \"03\" sum = 0+3 = 3 → symmetric\n  - 1212: first half \"12\" sum = 1+2 = 3, second half \"12\" sum = 1+2 = 3 → symmetric\n  - 1221: first half \"12\" sum = 1+2 = 3, second half \"21\" sum = 2+1 = 3 → symmetric\n  - 1230: first half \"12\" sum = 1+2 = 3, second half \"30\" sum = 3+0 = 3 → symmetric\n  - Total: 4\n\nHere's my solution:\n\n```python\ndef countSymmetricIntegers(low, high):\n    count = 0\n    \n    for num in range(low, high + 1):\n        num_str = str(num)\n        num_digits = len(num_str)\n        \n        # Skip numbers with odd number of digits\n        if num_digits % 2 == 1:\n            continue\n            \n        # Split the number into two halves\n        n = num_digits // 2\n        first_half = num_str[:n]\n        second_half = num_str[n:]\n        \n        # Calculate sum of digits in each half\n        first_sum = sum(int(digit) for digit in first_half)\n        second_sum = sum(int(digit) for digit in second_half)\n        \n        # Check if the sums are equal\n        if first_sum == second_sum:\n            count += 1\n    \n    return count\n```",
