ively, since we want to cover the **most expensive** days with passes, we can:\n   - Let `sorted_F = sorted(F, reverse=True)`\n   - Compute prefix sums: `prefix[i] = sum of first i elements` (i.e., top i fares)\n5. Then, for each possible number of batches $ k $ from 0 to $ K_{\\max} = \\lceil N / D \\rceil $:\n   - Number of passes available: $ \\text{passes} = k \\times D $\n   - Number of days we can cover with passes: $ \\min(\\text{passes}, N) $\n   - So, we cover the top $ \\min(k \\times D, N) $ most expensive days with passes.\n   - The remaining $ N - \\min(k \\times D, N) $ days are paid at regular fare.\n   - The sum of regular fares for the remaining days = total fare sum - sum of top $ \\min(k \\times D, N) $ fares.\n6. Total cost = $ k \\times P + (\\text{total sum} - \\text{sum of top } \\min(k \\times D, N) \\text{ fares}) $\n7. Track the minimum total cost over all $ k $.\n\nBut note: we can also compute total sum first, and then subtract prefix sum.\n\nAlternatively, we can compute the sum of the **smallest** $ N - m $ fares directly, but it's easier to do:\n\n- total_sum = sum(F)\n- sum_covered = prefix[min(k*D, N)]\n- cost = k * P + (total_sum - sum_covered)\n\nYes.\n\n---\n\n### Edge Cases\n\n- $ D > N $: one batch gives more passes than needed. Still, we can buy one batch and use only N passes.\n- $ P $ is very large: better to pay regular fare.\n- $ P $ is very small: buy many batches.\n\n---\n\n### Implementation Steps\n\n1. Read N, D, P\n2. Read list F\n3. Sort F in descending order\n4. Compute prefix sums for the sorted F\n5. total_sum = sum(F)\n6. Let K_max = ceil(N / D) = (N + D - 1) // D\n7. Initialize min_cost = total_sum (i.e., k=0: no passes)\n8. For k in range(1, K_max + 1):\n   - passes = k * D\n   - covered = min(passes, N)\n   - sum_covered = prefix[covered]  (sum of top 'covered' fares)\n   - cost = k * P + (total_sum - sum_covered)\n   - update min_cost\n9. Print min_cost\n\nBut note: k=0 is already considered as total_sum.\n\nW