do:\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    N = int(data[0])\n    D = int(data[1])\n    P = int(data[2])\n    F = list(map(int, data[3:3+N]))\n    \n    total_sum = sum(F)\n    F.sort(reverse=True)\n    \n    # Build prefix sum: prefix[i] = sum of first i elements (i from 0 to N)\n    prefix = [0]\n    for i in range(N):\n        prefix.append(prefix[-1] + F[i])\n    \n    # Maximum number of batches to consider: k from 0 to ceil(N/D)\n    k_max = (N + D - 1) // D  # This is ceil(N/D)\n    \n    min_cost = total_sum  # k = 0\n    \n    for k in range(1, k_max + 1):\n        passes = k * D\n        covered = min(passes, N)\n        sum_covered = prefix[covered]\n        cost = k * P + (total_sum - sum_covered)\n        if cost < min_cost:\n            min_cost = cost\n    \n    print(min_cost)\n\nmain()\n```\n\nWait: we didn't include k=0 in the loop? We initialized `min_cost = total_sum`, which is k=0, then loop from k=1 to k_max.\n\nBut what if k_max is 0? (N=0) — but N>=1, so safe.\n\nAlternatively, we can include k=0 in the loop.\n\nBut it's fine.\n\nBut let's test with N=1, D=1: k_max = (1+1-1)//1 = 1//1 = 1 → k in [1,1] → k=1.\n\nk=0 is handled by initialization.\n\nBut what if k_max is 0? Only if N=0, but N>=1, so k_max >=1 when D>=N? Wait: if N=1, D=2: k_max = (1+2-1)//2 = 2//2 = 1 → ceil(1/2)=1.\n\nBut ceil(1/2)=1, correct.\n\nBut if N=0, but constraint says N>=1.\n\nSo safe.\n\nBut let's make sure: k_max = ceil(N/D) = (N + D - 1) // D\n\nYes.\n\nBut actually, we might need to consider k beyond ceil(N/D)? No, because k = ceil(N/D) gives at least N passes, so we can cover all days. Any more batches would only increase cost (since P>0), so not optimal.\n\nSo k from 0 to ceil(N/D) is sufficient.\n\nBut what if P=0? Then more batches don't hurt. But constraint says P>=1, so safe.\n\nSo the code should be correct.\n\nLet me run through sample 1 again in code logic.\n\nN=5, D=2, P=10, F=[7,1,6,3,6]\n\nsorted F = [7,6,6,3,1