    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_350",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nTakahashi will embark on an adventure.\nDuring the adventure, N events will occur.\r\nThe i-th event (1\\leq i\\leq N) is represented by a pair of integers (t _ i,x _ i) (1\\leq t _ i\\leq 2,1\\leq x _ i\\leq N) and is as follows:\n\n- If t _ i=1, he finds one potion of type x _ i. He can choose to pick it up or discard it.\n- If t _ i=2, he encounters one monster of type x _ i. If he has a potion of type x _ i, he can use one to defeat the monster. If he does not defeat it, he will be defeated.\n\nDetermine whether he can defeat all the monsters without being defeated.\nIf he cannot defeat all the monsters, print -1.\nOtherwise, let K be the maximum number of potions he has at some point during the adventure.\r\nLet K _ {{\\min}} be the minimum value of K across all strategies where he will not be defeated.\r\nPrint the value of K _ {{\\min}} and the actions of Takahashi that achieve K _ {{\\min}}.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN\r\nt _ 1 x _ 1\r\nt _ 2 x _ 2\r\n\\vdots\r\nt _ N x _ N\n\nOutput\n\nIf Takahashi cannot defeat all the monsters, print -1.\r\nIf he can, print the value of K _ {{\\min}} in the first line, and in the second line, for each i such that t _ i=1 in ascending order, print 1 if he picks up the potion found at the i-th event, and 0 otherwise, separated by spaces.\r\nIf multiple sequences of actions achieve K _ {{\\min}} and allow him to finish the adventure without being defeated, you may print any of them.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1\\leq N\\leq2\\times10^5\n- 1\\leq t _ i\\leq2\\ (1\\leq i\\leq N)\n- 1\\leq x _ i\\leq N\\ (1\\leq i\\leq N)\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n13\r\n1 2\r\n1 3\r\n1 1\r\n1 3\r\n1 2\r\n2 3\r\n1 3\r\n1 3\r\n2 3\r\n1 3\r\n2 2\r\n2 3\r\n2 1\n\nSample Output 1\n\n3\r\n1 1 1 0 0 1 0 1\r\n\nThe sample output corresponds to the following actions:\n\n- Find potions of types 2,3,1 in this order. Pick up all of them.\n- Find potions of types 3,2 in this order. Do not pick up any of them.\n- Encounter a type-3 monster. Use one type-3 potion to defeat it.\n- Find a type-3 potion. Pick it up.\n- Find a type-3 potion. Do not pick it up.\n- Encounter a type-3 monster. Use one type-3 potion to defeat it.\n- Find a type-3 potion. Pick it up.\n- Encounter a type-2 monster. Use one type-2 potion to defeat it.\n- Encounter a type-3 monster. Use one type-3 potion to defeat it.\n- Encounter a type-1 monster. Use one type-1 potion to defeat it.\n\nIn this sequence of actions, the value of K is 3.\nThere is no way to avoid defeat with K\\leq 2, so the sought value of K _ {{\\min}} is 3.\r\nThere are multiple sequences of actions that satisfy K=3 and allow him to avoid defeat; you may print any of them.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n4\r\n2 3\r\n1 4\r\n2 1\r\n1 2\n\nSample Output 2\n\n-1\r\n\nHe will inevitably be defeated by the first monster he encounters.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n30\r\n1 25\r\n1 2\r\n1 10\r\n1 18\r\n2 18\r\n1 11\r\n2 11\r\n1 21\r\n1 6\r\n2 2\r\n2 10\r\n1 11\r\n1 24\r\n1 11\r\n1 3\r\n1 2\r\n1 18\r\n2 25\r\n1 8\r\n1 10\r\n1 11\r\n2 18\r\n2 10\r\n1 10\r\n2 2\r\n1 24\r\n1 10\r\n2 10\r\n1 25\r\n2 6\n\nSample Output 3\n\n4\r\n1 1 1 1 1 0 1 0 0 0 0 1 1 0 1 0 1 0 0 0",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We are given a sequence of $ N $ events. Each event is either:\n\n- $ t_i = 1 $: find a potion of type $ x_i $ — we can choose to **pick it up (1)** or **discard it (0)**.\n- $ t_i = 2 $: encounter a monster of type $ x_i $ — we **must** use a potion of the same type to defeat it. If we don't have one, we are defeated.\n\nOur goal:\n- Determine if Takahashi can defeat **all monsters**.\n- If **not**, print `-1`.\n- If **yes**, then:\n  - Let $ K $ be the **maximum number of potions** he holds at any point during the adventure.\n  - Let $ K_{\\min} $ be the **minimum possible** such $ K $ over all valid strategies.\n  - Print $ K_{\\min} $ on the first line.\n  - On the second line, print the decisions (1 or 0) for **each pickup event (t_i = 1)** in order, indicating whether he picked up that potion.\n\n---\n\n### Strategy\n\nWe need to:\n1. **Simulate the adventure**, making decisions about which potions to pick.\n2. **Ensure that we never fail to defeat a monster**.\n3. **Minimize the peak potion inventory size**.\n\nWe cannot simulate all strategies (too expensive). Instead, we can use a **greedy + simulation with lookahead** strategy.\n\nBut note: We are allowed to **drop potions**, but once dropped, we can't pick them back. So the decision is only at pickup time.\n\n---\n\n### Key Observations\n\n- We **must** defeat every monster. So when a monster of type $ x $ appears, we must have **at least one** potion of type $ x $ available.\n- We can only pick up potions when we find them (and choose to).\n- We can **choose to skip** picking up a potion, but we can't pick it later.\n- We want to **minimize the maximum number of potions held at any time**.\n\nSo this is a **resource management** problem with:\n- **Inventory** of potions (count per type).\n- **Events** that add (with choice) or consume potions.\n\n---\n\n### Plan\n\nWe can solve this in two phases:\n\n#### Phase 1: Check Feasibility\nWe need to know **how many potions of each type** we **must** pick up.\n\nIdea:\n- For each monster event of type $ x $, we need a potion of type $ x $.\n- But we can only use potions picked up **before** that event.\n- So for each type $ x $, we can compute:\n  - The **number of monster events** of type $ x $ → required potions.\n  - The **pickup events** of type $ x $ → available sources.\n\nBut: we may pick up more than needed, but we want to minimize peak inventory.\n\nHowever, **we cannot pick up a potion after the monster appears**, so all potions used for a monster must come from pickups **before** that event.\n\nSo for feasibility:\n- For each type $ x $, let’s collect the **indices** (in order) of:\n  - Pickup events (t=1, x=x)\n  - Monster events (t=2, x=x)\n\nThen, for each monster event, we need a potion picked up **before** it.\n\nSo we can simulate **greedily**:\n- For each type, we can try to assign the **latest possible** pickup before each monster (to delay pickup and reduce inventory peak).\n- But we need to simulate inventory over time to track peak.\n\nAlternatively, we can use **binary search on $ K $** (the maximum inventory size), and for a fixed $ K $, simulate whether it's possible to complete the adventure **without exceeding $ K $ potions at any time**, and defeating all monsters.\n\nThis is promising.\n\n---\n\n### Binary Search on $ K $\n\nLet’s define a function `can_complete(K_max)` that returns:\n- `True` and the pickup decisions (for t=1 events), if possible to complete without ever holding more than `K_max` potions.\n- `False` otherwise.\n\nThen we can:\n- Binary search on $ K_{\\min} \\in [0, N] $\n- Find the **minimum** $ K $ such that `can_complete(K)` is `True`\n- Then output that $ K $ and the corresponding decisions.\n\nBut note: We also need to output the **decisions** for pickup events.\n\nAlternatively, we can simulate **greedily with a strategy** that minimizes inventory:\n\n---\n\n### Greedy Strategy (without binary search)\n\nWe can simulate the events in order, and:\n- Keep track of current inventory: `inventory[x]` = number of type-x potions held.\n- Keep track of total current potion count: `total_potions`\n- We want to **avoid exceeding** a certain peak, but we don't know it yet.\n\nBut we are to **minimize the peak**.\n\nBetter idea:\n\n#### Step 1: Check if it's possible at all\n\nFor each type $ x $:\n- Let `monsters[x]` = list of event indices where a monster of type $ x $ appears.\n- Let `pickups[x]` = list of event indices where a potion of type $ x $ is found.\n\nFor type $ x $, we need at least `len(monsters[x])` pickups **before or at** the last monster.\n\nBut more precisely: we must be able to **assign** a distinct pickup event (before the monster) to each monster.\n\nSo for each type $ x $, sort the monster events by time.\nWe need to assign each monster to a pickup event of type $ x $ that occurs **before** the monster.\n\nThis is a **matching**: earliest available pickup for earliest monster? No — better: **latest possible pickup before monster** to delay pickup (helps reduce inventory peak).\n\nBut for **feasibility**, we can do:\n\n- For each type $ x $, sort pickup events (by index).\n- Sort monster events (by index).\n- Try to assign pickups to monsters in **increasing order of monster time**:\n  - For each monster (in order), pick the **earliest available** pickup event that is before it.\n  - If we can't assign all monsters, return `-1`.\n\nBut that gives us **which pickups must be taken**.\n\nWait: actually, we can use **greedy matching**: for each type, match monsters to pickups in chronological order.\n\nBut we want to **minimize peak inventory**, so we may want to **postpone pickups as much as possible**.\n\nSo better: for each type, **match monsters to pickups in reverse order**:\n- Sort monsters descending.\n- For each monster (latest first), assign the **latest pickup** before it that hasn't been used.\n- This is the standard way to minimize early resource usage.\n\nBut we need **global** peak inventory.\n\nSo plan:\n\n#### Step 1: For each type, determine **mandatory pickups**\nUse **greedy matching from last monster backward**:\n- For type $ x $, sort pickup events (indices) → `picks`\n- Sort monster events (indices) → `mons`\n- If `len(picks) < len(mons)` → impossible → return -1\n- Now, try to assign each monster a pickup:\n  - Traverse monsters in **reverse order** (latest first)\n  - For current monster at time `m`, pick the **latest** pickup `p < m` that hasn't been used.\n  - If none, return impossible.\n\nThis gives us a set of **required pickups**.\n\nBut: there might be multiple ways to assign, and we want to **minimize peak inventory**, so we want to **delay pickups as much as possible**.\n\nSo this reverse matching gives us a valid assignment with **minimal early usage**.\n\nSo:\n- Let `must_pickup[i] = True` for each pickup event `i` that is used in this matching.\n\nBut: is it safe to skip other pickups? Yes — we only need enough potions.\n\nBut: we may need to **pick up extra potions** if they are used to cover multiple monsters, but we already matched exactly `len(mons)` pickups.\n\nSo: we **must** pick up at least the ones in the matching.\n\nBut: can we pick up more? Yes, but that may increase peak inventory.\n\nSo to **minimize peak**, we should **only pick up the necessary ones**, **unless** picking up an extra one earlier allows us to **avoid holding many potions** later? Unlikely.\n\nActually: holding more potions increases inventory, so to minimize peak, we should **only pick up what is necessary**, and **as late as possible**.\n\nBut: if we pick up too late, we might have to hold many potions at once.\n\nWait: no — we want to **minimize the maximum number held at any time**.\n\nSo we want to **smooth out** the inventory.\n\nBut here’s a better idea:\n\n#### Step 2: Simulate with required pickups only\n\nIdea:\n- From the reverse matching, we get a set of **required pickup events**.\n- For other pickup events, we can **skip** them.\n- Then simulate the adventure:\n  - For each event:\n    - If it's a pickup and it's in required set → pick it up.\n    - Else → skip.\n  - For monster: use one potion of that type (if available).\n- If we ever can't defeat a monster → impossible (but our matching should prevent this).\n\nBut: is this sufficient? Yes, because we matched each monster to a pickup before it, and we pick it up, so we will have it.\n\nBut: what if the same potion is used for multiple monsters? No — each pickup gives one potion, each monster consumes one.\n\nSo we need one potion per monster.\n\nBut: a potion picked up early can be used for a late monster.\n\nSo the matching ensures that for each monster, there is a pickup before it that we pick up.\n\nBut: we might pick up multiple potions of same type — that's fine.\n\nNow, during simulation, we can compute:\n- `inventory[x]` = current count\n- `total = sum(inventory)`\n- `peak = max(peak, total)`\n\nThen `peak` is the value of $ K $ for this strategy.\n\nBut is this **minimal possible peak**?\n\nNot necessarily: maybe by picking up an **extra** potion earlier, we can **avoid holding many potions** later? Unlikely — adding more potions can only increase inventory.\n\nBut: consider this:\n- Suppose we have:\n  - Event 1: pickup type 1\n  - Event 2: pickup type 1\n  - Event 3: monster type 1\n  - Event 4: monster type 1\n\nWe only need two pickups. But if we pick up both at 1 and 2, we hold 2 at time 2.\nBut if we skip first, pick second, we hold 1 at time 2, and use both at 3 and 4.\n\nBut peak is 1 vs 2? Wait: at time 2, if we pick up both, we have 2.\nIf we skip first, pick second: at time 2 we have 1 → better.\n\nBut our reverse matching would:\n- Monsters at 3,4\n- Pickups at 1,2\n- For monster at 4: assign latest pickup before 4 → pickup at 2\n- For monster at 3: assign latest pickup before 3 → pickup at 1\n→ so we pick both.\n\nBut we could have picked only the second one? No — we need two potions.\n\nWe must pick up two. But we can pick up the one at 2, and also we need one more. The only other is at 1.\n\nSo we must pick up one at 1 and one at 2.\n\nBut what if we had:\n- Event 1: pickup type 1\n- Event 2: pickup type 1\n- Event 3: pickup type 1\n- Event 4: monster type 1\n- Event 5: monster type 1\n\nThen we need two pickups. Reverse matching:\n- Monster at 5: assign pickup at 3\n- Monster at 4: assign pickup at 2\n→ so we pick up at 2 and 3, skip 1.\n\nPeak: at time 3, we have 2 potions (if we pick 2 and 3).\n\nBut if we pick 1 and 3: we have:\n- time1: pick → 1\n- time2: skip → 1\n- time3: pick → 2\n→ same peak.\n\nBut if we pick 1 and 2: peak at time2: 2.\n\nSo all same.\n\nBut what if:\n- Event1: pickup type1\n- Event2: monster type1\n- Event3: monster type1\n- Event4: pickup type1\n- Event5: pickup type1\n\nThen we need two potions before event3.\n\nOnly pickup before event3 is event1.\n\nSo we must pick event1.\n\nThen for second monster at event3: we need another potion, but no pickup between 1 and 3 → impossible? But we have only one pickup before event3.\n\nSo we cannot defeat both monsters.\n\nBut our matching:\n- Monsters at 2,3\n- Pickups at 1,4,5\n- For monster at 3: need pickup before 3 → only 1\n- For monster at 2: need pickup before 2 → only 1\n→ both want pickup at 1 → but only one potion → impossible.\n\nSo we return -1.\n\nSo the reverse matching per type is correct.\n\nBut back to minimizing peak.\n\nOur strategy:\n1. For each type, collect pickup and monster events.\n2. For each type:\n   - If number of pickups < number of monsters → return -1.\n   - Sort monster events (indices) → ascending\n   - Sort pickup events (indices) → ascending\n   - Use greedy matching: for each monster in **reverse order**, assign the **rightmost (latest) pickup** that is before the monster and not yet taken.\n   - If cannot assign, return -1.\n3. This gives us a set `required_picks` = set of event indices (where t_i=1) that must be picked up.\n4. Then, simulate the adventure:\n   - inventory = [0]*(N+1)\n   - total_potions = 0\n   - peak = 0\n   - decisions = []  # for each pickup event, record 1 if in required_picks, else 0\n   - For i in range(N):\n        if t_i == 1:\n            if i in required_picks:\n                pick = True\n                inventory[x_i] += 1\n                total_potions += 1\n            else:\n                pick = False\n            # record decision\n        else: # t_i == 2\n            if inventory[x_i] <= 0:\n                # This should not happen if matching is correct\n                return -1\n            inventory[x_i] -= 1\n            total_potions -= 1\n        peak = max(peak, total_potions)\n5. Then output peak and the decisions for pickup events.\n\nBut wait: is this peak the **minimal possible**?\n\nNot necessarily. Because there might be **multiple valid matchings**, and we want the one that minimizes the **peak inventory**.\n\nSo we need to choose a matching (i.e., which pickups to use) such that the **maximum inventory over time is minimized**.\n\nSo we cannot just use any matching — we need to choose the matching that minimizes the peak.\n\nBut how?\n\nAlternative approach:\n\nWe can try to **simulate with delayed pickup**: only pick up a potion if we **need it for a future monster** and we **won't be able to pick it up later**.\n\nBut we can use a **greedy simulation with lookahead**:\n\nIdea from known problems (e.g., CodeForces \"Potion Making\" or \"Monsters\"):\n\n- We can simulate forward, and whenever we see a monster we cannot defeat, we **look back** to the most recent unpicked potion of that type and **retroactively pick it up**? Not possible.\n\nInstead, we can use a **greedy with future demand**.\n\nStandard solution idea for such problems:\n\n1. Precompute for each type the list of monster events.\n2. We'll maintain:\n   - `inventory` array\n   - `ans` decisions\n   - `peak`\n3. But we don't know which pickups to skip.\n\nBetter idea: use a **priority queue** of **future monster events**.\n\nBut here's a known efficient solution:\n\n- We can use a **greedy strategy**: always pick up a potion **unless we are sure we won't need it**.\n- But we don't know.\n\nAlternatively, we can use **event-based simulation with requirement propagation**.\n\nActually, a known approach for minimizing peak inventory in such \"consume in future\" problems is:\n\n- **Work backwards** to determine the **latest time** we can pick up a potion for each monster.\n- Then, we **must** have picked up the potion by that time.\n- Then, simulate forward: pick up a potion if the number of **pending monster demands** for that type exceeds the number of **future pickups** of that type.\n\nLet me formalize.\n\n#### Step 1: For each type, compute demand and supply from the end\n\nIdea:\n- Traverse events **from end to start**.\n- For each type $ x $, let `deficit[x]` = number of monsters of type $ x $ seen minus number of potions we've decided to pick up (or can pick up) from the future.\n- When we see a monster: `deficit[x] += 1`\n- When we see a pickup: if `deficit[x] > 0`, then we **must** pick this potion (because there's a future monster that cannot be satisfied otherwise), so we reduce `deficit[x] -= 1`, and mark this pickup as **required**.\n- Otherwise, we can skip.\n\nThis is the **standard greedy for \"minimum pickups\" or \"minimum resource\"**.\n\nBut here, we want to minimize **peak inventory**, not number of pickups.\n\nBut: if we pick up only when necessary (i.e., when `deficit[x] > 0`), then we are **delaying pickups as much as possible**, which tends to reduce inventory peak.\n\nIn fact, this strategy minimizes the **time** we hold potions, which helps reduce peak.\n\nSo:\n\n- Initialize:\n  - `deficit = [0]*(N+1)`\n  - `required = [False]*N`  # for each event i, if it's a pickup and we must pick\n- Traverse i from N-1 down to 0:\n  - if t_i == 2:  # monster\n      deficit[x_i] += 1\n  - if t_i == 1:  # potion\n      if deficit[x_i] > 0:\n          required[i] = True\n          deficit[x_i] -= 1\n      else:\n          required[i] = False\n\nThis ensures that we pick up a potion only if it is **needed** to cover a future monster that cannot be covered by later pickups.\n\nThis gives a valid strategy: because every monster will have a potion available (since we only skip a pickup if the deficit is 0, meaning future pickups can cover future monsters).\n\nAnd it minimizes unnecessary pickups.\n\nNow, simulate forward to compute the peak inventory:\n\n- `inventory = [0]*(N+1)`\n- `total = 0`\n- `peak = 0`\n- `decisions = []`\n- For i in range(N):\n  - if t_i == 1:\n      if required[i]:\n          inventory[x_i] += 1\n          total += 1\n          decisions.append(1)\n      else:\n          decisions.append(0)\n  - else: # t_i == 2\n      if inventory[x_i] <= 0:\n          # This should not happen if the backward pass is correct\n          print(-1)\n          exit()\n      inventory[x_i] -= 1\n      total -= 1\n  - peak = max(peak, total)\n\nThen output `peak` and the decisions (only for t_i=1 events).\n\nBut note: the problem asks to output the decisions **only for events where t_i=1**, in order, as space separated 1/0.\n\nAnd if impossible, output -1.\n\nBut in the backward pass, we never check if it's possible. We only mark pickups.\n\nBut if for a monster, there is no future pickup to cover it, then `deficit[x]` will remain positive, but we don't fail.\n\nWait: the backward pass starts at the end. When we see a monster, we increase deficit. When we see a pickup, if deficit>0, we use it to cover one deficit.\n\nAt the end, if any `deficit[x] > 0`, it means there are monsters that cannot be covered.\n\nBut we don't check that.\n\nSo after the backward pass, we must check that all deficits are 0? No — we only care during simulation.\n\nActually, during the backward pass, we are not propagating to the beginning.\n\nBut the deficit at the beginning should be 0 for all types if it's possible.\n\nBut we don't need to check separately: during forward simulation, if we try to defeat a monster and have no potion, we can catch it.\n\nBut the backward pass ensures that we pick up enough potions.\n\nBut let's test with Sample Input 2:\n\n```\n4\n2 3\n1 4\n2 1\n1 2\n```\n\nEvents:\n0: t=2, x=3 → monster type3\n1: t=1, x=4 → pickup type4\n2: t=2, x=1 → monster type1\n3: t=1, x=2 → pickup type2\n\nBackward pass (i=3 down to 0):\n\ni=3: t=1, x=2 → deficit[2] = 0 → skip, required[3]=False\ni=2: t=2, x=1 → deficit[1] += 1 → deficit[1]=1\ni=1: t=1, x=4 → deficit[4]=0 → skip, required[1]=False\ni=0: t=2, x=3 → deficit[3] +=1 → deficit[3]=1\n\nNow, no pickup for type1 or type3, so deficit[1] and deficit[3] remain 1, but we don't use them.\n\nNow forward simulation:\n\ni=0: monster type3 → inventory[3]=0 → cannot defeat → we should output -1.\n\nSo during forward simulation, we detect failure.\n\nSo we can do:\n\n- After backward pass, simulate.\n- If at any monster, no potion available, print -1 and exit.\n\nBut is that correct?\n\nBut the backward pass was supposed to ensure we pick up enough, but it didn't pick up any potion for type3 or type1, because there was no pickup before them.\n\nIn backward pass, for a monster at the end, we increase deficit, but if there's no pickup after that (but we are going backward), we mean before in time.\n\nWait: we are iterating from last event to first.\n\nFor a monster at event i, we do `deficit[x] += 1`.\n\nThen when we see a pickup of type x at j < i, we can use it to cover if deficit[x]>0.\n\nBut in this case:\n\n- i=3: pickup type2 → deficit[2]=0 → skip\n- i=2: monster type1 → deficit[1] = 1\n- i=1: pickup type4 → deficit[4]=0 → skip\n- i=0: monster type3 → deficit[3] = 1\n\nNow, after this, deficit[1]=1, deficit[3]=1, but no pickup of type1 or type3 at all.\n\nSo during forward simulation:\n\n- At i=0: monster type3 → need potion, but we haven't picked any, and no pickup of type3 → fail.\n\nSo we catch it.\n\nBut we could check after backward pass: if any d