nBut our backward pass produced a different set.\n\nBut our peak is 3.\n\nNow, is it possible to have peak=2? The sample says no.\n\nSo our method gives peak=3, which is optimal.\n\nBut the decisions are different.\n\nBut the problem allows any.\n\nNow, what about Sample Input 3?\n\nWe'll trust the method.\n\nBut wait: in the backward pass, we set `required[i] = True` only if `deficit[x_i] > 0` at that point.\n\nAnd we update deficit.\n\nAnd after the pass, if any deficit[x] > 0, we output -1.\n\nBut in the backward pass, we only care about the deficit at the time of the event.\n\nThis is the standard solution for such problems (e.g., \"Minimum Number of Arrows to Burst Balloons\" type, or \"Task Scheduler\" with deadlines).\n\nThis is known as the **greedy covering from the end**.\n\nSo we'll implement:\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Read N.\n2. events = []\n3. for i in range(N): read t, x\n4. deficit = [0]*(N+1)   # 1-indexed, up to N\n5. required = [False]*N   # for each event index\n6. for i in range(N-1, -1, -1):\n      if t_i == 2:\n          deficit[x_i] += 1\n      else: # t_i == 1\n          if deficit[x_i] > 0:\n              required[i] = True\n              deficit[x_i] -= 1\n          else:\n              required[i] = False\n7. if any(deficit[x] > 0 for x in range(1, N+1)):\n        print(-1)\n        exit()\n8. Now simulate to compute peak inventory.\n   - inventory = [0]*(N+1)\n   - total = 0\n   - peak = 0\n   - decisions = []   # will collect decision for each t_i=1 event, in order\n   - for i in range(N):\n        if events[i][0] == 1:\n            if required[i]:\n                inventory[events[i][1]] += 1\n                total += 1\n                decisions.append(1)\n            else:\n                decisions.append(0)\n        else:\n            x = events[i][1]\n            if inventory[x] <= 0:\n                # This should not happen, but for safety\n                print(-1)\n                exit()\n            inventory[x] -= 1\n            total