      "generated_answer": "I need to find the champion team in a tournament where a team is the champion if no other team is stronger than it.\n\nLet me analyze the problem step by step:\n\n1. We have a 2D boolean matrix `grid` where `grid[i][j] == 1` means team i is stronger than team j\n2. A champion is a team that has no other team stronger than it\n3. This means the champion team should win against all other teams (i.e., have `grid[champion][j] == 1` for all j != champion)\n4. Alternatively, I can think of it as finding the team that has the most wins, but given the constraints about transitivity, there should be exactly one champion\n\nLooking at the examples:\n- Example 1: grid = [[0,1],[0,0]]\n  - Team 0 vs Team 1: grid[0][1] = 1, so team 0 is stronger\n  - Team 1 vs Team 0: grid[1][0] = 0, so team 0 is stronger (consistent)\n  - Team 0 beats team 1, so team 0 is champion\n\n- Example 2: grid = [[0,0,1],[1,0,1],[0,0,0]]\n  - Team 0: beats team 2, loses to team 1\n  - Team 1: beats team 0 and team 2\n  - Team 2: loses to both team 0 and team 1\n  - Team 1 beats everyone, so team 1 is champion\n\nThe champion is the team that has the maximum number of 1's in its row (i.e., beats the most teams). Given the transitivity constraint, the team with the most wins will be the champion.\n\nActually, since the champion must not have any team stronger than it, the champion should have `grid[champion][j] == 1` for all j != champion. So I need to find the team that beats all other teams.\n\nBut looking at the constraint: \"The input is generated such that if team a is stronger than team b and team b is stronger than team c, then team a is stronger than team c\" - this suggests a total ordering, so there should be exactly one team that is stronger than all others.\n\nI can find the champion by:\n1. For each team i, count how many teams it beats (sum of row i)\n2. The team with the maximum count is the champion\n3. Or more directly, since the champion beats all other teams, it should have exactly (n-1) wins\n\n```python\ndef findChampion(grid):\n    n = len(grid)\n    \n    for i in range(n):\n        # Count how many teams team i beats\n        wins = sum(grid[i])\n        # If team i beats all other teams, it's the champion\n        if wins == n - 1:\n            return i\n    \n    # According to the problem constraints, there should always be a champion\n    # So we should never reach here\n    return -1\n```",
