lid.\n\nSo we don't require uniqueness — just possibility.\n\nNow, implement the checks:\n\n### Helper: insertion\nTo check if `t_prime` can be obtained by inserting one char into `s`:\n- `len(t_prime) == len(s) + 1`\n- We can find one position `i` in `t_prime` such that removing `t_prime[i]` gives `s`\n\nWe can do:\n```python\ni = 0\nwhile i < len(s) and s[i] == t_prime[i]:\n    i += 1\n# now skip one in t_prime\nreturn s[i:] == t_prime[i+1:]\n```\n\nBut we must be careful: the insertion could be at the end.\n\nAlternatively, we can do:\n\n```python\ndef can_insert(s, t_prime):\n    if len(t_prime) != len(s) + 1:\n        return False\n    i = 0\n    while i < len(s) and s[i] == t_prime[i]:\n        i += 1\n    # now skip t_prime[i], and compare rest\n    return s[i:] == t_prime[i+1:]\n```\n\nSimilarly, deletion is symmetric:\n\n```python\ndef can_delete(s, t_prime):\n    if len(t_prime) != len(s) - 1:\n        return False\n    i = 0\n    while i < len(t_prime) and s[i] == t_prime[i]:\n        i += 1\n    # skip s[i], compare rest\n    return s[i+1:] == t_prime[i:]\n```\n\nBut note: we can reuse the insertion function: `can_delete(s, t_prime)` is equivalent to `can_insert(t_prime, s)`, because deleting from `s` to get `t_prime` is same as inserting into `t_prime` to get `s`.\n\nBut we can write it directly.\n\nSubstitution:\n\n```python\ndef can_substitute(s, t_prime):\n    if len(s) != len(t_prime):\n        return False\n    diff = 0\n    for a, b in zip(s, t_prime):\n        if a != b:\n            diff += 1\n    return diff == 1\n```\n\nAnd identity:\n\n```python\ns == t_prime\n```\n\nNow, overall:\n\n```python\ndef is_valid(s, t_prime):\n    if s == t_prime:\n        return True\n    if len(t_prime) == len(s) + 1:\n        return can_insert(s, t_prime)\n    if len(t_prime) == len(s) - 1:\n        return can_delete(s, t_prime)\n    if len(t_prime) == len(s):\n        return can_substitute(s, t_prime)\n    return False\n```\n\nBut note: if `s == t_prime`, we re