rt(s, t_prime)`: returns True if we can insert one char into `s` to get `t_prime`\n  - requires `len(t_prime) == len(s) + 1`\n  - then check if we can skip one char in `t_prime` to match `s`\n\nSimilarly for deletion.\n\nBut note: we can write a helper for insertion and use it for deletion by swapping.\n\nBut for clarity, we write separately.\n\nAlternatively, we can write a function that checks if two strings differ by one insertion.\n\nBut let's write:\n\n```python\ndef can_insert(s, t_prime):\n    # t_prime should be one longer than s\n    if len(t_prime) != len(s) + 1:\n        return False\n    i = 0\n    while i < len(s) and s[i] == t_prime[i]:\n        i += 1\n    # now skip t_prime[i], and the rest of s should match t_prime[i+1:]\n    return s[i:] == t_prime[i+1:]\n\ndef can_delete(s, t_prime):\n    # t_prime should be one shorter than s\n    if len(t_prime) != len(s) - 1:\n        return False\n    i = 0\n    while i < len(t_prime) and s[i] == t_prime[i]:\n        i += 1\n    # skip s[i], then s[i+1:] should match t_prime[i:]\n    return s[i+1:] == t_prime[i:]\n\ndef can_substitute(s, t_prime):\n    if len(s) != len(t_prime):\n        return False\n    diff = 0\n    for a, b in zip(s, t_prime):\n        if a != b:\n            diff += 1\n            if diff > 1:\n                return False\n    return diff == 1\n```\n\nAnd then:\n\n```python\ndef is_valid(s, t_prime):\n    if s == t_prime:\n        return True\n    if len(t_prime) == len(s) + 1:\n        return can_insert(s, t_prime)\n    if len(t_prime) == len(s) - 1:\n        return can_delete(s, t_prime)\n    if len(s) == len(t_prime):\n        return can_substitute(s, t_prime)\n    return False\n```\n\nNow, process input.\n\nLet's code accordingly.\n\nNote: total length of all S_i is at most 500,000, so even if we do a loop per string, it's acceptable.\n\nWe'll read all.\n\nLet me write the full code.\n\nImportant: we must not output anything extra.\n\nWe assume input from stdin.\n\n```python\nimport 