      "generated_answer": "Let's break down the problem step by step.\n\n---\n\n### **Understanding the Problem**\n\nWe are given two strings `S` and `T` of equal length. Each character is either:\n- a lowercase English letter, or\n- the symbol `@`\n\nWe are allowed to:\n1. **Replace any `@`** with one of the letters: `'a', 't', 'c', 'o', 'd', 'e', 'r'` (these are the letters in \"atcoder\").\n2. **Freely rearrange** the characters in each row independently (this is the \"cheat\").\n\nOur goal is to determine whether it's possible to **make both strings equal** after:\n- replacing each `@` in both strings with one of the allowed letters,\n- and permuting the characters in each string arbitrarily.\n\nSo effectively, since we can rearrange both strings freely, we only care about the **multiset (frequency count)** of characters in each string, after optimally replacing the `@` symbols.\n\n---\n\n### **Key Insight**\n\nBecause we can rearrange both strings arbitrarily, the order doesn't matter. We only care about whether we can make the **frequency counts** of characters in both strings **equal**, using the following operations:\n- Replace any `@` with a letter from the set `{'a', 't', 'c', 'o', 'd', 'e', 'r'}`.\n- We cannot replace non-`@` letters.\n\nSo, we can:\n- Count the frequency of each character in `S` and `T`.\n- Count how many `@` characters are in each string (say, `s_at` and `t_at`).\n- The `@` characters can be used to supply any of the letters in `'atcoder'`.\n\n---\n\n### **Strategy**\n\nLet’s define:\n- `freq_s` and `freq_t`: frequency maps of characters in `S` and `T`, excluding `@`.\n- `wild_s = count of @ in S`\n- `wild_t = count of @ in T`\n\nWe want to assign replacements to `@` in both strings so that the final frequency of each letter is the same in both strings.\n\nBut since we can rearrange freely, we just need to **balance the total available counts** of each character, using the wildcards.\n\nLet’s define the **deficit** and **surplus**.\n\nFor each letter:\n- If `freq_s[letter] > freq_t[letter]`, then `T` needs to use some of its `@` to cover the difference.\n- If `freq_t[letter] > freq_s[letter]`, then `S` needs to use some of its `@` to cover the difference.\n\nBut note:\n- Only letters in `'atcoder'` can be substituted via `@`.\n- For letters **not in `'atcoder'`**, we **cannot** use `@` to replace them. So the counts **must match exactly**.\n\n---\n\n### **Algorithm**\n\n1. Count frequency of each character in `S` and `T`, excluding `@`.\n2. Count number of `@` in each string.\n3. Define `atcoder = set('atcoder')`\n4. For each letter from 'a' to 'z':\n   - If the letter is **not** in `atcoder`:\n     - Then `freq_s[letter]` must equal `freq_t[letter]`. Otherwise, return \"No\".\n   - Else (letter is in `atcoder`):\n     - Compute the difference:\n       - If `freq_s[letter] < freq_t[letter]`: T has more → S must use `@` to cover `freq_t[letter] - freq_s[letter]`\n       - If `freq_s[letter] > freq_t[letter]`: S has more → T must use `@` to cover `freq_s[letter] - freq_t[letter]`\n5. Sum up how many `@` are needed in `S` and in `T`.\n6. If `wild_s >= needed_in_s` and `wild_t >= needed_in_t`, then \"Yes\", else \"No\".\n\nWait — actually:\n- The total number of `@` in `S` can be used to **supply missing letters in S**.\n- Similarly for `T`.\n\nSo:\n- For each letter in `'atcoder'`, compute:\n  - `deficit_s = max(0, freq_t[letter] - freq_s[letter])` → how many S needs to produce using `@`\n  - `deficit_t = max(0, freq_s[letter] - freq_t[letter])` → how many T needs to produce using `@`\n- Total needed from `S`'s wildcards: `sum(deficit_s over all atcoder letters)`\n- Total needed from `T`'s wildcards: `sum(deficit_t over all atcoder letters)`\n\nThen check:\n- `wild_s >= total_deficit_s`\n- `wild_t >= total_deficit_t`\n\nBut also, we can only use `@` for letters in `'atcoder'`. For other letters, frequencies must match.\n\n---\n\n### **Steps in Code**\n\n1. Read `S` and `T`.\n2. Build frequency maps for `S` and `T` (excluding `@`).\n3. Count `@` in both.\n4. For each letter not in `'atcoder'`, check if `freq_s[letter] == freq_t[letter]`. If not, print \"No\".\n5. For letters in `'atcoder'`, compute:\n   - How many more S needs: `max(0, freq_t[letter] - freq_s[letter])`\n   - How many more T needs: `max(0, freq_s[letter] - freq_t[letter])`\n6. Sum these.\n7. If `wild_s >= needed_by_s` and `wild_t >= needed_by_t`, print \"Yes\", else \"No\".\n\n---\n\n### **Edge Cases**\n\n- Non-atcoder letters must match exactly.\n- `@` cannot be used to represent letters outside `'atcoder'`.\n\n---\n\n### **Implementation**\n\n```python\ndef solve():\n    S = input().strip()\n    T = input().strip()\n    \n    from collections import Counter\n    \n    atcoder = set('atcoder')\n    \n    # Count frequency of non-@ characters\n    freq_s = Counter()\n    freq_t = Counter()\n    wild_s = 0\n    wild_t = 0\n    \n    for c in S:\n        if c == '@':\n            wild_s += 1\n        else:\n            freq_s[c] += 1\n            \n    for c in T:\n        if c == '@':\n            wild_t += 1\n        else:\n            freq_t[c] += 1\n            \n    # Check all letters that appear in either string\n    all_chars = set(freq_s.keys()) | set(freq_t.keys())\n    \n    # For letters not in 'atcoder', counts must match exactly\n    for char in all_chars:\n        if char not in atcoder:\n            if freq_s[char] != freq_t[char]:\n                print(\"No\")\n                return\n                \n    # For letters in 'atcoder', compute how many @ are needed in each string\n    needed_by_s = 0  # S needs to use @ to match T's count\n    needed_by_t = 0  # T needs to use @ to match S's count\n    \n    for char in atcoder:\n        cnt_s = freq_s[char]\n        cnt_t = freq_t[char]\n        if cnt_s < cnt_t:\n            needed_by_s += cnt_t - cnt_s\n        elif cnt_t < cnt_s:\n            needed_by_t += cnt_s - cnt_t\n    \n    # Check if we have enough @ in each string\n    if wild_s >= needed_by_s and wild_t >= needed_by_t:\n        print(\"Yes\")\n    else:\n        print(\"No\")\n\nsolve()\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Test with Examples**\n\n#### Sample 1: `ch@ku@ai` and `choku@@i`\n\n- S: ch@ku@ai → non-@: c,h,k,u,a,i → freq: c:1,h:1,k:1,u:1,a:1,i:1; @:2\n- T: choku@@i → non-@: c,h,o,k,u,i → freq: c:1,h:1,o:1,k:1,u:1,i:1; @:2\n\nNon-atcoder letters: all are in atcoder or not? \n- 'h','k','u','i' — are they in atcoder? atcoder = {'a','t','c','o','d','e','r'}\n- 'h' → not in → must match: S has h:1, T has h:1 → ok\n- 'k' → not in → S:1, T:1 → ok\n- 'u' → not in → S:1, T:1 → ok\n- 'i' → not in → S:1, T:1 → ok\n- 'c' → in → S:1, T:1 → no deficit\n- 'o' → in → S:0, T:1 → S needs 1\n- 'a' → in → S:1, T:0 → T needs 1\n- 'd','e','t' → 0 in both\n\nSo:\n- needed_by_s = 1 (for 'o')\n- needed_by_t = 1 (for 'a')\n- wild_s = 2 >= 1 → ok\n- wild_t = 2 >= 1 → ok → \"Yes\" ✅\n\n#### Sample 2: `ch@kud@i` and `akidu@ho`\n\nS: ch@kud@i → non-@: c,h,k,u,d,i → @:2  \nT: akidu@ho → non-@: a,k,i,d,u,h,o → @:1\n\nNon-atcoder letters: c, k, u, i, d, h, a, o — only c,h,k,u,i,d are not in atcoder? Wait:\n\natcoder = {a,t,c,o,d,e,r}\n\nSo:\n- 'c' → in\n- 'h' → not in → S:1, T:1 → ok\n- 'k' → not in → S:1, T:1 → ok\n- 'u' → not in → S:1, T:1 → ok\n- 'i' → not in → S:1, T:1 → ok\n- 'd' → in\n- 'a' → in\n- 'o' → in\n\nNow, in-atcoder letters:\n- a: S:0, T:1 → S needs 1\n- c: S:1, T:0 → T needs 1\n- d: S:1, T:1 → ok\n- o: S:0, T:1 → S needs 1\n- t,e,r: 0\n\nSo:\n- needed_by_s = 1 (a) + 1 (o) = 2\n- needed_by_t = 1 (c)\n\nwild_s = 2 → ok  \nwild_t = 1 → ok → \"Yes\" ✅\n\n#### Sample 3: `aoki` and `@ok@`\n\nS: aoki → a,o,k,i → @:0  \nT: @ok@ → o,k → @:2\n\nNon-atcoder:\n- 'k': S:1, T:1 → ok\n- 'i': S:1, T:0 → not in atcoder → must match → 1 != 0 → \"No\" ✅\n\n#### Sample 4: `aa` and `bb`\n\na: in atcoder, b: not in atcoder\n\nb not in atcoder:\n- S: b count = 0\n- T: b count = 2? No — S is \"aa\", T is \"bb\"\n\nS: a:2  \nT: b:2\n\nb not in atcoder → S has 0, T has 2 → not equal → \"No\" ✅\n\n---\n\n### **Final Code**\n\n```python\ndef solve():\n    S = input().strip()\n    T = input().strip()\n    \n    from collections import Counter\n    \n    atcoder = set('atcoder')\n    \n    freq_s = Counter()\n    freq_t = Counter()\n    wild_s = 0\n    wild_t = 0\n    \n    for c in S:\n        if c == '@':\n            wild_s += 1\n        else:\n            freq_s[c] += 1\n            \n    for c in T:\n        if c == '@':\n            wild_t += 1\n        else:\n            freq_t[c] += 1\n            \n    all_chars = set(freq_s.keys()) | set(freq_t.keys())\n    \n    for char in all_chars:\n        if char not in atcoder:\n            if freq_s[char] != freq_t[char]:\n                print(\"No\")\n                return\n                \n    needed_by_s = 0\n    needed_by_t = 0\n    \n    for char in atcoder:\n        cnt_s = freq_s[char]\n        cnt_t = freq_t[char]\n        if cnt_s < cnt_t:\n            needed_by_s += cnt_t - cnt_s\n        elif cnt_t < cnt_s:\n            needed_by_t += cnt_s - cnt_t\n            \n    if wild_s >= needed_by_s and wild_t >= needed_by_t:\n        print(\"Yes\")\n    else:\n        print(\"No\")\n\nsolve()\n```",
