      "generated_answer": "Let's break down the problem step by step.\n\n---\n\n### **Understanding the Problem**\n\nWe are to find **all 9×9 regions** in an N×M grid that satisfy the conditions of a **Tak Code**.\n\nA **Tak Code** has the following properties:\n\n1. It is a **9×9** region.\n2. The **top-left 3×3** and **bottom-right 3×3** regions are **all black** (`#`).\n3. All **14 cells adjacent** (in any direction: horizontal, vertical, diagonal) to either of these 3×3 regions must be **white** (`.`).\n\nNote:\n- The **adjacent cells** to a 3×3 block are the cells that touch it from outside, including diagonals.\n- Since the 3×3 blocks are in corners, the adjacent region is L-shaped.\n\n---\n\n### **Step-by-step Analysis of the Structure**\n\nLet’s define a 9×9 grid with coordinates from (0,0) to (8,8).\n\n#### **Top-left 3×3 block:**\n- Cells: rows 0–2, columns 0–2 → must all be `#`.\n\n#### **Bottom-right 3×3 block:**\n- Cells: rows 6–8, columns 6–8 → must all be `#`.\n\n#### **Adjacent cells to top-left 3×3:**\n- These are all cells that are adjacent (8-directionally) to any cell in (0–2, 0–2), but **not** in the 3×3 block itself.\n- This includes:\n  - Row 3, columns 0–3\n  - Column 3, rows 0–2\n  - But more precisely, the surrounding cells form a border.\n\nLet’s compute the **adjacent cells** to the top-left 3×3 block:\n- Rows: from -1 to 3 (but we only care about 0 to 8)\n- Cols: from -1 to 3\n- So adjacent cells are:\n  - (0,3), (1,3), (2,3) → right side\n  - (3,0), (3,1), (3,2), (3,3) → bottom and bottom-right\n  - (0,-1), etc. are out of bounds, so we only consider valid indices.\n\nActually, since we are inside a 9×9 region, the adjacent cells **within the 9×9 grid** to the top-left 3×3 block are:\n- Right: (0,3), (1,3), (2,3)\n- Below: (3,0), (3,1), (3,2)\n- Diagonally: (3,3)\n\nSo total 7 cells.\n\nSimilarly, for the **bottom-right 3×3 block** (rows 6–8, cols 6–8), adjacent cells are:\n- Above: (5,6), (5,7), (5,8)\n- Left: (6,5), (7,5), (8,5)\n- Diagonally: (5,5)\n\nAnother 7 cells.\n\nBut note: **(3,3)** and **(5,5)** are included.\n\nSo total adjacent cells: 7 + 7 = 14.\n\nAnd **all 14 must be white** (`.`).\n\nAlso, the two 3×3 blocks must be **fully black**.\n\nNote: The rest of the cells (the `?` in the example) can be anything.\n\n---\n\n### **Plan**\n\nFor each possible top-left corner (i, j) of a 9×9 region in the N×M grid:\n- Check if the region from (i, j) to (i+8, j+8) is within bounds (it will be if i+8 < N and j+8 < M).\n- Check:\n  1. The top-left 3×3 (relative: rows 0–2, cols 0–2) → all `#`\n  2. The bottom-right 3×3 (relative: rows 6–8, cols 6–8) → all `#`\n  3. The 14 adjacent cells (relative positions) → all `.`\n\nIf all conditions are satisfied, record (i+1, j+1) because the problem uses **1-based indexing**.\n\n---\n\n### **Adjacent Cell Positions (relative to 9×9 region)**\n\nLet’s define the 14 white cells:\n\n#### Adjacent to top-left 3×3:\n- (0,3), (1,3), (2,3) → right\n- (3,0), (3,1), (3,2) → below\n- (3,3) → diagonal\n\n→ 7 cells.\n\n#### Adjacent to bottom-right 3×3:\n- (5,5) → diagonal\n- (5,6), (5,7), (5,8) → above\n- (6,5), (7,5), (8,5) → left\n\n→ 7 cells.\n\nSo total 14.\n\n---\n\n### **Implementation Steps**\n\n1. Read N, M.\n2. Read N strings.\n3. For each i from 0 to N-9:\n   - For each j from 0 to M-9:\n     - Check if the 9×9 region starting at (i, j) satisfies:\n       - All in [i+0..2][j+0..2] are '#'\n       - All in [i+6..8][j+6..8] are '#'\n       - All 14 adjacent cells (using relative offsets) are '.'\n     - If yes, print (i+1, j+1)\n\n---\n\n### **Code**\n\n```python\ndef main():\n    import sys\n    data = sys.stdin.read().splitlines()\n    if not data:\n        return\n    \n    N, M = map(int, data[0].split())\n    grid = []\n    for i in range(1, 1+N):\n        grid.append(data[i].strip())\n    \n    # Relative positions for checks\n    # Top-left 3x3: (r, c) where r in [0,1,2], c in [0,1,2]\n    # Bottom-right 3x3: r in [6,7,8], c in [6,7,8]\n    # Adjacent cells to top-left 3x3 (within 9x9 region):\n    adj_top_left = [\n        (0,3), (1,3), (2,3),  # right\n        (3,0), (3,1), (3,2),  # below\n        (3,3)                 # bottom-right corner\n    ]\n    \n    # Adjacent cells to bottom-right 3x3:\n    adj_bottom_right = [\n        (5,5),                # top-left diagonal\n        (5,6), (5,7), (5,8),  # above\n        (6,5), (7,5), (8,5)   # left\n    ]\n    \n    results = []\n    \n    for i in range(N - 8):  # i from 0 to N-9\n        for j in range(M - 8):  # j from 0 to M-9\n            valid = True\n            \n            # Check top-left 3x3: must be all '#'\n            for r in range(3):\n                for c in range(3):\n                    if grid[i + r][j + c] != '#':\n                        valid = False\n                        break\n                if not valid:\n                    break\n            if not valid:\n                continue\n            \n            # Check bottom-right 3x3: must be all '#'\n            for r in range(6, 9):\n                for c in range(6, 9):\n                    if grid[i + r][j + c] != '#':\n                        valid = False\n                        break\n                if not valid:\n                    break\n            if not valid:\n                continue\n            \n            # Check adjacent cells to top-left 3x3: must be all '.'\n            for (dr, dc) in adj_top_left:\n                if grid[i + dr][j + dc] != '.':\n                    valid = False\n                    break\n            if not valid:\n                continue\n            \n            # Check adjacent cells to bottom-right 3x3: must be all '.'\n            for (dr, dc) in adj_bottom_right:\n                if grid[i + dr][j + dc] != '.':\n                    valid = False\n                    break\n            if not valid:\n                continue\n            \n            # If we passed all checks\n            results.append((i+1, j+1))  # 1-indexed\n    \n    # Output results in lexicographical order (they are already due to loop order)\n    for (i, j) in results:\n        print(i, j)\n\nmain()\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Explanation of Sample Input 1**\n\nThe sample input has a 19×18 grid.\n\nWe check all 9×9 regions.\n\nThe valid ones are at:\n- (1,1): top-left\n- (1,10): near top-right\n- (7,7): middle\n- (10,2): as shown\n\nOur code will find these and print them in order.\n\n---\n\n### **Final Output**\n\nThe function reads from stdin, processes the grid, and prints all valid top-left positions (in 1-indexed) in lex order.\n\nIt handles all constraints and edge cases (like no valid regions).\n\nWe simply call `main()` at the end.\n\n✅ This should pass all sample cases.",
