ations that results in Alice being on stair k at least once is a way**.\n\nBut the output is finite: 2 for k=0, 4 for k=1.\n\nSo we suspect that **only sequences that reach k for the first time are considered?** But no — in example 1, the second path reaches 0, then leaves, then comes back.\n\nBut the problem says: \"**it is possible that Alice reaches the stair k, and performs some operations to reach the stair k again.**\" — meaning such paths are allowed and count as valid.\n\nBut then why only 2 ways for k=0?\n\nWait — look again at the explanation:\n\n> The 2 possible ways of reaching stair 0 are:\n\n1. 1 → 0  \n2. 1 → 0 → 1 → 0\n\nSo both ways **end** at 0? Or just **pass through** 0?\n\nBut in the second path, she reaches 0, then goes to 1, then back to 0 — so she reaches 0 **twice**, but the sequence is counted **once**.\n\nSo each **sequence** that causes **at least one visit to k** is a way.\n\nBut then why only 2?\n\nBecause **the problem counts distinct sequences** that **eventually reach k**, but **only finite sequences?** Or **all finite sequences that reach k**?\n\nBut sequences can be arbitrarily long.\n\nWait — unless the number of such sequences is actually **finite** due to constraints.\n\nBut consider k=0:\n\n- Alice starts at 1.\n- She can:\n  - Go down to 0 → one way.\n  - Or: down to 0, up to 1 (jump becomes 1), down to 0 → second way.\n  - Or: down to 0, up to 1 (jump=1), down to 0, up to 2^1=2 → 1+2=3, then down 3 times? But then she can come back?\n\nBut she can do:\n- 1 → 0 → 1 → 0 → 1 → 0 → ... → 0\n\nAny even number of \"up then down\" cycles?\n\nBut that would give **infinitely many** sequences.\n\nBut output is 2.\n\nSo clearly, **only certain sequences are allowed**.\n\nWait — re-read the example:\n\n> Alice starts at stair 1.\n> \n> Using an operation of the first type, she goes down 1 stair to reach stair 0.\n> \n> Alice starts at stair 1.\n> \n> Using an operation of the first type, she goes down 1 stair to reach stair 0.\n> Using an operation of the second type, she goes up 2^0 stairs to reach stair 1.\n> Using an operation of the first type, she goes down 1 stair to reach stair 0.\n\nSo both sequences **end** at 0? But the second one ends at 0.\n\nBut the problem doesn't say sequences must end at k. It says \"reach\" k.\n\nBut if we allow any sequence that ever visits k, then we can have infinitely many (by looping).\n\nBut the answer is finite.\n\nTherefore, we must interpret the problem as: **count the number of finite sequences of operations such that the first time Alice lands on stair k is at the end of the sequence**, and **no further operations are performed**?\n\nBut the note says: \"it is possible that Alice reaches the stair k, and performs some operations to reach the stair k again.\" — implying that **multiple visits are allowed**, but the **way** is the entire sequence.\n\nBut then again, infinite sequences would give infinite ways.\n\nBut output is finite.\n\nSo the only logical conclusion is that **the number of distinct finite sequences that cause Alice to visit stair k at least once is finite** — which only makes sense if **after some point, further operations cannot lead back to k**, or **the structure limits the number of paths**.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we are to count **distinct paths that reach k for the first time**, and **after that, no more operations** — but the example 1 includes a path that visits 0, leaves, and comes back — so it's not the first time.\n\nWait — in example 1, k=0.\n\n- Path 1: 1 → 0 (first time at 0)\n- Path 2: 1 → 0 (already reached 0), then →1, then →0 (reaches 0 again)\n\nSo both paths **end at 0**, and **include at least one visit to 0**, but the second one visits 0 twice.\n\nBut the problem says \"reach stair k\" — so any sequence that lands on k at least once is a valid way.\n\nBut again, we could have:\n- 1 → 0 → 1 → 0 → 1 → 0 (three visits)\n\nSo that would be a third way?\n\nBut output is 2.\n\nSo that can't be.\n\nUnless **the sequences are considered only up to the first time they reach k**?\n\nBut in example 2, k=1:\n\nOne of the ways is: 1 → 0 → 1 → 0 → 1\n\nShe starts at 1 (already at k=1), so she has already reached k.\n\nBut the problem says: \"Alice starts on stair 1 and wants to reach stair k\"\n\nSo if k=1, then **starting at stair 1** means she has already reached k.\n\nSo one way is: do nothing.\n\nBut the example says: \"Alice starts at stair 1. Alice is at stair 1.\" — that's one way.\n\nSo **being at k at the start counts as a way**.\n\nBut then why are there 4 ways?\n\nLet’s list them:\n\n1. Start at 1 → already at k=1 → one way (empty sequence)\n2. 1 → 0 → 1 → reaches 1 again\n3. 1 → 2 → 1 → reaches 1\n4. 1 → 0 → 1 → 0 → 2 → 1 → reaches 1\n\nSo all these sequences **end at 1**, and **include at least one visit to 1**.\n\nBut again, we could do:\n- 1 → 0 → 1 → 0 → 1 → 0 → 1 → ... → 1\n\nArbitrarily many times — infinite sequences.\n\nBut output is 4.\n\nSo clearly, **only finite number of such sequences exist**.\n\nThis suggests that **only sequences that never go above a certain stair** are possible, or that **the jump values grow exponentially**, so after a few jumps, you overshoot k by a lot, and coming back requires many down steps, but you cannot do down steps consecutively.\n\nAh! That's a key constraint: **you cannot use down operation consecutively**.\n\nSo to go down multiple stairs, you must interleave up jumps.\n\nBut up jumps are of size 2^jump, and jump increases every time you use an up jump.\n\nSo once you use a big jump, coming back requires many down steps, but you can't do them consecutively — you must alternate with up jumps, which make you go even higher.\n\nSo it might be impossible to return from large stairs.\n\nIn fact, this problem resembles known problems like \"Minimum One Bit Operations to Make Integers Zero\" or \"Reach a Number with Powers of Two\", but with state (jump count and last move).\n\nBut the key insight might be that **the number of reachable states before passing k is logarithmic**, because 2^jump grows fast.\n\nLet’s try to model the state.\n\n---\n\n### **State: (pos, jump, last_was_down)**\n\n- pos: current stair\n- jump: current jump level (starts at 0)\n- last_was_down: boolean (to prevent consecutive down moves)\n\nBut k can be up to 10^9, so we can't do BFS.\n\nBut note: jump increases only when you do an up move, and starts at 0. The maximum jump needed is about log2(k) + a few, since 2^30 ~ 1e9.\n\nSo jump is at most ~31.\n\nBut position can be large.\n\nBut perhaps we can use **memoization** with state (pos, jump, last_was_down), but pos can be large.\n\nBut we can only reach positions that are sums of subsets of {2^0, 2^1, ..., 2^m} minus some down steps, but down steps are limited by the no-consecutive rule.\n\nAlternatively, think **backwards** or find a **pattern**.\n\nLet’s look at small k:\n\n---\n\n### **k = 0**\n\nStart at 1.\n\nWe want to reach 0.\n\nWays:\n\n1. 1 → 0 (down) — valid, ends at 0.\n\nCan we do more?\n\n2. 1 → 0 (down) → now at 0, jump=0  \n   From 0, cannot go down.  \n   Can go up: 0 + 2^0 = 1 → now at 1, jump=1, last move was up  \n   Then from 1, can go down to 0 → 1 → 0\n\nSo sequence: down, up, down → reaches 0 again.\n\nIs this allowed? Yes — she reaches 0 at the end.\n\nCan we do more?\n\nFrom 0 again, can we go up? 0 + 2^1 = 2 → to 2, jump=2  \nThen from 2, can go down to 1 (last move was up, so down allowed)  \nThen from 1, can go down to 0? But last move was down, so **cannot do down again** — not allowed.\n\nSo from 1, after coming from 2, we are at 1, last move was down.\n\nCan we go up? Yes: 1 + 2^2 = 1+4=5, jump=3  \nThen from 5, go down to 4, then cannot go down again.\n\nBut to get back to 0, we need to go down 4 more times — impossible without consecutive downs.\n\nAlternatively, from 2, go down to 1, then up to 1+2^2=5, etc. — overshoots.\n\nBut can we do:  \n1 → 0 (down) → 1 (up, jump=1) → 0 (down) → 2 (up, jump=2) → 1 (down) → now at 1, last move down, cannot go down to 0.\n\nSo stuck.\n\nBut we can go up: 1 + 2^2 = 5 → jump=3  \nThen down to 4, up to 4+8=12, etc. — never coming back to 0.\n\nSo only two ways to reach 0:\n\n1. 1 → 0\n2. 1 → 0 → 1 → 0\n\nBut wait, is there:  \n1 → 2 (up, jump=0→1) → 1 (down) → 0 (down)?  \nBut from 1 to 0: last move was down (from 2→1), so cannot do down again → invalid.\n\nSo cannot do two downs in a row.\n\nSo 1 → 2 → 1 → 0 is invalid.\n\nAnother try:  \n1 → 2 (up) → 1 (down) → then up: 1 + 2^1 = 3, jump=2 → 3 → 2 → 1 → 0?  \nBut 3→2 (down), then 2→1 (down) — consecutive down → invalid.\n\nSo to go down multiple stairs, must do: down, up, down, up, etc.\n\nBut up jumps are large.\n\nSo perhaps only limited ways to return to 0.\n\nBut in the sequence:  \n1 → 0 → 1 → 0 → 1 → 0 — is this possible?\n\n1 → 0 (down)  \n0 → 1 (up, jump=1)  \n1 → 0 (down) — last was up, so ok  \n0 → 2 (up, jump=2) — 0+4=4? Wait: 2^jump, jump is now 1, so after up, jump becomes 2.\n\nAfter first up: jump=1  \nAfter second up: jump=2\n\nSo from 0, after jump=1: up to 0 + 2^1 = 2 → to 2  \nThen from 2, can go down to 1  \nThen from 1, last move was down, so cannot go down to 0.\n\nSo stuck.\n\nSo we cannot do: 0 → 2 → 1 → 0, because 1→0 would be second consecutive down.\n\nSo only way to get from 2 to 0 is: 2→1 (down), then up to 1+2^2=5, then down to 4, etc. — can't reach 0.\n\nSo only two ways to reach 0:\n\n1. 1 → 0\n2. 1 → 0 → 1 → 0\n\nNo more.\n\nSimilarly for k=1, output is 4.\n\nLet’s list them:\n\n1. Start at 1 — already at k=1 → one way (do nothing)\n\n2. 1 → 0 → 1  \n   down, then up (2^0=1) → 0+1=1\n\n3. 1 → 2 (up, jump=0→1) → 1 (down)\n\n4. 1 → 0 → 1 → 0 → 2 (up with jump=1: 0+2=2) → 1 (down)\n\nAre there more?\n\nTry: 1 → 2 → 3 (up with jump=1: 2+2=4? No: from 2, up: 2 + 2^1 = 2+2=4, jump becomes 2) → 4 → 3 (down) → 2 (down) — consecutive down → invalid.\n\nOr 4 → 3 (down), then up: 3+4=7, etc. — hard to get to 1.\n\nOr: 1 → 0 → 1 → 2 (up, jump=1) → 1 (down) — this is already counted? But she was at 1 before.\n\nBut the sequence is: down, up, up, down → ends at 1.\n\nIs this different from previous?\n\nLet’s see:\n\n- Way 2: down, up → ends at 1\n- Way 4: down, up, down, up (to 2), down → wait no, way 4 is: 1→0 (d), 0→1 (u), 1→0 (d), 0→2 (u), 2→1 (d)\n\nSo sequence: d, u, d, u, d\n\nBut the new one: d, u, u, d → 1→0→1→3? No:\n\nFrom 1: down to 0  \n0: up to 1 (jump=1)  \n1: up to 1+2^1=3 (jump=2)  \n3: down to 2  \nThen can go down to 1? 2→1: down, but last was down? 3→2 is down, then 2→1 is down — consecutive → invalid.\n\nSo cannot.\n\nAnother try: 1 → 2 → 1 → 2 → 3 → 2 → 1?  \n1→2 (u, j=1)  \n2→1 (d)  \n1→3 (u, j=2: 1+4=5? 2^1=2, so 1+2=3) → j=2  \n3→2 (d)  \n2→1 (d) — consecutive down → invalid.\n\nSo not allowed.\n\nSo only 4 ways for k=1.\n\n---\n\n### **Pattern Recognition**\n\nLet’s define f(k) = number of ways to reach stair k.\n\nFrom examples:\n- f(0) = 2\n- f(1) = 4\n\nIs there a recurrence?\n\nNote that every time you do an up move, you jump by 2^jump, and jump increases.\n\nThe down move is only by 1, and cannot be consecutive.\n\nAlso, you start at 1.\n\nAnother idea: **binary representation** or **Gray code** like behavior.\n\nBut known problem: Leetcode 1611. Minimum One Bit Operations to Make Integers Zero — but not exactly.\n\nAlternatively, think recursively.\n\nLet’s consider that any path to k must consist of a sequence of up jumps (of sizes 2^0, 2^1, ..., 2^{m-1}) and down steps, with no two consecutive downs.\n\nBut the up jumps are in order: you can't reuse a jump size; each up increases jump by 1.\n\nSo the up jumps are of sizes 1,2,4,8,... and you can choose to do them or not, but once you skip one, you can't come back? No, you can do up jumps at any time, but the jump counter increases only when you do an up.\n\nAnd you can do down steps in between.\n\nBut the order matters.\n\nBut the state is (pos, jump, last_was_down)\n\nBut jump is at most about 31 for k up to 1e9.\n\nAnd pos might be bounded because after a few up jumps, you overshoot and can't come back.\n\nIn fact, to reach k, the maximum jump level you can use is about floor(log2(k)) + 10, but practically, since 2^30 > 1e9, jump <= 31.\n\nSo we can try **DFS with memoization** over (pos, jump, last_down), but pos can be large.\n\nBut note: we only care about paths that eventually reach k.\n\nBut since the problem asks for the number of ways to reach k, and the examples are small, perhaps the answer depends on the binary representation of k+1 or something.\n\nLet’s try to compute f(2).\n\n### **k = 2**\n\nWays to reach 2:\n\n1. 1 → 2 (up with jump=0: +1) — but 1+1=2? 2^0=1, so 1+1=2 → yes. So one way: up.\n\n2. 1 → 0 → 1 → 2:  \n   down, up (to 1, jump=1), up (1+2=3)? No, 1+2^1=3, not 2.\n\n   Can we go to 2 from 1 with jump=1? 1+2=3.\n\n   So no.\n\n   How about: 1 → 2 (up, j=1) → but first up is 2^0=1.\n\n   After first up, jump becomes 1.\n\n   So from 1: up to 2 (1+1=2), jump=1.\n\n   Then from 2, can go down to 1.\n\n   Then from 1, can go up to 1+2^1=3.\n\n   Not helpful.\n\n   Can we do: 1 → 0 → 2?  \n   From 0, can we up with jump=0? But Alice's jump starts at 0, and only increases on up.\n\n   But in the path: start at 1, down to 0 — jump is still 0.\n\n   Then from 0, up: 0 + 2^0 = 1 — to 1.\n\n   To get to 2 from 0, need to up with jump=1: 0+2=2.\n\n   So how to get jump=1 at 0?\n\n   Must have done an up move before.\n\n   So: 1 → 2 (up, j=0→1) → 1 (down) → 0 (down)? No, consecutive down.\n\n   1 → 2 (up) → 1 (down) — last move down, cannot go to 0.\n\n   So cannot.\n\n   Another way: 1 → 0 (d) → 1 (u, j=1) → 3 (u, j=2: 1+4=5? 2^1=2, so 1+2=3) → 2 (d)\n\n   So: 1→0→1→3→2\n\n   Sequence: d, u, u, d — valid?  \n   - 1→0: d  \n   - 0→1: u, jump=1  \n   - 1→3: u, jump=2  \n   - 3→2: d — last was u, so ok.\n\n   So yes, reaches 2.\n\n   So way 2: 1→0→1→3→2\n\n3. 1 → 2 (up) — already have\n\n   Is there: 1 → 2 → 3 → 2?  \n   1→2 (u, j=1)  \n   2→3 (u, j=2: 2+4=6? 2^1=2, so 2+2=4) — wait, after first up, jump=1, so second up is 2^1=2.\n\n   So 2+2=4, to 4, jump=2.\n\n   Then down to 3, then cannot down to 2 if we want to go to 2, but 3→2 is down, then from 2 can stop.\n\n   But she reaches 2 at the beginning.\n\n   But the sequence: 1→2 (reaches 2) — that's one way.\n\n   If she continues: 2→4→3, she is at 3, not 2.\n\n   To get to 2 from 4: 4→3 (d), 3→2 (d) — consecutive down — invalid.\n\n   So cannot.\n\n4. 1 → 0 → 1 → 0 → 2?  \n   1→0 (d)  \n   0→1 (u, j=1)  \n   1→0 (d) — last was u, so ok  \n   0→2 (u, j=2: 0+4=4? 2^1=2, so after up at 0 with jump=1: 0+2=2) — yes, if jump=1.\n\n   But after first up (0→1), jump becomes 1.\n\n   Then down to 0.\n\n   Then from 0, up with jump=1: 0+2=2.\n\n   So sequence: d, u, d, u — ends at 2.\n\n   So way: 1→0→1→0→2\n\n   So reaches 2.\n\n   So way 3: 1→2 (direct)\n   way 4: 1→0→1→3→2\n   way 5: 1→0→1→0→2\n\n   But is 1→2 counted once.\n\n   Also, can we do: 1 → 2 (up) and stop — one way.\n\n   Or continue and come back? But to come back to 2, need to go up and down.\n\n   But from 2, go up to 2+2^1=4, then down to 3, then cannot down to 2.\n\n   So only ways to reach 2:\n\n   - 1 → 2  [up]\n   - 1 → 0 → 1 → 0 → 2  [d,u,d,u]\n   - 1 → 0 → 1 → 3 → 2  [d,u,u,d]\n\n   Is there more?\n\n   What about: 1 → 0 → 1 → 2? But from 1, with jump=1, up is +2, so to 3, not 2.\n\n   Cannot.\n\n   Or: 1 → 2 → 1 → 2? But 1→2 with jump=1: 1+2=3, not 2.\n\n   So no.\n\n   So at least 3 ways.\n\n   But wait, in the first way, she starts at 1, does up to 2 — reaches 2.\n\n   Second: d,u,d,u to 2\n\n   Third: d,u,u,d to 2\n\n   Are there more?\n\n   Try: 1 → 0 → 1 → 0 → 1 → 0 → 2?  \n   1→0 (d)  \n   0→1 (u, j=1)  \n   1→0 (d)  \n   0→1 (u, j=2: 0+4=4? 2^1=2, so 0+2=2) — wait, after second up, jump=2.\n\n   So from 0, with jump=1, up to 0+2=2.\n\n   So cannot go to 1 with jump=2.\n\n   So from 0, after jump=1, up to 2.\n\n   So cannot do 0→1 with jump>=1.\n\n   Only with jump=0.\n\n   So once you use an up move, jump increases, so you can't do small up moves again