      "generated_answer": "I need to solve this step by step.\n\nThe problem asks me to:\n1. Start with a bank account balance of 100 dollars\n2. Round the purchase amount to the nearest multiple of 10\n3. If there are two equally close multiples of 10, choose the larger one\n4. Return the remaining balance after the purchase\n\nLet me think about the rounding logic:\n- For any number, I need to find the nearest multiple of 10\n- If the number is exactly halfway between two multiples (like 15 is between 10 and 20), I should choose the larger one\n\nThe key insight is that when rounding to the nearest 10:\n- Numbers ending in 0-4 round down to the lower multiple of 10\n- Numbers ending in 5-9 round up to the higher multiple of 10\n\nBut wait, let me verify this with the examples:\n- Example 1: purchaseAmount = 9\n  - Nearest multiples of 10 are 0 and 10\n  - |0-9| = 9, |10-9| = 1\n  - 10 is closer, so roundedAmount = 10\n  - Balance = 100 - 10 = 90 ✓\n\n- Example 2: purchaseAmount = 15\n  - Nearest multiples of 10 are 10 and 20\n  - |10-15| = 5, |20-15| = 5\n  - Both are equally close, so we choose the larger one: 20\n  - Balance = 100 - 20 = 80 ✓\n\nSo the rule is: if the last digit is 5 or greater, round up to the next multiple of 10; if the last digit is less than 5, round down to the previous multiple of 10.\n\nI can implement this by:\n1. Finding the remainder when purchaseAmount is divided by 10\n2. If remainder >= 5, round up: (purchaseAmount // 10 + 1) * 10\n3. If remainder < 5, round down: (purchaseAmount // 10) * 10\n\nOr more simply: use the formula `round(purchaseAmount / 10) * 10`, but I need to be careful about the rounding behavior.\n\nActually, Python's `round()` function uses \"round half to even\" (banker's rounding), but the problem specifies that when there's a tie, we choose the larger multiple. So I need to implement custom rounding.\n\nFor this specific case of rounding to nearest 10 with ties going to the larger value:\n- If purchaseAmount % 10 >= 5, then roundedAmount = ((purchaseAmount // 10) + 1) * 10\n- If purchaseAmount % 10 < 5, then roundedAmount = (purchaseAmount // 10) * 10\n\nLet me verify:\n- purchaseAmount = 9: 9 % 10 = 9 >= 5, so roundedAmount = (0 + 1) * 10 = 10 ✓\n- purchaseAmount = 15: 15 % 10 = 5 >= 5, so roundedAmount = (1 + 1) * 10 = 20 ✓\n- purchaseAmount = 14: 14 % 10 = 4 < 5, so roundedAmount = (1) * 10 = 10 ✓\n- purchaseAmount = 5: 5 % 10 = 5 >= 5, so roundedAmount = (0 + 1) * 10 = 10 ✓\n\nThis logic works.\n\nNow I'll write the function:\n\n```python\ndef accountBalanceAfterPurchase(purchaseAmount):\n    remainder = purchaseAmount % 10\n    if remainder >= 5:\n        roundedAmount = ((purchaseAmount // 10) + 1) * 10\n    else:\n        roundedAmount = (purchaseAmount // 10) * 10\n    \n    return 100 - roundedAmount\n```",
