etween 9 and 17 inclusive.\n\n> So condition: `9 <= (T + X_i) % 24 <= 17`\n\nBut: **modulo arithmetic with time**: if `(T + X_i) >= 24`, then it wraps.\n\nBut since we're doing `% 24`, it's handled.\n\nHowever, we must be cautious: if `(T + X_i) % 24` is 25, it becomes 1, etc.\n\nBut we are doing modulo correctly.\n\nSo plan:\n\n- Try every possible UTC start time `T` from `0` to `23`.\n- For each `T`, compute total number of employees who can attend:\n  - For each base `i`, compute `local_start = (T + X_i) % 24`\n  - If `9 <= local_start <= 17`, then all `W_i` employees can attend.\n- Sum up for all such bases.\n- Track the maximum over all `T`.\n\nThen output the maximum.\n\n---\n\n### **Why try only integer T from 0 to 23?**\n\nBecause the meeting is one hour long and time is in whole hours, and constraints say all inputs are integers. So we only need to consider integer UTC hours.\n\nAlso, since time wraps every 24 hours, checking T in 0 to 23 covers all possibilities.\n\n---\n\n### **Algorithm**\n\n```python\nRead N\nbases = []\nfor i in range(N):\n    read W_i, X_i\n\nmax_employees = 0\n\nfor T in range(0, 24):  # UTC meeting start time\n    total = 0\n    for W, X in bases:\n        local_start = (T + X) % 24\n        if 9 <= local_start <= 17:\n            total += W\n    max_employees = max(max_employees, total)\n\nprint(max_employees)\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Test on Sample 1**\n\nInput:\n```\n3\n5 0\n3 3\n2 18\n```\n\nTry all T from 0 to 23, but let's try T = 14 (as suggested):\n\n- Base 1: X=0 → local_start = (14+0)%24 = 14 → 9<=14<=17 → yes → +5\n- Base 2: X=3 → (14+3)=17 → yes → +3\n- Base 3: X=18 → (14+18)=32%24=8 → 8 not in [9,17] → no\n- Total = 8\n\nNow, is there a better T?\n\nTry T=15:\n- Base1: 15 → ok → 5\n- Base2: 18 → (15+3)=18 → 18 not <=17 → no\n- Base3: (15+18)=33%24=9 → 9 is ok → +2\n- Total = 5+2 = 7 < 8\n\nT=13:\n- Base1: 13 → ok → 5\n- Base2: 16 → ok → 3\n- Base3: (13+18)=31%24=7 → no → total=8\n\nT=12:\n- Base1:12 ok\n- Base2:15 ok\n- Base3: (12+18)=30%24=6 → no → 8\n\nT=5:\n- Base3: (5+18)=23 → 23 not in [9,17] → no\n- Base1:5 → no\n- Base2:8 → no → total=0\n\nSo max is 8 → correct.\n\n---\n\n### **Sample Input 2**\n\n```\n2\n1 10\n1000000 20\n```\n\nTry T such that both can attend?\n\nWe want to maximize.\n\nTry T where second base (1000000 people) can attend:\n- Local start = (T + 20) % 24 ∈ [9,17]\n- So T + 20 ≡ s (mod 24), s ∈ [9,17]\n→ T ≡ s - 20 (mod 24)\n→ s - 20 ∈ [-11, -3] → mod 24: [13, 21]\n\nSo T ∈ [13,21]\n\nNow check first base: X=10 → local start = (T + 10) % 24\nWe need: (T+10) % 24 ∈ [9,17]\n\nTry T=13:\n- Base2: (13+20)=33%24=9 → ok → 1000000\n- Base1: (13+10)=23 → 23 not in [9,17] → no → total=1000000\n\nT=7:\n- Base2: (7+20)=27%24=3 → no\n- Base1: (7+10)=17 → ok → total=1\n\nSo best is 1000000 → output 1000000 → correct.\n\n---\n\n### **Sample Input 3**\n\n```\n6\n31 3\n20 8\n11 5\n4 3\n47 14\n1 18\n```\n\nWe'll simulate code.\n\nBut we can trust the logic.\n\nWe'll write the code accordingly.\n\n---\n\n### **Final Code**\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().splitlines()\n    n = int(data[0])\n    bases = []\n    for i in range(1, n+1):\n        w, x = map(int, data[i].split())\n        bases.append((w, x))\n    \n    max_employees = 0\n    for T in range(24):  # UTC start hour\n        total = 0\n        for W, X in bases:\n            local_start = (T + X) % 24\n            if 9 <= local_start <= 17:\n                total += W\n        if total > max_employees:\n            max_employees = total\n            \n    print(max_employees)\n\nmain()\n```\n\nLet me run it mentally on sample 3.\n\nBut better to just write and trust.\n\nBut for verification:\n\nTry to find best T.\n\nBut it's tedious. We can code it, but since we are to output code, we just write.\n\nFinal code below.",
