    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nfrom collections import Counter\nimport math\n\ndef minLengthAnagramConcatenation(s: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a string s that is a concatenation of anagrams of some string t,\n    finds the minimum possible length of the string t.\n    \n    The problem implies that if s is a concatenation of k anagrams of t, \n    then s can be divided into k segments, each of length m = len(t), \n    where every segment has the same character frequency distribution.\n    \n    1. The length m must be a divisor of n = len(s).\n    2. For a fixed m, s can be split into n/m blocks of length m.\n    3. All blocks must have the exact same character frequency counts to be anagrams of each other.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(s)\n    \n    # Helper to check if a block size 'm' allows for k = n/m segments that are all anagrams.\n    def is_valid(m):\n        k = n // m\n        if k == 1:\n            return True\n        \n        # Calculate the target frequency for each character in any of the segments\n        # Since all segments must be anagrams, each must have exactly 1/k of total character counts.\n        total_counts = Counter(s)\n        target_counts = {char: count // k for char, count in total_counts.items()}\n        \n        # Check each segment\n        for i in range(0, n, m):\n            segment = s[i:i + m]\n            if Counter(segment) != target_counts:\n                return False\n        return True\n\n    # Get all divisors of n\n    divisors = []\n    for i in range(1, int(math.sqrt(n)) + 1):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            divisors.append(i)\n            if i*i != n:\n                divisors.append(n // i)\n    \n    # Sort divisors to check from smallest to largest for minimum length\n    divisors.sort()\n    \n    for m in divisors:\n        if is_valid(m):\n            return m\n            \n    return n\n\n```",
