    "prediction": "gpt-oss-120b",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef count_pairs(coordinates: List[List[int]], k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the number of pairs (i, j) with i < j such that\n    (x_i XOR x_j) + (y_i XOR y_j) == k.\n\n    The algorithm iterates through the points once, keeping a hash map\n    of previously seen points. For each new point (x, y) it tries every\n    possible split a + b = k (0 ≤ a ≤ k) and looks for a previously\n    stored point (x ^ a, y ^ b). Because k ≤ 100, this costs at most\n    101 look‑ups per point, giving O(n·k) time which is fast enough for\n    n ≤ 5·10⁴.\n    \"\"\"\n    # map from (x, y) tuple to its frequency among already processed points\n    seen = {}\n    total = 0\n\n    for x, y in coordinates:\n        # try all ways to split k into a (for x xor) and b (for y xor)\n        for a in range(k + 1):\n            b = k - a\n            target = (x ^ a, y ^ b)\n            total += seen.get(target, 0)\n\n        # store the current point for future queries\n        key = (x, y)\n        seen[key] = seen.get(key, 0) + 1\n\n    return total\n```",
