      "generated_answer": "I need to analyze this problem step by step.\n\nGiven:\n- Two strings s1 and s2 of length 4\n- Operation: choose indices i and j where j - i = 2, then swap characters at those indices\n- For a string of length 4, the valid pairs with j - i = 2 are:\n  - i = 0, j = 2 (positions 0 and 2)\n  - i = 1, j = 3 (positions 1 and 3)\n\nSo I can only swap:\n- Character at position 0 with character at position 2\n- Character at position 1 with character at position 3\n\nLet me think about what this means:\n- Positions 0 and 2 can be swapped with each other\n- Positions 1 and 3 can be swapped with each other\n- But positions 0,2 cannot be swapped with positions 1,3\n\nThis means:\n- The characters at even indices (0,2) can only move among even indices\n- The characters at odd indices (1,3) can only move among odd indices\n\nSo for s1 to be transformable into s2:\n1. The multiset of characters at even positions in s1 must equal the multiset of characters at even positions in s2\n2. The multiset of characters at odd positions in s1 must equal the multiset of characters at odd positions in s2\n\nLet me verify with the examples:\n\nExample 1: s1 = \"abcd\", s2 = \"cdab\"\n- s1 even positions (0,2): 'a', 'c' → {'a', 'c'}\n- s1 odd positions (1,3): 'b', 'd' → {'b', 'd'}\n- s2 even positions (0,2): 'c', 'a' → {'a', 'c'} \n- s2 odd positions (1,3): 'd', 'b' → {'b', 'd'}\n- Both match, so return true\n\nExample 2: s1 = \"abcd\", s2 = \"dacb\"\n- s1 even positions (0,2): 'a', 'c' → {'a', 'c'}\n- s1 odd positions (1,3): 'b', 'd' → {'b', 'd'}\n- s2 even positions (0,2): 'd', 'c' → {'c', 'd'}\n- s2 odd positions (1,3): 'a', 'b' → {'a', 'b'}\n- Even positions don't match ({'a','c'} vs {'c','d'}), so return false\n\nThe solution is to check if the sorted characters at even positions are the same in both strings, and the sorted characters at odd positions are the same in both strings.\n\n```python\ndef canBeEqual(s1, s2):\n    # Check if characters at even positions (0,2) are the same in both strings\n    even_s1 = sorted([s1[0], s1[2]])\n    even_s2 = sorted([s2[0], s2[2]])\n    \n    # Check if characters at odd positions (1,3) are the same in both strings\n    odd_s1 = sorted([s1[1], s1[3]])\n    odd_s2 = sorted([s2[1], s2[3]])\n    \n    # Both even and odd positions must match\n    return even_s1 == even_s2 and odd_s1 == odd_s2\n```",
