ng can start anywhere and be of any length, but must be contiguous.\n\nBecause `S` is periodic, any substring longer than 12 will eventually repeat patterns.\n\nBut since we're only dealing with `W, B ≤ 100`, the maximum length of a substring we care about is at most `W + B ≤ 200`.\n\nSo we can **simulate** a sufficiently long portion of `S` and check all substrings of length up to, say, 200.\n\nBut even better: since the pattern repeats every 12 characters, any substring that wraps around multiple periods will have counts that are periodic.\n\nBut we can use a **sliding window** over **two periods** to capture all possible substrings of length up to 24, but we may need more.\n\nWait: the maximum length of the substring we care about is `W + B ≤ 200`, so we need to check substrings of length up to 200.\n\nBut since the pattern is periodic, we can **generate a string of S repeated enough times** so that we can capture any substring of length up to 200.\n\nHow many repetitions?\n\nWe want to be able to start at any offset in the pattern and go up to 200 characters.\n\nSo if we generate `S_repeated = pattern * k`, we need `k` such that even starting at the last character of a pattern, we can go 200 characters forward.\n\nSo `k` should be large enough so that `k * 12 >= 200 + 12` → say `k = 20` → 240 characters.\n\nSo we can generate a string of 240 characters (20 repetitions) and check all substrings of length from 1 to 200.\n\nBut note: `W + B` is the total length of the substring, and `W + B >= 1`, up to 200.\n\nSo plan:\n\n1. Define `pattern = \"wbwbwwbwbwbw\"` (length 12)\n2. Generate a long string: `S_long = pattern * 20` (240 chars) → enough to cover any substring of length ≤ 200 starting at any offset\n3. Read `W` and `B`\n4. total_len = W + B\n5. If total_len == 0: not possible by constraint, but W+B>=1, so skip\n6. Iterate over all substrings of `S_long` of length `total_len`:\n   - For start index `i` from 0 to `len(S_long) - total_len`\n   - Extract substring `s = S_long[i:i+total_len]`\n   - Count w's and b's\n   - If count_w == W and count_b == B → print \"Yes\" and return\n7. If none found, print \"No\"\n\nBut wait: is it possible that a valid substring only appears much later in the infinite string?\n\nNo! Because the string is **periodic**. The sequence of counts of w and b in substrings of fixed length is also periodic. So all possible substrings (in terms of character content) that can appear, appear within a bounded window.\n\nMore precisely: since the pattern repeats every 12 characters, any substring of length L is determined by its starting position modulo 12 and its length.\n\nSo if we take a string of length `12 + L - 1`, we can capture all possible substrings of length L that can ever appear.\n\nBut we don't know L in advance. However, since `L = W + B ≤ 200`, we can take `S_long = pattern * ( (200 // 12) + 2 )` → about 18 reps → 216 characters.\n\nSo 20 reps → 240 → safe.\n\nBut worst-case: we need to check all substrings of length up to 200 → 240 - 200 + 1 = 40 substrings per starting phase? Actually, number of substrings is about 240 * 200 → 48,000 → acceptable.\n\nBut 240 * 200 = 48,000 iterations, each counting up to 200 chars → 48,000 * 200 = 9.6e6 operations → acceptable in Python.\n\nBut we can optimize counting by using a sliding window.\n\nBut since constraints are small (W, B <= 100), we can do a double loop.\n\nAlternatively, for each starting position, we can slide and update counts.\n\nBut for clarity, let's do:\n\n- Precompute `S_long = pattern * 20` (240 chars)\n- For each possible length `L = W + B`\n- For each start index `i` in `range(0, len(S_long) - L + 1)`\n   - substring = S_long[i:i+L]\n   - count_w = substring.count('w')\n   - count_b = substring.count('b')\n   - if count_w == W and count_b == B: print \"Yes\", return\n\nAfter loop: print \"No\"\n\nBut wait: what if `W + B = 0`? But constraint says `W + B >= 1`, so we don't have to worry.\n\nLet’s test with sample inputs.\n\n---\n\n### Sample 1: W=3, B=2 → L=5\n\nWe need a substring of length 5 with 3 w and 2 b.\n\nThe sample says: take 11th through 15th characters (1-indexed) → so 10 to 14 (0-indexed)\n\nLet’s generate first 15 chars of S:\n\npattern = \"wbwbwwbwbwbw\" → 12 chars\n\nSo S_long = pattern * 2 → 24 chars\n\nFirst 15: \"wbwbwwbwbwbw\" + \"wbw\" → \"wbwbwwbwbwbw\" + \"wbw\"\n\nIndices 0 to 14:\n\n0: w  \n1: b  \n2: w  \n3: b  \n4: w  \n5: w  \n6: b  \n7: w  \n8: b  \n9: w  \n10: b  \n11: w  \n12: w  \n13: b  \n14: w\n\nSo substring from 10 to 14: indices 10,11,12,13,14 → b, w, w, b, w → bwwbw\n\nCount w: positions 11,12,14 → 3 w  \nCount b: 10,13 → 2 b → matches\n\nSo yes.\n\nOur code should find this.\n\n---\n\n### Sample 2: W=3, B=0 → need \"www\"\n\nIs \"www\" a substring of S?\n\nLook at pattern: \"wbwbwwbwbwbw\"\n\nWhere are consecutive w's?\n\nLook: index 4,5 → \"ww\" → two w's\n\nThen index 5,6: w,b → break\n\nindex 11 and 12: w,w (if we go to next period) → but pattern ends with w, next starts with w → so \"w\" + \"w\" → so \"ww\" again\n\nBut do we ever get \"www\"?\n\nCheck: after index 5: w, then index 6: b → so no\n\nAfter index 11: w, then next period: index 12: w (same as index 0 of next) → w, then index 13: b → so ww again\n\nIs there any \"www\"?\n\nLook at pattern: ...w w b... → two w's\n\nThen later: ...b w b w... → isolated\n\nOnly two consecutive w's at positions 4,5 → \"ww\"\n\nAnd across boundary: end of pattern is 'w', start is 'w' → so we get \"ww\", but next is 'b' → so \"wwb\" → still only two w's\n\nWait: is there a place with three w's?\n\nLet’s check:\n\npattern: w b w b w w b w b w b w\n\nPositions: \n- 4: w\n- 5: w → two\n- 6: b\n\nThen:\n- 11: w (end)\n- 12: w (start of next) → w\n- 13: b → so only two again\n\nNo three consecutive w's.\n\nBut do we need consecutive? No! The substring just needs to have **3 w's and 0 b's**, so it must be \"www\", which is three consecutive w's.\n\nSince \"www\" never appears, answer is \"No\" → correct.\n\nSo our c