  # ensures we have more than total_len + 12\n    S_long = pattern * repeats\n    if total_len > len(S_long):\n        # Should not happen with our repeats, but just in case\n        S_long = pattern * ((total_len // 12) + 3)\n    \n    found = False\n    n = len(S_long)\n    for i in range(0, n - total_len + 1):\n        substr = S_long[i:i+total_len]\n        count_w = substr.count('w')\n        count_b = substr.count('b')\n        if count_w == W and count_b == B:\n            found = True\n            break\n    \n    print(\"Yes\" if found else \"No\")\n\nsolve()\n```\n\nLet’s test with samples.\n\nSample 1: W=3, B=2 → total_len=5\n\nWe’ll find substring starting at index 10: \"bwwbw\" → count_w: 3, count_b:2 → yes\n\nSample 2: W=3, B=0 → need \"www\"\n\nWe’ll check all substrings of length 3:\n\nIs there any with 3 w and 0 b? Only if \"www\" exists.\n\nBut in S_long, does \"www\" appear?\n\nLook at pattern: \"wbwbwwbwbwbw\"\n\n\"ww\" at index 4-5: \"ww\", then next is 'b' → \"wwb\"\n\nAt the boundary: last char 'w', first char 'w' → so \"w\" + \"w\" = \"ww\", but then next is 'b' → so \"wwb\" again\n\nNo \"www\"\n\nSo no substring of 3 w's → prints \"No\" → correct.\n\nSample 3: W=92, B=66 → total_len=158\n\nWe need to see if any substring of length 158 has 92 w and 66 b.\n\nWe’ll simulate.\n\nBut we can trust the code.\n\nWe’ll run it.\n\nBut we can’t run here, but logic is sound.\n\nBut note: 92 + 66 = 158\n\nAverage per 12 chars: 7w,5b → over 158 chars, expected:\n\nNumber of full periods in 158: 158 // 12 = 13 * 12 = 156, remainder 2\n\nSo 13 periods: 13*7 = 91 w, 13*5 = 65 b\n\nThen 2 more chars: depends on starting phase.\n\nSo total w: 91 + (w in next 2 chars of pattern, starting at offset)\n\nSimilarly for b.\n\nSo possible counts:\n\nLet’s define for each starting offset mod 12, the count in next 158 chars.\n\nBut our code does that by simulation.\n\nFor example, if we start at offset 0:\n\n- 13 full periods: 13*12 = 156 chars → 91w,65b\n- then 2 more