e'll write accordingly.\n\nBut note: we are using BFS and marking visited.\n\nWe must not visit vertex 1.\n\nWe already marked visited[1]=True.\n\nSo when traversing from neighbor, we won't go back to 1.\n\nPerfect.\n\nLet me run sample 3 mentally? Not necessary — the logic is sound.\n\nBut note: performance — we visit each node once → O(N)\n\nGood for 3e5.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Final Code\n\n```python\nfrom collections import deque\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    n = int(data[0])\n    graph = [[] for _ in range(n+1)]\n    index = 1\n    for _ in range(n-1):\n        u = int(data[index])\n        v = int(data[index+1])\n        index += 2\n        graph[u].append(v)\n        graph[v].append(u)\n    \n    # Mark vertex 1 as visited so we don't go back to it\n    visited = [False] * (n + 1)\n    visited[1] = True\n    neighbors_of_1 = graph[1]\n    \n    max_size = 0\n    queue = deque()\n    \n    for neighbor in neighbors_of_1:\n        if visited[neighbor]:\n            continue\n        # Start BFS from this neighbor\n        queue.append(neighbor)\n        visited[neighbor] = True\n        size = 0\n        while queue:\n            node = queue.popleft()\n            size += 1\n            for nb in graph[node]:\n                if not visited[nb]:\n                    visited[nb] = True\n                    queue.append(nb)\n        if size > max_size:\n            max_size = size\n    \n    print(n - max_size)\n\nmain()\n```\n\nWait — we are reusing the same deque. But after first BFS, queue is empty? Yes — because we pop all.\n\nBut to be safe, we can create a new deque inside the loop.\n\nBut it's okay — after first BFS, queue is empty.\n\nBut what if we break early? No.\n\nBut let's reset: better to create queue inside the loop.\n\nAlternatively, we can clear it, but easier to define inside.\n\nLet me fix:\n\n```python\nfrom collections import deque\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    n = int(data[0])\n    graph = [[] for _ in range(n+1)]\n    index = 1\n    for _ in range(n-1):\n        u = int(data[index])\n        v = int(data[index+1])\n        index += 2\n        graph[u].append(v)\n        graph[v].append(u)\n    \n    visited = [False] * (n + 1)\n    visited[1] = True\n    neighbors_of_1 = graph[1]\n    \n    max_size = 0\n    \n    for neighbor in neighbors_of_1:\n        if visited[neighbor]:\n            continue\n        queue = deque([neighbor])\n        visited[neighbor] = True\n        size = 0\n        while queue:\n            node = queue.popleft()\n            size += 1\n            for nb in graph[node]:\n                if not visited[nb]:\n                    visited[nb] = True\n                    queue.append(nb)\n        if size > max_size:\n            max_size = size\n    \n    print(n - max_size)\n\nmain()\n```\n\nThis should work.\n\nLet me test with sample 1:\n\n- n=9\n- graph[1] = [2,6]\n- Start with neighbor=2: not visited → BFS: 2, then 3,4,5 → size=4 → max_size=4\n- Then neighbor=6: not visited → BFS: 6,7,8,9 → size=4 → max_size=4\n- Print 9-4=5 → correct.\n\nSample 2: n=6, graph[1]=[2], BFS from 2: 2,3,4,5,6 → size=5 → 6-5=1 → correct.\n\nSo we are good.\n\nSubmit.\n\nNote: We assume input is well-formed.\n\nLet’s run with sample 3? We don’t have to, but logic holds.\n\nFinal code:\n\nWe'll write it.",
