    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_460",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a 0-indexed array of integers nums of length n, and a positive odd integer k.\nThe strength of x subarrays is defined as strength = sum[1] * x - sum[2] * (x - 1) + sum[3] * (x - 2) - sum[4] * (x - 3) + ... + sum[x] * 1 where sum[i] is the sum of the elements in the i^th subarray. Formally, strength is sum of (-1)^i+1 * sum[i] * (x - i + 1) over all i's such that 1 <= i <= x.\nYou need to select k disjoint subarrays from nums, such that their strength is maximum.\nReturn the maximum possible strength that can be obtained.\nNote that the selected subarrays don't need to cover the entire array.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums = [1,2,3,-1,2], k = 3\nOutput: 22\nExplanation: The best possible way to select 3 subarrays is: nums[0..2], nums[3..3], and nums[4..4]. The strength is (1 + 2 + 3) * 3 - (-1) * 2 + 2 * 1 = 22.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums = [12,-2,-2,-2,-2], k = 5\nOutput: 64\nExplanation: The only possible way to select 5 disjoint subarrays is: nums[0..0], nums[1..1], nums[2..2], nums[3..3], and nums[4..4]. The strength is 12 * 5 - (-2) * 4 + (-2) * 3 - (-2) * 2 + (-2) * 1 = 64.\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput: nums = [-1,-2,-3], k = 1\nOutput: -1\nExplanation: The best possible way to select 1 subarray is: nums[0..0]. The strength is -1.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= n <= 10^4\n-10^9 <= nums[i] <= 10^9\n1 <= k <= n\n1 <= n * k <= 10^6\nk is odd.",
    "prediction": "gpt-oss-120b",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**Python function**\n\n```python\ndef maxStrength(nums, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the maximum possible strength obtainable by selecting exactly k\n    disjoint sub‑arrays from `nums` (k is odd).\n\n    The strength of the selected sub‑arrays with sums s1 … sk is\n        Σ_{i=1..k} (-1)^{i+1} * s_i * (k-i+1)\n\n    Time   : O(n * k)   ( ≤ 10^6 operations because n·k ≤ 10^6 )\n    Memory : O(n)\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(nums)\n\n    # prefix sums: pref[i] = sum_{0..i-1}\n    pref = [0] * (n + 1)\n    for i in range(n):\n        pref[i + 1] = pref[i] + nums[i]\n\n    # DP for t = 0 (no sub‑array selected) – strength is 0 everywhere\n    prev = [0] * n          # prev[i] = DP[t-1][i]\n\n    for t in range(1, k + 1):\n        # weight of the t‑th chosen sub‑array\n        weight = ((-1) ** (t + 1)) * (k - t + 1)\n\n        cur = [float('-inf')] * n   # DP[t][i]\n        best = float('-inf')        # max_{j ≤ i} (DP[t-1][j-1] - weight * pref[j])\n\n        for i in range(n):\n            # candidate for starting a new sub‑array at position j = i\n            if i > 0:\n                cand = prev[i - 1] - weight * pref[i]\n            else:\n                # j = 0, previous strength is 0 only when t == 1\n                cand = (0 if t == 1 else float('-inf')) - weight * pref[0]\n\n            if cand > best:\n                best = cand\n\n            # best possible value if the t‑th sub‑array ends at i\n            val = best + weight * pref[i + 1]\n\n            # DP[t][i] = max(DP[t][i-1], val)\n            if i > 0 and cur[i - 1] > val:\n                cur[i] = cur[i - 1]\n            else:\n                cur[i] = val\n\n        prev = cur    # move to the next t\n\n    # answer is the best value after processing the whole array with k sub‑arrays\n    return int(prev[-1])\n```\nThis implementation follows the dynamic‑programming recurrence  \n\n\\[\n\\text{DP}[t][i] = \\max\\Bigl(\\text{DP}[t][i-1],\n\\; \\max_{0\\le j\\le i}\\bigl(\\text{DP}[t-1][j-1] + w_t\\cdot(\\text{pref}[i+1]-\\text{pref}[j])\\bigr)\\Bigr)\n\\]\n\nand keeps the inner maximum in a running variable `best`, turning the\noverall complexity into linear in `n` for each of the `k` stages.",
