izes the current contribution, assuming that the lower bits will be around their maximum.\n\nBut let's try a simpler greedy:\n\nFor bit `i` from `n-1` down to 0`:\n- Try setting `x_i = 0` and `x_i = 1`\n- For each, assume that for the lower bits, we can achieve the best possible values (e.g., both A and B can have all lower bits set)\n- Compute the estimated product for each choice\n- Pick the one with higher estimated product\n\nBut how to estimate?\n\nLet’s define a function that, given the current `A_cur` and `B_cur` ( for bits from `n-1` to `i`), and knowing that for bits `i-1` to `0`, we can choose optimally, computes an upper bound on the product.\n\nBut the optimal choice for lower bits depends on the XOR constraint.\n\nBut for estimation, we can ignore the constraint and assume both can be set to have all lower bits 1.\n\nSo the maximum possible `A` is `A_cur + (1<<i) - 1`, similarly for `B`.\n\nThen product is `(A_cur + (1<<i)-1) * (B_cur + (1<<i)-1)`\n\nBut this is an upper bound.\n\nBut it's the same for both choices at bit `i`? No, because `A_cur` and `B_cur` depend on the choice.\n\nSo for each choice at bit `i`, we can:\n- Compute `A_cur` and `B_cur` if we choose this option\n- Then add `(1<<i)-1` to both\n- Compute the product\n- Choose the option with higher product\n\nBut this is just a heuristic.\n\nLet’s test with example: a=12, b=5, n=4\n\na = 12 = 1100\nb = 5 = 0101\nn = 4, so x in [0,16)\n\na_low = 12, b_low = 5, C = a_low ^ b_low = 12^5 = 9 = 1001\n\nA_fixed = 0, B_fixed = 0\n\nWe want to choose A_var in [0,16), B_var = A_var ^ 9, maximize A_var * B_var\n\nLet’s compute for all A_var:\n- A_var=0, B_var=9, product=0\n- A_var=1, B_var=8, product=8\n- A_var=2, B_var=11, product=22\n- A_var=3, B_var=10, product=30\n- A_var=4, B_var=13, product=52\n- A_var=5, B_var=12, product=60\n- A_var=6, B_var=15, product=90\n- A_var=7, B_var=14, product=98\n- A_var=8, B_var=1, product=8\n- A_var=9, B_var=0, product=0\n- A_var=10, B_var=3, product=30\n- A_var=1