can do iterative, but let's do recursive and hope.\n\nAlternatively, we can use a Fenwick tree approach, but we already decided.\n\nLet's go with the segment tree.\n\n---\n\n### Final Decision\n\nGiven the complexity of implementing a full segment tree in Python within constraints, and the fact that only **two positions in `diff` change per flip**, but we can't update the string fast, perhaps the intended solution is to use a **lazy propagation segment tree**.\n\nBut there is a simpler solution using a **balanced BST of segments**? Probably not.\n\nAnother idea: since the string is only 0 and 1, and we only care about alternation, we can represent the string as alternating by default, but with flips, it might break.\n\nBut not helpful.\n\n---\n\nGiven the time, I'll implement the **segment tree with lazy propagation**.\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Build a segment tree where each node covers [l, r]\n2. Each node stores:\n   - `lv`: value at l\n   - `rv`: value at r\n   - `good`: whether the segment is good\n   - `lazy`: whether the segment is flipped\n3. Support:\n   - `build(idx, l, r)`\n   - `push(idx, l, r)`: propagate lazy to children\n   - `update(idx, l, r, ul, ur)`: flip range [ul, ur]\n   - `query(idx, l, r, ql, qr)`: return a node for the range [ql, qr]\n\nBut query return node is complex because we have to merge.\n\nWe can have the query return a node, and we merge nodes.\n\nLet's define a function to merge two nodes:\n\n```python\ndef merge(left, right):\n    if left is None:\n        return right\n    if right is None:\n        return left\n    good = left.good and right.good and (left.rv != right.lv)\n    return Node(left.lv, right.rv, good)\n```\n\nBut we also need to handle lazy in the children.\n\nSo better to do in the tree.\n\nLet's code accordingly.\n\nBut to save time, and since this is complex, and the constraints are high, but we have to.\n\nBut note: there is a known solution using a set of positions where `s[i] == s[i+1]`, and using a balanced BST, and for