] may not\n  - i = l: s[l] and s[l+1] -> both may be in [l, r] if l+1<=r, so both change -> may preserve equality\n  - But only if exactly one of the two is flipped, the equality may change.\n\nBut in our method, we are not flipping explicitly, but we can compute the current value before and after.\n\nBut we are about to flip, so we need to:\n- For each i in {l-1, l, r, r+1} that is in [0, N-2] (valid for bad), \n  - compute current s[i], s[i+1] -> if equal, then i is in bad_set\n  - after flip, compute new s[i], s[i+1] -> if equal, should be in bad_set\n  - if the status changes, add or remove i from bad_set\n\nBut to compute the current value, we use the Fenwick tree for the flip state.\n\nAnd after the flip, we update the Fenwick tree.\n\nSo algorithm for flip(l, r) (0-indexed):\n- update the XOR Fenwick tree at l and r+1 with 1\n- for each i in {l-1, l, r} ( because bad[i] for i in [0, N-2] )\n  - actually, the positions in `bad` that might change are: l-1, and r\n  - because:\n    - bad[l-1]: involves s[l-1] ( not in [l,r] ) and s[l] ( in [l,r] ) -> one flipped, so may change\n    - bad[r]: involves s[r] ( in [l,r] ) and s[r+1] ( not in [l,r] ) -> may change\n    - bad[i] for i in [l, r-1]: both s[i] and s[i+1] are flipped -> s[i] == s[i+1] remains the same\n  - so only i = l-1 and i = r\n- for i in [l-1, r] if i in [0, N-2]:\n  - compute current s[i] and s[i+1] using the Fenwick tree (after the update)\n  - but wait, the update is done, so the current state is after flip\n  - but we need to know the state before and after to update bad_set\n\nSo we must compute before the update.\n\nSo for each i in {l-1, r} ( if in [0, N-2] ):\n- compute s[i] and s[i+1] before the flip\n- determine if i is in bad_set\n- after the flip, compute s[i] and s[i+1] again\n- determine if i should be in bad_set\n- if different, toggle the membership in bad_set\n\nBut to compute s[i] before the flip, we use the current flip state (before update).\n\nSo the steps for a flip [l, r] (0-indexed):\n1. For each i in {l-1, r} if 0 <= i <= N-2:\n   - current_s_i = s0[i] XOR (prefix_xor(i))\n   - current_s_i1 = s0[i+1] XOR (prefix_xor(i+1))\n   - is_bad_before = (current_s_i == current_s_i1)\n   - if is_bad_before and i in bad_set: then it will be removed, so remove it later\n   - if not is_bad_before and i not in bad_set: then it will be added, so add later\n   - but we can't decide until after, so store the before state.\n2. Then update the XOR Fenwick tree at l and r+1 with 1.\n3. For each i in {l-1, r} if in [0, N-2]:\n   - current_s_i = s0[i] XOR (prefix_xor(i))\n   - current_s_i1 = s0[i+1] XOR (prefix_xor(i+1))\n   - is_bad_after = (current_s_i == current_s_i1)\n   - if is_bad_after:\n        if i not in bad_set: add it\n   - else:\n        if i in bad_set: remove it\n\nBut we can do it in one go after the update, but we need to know the before state.\n\nSo better to save the before state.\n\nWe can do:\n\n for i in {l-1, r}:\n   before = (s[i] == s[i+1]) using current state\n   after = (s[i] == s[i+1]) using state after flip ( but we haven't updated, so we can compute after state by flipping only the positions in [l,r] in the current state)\n\n actually, for i = l-1:\n   s[i] is not flipped, s[i+1] is flipped, so after: s[i] = s[i], s[i+1] = 1 - s[i+1]\n   so after: s[i] == s[i+1] iff s[i] == 1 - s[i+1] iff s[i] != s[i+1] before\n\n similarly, for i = r:\n   s[i] is flipped, s[i+1] is not, so after: s[i] = 1 - s[i], s[i+1] = s[i+1]\n   so after: s[i] == s[i+1] iff 1 - s[i] == s[i+1] iff s[i] != s[i+1] before\n\n for i in [l, r-1]: no change in bad[i]\n\n so for i = l-1 and i = r:\n   after: bad[i] = not (before bad[i])\n\n so we can simply: for i in {l-1, r} if in [0, N-2]:\n   if i in bad_set:\n        remove i\n   else:\n        add i\n\n because it toggles.\n\n is that true?\n\n for i = l-1:\n   before: s[i] == s[i+1] -> bad\n   after: s[i] == 1 - s[i+1] -> s[i] != s[i+1] -> not bad\n   so if it was in bad_set, remove it.\n\n   before: s[i] != s[i+1] -> not in bad_set\n   after: s[i] == 1 - s[i+1] -> if s[i] == 1 - s[i+1] -> s[i] == s[i+1] after? \n        after: s[i+1] is flipped, so s[i+1]' = 1 - s[i+1]\n        so s[i] == s[i+1]' -> s[i] == 1 - s[i+1]\n        which is true if s[i] != s[i+1] before\n        so after: s[i] == s[i+1]' -> so bad\n\n   so if it was not in bad_set, add it.\n\n   so indeed, it toggles.\n\n for i = r:\n   before: s[r] == s[r+1] -> bad\n   after: s[r]' = 1 - s[r], s[r+1] unchanged\n   so s[r]' == s[r+1] iff 1 - s[r] == s[r+1] iff s[r] != s[r+1] before\n   so if before: s[r] == s[r+1], then after: 1 - s[r] == s[r+1] only if s[r+1] = 1 - s[r] = 1 - s[r+1] -> 2*s[r+1] = 1 -> not integer, wait.\n\n   if before: s[r] == s[r+1] = say 0\n   after: s[r] = 1, s[r+1] = 0, so 1 != 0 -> not bad\n\n   if before: s[r] = 0, s[r+1] = 1, then after: s[r] = 1, s[r+1] = 1, so 1==1 -> bad\n\n   so after: bad if s[r] != s[r+1] before\n\n   so bad after = (s[r] != s[r+1]) before = not (s[r] == s[r+1]) before = not (before bad)\n\n   so again, toggles.\n\n Therefore, for i in {l-1, r} if in [0, N-2]:\n   if i in bad_set, remove it\n   else, add it\n\n and for the flip, we only need to update the XOR Fenwick tree at l and r+1.\n\n So we don't need to compute the actual values at all.\n\n So the plan is:\n\n- s0 = initial string as list of int\n- Use a XOR Fenwick tree (or simply a difference array with a Fenwick tree for XOR) to support range flip and point query for the flip state.\n- But we don't even need to compute the actual values for the bad array update, because we know it toggles.\n- We only need to compute the actual values for the type 2 query? No, for type 2 query, we only need to know if there is any bad position in [l, r-1]\n\n and we have a set `bad_set` of positions where s[i] == s[i+1]\n\n so for a type 2 query [l, r] (0-indexed):\n   if l == r:\n        print \"Yes\"\n   else:\n        we need to know if there is any i in [l, r-1] such that i in bad_set\n        if there is at least