left of size s.\n  - now 2 slimes of size 2s → merge into 1 of size 4s.\n  - so final: 1 (size s) + 1 (size 4s) → total 2.\n\nYes! So the number of remaining slimes is the **number of ones in the binary representation of the count**.\n\nBut wait: what if multiple sizes?\n\nAh! But different sizes don't interact unless merging makes them equal.\n\nBut in this problem, we start with different sizes, and merging doubles the size. So two different initial sizes will **never** produce the same size unless they are related by powers of two.\n\nBut consider: size 3 and size 6. 3*2=6. So if we have size 3, merging produces size 6, which may combine with existing size 6.\n\nSo **sizes that are in the same \"power-of-2 chain\" can interact**.\n\nTherefore, we **cannot** process each initial size independently.\n\nBut we **can** group sizes by their **odd base**.\n\nIdea:\n\nAny size can be written as: `s = base * 2^k`, where `base` is odd.\n\nThen, when we merge, we double the size → `2s = base * 2^(k+1)` → same base.\n\nSo slimes with the same **odd base** can eventually be merged together.\n\nSlimes with different odd bases will never interact.\n\nTherefore, we can:\n- For each slime size `s`, reduce it to its odd base: divide by 2 while even.\n- Then, group all counts by odd base.\n- For each group, we have a multiset of counts at different exponents (levels).\n\nBut we need to simulate merging from lower exponents to higher.\n\nFor a fixed odd base, we can collect all `(exponent, count)` pairs, sort by exponent, and simulate merging from low to high.\n\nAt each level, we have `c` slimes:\n- `c % 2` remain\n- `c // 2` carried to next level\n\nThis is exactly like binary addition across levels.\n\nSo algorithm:\n\n1. Group by odd base.\n2. For each group:\n   - Create a list of (exponent, count) where `s = base * 2^k`\n   - But multiple input sizes might have same base and different k.\n   - So use a dictionary: `group[base][k] += count`\n3. For each base, get all exponents