nSo popcount is the same as popcount of 1000000000.\n\nSo we need popcount(1000000000)\n\nCompute:\n\n1000000000 in binary:\n\nWe can compute:\n\n```python\nbin(1000000000).count('1')\n```\n\nBut let me calculate:\n\n1000000000 = 10^9\n\n2^30 = 1073741824\n2^29 = 536870912\n\n10^9 - 2^29 = 1000000000 - 536870912 = 463129088\n2^28 = 268435456\n463129088 - 268435456 = 194693632\n2^27 = 134217728\n194693632 - 134217728 = 60475904\n2^25 = 33554432\n60475904 - 33554432 = 26921472\n2^24 = 16777216\n26921472 - 16777216 = 10144256\n2^23 = 8388608\n10144256 - 8388608 = 1755648\n2^20 = 1048576\n1755648 - 1048576 = 707072\n2^19 = 524288\n707072 - 524288 = 182784\n2^17 = 131072\n182784 - 131072 = 51712\n2^15 = 32768\n51712 - 32768 = 18944\n2^14 = 16384\n18944 - 16384 = 2560\n2^11 = 2048\n2560 - 2048 = 512\n2^9 = 512\n512 - 512 = 0\n\nSo powers: 29,28,27,25,24,23,20,19,17,15,14,11,9\n\nCount: 13\n\nYes! So popcount(1000000000)=13\n\nAnd since T = 1000000000 << 9, same popcount.\n\nSo answer=13.\n\nTherefore, the solution is:\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    n = int(data[0])\n    from collections import defaultdict\n    groups = defaultdict(int)  # base -> total T\n    \n    index = 1\n    for i in range(n):\n        s = int(data[index]); c = int(data[index+1]); index += 2\n        # remove factors of 2 from s to get base, count k\n        temp = s\n        k = 0\n        while temp % 2 == 0:\n            temp //= 2\n            k += 1\n        base = temp\n        # T += c * (2^k)\n        # But note: 2^k is the multiplier for the level\n        groups[base] += c * (1 << k)  # 1<<k = 2^k\n    \n    total_slimes = 0\n    for T in groups.values():\n        total_slimes += bin(T).count('1')\n    \n    print(total_slimes)\n\nmain()\n```\n\nLet me test with sample 1:\n\n- s=3: temp=3, k=0, base=3, T += 3 * 1 = 3\n- s=5: k=0, base=5, T += 1 * 1 = 1\n- s=6: temp=6→3, k=1, base=3, T += 1 * (2^1)=2 → so group[3]=3+2=5\n- group[5]=1\n- popc