ount: \nrow0: 4\nrow1: 3 (missing (1,4))\nrow2: 4\nrow3: 4\nrow4: 4\ntotal = 4+3+4+4+4 = 19\n\nr_min = 0, r_max = 4\nc_min = 2, c_max = 5\narea = (4-0+1)*(5-2+1) = 5 * 4 = 20\nn = 19\narea = n+1 → yes.\n\nFind the `.` in [0,4]x[2,5]\n\nWe know (1,4) is '.' → 0-based (1,4) → 1-based (2,5)\n\nOutput: \"2 5\" → matches.\n\n---\n\n### Conclusion\n\nThe algorithm is:\n\n1. Read H, W\n2. Read grid\n3. Find all positions where grid[i][j] == '#'\n4. If no `#`, then impossible, but constraints say at least 2x2 rectangle, so at least 4 `#` originally, now 3, so there are `#`.\n5. Find:\n   - r_min = min i where grid[i][j]=='#'\n   - r_max = max i where grid[i][j]=='#'\n   - c_min = min j where grid[i][j]=='#'\n   - c_max = max j where grid[i][j]=='#'\n6. The original rectangle is from (r_min, c_min) to (r_max, c_max)\n7. The area is (r_max - r_min + 1) * (c_max - c_min + 1)\n8. Count of `#` is n\n9. We expect area = n + 1\n10. Then, iterate over i from r_min to r_max, j from c_min to c_max, find the cell where grid[i][j] == '.', that is the answer.\n11. Output (i+1, j+1) for 1-based indexing.\n\nBut is it possible that there is more than one '.' in the rectangle? Then area - n > 1, but we expect area = n+1, so only one '.'.\n\nBut what if due to input, area != n+1?\n\nBut from our reasoning, it must be.\n\nBut let's verify:\n\n- All `#` are within [r_min, r_max] x [c_min, c_max] by definition.\n- The original rectangle must be at least this bounding box.\n- But if it were larger, it would include cells outside, which are '.' and not `#`, so those would be missing, but only one missing, so only one cell outside can be missing, but extending the rectangle adds multiple cells, so multiple missing — impossible.\n- Therefore, original rectangle is exactly this bounding box.\n- It had area cells, all `#`.\n- Now, one is missing, so n = area - 1 → area = n+1.\n- So there is exactly one '.' in the box.\n\nTherefore, the algorithm is sound.\n\n### Code\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().splitlines()\n    if not data: \n        return\n    \n    H, W = map(int, data[0].split())\n    grid = []\n    for i in range(1, 1+H):\n        grid.append(data[i].strip())\n    \n    # Find all '#' positions\n    sharp_positions = []\n    for i in range(H):\n        for j in range(W):\n            if grid[i][j] == '#':\n                sharp_positions.append((i, j))\n    \n    # Find bounding box of '#'\n    rows = [pos[0] for pos in sharp_positions]\n    cols = [pos[1] for pos in sharp_positions]\n    \n    r_min = min(rows)\n    r_max = max(rows)\n    c_min = min(cols)\n    c_max = max(cols)\n    \n    # Now, find the '.' in the rectangle [r_min, r_max] x [c_min, c_max]\n    for i in range(r_min, r_max + 1):\n        for j in range(c_min, c_max + 1):\n            if grid[i][j] == '.':\n                print(i+1, j+1)\n                return\n\nmain()\n```\n\nLet's test with samples.\n\nSample 1: finds '.' at (1,3) in 0-based → prints (2,4) → correct.\n\nSample 2: (0,1) → (1,2) → correct.\n\nSample 3: (1,4) → (2,5) → correct.\n\nIt works.\n\nNote: We assume there is exactly one '.' in the bounding box, which is guaranteed by the problem.\n\nWe can add a fallback, but not needed.\n\nFinal code:",
