    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "I need to find the maximum strength of a group of students, where strength is the product of their scores.\n\nLet me think through this step by step:\n\n1. Since the array size is small (≤ 13), I can use a brute force approach by checking all possible non-empty subsets.\n2. I need to consider all possible combinations of students.\n3. The challenge is handling negative numbers - an even number of negatives gives a positive product, while an odd number gives a negative product.\n\nKey insights:\n- If I have negative numbers, I want an even count of them in my product (unless all options lead to negative)\n- Zero values should generally be excluded unless all numbers are zero or I have no choice\n- I need to check all possible subsets since the optimal solution might not be intuitive\n\nApproach:\n1. Generate all possible non-empty subsets of the array\n2. Calculate the product for each subset\n3. Return the maximum product\n\nSince the constraint is small (n ≤ 13), I can use bit manipulation to generate all subsets.\n\n```python\ndef maxStrength(nums):\n    n = len(nums)\n    max_product = float('-inf')\n    \n    # Check all possible non-empty subsets using bit manipulation\n    for mask in range(1, 1 << n):  # Start from 1 to exclude empty set\n        product = 1\n        for i in range(n):\n            if mask & (1 << i):  # If i-th bit is set\n                product *= nums[i]\n        max_product = max(max_product, product)\n    \n    return max_product\n```",
