- sum squares = 225+36=261\n\nExample 2:\n- cnt[0]=2, cnt[1]=1, cnt[2]=4\n- res=[0,0,0]\n- b=31..3: c=0\n- b=2: c=min(4,3)=3, add 4 to all: [4,4,4]\n- b=1: c=min(1,3)=1, add 2 to first: [6,4,4]\n- b=0: c=min(2,3)=2, add 1 to first two: [7,5,4]\n- sum squares = 49+25+16=90\n\nPerfect.\n\nNow, note: we are iterating from b=31 down to 0, which is correct.\n\nBut what if a number has bits above 31? But nums[i] <= 1e9 < 2^30, so 31 is enough.\n\nSo we can use 32 bits.\n\nFinal code.\n\nOne optimization: we can break early if (1<<b) is 0 for b>31, but we are looping only to 31.\n\nSo it's fine.\n\nWe'll write the function.\n\nNote: the inner loop for i in range(c) might be up to k, and we do 32 times, so total operations 32*k, which for k=1e5 is 3.2e6, acceptable in Python.\n\nSo we are good.\n\nFinal function:\n\n```python\ndef maxSumOfSquares(nums, k):\n    mod = 10**9 + 7\n    cnt = [0] * 32\n    for num in nums:\n        for b in range(32):\n            if num & (1 << b):\n                cnt[b] += 1\n    \n    res = [0] * k\n    \n    for b in range(31, -1, -1):\n        c = min(cnt[b], k)\n        for i in range(c):\n            res[i] += (1 << b)\n    \n    total = 0\n    for x in res:\n        total = (total + x * x) % mod\n    return total\n```\n\nBut note: we are modifying `res[0]` to `res[c-1]`, and we assume `res` is sorted in descending order. Initially it is (all zeros). After each step, as established, it remains sorted.\n\nSo we don't need to sort.\n\nThis should be correct.\n\nLet me run through a small example.\n\nSuppose nums=[3,3], k=1\n\n3: 11, so bit0:2, bit1:2\n\nk=1\n\nres=[0]\n\nb=1: c=min(2,1)=1, add 2: [2]\n\nb=0: c=min(2,1)=1, add 1: [3]\n\nsum squares=9\n\nIs that correct?\n\nWe have two 3s. We can do:\n\nChoose i=0,j=1: \n- nums[0] = 3&3=3\n- nums[1] = 3|3=3\nNo change.\n\nBut we can do:\n\nActually, 3&3=3, 3|3=3, so no change.\n\nBut we can only choose one element, so 3^2=9.\n\nBut can we make a larger number?\n\nTotal sum is 6. If we could 