**end days** (a_i + 1) and subtract b_i at that point.\n\nBut we want the **first day** where total ≤ K.\n\nWe can:\n\n- Start with total = sum of all b_i (on day 1).\n- Then, for each day `d` in increasing order, subtract all `b_i` such that `a_i == d-1` (i.e., medicine `i` stops after day `d-1`, so not taken on day `d`).\n\nBut days can be up to 1e9, so we can't iterate day-by-day.\n\nBut the number of **distinct event days** (when a medicine ends) is at most N.\n\nSo:\n\n- Collect all `a_i` values.\n- Group the `b_i` by `a_i`: for each end day `a_i`, we know that on day `a_i + 1`, we stop taking `b_i` pills.\n- Sort the end days.\n- Then simulate the total pill count at the **transition points**.\n\nThe total pill count is constant between transition points.\n\nSo the days when the total changes are: `d = a_i + 1` for some i.\n\nAlso, we must check day 1, 2, ..., up to the first transition.\n\nBut we can do:\n\n- Let events: for each medicine, at day `a_i + 1`, we subtract `b_i`.\n- Sort events by day.\n- Start with total = sum(b_i) at day 1.\n- Then, iterate over event days in increasing order: at each event day `d`, the total decreases by the sum of `b_i` for which `a_i + 1 == d`.\n- After each event, the total is constant until the next event.\n\nSo we can:\n\n1. Compute total = sum(b_i)\n2. If total ≤ K, return 1.\n3. Create list of (a_i + 1, b_i), group by day.\n4. Sort by day.\n5. Traverse events in order:\n   - Let current_day = 1\n   - For each event at day `d`:\n     - The total remains constant from `current_day` to `d - 1`.\n     - So if total > K, and we are going to reduce at day `d`, then we check: is there any day in `[current_day, d-1]` where total ≤ K? No, because total hasn't changed.\n     - But if total > K, we go to next event.\n     - When we reduce at day `d`, new_total = total - sum_b_at_d\n     - Then, starting from day `d`, the total is new_total.\n     - If new_total ≤ K, then answer is `d`.\n6. If we go through all events and total nev