only after the last medicine ends.\n\nBut the last event day is max(a_i)+1.\n\nBut our algorithm will return the event day when total becomes ≤ 0.\n\nSo if K = 0, we return the event day when total becomes 0.\n\nBut what if total becomes 0 at an earlier event? Only if all b_i have been subtracted by that day.\n\nSo yes, it will be correct.\n\nBut note: it's possible that total becomes 0 before max(a_i)+1? Only if all medicines have a_i < d, so at d = max(a_i)+1, but if multiple medicines end at different times, the last subtraction brings it to 0.\n\nSo algorithm is correct.\n\nLet’s code accordingly.\n\nComplexity: O(N log N) due to sorting event days.\n\nN up to 300,000 → acceptable.\n\n---\n\n### Code:\n\n```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not data:\n        return\n    \n    n = int(data[0])\n    k = int(data[1])\n    \n    medicines = []\n    total = 0\n    events = defaultdict(int)\n    \n    index = 2\n    for i in range(n):\n        a = int(data[index])\n        b = int(data[index+1])\n        index += 2\n        medicines.append((a, b))\n        total += b\n        event_day = a + 1\n        events[event_day] += b\n    \n    # If day 1 already satisfies\n    if total <= k:\n        print(1)\n        return\n    \n    # Get all event days and sort\n    event_days = sorted(events.keys())\n    \n    for day in event_days:\n        total -= events[day]\n        if total <= k:\n            print(day)\n            return\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\nLet’s test with sample 1:\n\n- events: {2:9, 3:5, 5:2, 7:3}\n- event_days = [2,3,5,7]\n- total starts at 19\n- day 2: 19-9=10 >8 → continue\n- day 3: 10-5=5 ≤8 → print 3 → correct.\n\nSample 2: total=19 ≤100 → print 1 → correct.\n\nSample 3: we are told answer is 492686569.\n\nLet me see: one of the inputs is `492686568 73361868` → event at 492686569.\n\nSo likely, after subtracting that batch, total drops to ≤ K