      "generated_answer": "Let's solve this step by step.\n\n---\n\n### **Problem Understanding**\n\nWe are given:\n- `N` products\n- Each product has:\n  - A price `P_i`\n  - A count of functions `C_i`\n  - A sorted list of `C_i` distinct function IDs (integers from 1 to M)\n\nWe are to determine if there exists a **pair of products (i, j)** such that:\n\n1. `P_i >= P_j`  \n2. Product `j` has **all** the functions that product `i` has  \n3. Either:\n   - `P_i > P_j`, **or**\n   - Product `j` has **at least one function** that product `i` does **not** have\n\nIf such a pair exists, print `\"Yes\"`, otherwise `\"No\"`.\n\n---\n\n### **Interpretation of Conditions**\n\nLet’s rephrase the conditions clearly.\n\nWe are checking if **product i is strictly worse than product j**, or equivalently, **product j is strictly better** than product i in some sense.\n\nBut note: we are checking for existence of `(i, j)` such that:\n- `i` is **not cheaper** than `j` (`P_i >= P_j`)\n- `j` has **all functions of i** (`functions_i ⊆ functions_j`)\n- And **either**:\n  - `i` is **more expensive** than `j`, or\n  - `j` has **more functions** than `i` (i.e., `functions_j` strictly contains `functions_i`)\n\nSo, in plain terms:  \nIs there a product `i` that costs **at least as much** as another product `j`, but `j` has **all its functions and possibly more**, and **either** is cheaper or has **extra functions**?\n\nThat means: **product i is dominated by product j** — you pay at least as much for `i`, but get less (or equal) functionality. So `j` is a better deal.\n\nHence, we are checking for **dominance**.\n\n---\n\n### **Algorithm Steps**\n\nWe need to:\n1. Read `N` and `M`\n2. For each product, store:\n   - Price `P`\n   - Set of functions (for fast subset checking)\n3. For every pair `(i, j)` where `i != j` (but can be any two, including same price/function):\n   - Check if `P_i >= P_j`\n   - Check if `functions_i` is a subset of `functions_j`\n   - Check if either:\n     - `P_i > 