data[idx + 1])\n        idx += 2\n        functions = list(map(int, data[idx:idx + C]))\n        idx += C\n        products.append((P, set(functions)))\n    \n    # Now check all pairs (i, j)\n    for i in range(N):\n        for j in range(N):\n            if i == j:\n                continue\n            P_i, funcs_i = products[i]\n            P_j, funcs_j = products[j]\n            \n            if P_i >= P_j:\n                if funcs_i <= funcs_j:  # All functions of i are in j\n                    if (P_i > P_j) or (funcs_i < funcs_j):\n                        print(\"Yes\")\n                        return\n    print(\"No\")\n\nsolve()\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Test with Sample 1**\n\nInput:\n```\n5 6\n10000 2 1 3\n15000 3 1 2 4\n30000 3 1 3 5\n35000 2 1 5\n100000 6 1 2 3 4 5 6\n```\n\nWe are told (i,j) = (4,3) works → i=4 (0-indexed i=3), j=3 (0-indexed j=2)\n\nCheck:\n- Product 4 (i=3): P=35000, funcs={1,5}\n- Product 3 (j=2): P=30000, funcs={1,3,5}\n\nCheck:\n- P_i=35000 >= P_j=30000 → yes\n- funcs_i={1,5} ⊆ funcs_j={1,3,5} → yes\n- P_i > P_j → 35000 > 30000 → yes → satisfies\n\nSo prints \"Yes\" → correct.\n\n---\n\n### **Sample 2**\n\n```\n4 4\n3 1 1\n3 1 2\n3 1 2\n4 2 2 3\n```\n\nCheck all pairs.\n\nSuppose i=0: P=3, {1}\n- j=1: P=3, {2} → {1} not subset of {2}\n- j=2: same\n- j=3: {2,3} → {1} not subset\n\ni=1: {2}, P=3\n- j=0: {1} → {2} not subset of {1}\n- j=2: {2} → subset? {2} ⊆ {2} → yes, but P_i=3 >= P_j=3 → yes, but P_i not > P_j, and {2} not proper subset → no\n- j=3: {2} ⊆ {2,3} → yes, P_i=3 >= P_j=4? → no → skip\n\ni=2: same as i=1\n\ni=3: {2,3}, P=4\n- j=0: P=3, {1} → {2,3} not subset of {1}\n- j=1: P=3, {2} → {2,3} not subset of {2}\n- j=2: same\n\nNo pair satisfies → \"No\" → correct.\n\n---\n\n### **Final Code**\n\nWe'll now write the final executable function that reads from stdin and prints the result.\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not data:\n        print(\"No\")\n        return\n    \n    n = int(data[0])\n    m = int(data[1])\n    products = []\n    index = 2\n    for _ in range(n):\n        price = int(data[index]); index += 1\n        count = int(data[index]); index += 1\n        functions = set()\n        for _ in range(count):\n            functions.add(int(data[index])); index += 1\n        products.append((price, functions))\n    \n    for i in range(n):\n        pi, fi = products[i]\n        for j in range(n):\n            if i == j:\n                continue\n            pj, fj = products[j]\n            if pi >= pj:\n                if fi.issubset(fj):\n                    if (pi > pj) or (len(fj) > len(fi)):\n                        print(\"Yes\")\n                        return\n    print(\"No\")\n\nsolve()\n```\n\nWait — is `len(fj) > len(fi)` equivalent to `fi < fj`?\n\nYes, **if** `fi` is a subset of `fj`, then `len(fj) > len(fi)` iff `fj` has more elements iff `fi` is a proper subset.\n\nSo we can use either.\n\nBut to be safe and explicit, we can use `fi < fj` (proper subset).\n\nAlternatively, `len(fj) > len(fi)` is faster.\n\nBut both are correct.\n\nLet’s use `fi.issubset(fj)` and `len(fj) > len(fi)` for clarity.\n\nBut note: it's possible (though unlikely due to input constraints) that two sets have same size, one is subset → then equal. So if `fi ⊆ fj` and `len(fi) == len(fj)`, then `fi == fj`.\n\nSo yes, `len(fj) > len(fi)` is equivalent to proper superset when subset holds.\n\nSo both are fine.\n\nWe'll go with `len(fj) > len(fi)`.\n\nFinal code:\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    n = int(data[0])\n    m = int(data[1])\n    products = []\n    idx = 2\n    for i in range(n):\n        p = int(data[idx]); idx += 1\n        c = int(data[idx]); idx += 1\n        funcs = set()\n        for j in range(c):\n            funcs.add(int(data[idx])); idx += 1\n        products.append((p, funcs))\n    \n    for i in range(n):\n        pi, fi = products[i]\n        for j in range(n):\n      