ime_prefix[t]` = number of rows with `row_time[i] <= t`\n- `col_time_prefix[t]` = number of columns with `col_time[j] <= t`\n\nBut note: we must not double count — each cell is counted once.\n\nBut in this method, we are assigning each cell to either row or column based on time, so no double count.\n\nSo algorithm:\n\n1. Initialize:\n   - `row_time = [0]*(H+1)`  # 1-indexed\n   - `row_color = [0]*(H+1)`\n   - `col_time = [0]*(W+1)`\n   - `col_color = [0]*(W+1)`\n\n2. For op_idx from 1 to M:\n   - Read T, A, X\n   - If T == 1:\n     - row_time[A] = op_idx\n     - row_color[A] = X\n   - Else:\n     - col_time[A] = op_idx\n     - col_color[A] = X\n\n3. Build frequency arrays for row times and col times:\n\n   - `row_time_count = [0]*(M+2)`  # index 0 to M+1\n   - For i from 1 to H: `row_time_count[row_time[i]] += 1`\n\n   - `col_time_count = [0]*(M+2)`\n   - For j from 1 to W: `col_time_count[col_time[j]] += 1`\n\n4. Build prefix sums:\n\n   - `row_time_prefix[t] = sum_{i=0}^{t} row_time_count[i]`\n   - `col_time_prefix[t] = sum_{i=0}^{t} col_time_count[i]`\n\n   Actually, we only need the prefix function as a function.\n\n   We can compute:\n\n   ```python\n   row_time_prefix = [0]*(M+2)\n   for t in range(M+1):\n       row_time_prefix[t] = (row_time_prefix[t-1] if t-1 >=0 else 0) + row_time_count[t]\n   ```\n\n   But easier: just compute cumulative.\n\n   Actually, we want `row_time_prefix[t]` = number of rows with time <= t\n\n   So:\n\n   ```python\n   row_time_prefix = [0]*(M+2)\n   for t in range(M+1):\n       row_time_prefix[t] = row_time_count[t] + (row_time_prefix[t-1] if t > 0 else 0)\n   ```\n\n   Similarly for col.\n\n5. Initialize a dictionary `color_count` to accumulate counts.\n\n6. For each row i from 1 to H:\n   - t_r = row_time[i]\n   - c = row_color[i]\n   - if t_r == 0: then this row was never updated? But initial is 0, so it's valid.\n   - number of columns j with `col_time[j] < t_r` = \n        if t_r == 0: 0\n        else: `col_time_prefix[t_r - 1]`   [since col_time_prefix[t] = count of col with time <= t]\n\n   - So add `col_time_prefix[t_r - 1]` to `color_count[c]`\n\n7. For each column j from 1 to W:\n   - t_c = col_time[j]\n   - c = col_color[j]\n   - number of rows i with `row_time[i] <= t_c` = `row_time_prefix[t_c]`\n   - add `row_time_prefix[t_c]` to `color_count[c]`\n\n8. But wait: is that correct?\n\nLet me verify:\n\n- For row i: it contributes to color `row_color[i]` for all columns j where `col_time[j] < row_time[i]`\n  → count = number of such j = `col_time_prefix[row_time[i] - 1]`\n\n- For column j: it contributes to color `col_color[j]` for all rows i where `row_time[i] <= col_time[j]`\n  → count = `row_time_prefix[col_time[j]]`\n\nYes.\n\nAnd every cell is counted exactly once: either row wins (row_time > col_time) or column wins (col_time >= row_time)\n\nSo total sum should be H * W.\n\nWe can use this to verify.\n\n9. Then:\n   - K = number of colors with count > 0\n   - Output K\n   - Then for each color in sorted order: print `color count`\n\nBut note: color 0 might be present.\n\nAlso, `col_time_prefix[t]` for t < 0 → 0\n\nSo in code, define:\n\n```python\ndef get_col_prefix(t):\n    if t < 0:\n        return 0\n    return col_time_prefix[t]  # which is count of columns with time <= t\n```\n\nSimilarly, `row_time_prefix[t]` for t > M is `row_time_prefix[M]`, but we only go up to M.\n\nWe'll make arrays of size M+2, index 0 to M+1.\n\nInitialize with 0.\n\n---\n\n### **Edge Cases**\n\n- No operations: all cells color 0\n  - row_time[i] = 0 for all i\n  - col_time[j] = 0 for all j\n  - For each row i: t_r = 0 → columns with col_time < 0 → 0 → so row contributes 0\n  - For each col j: t_c = 0 → rows with row_time <= 0 → all rows (since all are 0) → H\n    - So each column contributes H → total W * H → correct\n    - color 0 gets H * W\n\nBut wait: each column j adds `row_time_prefix[0]` = H → so total = W * H → correct.\n\nBut then color 0 is counted only once? No: we are adding for each column, same color 0.\n\nSo `color_count[0] += H` for each column → total `W * H`\n\nYes.\n\nBut in this case, rows contribute 0, columns contribute all.\n\nBut is that correct?\n\nFor cell (i,j): row_time[i] = 0, col_time[j] = 0 → col_time >= row_time → column wins → color = col_color[j] = 0\n\nYes, so all cells get color 0 from column.\n\nBut both row and column have color 0, so same.\n\nBut in our counting, rows don't contribute (since t_r=0, `col_time_prefix[-1]=0`), columns do.\n\nSo it's correct.\n\nAnother case: a row is updated at time 1, color 5.\n\nThen for that row: t_r=1, so columns with col_time < 1 → col_time = 0 → count = number of columns with time 0.\n\nBut if a column was updated at time 2, then col_time=2, which is not < 1 → so not included.\n\nYes.\n\n---\n\n### **Implementation Steps**\n\nLet’s code accordingly.\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Read H, W, M\n2. Initialize arrays for row and col time/color\n3. For each operation (index i from 1 to M):\n   - read T, A, X\n   - if T == 1: update row A\n   - else: update col A\n4. Build `row_time_count` array for times 0..M\n5. Build `col_time_count` similarly\n6. Build prefix sums:\n   - `row_time_prefix[t]` = sum of row_time_count[0..t]\n   - `col_time_prefix[t]` = sum of col_time_count[0..t]\n7. Initialize `color_count = {}`\n8. For each row i in 1..H:\n   - t = row_time[i]\n   - c = row_color[i]\n   - prev_cols = col_time_prefix[t-1] if t-1 >=0 else 0\n   - But if t==0, then t-1 = -1 → 0\n   - So: `prev_cols = col_time_prefix[t-1]` if t>=1 else 0\n   - Actually, we can define `col_time_prefix[-1] = 0`\n   - So: `prev_cols = col_time_prefix[t-1]` if t>=1 else 0\n   - Or better: make `col_time_prefix` array from -1 to M, or handle with max(0, ...)\n\n   Instead, let's make arrays of size M+2, and define:\n\n   ```python\n   col_time_prefix = [0]*(M+2)  # index 0 to M+1\n   # col_time_prefix[t] for t from 0 to M\n   # for t < 0, use 0\n   ```\n\n   So:\n\n   ```python\n   cols_before = col_time_prefix[t-1] if t-1 >= 0 else 0\n   ```\n\n   But we can precompute `col_time_prefix` for indices 0..M, and for t-1 < 0, use 0.\n\n9. Similarly for columns.\n\n10. Then output.\n\nBut note: `col_time_prefix[t]` is defined as sum_{i=0}^t col_time_count[i]\n\nSo we need to compute that.\n\n---\n\n### **Code Implementation**\n\nLet’s write the code accordingly.\n\nWe assume:\n- H, W, M up to 200,000 → arrays of size 200,000+ are fine.\n\nNote: time indices go from 0 to M (M up to 200,000)\n\nWe'll do:\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not data:\n        return\n    \n    it = iter(data)\n    H = int(next(it)); W = int(next(it)); M = int(next(it))\n    \n    # Initialize row and col arrays (1-indexed)\n    row_time = [0] * (H+1)   # row_time[i] = last op index for row i\n    row_color = [0] * (H+1)  # row_color[i] = color of last op on row i\n    col_time = [0] * (W+1)\n    col_color = [0] * (W+1)\n    \n    # Read M operations\n    for idx in range(1, M+1):\n        T = int(next(it)); A = int(next(it)); X = int(next(it))\n        if T == 1:\n            row_time[A] = idx\n            row_color[A] = X\n        else:  # T == 2\n            col_time[A] = idx\n            col_color[A] = X\n    \n    # Count frequency of row times and col times\n    # Time from 0 to M (M up to 200000)\n    max_time = M\n    row_time_count = [0] * (max_time + 2)  # index 0 to max_time+1\n    col_time_count = [0] * (max_time + 2)\n    \n    for i in range(1, H+1):\n        t = row_time[i]\n        if t <= max_time:\n            row_time_count[t] += 1\n        # t should be in [0, M]\n    \n    for j in range(1, W+1):\n        t = col_time[j]\n        if t <= max_time:\n            col_time_count[t] += 1\n    \n    # Build prefix sums: prefix[t] = sum_{i=0}^{t} count[i]\n    # We'll make arrays of length max_time+2\n    row_time_prefix = [0] * (max_time + 2)\n    col_time_prefix = [0] * (max_time + 2)\n    \n    # For t from 0 to max_time\n    for t in range(max_time + 1):\n        row_time_prefix[t] = row_time_count[t] + (row_time_prefix[t-1] if t-1 >= 0 else 0)\n        col_time_prefix[t] = col_time_count[t] + (col_time_prefix[t-1] if t-1 >= 0 else 0)\n    # For t > max_time, we don't use, but for t = max_time+1, leave 0 or unused\n    \n    # But we need prefix for t-1, so for t=0, t-1=-1 → 0\n    # We'll handle in loop\n    \n    from collections import defaultdict\n    color_count = defaultdict(int)\n    \n    # Process rows: for each row i\n    for i in range(1, H+1):\n        t_r = row_time[i]\n        c = row_color[i]\n        # Number of columns with col_time[j] < t_r\n        if t_r == 0:\n            cols_before = 0\n        else:\n            # cols with time <= t_r-1\n            if t_r - 1 <= max_time:\n                cols_before = col_time_prefix[t_r - 1]\n            else:\n                cols_before = col_time_prefix[max_time]  # but t_r <= M, so t_r-1 <= M-1\n        color_count[c] += cols_before\n    \n    # Process columns: for each column j\n    for j in range(1, W+1):\n        t_c = col_time[j]\n        c = col_color[j]\n        # Number of rows with row_time[i] <= t_c\n        if t_c > max_time:\n            rows_up_to = row_time_prefix[max_time]\n        else:\n            rows_up_to = row_time_prefix[t_c]\n        color_count[c] += rows_up_to\n    \n    # Now, collect results\n    # Only include colors with count > 0\n    # But all should be > 0 if they appear\n    result = []\n    for color in color_count:\n        if color_count[color] > 0:\n            result.append( (color, color_count[color]) )\n    \n    result.sort()  # sort by color\n    K = len(result)\n    \n    print(K)\n    for color, cnt in result:\n        print(color, cnt)\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Test with Sample 1**\n\nInput:\n```\n3 4 4\n1 2 5\n2 4 0\n1 3 3\n1 3 2\n```\n\nOperations:\n\n- op1 (T=1, A=2, X=5): row 2 → time=1, color=5\n- op2 (T=2, A=4, X=0): col 4 → time=2, color=0\n- op3 (T=1, A=3, X=3): row 3 → time=3, color=3\n- op4 (T=1, A=3, X=2): row 3 → time=4, color=2\n\nFinal:\n\n- row_time: [0, 0, 1, 4]  # row1:0, row2:1, row3:4\n- row_color: [0,0,5,2]\n- col_time: [0,0,0,2]  # col1:0, col2:0, col3:0, col4:2\n- col_color: [0,0,0,0]\n\nNow, build count arrays:\n\n- row_time_count:\n  - t=0: 1 (row1)\n  - t=1: 1 (row2)\n  - t=4: 1 (row3)\n  - others 0\n\n- col_time_count:\n  - t=0: 3 (col1,2,3)\n  - t=2: 1 (col4)\n\nPrefix:\n\n- col_time_prefix:\n  - t=0: 3\n  - t=1: 3 (no t=1)\n  - t=2: 4\n  - t=3: 4\n  - t=4: 4\n\n- row_time_prefix:\n  - t=0: 1\n  - t=1: 2\n  - t=2: 2\n  - t=3: 2\n  - t=4: 3\n\nNow process rows:\n\n- row1: t=0, c=0 → cols_before = 0 → add 0\n- row2: t=1, c=5 → cols_before = col_time_prefix[0] = 3 → add 3 to color 5\n- row3: t=4, c=2 → cols_before = col_time_prefix[3] = 4 → add 4 to color 2\n\nProcess columns:\n\n- col1: t=0, c=0 → rows_up_to = row_time_prefix[0] = 1 → add 1 to color 0\n- col2: t=0, c=0 → add 1 → total 2\n- col3: t=0, c=0 → add 1 → total 3\n- col4: t=2, c=0 → rows_up_to = row_time_prefix[2] = 2 → add 2 → total 5\n\nSo:\n- color 0: 5\n- color 2: 4\n- color 5: 3\n\nOutput:\n```\n3\n0 5\n2 4\n5 3\n```\n\nMatches sample.\n\n---\n\n### **Sample 2**\n\nInput:\n```\n1 1 5\n1 1 1\n1 1 10\n2 1 100\n1 1 1000\n2 1 10000\n```\n\nH=1, W=1\n\nOperations:\n\n1. T=1, A=1, X=1 → row1: time=1, color=1\n2. T=1, A=1, X=10 → row1: time=2, color=10\n3. T=2, A=1, X=100 → col1: time=3, color=100\n4. T=1, A=1, X=1000 → row1: time=4, color=1000\n5. T=2, A=1, X=10000 → col1: time=5, color=10000\n\nFinal:\n\n- row1: time=4, color=1000\n- col1: time=5, color=10000\n\nNow:\n\n- row_time_count: t=4:1\n- col_time_count: t=5:1\n\nPrefix:\n- col_time_prefix[3] = 0 (no col with time<=3? col_time[1]=5 → so col_time_count[5]=1, others 0)\n  - t=0..4: 0, t=5:1\n  - so col_time_prefix[3]=0, col_time_prefix[4]=0\n\n- row_time_prefix: t=4:1\n\nProcess row1: t=4, c=1000 → cols_before = col_time_prefix[3] = 0 → color 1000 +=0\n\nProcess col1: t=5, c=10000 → rows_up_to = row_time_prefix[5] = 1 (since row_time_prefix[4]=1, and 5>4 → but we only computed up to M=5? yes, row_time_prefix[5] = row_time_prefix[4] =1)\n\nSo color 10000 +=1\n\nOutput:\n```\n1\n10000 1\n```\n\nCorrect.\n\n---\n\n### **Sample 3**\n\n```\n5 5 10\n1 1 1\n1 2 2\n1 3 3\n1 4 4\n1 5 5\n2 1 6\n2 2 7\n2 3 8\n2 4 9\n2 5 10\n```\n\nAll rows updated at time 1..5, colors 1..5\n\nAll columns updated at time 6..10, colors 6..10\n\nSo for each row i: time = i, color = i\n\nFor each col j: time = j+5, color = j+5\n\nNow, for any cell (i,j): row_time = i (1..5), col_time = j+5 (6..10) → col_time > row_time → column wins\n\nSo all cells get column color.\n\nEach column j has color j+5, and it wins for all 5 rows → each color 6,7,8,9,10 has 5 cells.\n\nNow in code:\n\n- row_time: [1,2,3,4,5] for rows 1..5\n- col_time: [6,7,8,9,10] for cols 1..5\n\nrow_time_count: t=1..5: 1 each\n\ncol_time_count: t=6..10: 1 each\n\ncol_time_prefix[t] for t<6: 0, t=6:1, t=7:2, etc.\n\nProcess rows:\n\n- row i: t=i, c=i\n- cols_before = col_time_prefix[i-1] → since i=1..5, i-1=0..4 → all 0\n- so all rows contribute 0\n\nProcess columns:\n\n- col j: t=5+j, c=5+j\n- rows_up_to = row_time_prefix[5+j] → for j=1, t=6 → row_time_prefix[6] = row_time_prefix[5] = 5 (since all 5 rows have time<=5<6)\n- so each column adds 5\n\nSo colors 6,7,8,9,10 each get 5\n\nOutput:\n```\n5\n6 5\n7 5\n8 5\n9 5\n10 5\n```\n\nCorrect.\n\n---\n\n### Final Code\n\nWe'll now write the final code accordingly.\n\nNote: We must ensure arrays are large enough.\n\nLet’s code it.\n\nNote: We can make `row_time_prefix` and `col_time_prefix` arrays of size M+2, and for t > M, we use row_time_prefix[M] if needed, but in our case t_c <= M, so safe.\n\nBut in column processing, t_c can be up to M, so row_time_prefix[t_c] with t_c <= M → we computed up to M.\n\nBut our prefix arrays are of size M+2, indices 0..M+1, so safe.\n\nWe'll write accordingly.\n\nOne more thing: `row_time_prefix[t]` for t > M? no, t_c <= M.\n\nSo we are safe.\n\nFinal code:\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    it = iter(data)\n    H = int(next(it)); W = int(next(it)); M = int(next(it))\n    \n    row_time = [0] * (H+1)\n    row_color = [0] * (H+1)\n    col_time = [0] * (W+1)\n    col_color = [0] * (W+1)\n    \n    for idx in range(1, M+1):\n        T = int(next(it)); A = int(next(it)); X = int(next(it))\n        if T == 1:\n            row_time[A] = idx\n            row_color[A] = X\n        else:\n            col_time[A] = idx\n            col_color[A] = X\n    \n    max_time = M\n    size = max_time + 2\n    row_time_count = [0] * size\n    col_time_count = [0] * size\n    \n    for i in range(1, H+1):\n        t = row_time[i]\n        if t < size:\n            row_time_count[t] += 1\n    \n    for j in range(1, W+1):\n        t = col_time[j]\n        if t < size:\n            col_time_count[t] += 1\n    \n    # Build prefix sums: prefix[t] = sum_{i=0}^{t} count[i]\n    row_time_prefix = [0] * size\n    col_time_prefix = [0] * size\n    \n    for t in range(size):\n        if t == 0:\n            row_time_prefix[t] = row_time_count[t]\n            col_time_prefix[t] = col_time_count[t]\n        else:\n            row_time_prefix[t] = row_time_prefix[t-1] + row_time_count[t]\n            col_time_prefix[t] = col_time_prefix[t-1] + col_time_count[t]\n    \n    from collections import defaultdict\n    color_count = defaultdict(int)\n    \n    # Process each row\n    for i in range(1, H+1):\n        t_r = row_time[i]\n        c = row_color[i]\n        if t_r > 0:\n            # columns with time < t_r  => time <= t_r - 1\n            if t_r - 1 < size:\n                cols_before = col_time_prefix[t_r - 1]\n            else:\n                cols_before = col_time_prefix[-1]  # shouldn't happen\n        else:\n            cols_before = 0\n        color_count[c] += cols_before\n    \n    # Process each column\n    for j in range(1, W+1):\n        t_c = col_time[j]\n        c = col_color[j]\n        if t_c < size:\n            rows_up_to = row_time_prefix[t_c]\n        else:\n            rows_up_to = row_time_prefix[-1]\n        color_count[c] += rows_up_to\n    \n    # Prepare output\n    result = []\n    for color in color_count:\n        cnt = color_count[color]\n        if cnt > 0:\n            result.append((color, cnt))\n    \n    result.sort()\n    print(len(result))\n    for color, cnt in result:\n        print(color, cnt)\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n```\n\nLet me test with sample 1: it should work.\n\nBut note: in prefix array, we built for t in range(size), and size = M+2.\n\nFor t_r=4, we need col_time_prefix[3]. We built it.\n\nYes.\n\nWe'll run it.\n\nBut since we can't run here, we trust the logic.\n\nFinal answer.",
