      "generated_answer": "I need to find the length of the longest common prefix between any pair of integers from arr1 and arr2.\n\nLet me think through this step by step:\n\n1. For each number in arr1 and each number in arr2, I need to find their longest common prefix\n2. A common prefix is formed by digits starting from the leftmost digit\n3. I need to return the maximum length among all pairs\n\nMy approach:\n- For each number in arr1, generate all its prefixes\n- Store these prefixes in a set for quick lookup\n- For each number in arr2, check all its prefixes from longest to shortest\n- If a prefix exists in the set from arr1, that's a common prefix\n- Keep track of the maximum length found\n\nLet me trace through Example 1:\n- arr1 = [1,10,100], arr2 = [1000]\n- Prefixes from arr1: {1, 10, 100}\n- For 1000 in arr2: prefixes are 1000, 100, 10, 1\n- 1000 not in set, 100 is in set (length 3)\n- So maximum length is 3\n\nFor efficiency, I'll:\n1. Create a set of all prefixes from arr1\n2. For each number in arr2, check its prefixes in descending order of length\n3. Return the maximum length found\n\n```python\ndef longestCommonPrefix(arr1, arr2):\n    # Set to store all prefixes from arr1\n    prefixes = set()\n    \n    # Generate all prefixes from arr1\n    for num in arr1:\n        str_num = str(num)\n        for i in range(1, len(str_num) + 1):\n            prefixes.add(str_num[:i])\n    \n    max_length = 0\n    \n    # Check all prefixes from arr2\n    for num in arr2:\n        str_num = str(num)\n        # Check prefixes from longest to shortest\n        for i in range(len(str_num), 0, -1):\n            prefix = str_num[:i]\n            if prefix in prefixes:\n                max_length = max(max_length, i)\n                break  # No need to check shorter prefixes for this number\n    \n    return max_length\n```",
