we can choose to apply the **maximum discount available** among coupons that are eligible (i.e., `L_i <= P_j`) and not yet used.\n\nBut here's the catch: **we can skip applying a coupon to an item** — maybe save a high-discount coupon for a later item? But no: if a coupon is eligible for this item, it will be eligible for all more expensive items (since `P_j` is increasing). So if we don’t use it now, we can use it later.\n\nBut we want to **use the best coupons**, so we should **not** assign a coupon unless we have to.\n\nWait — actually, we want to **maximize number of high-discount coupons used**.\n\nBetter idea:\n\nUse a greedy algorithm:\n\n1. Sort items: `P` in ascending order.\n2. Sort coupons: by `L_i` in ascending order.\n3. Traverse items from **lowest price to highest**.\n4. For the current item with price `p`, add all coupons with `L_i <= p` into a **max-heap** (by `D_i`).\n5. Then, from the heap, pick the coupon with the **maximum discount** and apply it to **this item**.\n6. Remove that coupon from the heap (use it).\n7. Continue.\n\nBut is this optimal?\n\nWait — we are **forcing** to use a coupon on every item? No — we don't have to. We only have `M` coupons and `N` items. We can use at most `min(N, M)` coupons.\n\nBut we want to **maximize total discount**, so we should use a coupon **only if it gives positive discount**, which it always does.\n\nBut we may not want to use a coupon on a cheap item if we can use it on a more expensive one? But if the coupon is eligible (i.e., `L_i <= P_j`), and we can use it on a cheap item, we can also use it on expensive ones — so it's safe to **delay** using it.\n\nBut in this algorithm, we are adding all coupons with `L_i <= P_j` as we increase `P_j`, and then at each item, we **choose** to use the best available coupon.\n\nBut we **don’t have to** use a coupon on every item. So we should only use a coupon if it’s beneficial — but it always is.\n\nBut we are limited by number of coupons.\n\nSo the correct greedy:\n\n> We want to assign coupons to items to maximize total discount.\n> We can use at most one coupon per item.\n> A coupon `i` can be assigned to item `j` iff `P_j >= L_i`.\n\nIdea: **Use a priority queue (max-heap) of available discounts**.\n\nAlgorithm:\n\n1. Sort items by price: `P_sorted` ascending.\n2. Sort coupons by `L_i` ascending.\n3. Use an index `j = 0` to iterate over coupons.\n4. Use a max-heap (we'll use negative values in Python's min-heap) to store `D_i` of eligible coupons.\n5. For each item in increasing order of price:\n   a. Add all coupons with `L_i <= current item price` into the max-heap (push `-D_i` or use `heapq` as min-heap for negatives).\n   b. Among all coupons that are eligible (now in heap), if any, pick the one with **largest discount** and **use it on this item**.\n   c. But wait — should we assign a coupon to this item? Not necessarily — maybe save the coupon for later? But since we are going from low to high price, all future items will also qualify for this coupon (since price is increasing), so we can **postpone** using a coupon.\n\nBut we want to **use the best coupons**, but we are limited by number of items we can apply them to.\n\nActually, the correct known solution for such problems (e.g., \"assign coupons to minimize cost\") is:\n\n> Use a greedy: for each item (in increasing order), add all coupons with `L_i <= P_j` into a max-heap of discounts.\n> Then, **after processing all items**, we cannot assign more than `min(N, M)` coupons.\n\nWait — no.\n\nActually, here's the correct known approach:\n\nWe want to **maximize the number of high-discount coupons used**.\n\nBut we can use at most one per item.\n\nSo:\n\n- Process items from **lowest to highest** price.\n- For each item, add all coupons with `L_i <= current item price` into a **max-heap** (by discount).\n- Then, **pop the largest discount** from the heap and **use it** — but on which item? Actually, we are not assigning it to this item; instead, we are simulating: we are collecting all coupons that can be used on **this or future items**, and then we want to assign the best coupons to **some** items.\n\nBut here’s a twist: we can only use one coupon per item, so we can use at most `N` coupons.\n\nBut we have only `M`.\n\nSo the idea is:\n\nWe will simulate **which coupons can be used** — but we want to use the **best discounts possible**.\n\nBut we can delay.\n\nActually, the correct greedy is:\n\n> We are going to try to **use up to N coupons**, but we only have M.\n> We want to select a set of coupons (at most one per item) such that for coupon `i` used on item `j`, `P_j >= L_i`.\n\nWe can use a greedy:\n\n1. Sort items: `P` ascending.\n2. Sort coupons: `L_i` ascending.\n3. Traverse coupons in increasing `L_i`, but no.\n\nBetter: traverse items in increasing order, and for each item, we make available all coupons with `L_i <= P_j`. Then, we maintain a max-heap of available discounts.\n\nThen, **at each item**, we can choose to **use the best available coupon** (largest discount) on this item.\n\nWhy is this correct?\n\n- Because we are going from cheap to expensive items.\n- Any coupon that qualifies for a cheap item will qualify for more expensive ones.\n- So if we don’t use a coupon now, we can use it later.\n- But we want to **use the best coupons**, so we should **reserve them** for later? No — we can use them now or later.\n\nBut here's the key: **we are limited by the number of items** — we can use at most one coupon per item. So if we have more coupons than items, we want to use the best `N` discounts that are eligible.\n\nBut eligibility depends on item.\n\nSo the algorithm:\n\n- We want to assign coupons to items to maximize sum of discounts.\n- We can use a greedy: **try to use the largest discounts first**, but assign them to the **smallest eligible item** (to save larger items for high-L coupons).\n\nBut that’s complex.\n\nAlternatively, known solution from similar problems (e.g., AtCoder problems):\n\nUse:\n\n```python\nsort items: increasing\nsort coupons: increasing by L_i\nuse a priority queue (min-heap) for discounts of coupons that are eligible\nbut we want to maximize discount\n```\n\nActually, here's a known efficient solution:\n\n1. Sort items: `P` in ascending order.\n2. Sort coupons: by `L_i` in ascending order.\n3. Use a pointer `j = 0` for coupons.\n4. Use a **max-heap** (or min-heap for negative) to store discounts of coupons with `L_i <= current item price`.\n5. For each item (in increasing order of price):\n   - While `j < M` and `L_j <= P_i`: add `D_j` to max-heap, j++\n   - If heap is not empty, pop the largest discount and add it to total discount (use it on this item)\n   - But wait: are we forced to use a coupon on this item? No, but if we don't use it now, we might not get a chance later? But we can use it on a later item.\n\nBut here's the catch: **we are processing items one by one, and we can choose to use a coupon or not**.\n\nBut if we **skip** using a coupon on this item, we can use it on a later item.\n\nSo we should **not** use a coupon on this item unless we want to.\n\nBut we want to **maximize total discount**, so we want to use the **best coupons**, but we are limited by the number of items.\n\nSo we should **collect all eligible coupons** as we go, and then **use the best available coupon** on **each item**, but only if it gives a positive discount (which it does).\n\nBut we can only use one per item.\n\nSo the correct idea:\n\n> We are going to decide for each item: should we use a coupon on it? And which one?\n\nBut we want to **maximize total discount**, so we should use a coupon on an item only if it saves money — which it always does.\n\nSo we should use a coupon on as many items as possible, but only up to `M` coupons and `N` items.\n\nBut we want to use the **best discounts**.\n\nSo:\n\n- We maintain a max-heap of available discounts (coupons that can be used on current or future items).\n- For each item (from low to high price), we add all coupons with `L_i <= P_i` into the heap.\n- Then, we **choose to use the best available coupon** on **this item** — because:\n   - If we don't use it now, we can still use it later (since future items are more expensive, so `L_i <= P_i` implies `L_i <= P_{i+1}`).\n   - But we have to assign coupons to items — and we have `N` items.\n   - We want to use the best coupons, but we can only use one per item.\n\nBut if we use a coupon on a cheap item, we save `D_i` yen — same as using it on an expensive item.\n\nSo the **discount amount doesn't depend on the item**, only on the coupon.\n\nTherefore, we just want to **use the coupons with the largest discounts**, regardless of which item they are used on, as long as `P_j >= L_i`.\n\nSo the problem reduces to:\n\n> Select up to `N` coupons (but only `M` available) to use, such that for each coupon `i` used, there exists an item `j` with `P_j >= L_i`, and each item is used at most once.\n\nBut we can assign any eligible coupon to any item.\n\nSo we want to **select a set of coupons** (as many as possible, but up to `N`) with **maximum sum of discounts**, such that for each coupon `i` in the set, there is an item `j` with `P_j >= L_i`, and no two coupons share the same item.\n\nBut since we can assign freely, the only constraint is: the number of coupons we can use is limited by:\n- Number of items: `N`\n- Number of coupons: `M`\n- And for a coupon `i` to be usable, there must be **at least one item** with `P_j >= L_i`.\n\nBut we can use a coupon `i` if `min_item_price >= L_i`? No — only if **some** item has `P_j >= L_i`.\n\nSo first, a coupon `i` is usable only if `max(P_j) >= L_i`? No — if **any** item has `P_j >= L_i`.\n\nBut since we can choose which item to apply it to, coupon `i` can be used if **there exists** an item `j` with `P_j >= L_i`.\n\nSo let `max_P = max(P_j)`, but actually, we need to know for each coupon, is there an item with `P_j >= L_i`?\n\nBut we can precompute: sort `P`, then for a coupon with `L_i`, it can be used if `max(P) >= L_i`? No — if `max(P) >= L_i`, then yes, because the most expensive item qualifies.\n\nYes! Because if the **maximum** item price is >= `L_i`, then there exists at least one item (the most expensive one) that can use it.\n\nSo coupon `i` is usable iff `max(P) >= L_i`? No — wait, not necessarily: we might have to use that item for another coupon.\n\nBut for feasibility, we can say: coupon `i` can be used only if `max(P) >= L_i`.\n\nBut actually, no: if `L_i = 10`, and we have items [1, 2, 15], then coupon can be used on item with 15.\n\nBut if `L_i = 20`, and max(P)=15, then no item qualifies.\n\nSo coupon `i` is eligible for use only if `max(P) >= L_i`? No — if **any** item has `P_j >= L_i`, which is equivalent to `max(P) >= L_i`.\n\nYes.\n\nSo we can filter coupons: only those with `L_i <= max(P)` are usable.\n\nBut even then, we may not be able to use all of them, due to item limit.\n\nBut our goal is to **select a subset of coupons** (size at most `N`) with maximum sum of discounts, such that for each coupon in the subset, `L_i <= max(P)` (which is always true if we filter), and we can assign them to distinct items.\n\nBut can we always assign a set of `k` coupons to `k` distinct items, as long as for each coupon `i`, there is an item `j` with `P_j >= L_i`?\n\nNot necessarily: if two coupons require `L_i > P_j` for all but one item, then we can't assign both.\n\nBut if we have `k` coupons, we need `k` items such that for coupon `i`, the item `j` assigned has `P_j >= L_i`.\n\nThis is a matching problem.\n\nBut we can use a greedy assignment:\n\n- Sort coupons by `D_i` descending.\n- For each coupon in that order, assign it to the **smallest-priced item** that has `P_j >= L_i` and is not yet taken.\n- If no such item, skip the coupon.\n\nThis is a known greedy for assignment.\n\nWhy smallest-priced item?\n- To save larger-priced items for coupons with higher `L_i` requirements.\n\nThis is optimal.\n\nSo algorithm:\n\n1. Sort items: `P` in ascending order.\n2. Sort coupons: by `D_i` descending.\n3. Also, we need to consider `L_i` for eligibility.\n4. For each coupon in order of decreasing `D_i`:\n   a. Find the smallest-priced item that is not taken and has `P_j >= L_i`.\n   b. If found, use this coupon on that item, mark item as taken, add `D_i` to total discount.\n   c. Else, skip.\n\nHow to do step 4a efficiently?\n\nWe can use a data structure for unused items, sorted by price, and support:\n- Find first item with `P_j >= L_i`\n- Remove it\n\nWe can use a **sorted list** and binary search, but removal is O(n).\n\nAlternatively, use a **balanced BST** — in Python, we can use `sorted list` from `sortedcontainers`, but not in standard.\n\nAlternatively, use a **heap**? Not directly.\n\nAnother idea: use a **pointer array** or use a **union-find** for next available item? Overkill.\n\nSimpler: since we only care about price, and items are sorted by price, we can do:\n\n- Keep a boolean array `used[0..N-1]`\n- For each coupon, iterate from the first item with `P_j >= L_i` to the end, find the first unused item.\n\nBut that's O(N*M) in worst case — 2e5 * 2e5 = 40e9, too slow.\n\nWe need O(N log N).\n\nBetter: use a **DSU-like** structure or a **balanced BST**.\n\nAlternatively, use a **heap** of available items, but indexed by price.\n\nBut we need to find the smallest `P_j >= L_i`.\n\nIdea: use a sorted list of available items, and use binary search.\n\nWe can do:\n\n- Start with a sorted list of (price, index) of items.\n- For each coupon (in order of decreasing `D_i`):\n   - Use `bisect_left` to find the first item with `P_j >= L_i`\n   - Scan forward to find the first item that is still available? But scanning may be slow.\n\nBut if we remove items, we need a data structure.\n\nAlternatively, use a **balanced BST simulation** with a sorted list and lazy deletion? But deletion from list is O(n).\n\nAnother idea: use a **segment tree** or **Fenwick tree** to find the first available item with `P_j >= L_i` — overkill.\n\nKnown simpler approach in competitive programming:\n\nUse a **greedy with heap** in reverse order.\n\nActually, there is a well-known solution:\n\n> Sort coupons by `L_i` descending.\n> Sort items by `P_i` descending.\n> Use a pointer for items.\n> For each coupon in order of `L_i` descending:\n>   Add all items with `P_j >= L_i` into a max-heap of prices (or we don't need that)\n>   Then, choose the item with smallest price from those items? \n\nWait, no.\n\nLet me search for known problems.\n\nThis is very similar to: \"AtCoder - Shopping\" or \"Coupon problem\".\n\nActually, the correct efficient solution is:\n\n### **Final Algorithm (efficient)**\n\n1. Sort items in **increasing order**.\n2. Sort coupons in **increasing order of L_i**.\n3. Use a max-heap (for discounts) of eligible coupons.\n4. total_discount = 0\n5. j = 0  # pointer for coupons\n6. For each item in **increasing order of price**:\n   a. While j < M and L_j <= current item price:\n        heapq.heappush(max_heap, -D_j)   # max-heap using negative\n        j += 1\n   b. If max_heap is not empty:\n        discount = -heapq.heappop(max_heap)\n        total_discount += discount\n7. Answer = sum(P) - total_discount\n\nBut wait, this uses a coupon on **every item** as long as there is an eligible coupon available.\n\nIs this correct?\n\nLet's test with sample 1:\n\nSample 1:\n- N=3, M=3\n- P = [4,3,1] -> sorted: [1,3,4]\n- L = [4,4,2] -> sorted with D: let's sort coupons by L_i:\n   - coupon0: L=4, D=2\n   - coupon1: L=4, D=3\n   - coupon2: L=2, D=1\n   - sorted by L_i: [2,4,4] -> coupons: (2,1), (4,2), (4,3)\n\nNow process items in order: 1, 3, 4\n\n- Item price=1:\n   - add coupons with L_i <= 1: only if L_i<=1. But smallest L_i is 2 >1, so none.\n   - heap empty -> no coupon used.\n- Item price=3:\n   - add coupons with L_i <=3: L_i=2 -> add D=1\n   - heap = [-1]\n   - pop -> discount=1\n   - total_discount=1\n- Item price=4:\n   - add coupons with L_i<=4: the two with L_i=4: add D=2 and D=3 -> heap becomes [-2,-3] (min-heap of negatives, so -3 is smallest, but we want max)\n   - pop: get -3 -> discount=3\n   - total_discount=1+3=4\n\nTotal cost = sum(P) = 4+3+1=8, minus 4 = 4.  -> matches.\n\nBut is this correct? In the sample, they used:\n- coupon1 (D=3) on item0 (P=4) -> 4-3=1\n- coupon2 (D=1) on item1 (P=3) -> 3-1=2\n- item2: 1\n- total=4\n\nIn our assignment:\n- item1 (P=3): used coupon with D=1\n- item2 (P=4): used coupon with D=3\n- item0 (P=1): no coupon\n\nBut we used the same coupons, same discount sum=4.\n\nThe assignment is different, but total discount is the same.\n\nBut what about the coupon with D=2? We didn't use it, but we used D=1 and D=3, which is better.\n\nSo it's correct.\n\nBut in the algorithm, we used a coupon on item with P=3 and P=4.\n\nBut the coupon with D=3 has L_i=4, so it can only be used on items with P_j>=4.\n\nIn our simulation, when processing item with P=3, we only added coupon with D=1 (L=2<=3), so we used that.\n\nThen for P=4, we added the two coupons with L=4, so we added D=2 and D=3.\n\nThen we used the best available, which is D=3.\n\nBut the coupon with D=3 has L_i=4, and we used it on an item with P=4, which is valid.\n\nSo it's correct.\n\nBut in the algorithm, we are using the coupon with D=3 on the item with P=4, which is fine.\n\nBut what if we had an item with P=5 later? We could have saved it, but since we are processing in increasing order, and the discount is the same regardless of item, it doesn't matter.\n\nAnd because the coupon is eligible for all items with P_j>=4, and we are going from low to high, we can use it on any item from P=4 onward.\n\nAnd since we want to use the best coupons, we should use them as soon as we can, because there's no benefit to saving them.\n\nBut we might want to save a high-discount coupon for a later item if we can use it, but since the discount is the same, and we can only use one per item, it doesn't matter.\n\nHowever, there is a catch: **we might have more eligible coupons than items**, so we want to use the best ones.\n\nAnd by maintaining a max-heap of all eligible coupons so far, and at each item, we use the best available, we ensure that we use the best `k` coupons that are eligible for some item.\n\nBut are we guaranteed that the coupon with high `L_i` will be available when we reach an item with high enough price?\n\nYes, because we add coupons when `L_i <= current item price`, and we process items in increasing order.\n\nAnd we only use a coupon when we pop from the heap.\n\nSo if a coupon has `L_i=4`, we add it when we encounter an item with `P_j>=4`.\n\nAnd if we have multiple items with `P_j>=4`, we add the coupon once (when we first meet `P_j>=4`), and then it stays in the heap for future items.\n\nBut in our algorithm, we add coupons in the loop: we have a pointer `j` that iterates over coupons sorted by `L_i`, and we add a coupon when `L_i <= current item price`.\n\nSo it's added exactly once.\n\nAnd then, when we pop, we use the best discount available.\n\nAnd we do this for each item.\n\nBut we might use a coupon on an item that is not eligible? No, because we only add a coupon when `L_i <= current item price`, and at that point, the current item has `P_j >= L_i`, and all future items have `P_j >= current item price >= L_i`, so the coupon is eligible for all items from this point on.\n\nAnd when we use it, it is used on the current item, which has `P_j >= L_i`, so it's valid.\n\nBut in the algorithm, we are using the coupon on the item where we are in the loop, but we added it because `L_i <= current item price`, so it's eligible.\n\nSo the assignment is valid.\n\nAnd we use the best available discount at each item.\n\nBut is it optimal?\n\nYes, because:\n- We consider coupons in order of increasing `L_i` (via the pointer), so we add them as soon as an item is large enough to use them.\n- We maintain a max-heap of all coupons that can be used on the current or future items.\n- At each item, we have the option to use the best available coupon.\n- Since there's no advantage to saving a coupon (discount is fixed), and we can only use one per item, using the best available coupon on the current item is optimal.\n\nBut what if we skip using a coupon on a cheap item to save it for a later item? But the discount is the same, and we might not have enough items, so it's better to use it now.\n\nActually, no: if we have more coupons than items, we want to use the best ones.\n\nBut in this algorithm, by using a max-heap, we ensure that the best available coupon is used, and since we process all items, we will use the best `min(N, number of eligible coupons)` discounts.\n\nBut we might use a low-discount coupon on a cheap item and save a high-discount for later, but in this algorithm, we use the highest discount available at any item.\n\nAnd because the high-discount coupon will be available for all items after its `L_i` is met, it will be in the heap.\n\nSo when we get to an item, if a high-discount coupon is in the heap, we will use it (if it's the best).\n\nBut we use it on the current item, which is fine.\n\nAnd if there are multiple high-discount coupons, we will use them on different items.\n\nSo it should be correct.\n\nLet's test with sample 2.\n\nSample 2:\n- N=10, M=5\n- P = [9,7,1,5,2,2,5,5,7,6] -> sorted: [1,2,2,5,5,5,6,7,7,9]\n- L = [7,2,7,8,2] -> sorted by L_i: [2,2,7,7,8]\n- D = [3,2,4,1,2] -> corresponding: \n   - L=2,D=2 (from D[1]=2? Wait input: D_1...D_M = 3 2 4 1 2\n\nInput:\nL: 7 2 7 8 2\nD: 3 2 4 1 2\n\nSo coupons:\n0: L=7, D=3\n1: L=2, D=2\n2: L=7, D=4\n3: L=8, D=1\n4: L=2, D=2\n\nSort by L_i:\n- L=2, D=2 (coupon1)\n- L=2, D=2 (coupon4)\n- L=7, D=3 (coupon0)\n- L=7, D=4 (coupon2)\n- L=8, D=1 (coupon3)\n\nNow, sorted P: [1,2,2,5,5,5,6,7,7,9]\n\nProcess:\n\nj=0 (pointer for coupons)\nheap = []\n\nItem: 1\n- L_i <=1? smallest L_i=2>1, so no coupon added.\n- heap empty -> no coupon used.\n\nItem: 2\n- add coupons with L_i<=2: L=2 and L=2 -