      "generated_answer": "Let's carefully analyze the problem step by step.\n\n---\n\n### **Problem Understanding**\n\nWe are given `N` beans. Each bean has:\n- A **deliciousness** value `A_i`\n- A **color** `C_i`\n\nEach bean is of a different **type**, but **you can only distinguish beans by their color**.\n\nYou will:\n1. **Choose one color**\n2. Eat **one bean** of that color (but you cannot tell which one — you can't distinguish between beans of the same color)\n\nSo, if you choose a color that has multiple bean types, you might end up eating **any** of them — you don't know which one.\n\nTherefore, **the worst-case deliciousness** (i.e., the **minimum**) among all beans of that color is what matters.\n\nOur goal:\n> Choose a color such that the **minimum deliciousness** among beans of that color is **as large as possible**.\n\nIn other words:\n- For each color `c`, compute `min_A[c]` = minimum deliciousness among all beans of color `c`.\n- Then, choose the color `c` that **maximizes** this `min_A[c]`.\n- Output that maximum value.\n\n---\n\n### **Example Walkthrough**\n\n#### Sample Input 1:\n```\n4\n100 1\n20 5\n30 5\n40 1\n```\n\nGroup by color:\n- Color 1: beans with deliciousness [100, 40] → min = 40\n- Color 5: beans with deliciousness [20, 30] → min = 20\n\nNow take the maximum of these minimums: `max(40, 20) = 40`\n\nOutput: `40` ✅\n\n#### Sample Input 2:\n```\n10\n68 3\n17 2\n99 2\n92 4\n82 4\n10 3\n100 2\n78 1\n3 1\n35 4\n```\n\nGroup by color:\n- Color 1: [78, 3] → min = 3\n- Color 2: [17, 99, 100] → min = 17\n- Color 3: [68, 10] → min = 10\n- Color 4: [92, 82, 35] → min = 35\n\nNow, `max(3, 17, 10, 35)` = **35**\n\nOutput: `35` ✅\n\n---\n\n### **Algorithm Steps**\n\n1. Read `N`\n2. For each of the `N` lines:\n   - Read `A_i` (deliciousness) and `C_i` (color)\n   - Group by `C_i`, and keep track of the **minimum** `A_i` for each color\n3. After processing all, take the **maximum** of all the per-color minimums\n4. Print that value\n\nWe can use a dictionary to map color → list of deliciousness, or better: just keep the running minimum per color.\n\nBut since we only care about the **minimum per color**, we can:\n- For each color, keep the **minimum** value seen so far.\n\n---\n\n### **Implementation**\n\nWe'll:\n- Use a dictionary to store the minimum deliciousness for each color.\n- Iterate through the inputs.\n- For each color, update the minimum.\n- Finally, take the maximum over all color-minimums.\n\n---\n\n### **Code**\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().splitlines()\n    n = int(data[0])\n    \n    color_min = {}\n    \n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        a, c = map(int, data[i].split())\n        if c not in color_min:\n            color_min[c] = a\n        else:\n            color_min[c] = min(color_min[c], a)\n    \n    result = max(color_min.values())\n    print(result)\n\nsolve()\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Final Answer**\n\nThe function reads from stdin, processes the beans, computes the minimum deliciousness per color, then takes the maximum among them, and prints it.\n\nThis matches the required logic and constraints (efficient for up to 200,000 inputs).\n\n✅ **Correct and efficient solution.**",
