    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_133",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given an integer n.\nA string s is called good if it contains only lowercase English characters and it is possible to rearrange the characters of s such that the new string contains \"leet\" as a substring.\nFor example:\n\nThe string \"lteer\" is good because we can rearrange it to form \"leetr\" .\n\"letl\" is not good because we cannot rearrange it to contain \"leet\" as a substring.\n\nReturn the total number of good strings of length n.\nSince the answer may be large, return it modulo 10^9 + 7.\nA substring is a contiguous sequence of characters within a string.\n \n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: n = 4\nOutput: 12\nExplanation: The 12 strings which can be rearranged to have \"leet\" as a substring are: \"eelt\", \"eetl\", \"elet\", \"elte\", \"etel\", \"etle\", \"leet\", \"lete\", \"ltee\", \"teel\", \"tele\", and \"tlee\".\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: n = 10\nOutput: 83943898\nExplanation: The number of strings with length 10 which can be rearranged to have \"leet\" as a substring is 526083947580. Hence the answer is 526083947580 % (10^9 + 7) = 83943898.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= n <= 10^5",
    "prediction": "gpt-oss-120b",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "**Solution Explanation**\n\nFor a string `s` (only lower‑case English letters) let  \n\n```\ncnt(c) = number of occurrences of character c in s\n```\n\n`s` is *good* iff we can permute its characters and obtain a string that\ncontains the word **leet** as a contiguous block.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 1.   When is a string good ?\n\n`leet` needs the multiset `{ l , t , e , e }`.  \nTherefore a string can be rearranged to contain `leet` **iff**\n\n```\ncnt('l') ≥ 1 ,   cnt('t') ≥ 1 ,   cnt('e') ≥ 2                (1)\n```\n\nIf (1) holds we simply put one `l`, two `e` and one `t` together\n(`leet`) and place all remaining letters arbitrarily before or after the\nblock – a permutation always exists.\nSo the condition is *necessary and sufficient*.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 2.   Counting with Inclusion – Exclusion  \n\nAll strings of length `n` over 26 letters:  \n\n```\nTOTAL = 26^n\n```\n\nDefine the “bad” properties  \n\n```\nA : cnt('l') = 0          (no letter l)\nB : cnt('t') = 0          (no letter t)\nC : cnt('e') ≤ 1         (zero or one e)\n```\n\nA string is good **iff** it satisfies none of `A , B , C`.\nWe need  \n\n```\nGOOD = TOTAL – |A ∪ B ∪ C|\n```\n\nand we obtain `|A ∪ B ∪ C|` by the inclusion–exclusion principle.\n\nAll needed numbers are simple powers of the alphabet size after removing\nsome letters.\n\n| set                     | allowed letters | count formula                                   |\n|-------------------------|----------------|-------------------------------------------------|\n| A                       | 25 (all except `l`) | `25^n` |\n| B                       | 25 (all except `t`) | `25^n` |\n| C (zero e)              | 25 (all except `e`) | `25^n` |\n| C (exactly one e)       | choose position of the e ( `n` ways ) and fill the rest with 25 letters | `n·25^{n‑1}` |\n| **C** (union of both)   | `25^n + n·25^{n‑1}` |\n| A ∩ B                   | 24 (except `l` and `t`) | `24^n` |\n| A ∩ C (no `l`, ≤1 `e`) | 25 letters, `e` is special | `24^n + n·24^{n‑1}` |\n| B ∩ C (no `t`, ≤1 `e`) | same as A ∩ C | `24^n + n·24^{n‑1}` |\n| A ∩ B ∩ C (no `l`,`t`, ≤1 `e`) | 24 letters, `e` is special | `23^n + n·23^{n‑1}` |\n\nNow\n\n```\nGOOD = 26^n\n       – ( |A| + |B| + |C| )\n       + ( |A∩B| + |A∩C| + |B∩C| )\n       – |A∩B∩C|\n```\n\nAll terms are computed modulo `M = 1 000 000 007`.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.   Algorithm\n```\npow26 = 26^n                     (mod M)\npow25 = 25^n\npow24 = 24^n\npow23 = 23^n\n\npow25_1 = 25^{n‑1}\npow24_1 = 24^{n‑1}\npow23_1 = 23^{n‑1}\n\nC   = (pow25 + n·pow25_1)                mod M\nAC  = (pow24 + n·pow24_1)                mod M   # = BC\nABC = (pow23 + n·pow23_1)                mod M\n\nans =  pow26\n       – ( 2·pow25 + C )\n       + ( pow24 + AC + AC )\n       – ABC\nans = ans mod M (add M twice before final % to avoid negativity)\nreturn ans\n```\n\nAll exponentiations are performed by Python’s built‑in `pow(base, exp,\nmod)` → `O(log n)` time. The whole algorithm is `O(log n)` time,\n`O(1)` memory.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.   Correctness Proof  \n\nWe prove that the algorithm returns the number of good strings of length\n`n`.\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 1  \nA string of length `n` is good **iff** it satisfies condition (1):\n`cnt('l')≥1 , cnt('t')≥1 , cnt('e')≥2`.\n\n**Proof.**  \n*If* the condition holds, we can place the letters `l e e t` consecutively\nforming the substring `leet`; the remaining `n‑4` letters can be placed\nanywhere, giving a permutation that contains `leet`.  \n*Only if* the string could be rearranged to contain `leet`, the rearranged\nstring obviously contains at least one `l`, one `t` and two `e`’s, thus the\noriginal string contains them as well. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 2  \nFor every set listed in the table of Section&nbsp;2 the given formula equals\nthe number of length‑`n` strings that satisfy the corresponding\nrestrictions.\n\n**Proof.**  \nAll formulas are straightforward counts:\n\n* `25^n` – each position may be filled with any of the 25 allowed letters.\n* `n·25^{n‑1}` – choose the unique position of a distinguished letter\n  (the only `e`), the other positions use any of the 25 remaining letters.\n* For intersections we simply remove the forbidden letters from the\n  alphabet and repeat the same reasoning, distinguishing the case “zero\n  special letters’’ and “exactly one special letter”. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 3  \nLet `BAD = |A ∪ B ∪ C|`. The expression computed by the algorithm equals\n`TOTAL – BAD`.\n\n**Proof.**  \nBy Lemma&nbsp;2 each term in the inclusion–exclusion formula is exactly the\nquantity used by the algorithm.\nSubstituting those values into\n\n```\nGOOD = TOTAL – (|A|+|B|+|C|) + (|A∩B|+|A∩C|+|B∩C|) – |A∩B∩C|\n```\n\ngives the expression implemented. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Lemma 4  \n`GOOD` computed by the algorithm equals the number of good strings.\n\n**Proof.**  \nFrom Lemma&nbsp;1 a string is good precisely when it does **not** satisfy\nany of the properties `A, B, C`.  \nHence the set of good strings is the complement of `A ∪ B ∪ C`.  \nTherefore its cardinality is `TOTAL – |A ∪ B ∪ C|`.  \nBy Lemma&nbsp;3 the algorithm computes exactly this value. ∎\n\n\n\n##### Theorem  \nFor every integer `n (1 ≤ n ≤ 10^5)` the function `good_strings(n)` returned\nby the algorithm equals the number of good strings of length `n`\n(modulo `10^9+7`).\n\n**Proof.**  \nDirectly from Lemma&nbsp;4 the algorithm produces the exact (non‑modular)\ncount. All operations are performed modulo `M`, and modular arithmetic\npreserves equality of integers modulo `M`. Hence the returned value is the\ndesired answer modulo `M`. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.   Complexity Analysis  \n\n*Exponentiations*: `pow` needs `O(log n)` time each, four of them\n(`26,25,24,23`).  \nAll other operations are constant time.\n\n```\nTime   : O(log n)\nMemory : O(1)\n```\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 6.   Reference Implementation  (Python 3)\n\n```python\nMOD = 10 ** 9 + 7\n\ndef good_strings(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Return the number of length‑n strings (lowercase English letters)\n    that can be rearranged to contain the substring 'leet'.\n    Result is given modulo 1_000_000_007.\n    \"\"\"\n    # powers\n    pow26 = pow(26, n, MOD)\n    pow25 = pow(25, n, MOD)\n    pow24 = pow(24, n, MOD)\n    pow23 = pow(23, n, MOD)\n\n    # n‑1 powers (n ≥ 1 by constraints)\n    pow25_1 = pow(25, n - 1, MOD)\n    pow24_1 = pow(24, n - 1, MOD)\n    pow23_1 = pow(23, n - 1, MOD)\n\n    # |C|, |A∩C| (=|B∩C|), |A∩B∩C|\n    C   = (pow25 + n * pow25_1) % MOD\n    AC  = (pow24 + n * pow24_1) % MOD          # also BC\n    ABC = (pow23 + n * pow23_1) % MOD\n\n    # inclusion–exclusion\n    ans = pow26\n    ans = (ans - (2 * pow25 + C) ) % MOD\n    ans = (ans + (pow24 + AC + AC) ) % MOD\n    ans = (ans - ABC) % MOD\n\n    # make sure the result is non‑negative\n    return ans % MOD\n```\n\nThe function follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above and\nconforms to the required output format.",
