      "generated_answer": "I need to determine whether any two chords intersect on a circle with 2N points.\n\nLet me think about when two chords intersect on a circle:\n- Two chords (A,B) and (C,D) intersect if and only if the four endpoints are distinct and they alternate around the circle.\n- This happens when one chord has endpoints in different \"arcs\" created by the other chord.\n\nFor chords (a,b) and (c,d), they intersect if and only if the four points are distinct and when we traverse the circle, the endpoints alternate (e.g., a, c, b, d or a, d, b, c in circular order).\n\nA simpler way to check: for two chords (a,b) and (c,d) where a < b and c < d, they intersect if and only if:\n- a < c < b < d, or\n- c < a < d < b\n\nBut since we're on a circle, I need to be careful about the ordering. A better approach is to normalize each chord so that we consider the smaller endpoint first, then check if the intervals overlap in a crossing way.\n\nActually, let me use this approach:\n1. For each chord, store the two endpoints with the smaller one first.\n2. Sort the chords by their first endpoint.\n3. For each pair of chords, check if they intersect using the crossing condition.\n\nTwo chords (a,b) and (c,d) with a < b and c < d intersect if and only if:\na < c < b < d or c < a < d < b\n\nBut since we're on a circle with points numbered consecutively, this linear interval approach works because we can think of the circle as a line from 1 to 2N, and the chords either cross within this range or wrap around. However, the condition above works for the non-wrapping case.\n\nWait, I need to handle the circular nature correctly. Actually, the condition still holds because if we ensure a < b and c < d, then the intersection condition is simply that the intervals cross: one endpoint of each chord lies within the interval of the other chord.\n\nMore precisely: chords (a,b) and (c,d) with a < b and c < d intersect if and only if (a < c < b < d) or (c < a < d < b).\n\nLet me verify with the examples:\n\nExample 1: N=3, chords (1,3), (4,2), (5,6)\n- Normalize: (1,3), (2,4), (5,6)\n- Check (1,3) and (2,4): 1 < 2 < 3 < 4 → true, so they intersect. Answer: Yes ✓\n\nExample 2: N=3, chords (6,1), (4,3), (2,5)\n- Normalize: (1,6), (3,4), (2,5)\n- Sort by first: (1,6), (2,5), (3,4)\n- Check (1,6) and (2,5): 1 < 2 < 6 < 5? No, 6 > 5. But 1 < 2 < 5 < 6, so 1 < 2 < 5 < 6 which means 2,5 is contained in 1,6 but they don't cross.\n- For intersection: we need a < c < b < d or c < a < d < b\n- (1,6) and (2,5): a=1,b=6,c=2,d=5. Is 1 < 2 < 6 < 5? No (6 > 5). Is 2 < 1 < 5 < 6? No (2 > 1). So no intersection.\n- (1,6) and (3,4): 1 < 3 < 6 < 4? No. 3 < 1 < 4 < 6? No.\n- (2,5) and (3,4): 2 < 3 < 5 < 4? No. 3 < 2 < 4 < 5? No.\n- No intersections. Answer: No ✓\n\nExample 3: N=4, chords (2,4), (3,7), (8,6), (5,1)\n- Normalize: (2,4), (3,7), (6,8), (1,5)\n- Sort by first: (1,5), (2,4), (3,7), (6,8)\n- Check pairs:\n  - (1,5) and (2,4): 1 < 2 < 5 < 4? No. 2 < 1 < 4 < 5? No.\n  - (1,5) and (3,7): 1 < 3 < 5 < 7? Yes! 1 < 3 < 5 < 7, so 1 < 3 < 5 < 7 means a < c < b < d where a=1,b=5,c=3,d=7. So they intersect. Answer: Yes ✓\n\nThe algorithm:\n1. Read N\n2. Read N chords\n3. For each chord, store as (min(a,b), max(a,b))\n4. Sort chords by the first coordinate\n5. Check all pairs of chords to see if they intersect using the condition\n6. If any pair intersects, print \"Yes\", else \"No\"\n\nHowever, with N up to 2×10^5, checking all pairs O(N^2) might be too slow.\n\nI need a more efficient approach.\n\nAlternative approach:\n- Use a sweep line or interval tree\n- Or use the fact that chords intersect if their endpoints alternate\n\nEven better: I can use the fact that two chords (a,b) and (c,d) intersect if and only if the four points are distinct and exactly one of c,d lies between a and b (in circular order).\n\nBut since we've normalized to a < b and c < d, and assuming no wrapping issues in our comparison, the condition a < c < b < d or c < a < d < b works.\n\nWait, there's a more efficient way:\n- Sort all endpoints and use a stack or similar structure.\n\nActually, let me think of this as interval intersection in a circular way, but there's a well-known solution:\nTwo chords (a,b) and (c,d) intersect if and only if the points a, c, b, d are in alternating order around the circle.\n\nA simpler O(N^2) solution might work if N is small, but N can be up to 2×10^5, so O(N^2) is 4×10^10 operations, which is too slow.\n\nI need O(N log N).\n\nBetter approach:\n- Consider all 2N points in order around the circle.\n- As we traverse the circle, when we encounter the start of a chord, we push it onto a stack.\n- When we encounter the end of a chord, we check if it matches the top of the stack.\n- If not, then chords intersect.\n\nActually, this is similar to checking for valid parentheses or nested intervals.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Create events for each point: for chord (a,b), mark that point a and b belong to the same chord.\n2. Traverse points 1 to 2N in order.\n3. Use a stack: when we see the first endpoint of a chord, push the chord id.\n4. When we see the second endpoint, if it doesn't match the top of the stack, then there's an intersection.\n\nMore precisely:\n- For each chord, we don't know which endpoint we'll encounter first as we go around the circle.\n- But we can process in order of point index.\n- When we encounter a point that is part of a chord, if we haven't seen the other endpoint, we push this chord.\n- When we encounter the second endpoint of a chord, we pop from the stack, and it must be the same chord, otherwise chords are crossing.\n\nActually, the standard algorithm is:\n- Represent each chord by its two endpoints.\n- Traverse the circle in order.\n- Use a stack to keep track of \"open\" chords (chords for which we've seen one endpoint but not the other).\n- When we see the first endpoint of a chord, push it onto the stack.\n- When we see the second endpoint, it must match the top of the stack; if not, chords intersect.\n\nBut we need to know which endpoint is first. Since we're going in numerical order 1,2,3,...,2N, for a chord (a,b) with a < b, we'll see a first, then b.\n\nSo:\n1. For each chord, store min(a,b) as start, max(a,b) as end.\n2. Create events: at position min(a,b) - start chord i, at position max(a,b) - end chord i.\n3. Process positions 1 to 2N:\n   - If position has a start event, push chord id onto stack.\n   - If position has an end event, pop from stack. If the popped chord id is not the expected one, then chords intersect.\n\nActually, when we reach an end point, we check if the top of the stack is the chord that is ending. If not, then there's an intersection.\n\nMore precisely: when we reach the end point of chord i, we check if chord i is at the top of the stack. If not, then there's an intersection.\n\nBut we need to know which chord is ending. So:\n- At each position, we may have a chord starting or ending.\n- We maintain a stack of active chords (chords that have started but not ended).\n- When a chord ends, it must be the most recently started chord that hasn't ended (i.e., top of stack), otherwise chords cross.\n\nYes! This is the key insight: chords do not intersect if and only if they are properly nested, like parentheses.\n\nSo algorithm:\n1. For i in range(N): read chord, store as (min(a,b), max(a,b), i)\n2. Create an array \"event\" of size 2N+1, where event[p] tells us what happens at point p\n3. For each chord (s,e,i): event[s] = ('start', i), event[e] = ('end', i)\n4. stack = []\n5. for pos in range(1, 2N+1):\n   - if event[pos][0] == 'start': push event[pos][1] onto stack\n   - else: # 'end'\n     - if stack is empty or stack.pop() != event[pos][1]: then chords intersect\n6. If we complete the loop, no intersection.\n\nWait, when we get an end event for chord i, we need to pop and check if it's chord i. But we pop the top, so we need to check if the top of stack is chord i.\n\nActually:\n- When we see end of chord i, we check if i is at the top of the stack.\n- If yes, pop it.\n- If no, then some other chord is on top, meaning chords cross.\n\nBut we can't check the top without popping. So:\n- if stack is empty or stack[-1] != i: return \"Yes\" (intersection)\n- else: stack.pop()\n\nLet me test with examples.\n\nExample 1: N=3, chords (1,3), (4,2), (5,6)\n- Normalize: (1,3,0), (2,4,1), (5,6,2)\n- Events:\n  - pos 1: start chord 0\n  - pos 2: start chord 1  \n  - pos 3: end chord 0\n  - pos 4: end chord 1\n  - pos 5: start chord 2\n  - pos 6: end chord 2\n\n- pos 1: stack=[0]\n- pos 2: stack=[0,1]\n- pos 3: end chord 0, stack[-1]=1 != 0? Yes, 1 != 0, so intersection! Answer: Yes ✓\n\nExample 2: N=3, chords (6,1), (4,3), (2,5)\n- Normalize: (1,6,0), (3,4,1), (2,5,2)\n- Events:\n  - pos 1: start 0\n  - pos 2: start 2\n  - pos 3: start 1\n  - pos 4: end 1\n  - pos 5: end 2\n  - pos 6: end 0\n\n- pos 1: stack=[0]\n- pos 2: stack=[0,2]\n- pos 3: stack=[0,2,1]\n- pos 4: end chord 1, stack[-1]=1 == 1, so pop → stack=[0,2]\n- pos 5: end chord 2, stack[-1]=2 == 2, pop → stack=[0]\n- pos 6: end chord 0, stack[-1]=0 == 0, pop → stack=[]\n- No intersection found, so answer: No ✓\n\nExample 3: N=4, chords (2,4), (3,7), (8,6), (5,1)\n- Normalize: (2,4,0), (3,7,1), (6,8,2), (1,5,3)\n- Events:\n  - pos 1: start 3\n  - pos 2: start 0\n  - pos 3: start 1\n  - pos 4: end 0\n  - pos 5: end 3\n  - pos 6: start 2\n  - pos 7: end 1\n  - pos 8: end 2\n\n- pos 1: stack=[3]\n- pos 2: stack=[3,0]\n- pos 3: stack=[3,0,1]\n- pos 4: end chord 0, stack[-1]=1 != 0 → intersection! Answer: Yes ✓\n\nPerfect.\n\nNow, implement this.\n\nSteps:\n1. Read N\n2. chords = []\n3. For i in range(N):\n   - read a, b\n   - s, e = min(a,b), max(a,b)\n   - chords.append((s,e,i))\n4. Create events array of size 2N+1, initialize with None or ('none', -1)\n5. For each chord (s,e,i):\n   - events[s] = ('start', i)\n   - events[e] = ('end', i)\n6. stack = []\n7. for pos in range(1, 2N+1):\n   - if events[pos][0] == 'start':\n     - stack.append(events[pos][1])\n   - else: # 'end'\n     - if not stack or stack[-1] != events[pos][1]:\n       - print(\"Yes\")\n       - exit\n     - else:\n       - stack.pop()\n8. If we get here, print \"No\"\n\nBut what if events[pos] is not set? I should initialize events properly.\n\nSince all points from 1 to 2N are used (because all A_i, B_i are distinct and cover 2N points), every position has an event.\n\nActually, the constraint says \"all the values A_1,…,A_N,B_1,…,B_N are distinct\", and there are 2N of them, so they are exactly the points 1 to 2N in some order. So every position 1 to 2N has exactly one endpoint.\n\nSo I can proceed.\n\nLet me code accordingly.\n\nNote: I need to be careful with array indexing.\n\nI'll implement accordingly.\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not data:\n        return\n    \n    n = int(data[0])\n    chords = []\n    index = 1\n    \n    for i in range(n):\n        a = int(data[index]); b = int(data[index+1]); index += 2\n        s = min(a, b)\n        e = max(a, b)\n        chords.append((s, e, i))\n    \n    # Create events array, size 2n+1, index 0 unused, 1 to 2n used\n    events = [None] * (2 * n + 1)  # events[i] = (type, chord_id)\n    \n    for s, e, i in chords:\n        events[s] = ('start', i)\n        events[e] = ('end', i)\n    \n    stack = []\n    \n    for pos in range(1, 2 * n + 1):\n        event_type, chord_id = events[pos]\n        if event_type == 'start':\n            stack.append(chord_id)\n        else:  # 'end'\n            if not stack:\n                print(\"Yes\")\n                return\n            if stack[-1] != chord_id:\n                print(\"Yes\")\n                return\n            stack.pop()  # remove the top\n    \n    print(\"No\")\n\nsolve()\n```\n\nWait, let me double-check the logic.\n\nWhen we have an end event for chord_id, we check if the top of the stack is chord_id. If not, it means some other chord is on top, so chords must intersect.\n\nYes, that's correct.\n\nIn example 1: at pos 3, we have end chord 0, but stack is [0,1], so stack[-1] is 1, which is not 0, so we print \"Yes\".\n\nPerfect.\n\nI think this is correct.\n\nLet me run through a simple non-intersecting case: two chords (1,2) and (3,4)\n- events: pos1: start 0, pos2: end 0, pos3: start 1, pos4: end 1\n- pos1: stack=[0]\n- pos2: end 0, stack[-1]=0 == 0, pop → stack=[]\n- pos3: stack=[1]\n- pos4: end 1, stack[-1]=1 == 1, pop → []\n- print \"No\" ✓\n\nAnother intersecting: (1,3) and (2,4)\n- events: pos1: start 0, pos2: start 1, pos3: end 0, pos4: end 1\n- pos1: stack=[0]\n- pos2: stack=[0,1]\n- pos3: end 0, stack[-1]=1 != 0 → \"Yes\" ✓\n\nPerfect.\n\nNow, write the code accordingly.\n\nNote: I need to make sure that when I access events[pos], it's not None, but by problem constraints, every position 1 to 2N has an event, so it should be safe.\n\nI'll write the code as planned.\n\nOne more thing: the chord indices are from 0 to N-1, which is fine.\n\nFinal code:\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    n = int(data[0])\n    chords = []\n    index = 1\n    \n    for i in range(n):\n        a = int(data[index])\n        b = int(data[index + 1])\n        index += 2\n        s = min(a, b)\n        e = max(a, b)\n        chords.append((s, e, i))\n    \n    events = [None] * (2 * n + 1)\n    \n    for s, e, i in chords:\n        events[s] = ('start', i)\n        events[e] = ('end', i)\n    \n    stack = []\n    \n    for pos in range(1, 2 * n + 1):\n        typ, cid = events[pos]\n        if typ == 'start':\n            stack.append(cid)\n        else:  # 'end'\n            if not stack or stack[-1] != cid:\n                print(\"Yes\")\n                return\n            stack.pop()\n    \n    print(\"No\")\n\nsolve()\n```",
