      "generated_answer": "I need to implement a function that calculates the ceiling of X/10 for a given integer X.\n\nThe ceiling function ⌈a⌉ returns the smallest integer that is not less than a. For positive numbers, this means rounding up to the next integer if there's a fractional part. For negative numbers, I need to be careful - the ceiling is the smallest integer that is still greater than or equal to the value.\n\nLet me think through the examples:\n- X = 27: 27/10 = 2.7, ceiling is 3\n- X = -13: -13/10 = -1.3, ceiling is -1 (since -1 > -1.3 and -2 < -1.3)\n- X = 40: 40/10 = 4.0, ceiling is 4\n- X = -20: -20/10 = -2.0, ceiling is -2\n\nFor positive X, if X is divisible by 10, the result is X//10, otherwise it's X//10 + 1.\nFor negative X, if X is divisible by 10, the result is X//10, otherwise it's X//10 + 1 (because in Python, floor division rounds down, but we need the ceiling).\n\nActually, let me reconsider: in Python, for negative numbers, // performs floor division (rounds down). But for ceiling division, I can use a formula.\n\nThe ceiling of X/10 can be calculated as:\n- If X >= 0: (X + 9) // 10\n- If X < 0: X // 10\n\nWait, let me check with the examples:\n- X = 27: (27 + 9) // 10 = 36 // 10 = 3 ✓\n- X = -13: -13 // 10 = -2 (but expected is -1) ✗\n\nThat's not right. Let me reconsider.\n\nFor negative numbers: -13/10 = -1.3, ceiling is -1.\nIn Python, -13 // 10 = -2 (floor division).\n\nI can use the mathematical property: ⌈a⌉ = -⌊-a⌋\n\nSo ⌈X/10⌉ = -⌊-X/10⌋\n\nLet me verify:\n- X = 27: -⌊-27/10⌋ = -⌊-2.7⌋ = -(-3) = 3 ✓\n- X = -13: -⌊13/10⌋ = -⌊1.3⌋ = -(1) = -1 ✓\n- X = 40: -⌊-40/10⌋ = -⌊-4⌋ = -(-4) = 4 ✓\n- X = -20: -⌊20/10⌋ = -⌊2⌋ = -2 ✓\n\nPerfect! This formula works for all cases.\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    X = int(sys.stdin.readline().strip())\n    \n    # Calculate ceiling of X/10 using the formula: ceil(a) = -floor(-a)\n    result = -(-X // 10)\n    \n    print(result)\n\nsolve()\n```",
