    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "I need to find the total area covered by one or more rectangular sheets on a coordinate plane, accounting for overlaps.\n\nThis is a classic \"area of union of rectangles\" problem. I'll use a coordinate compression approach:\n\n1. Collect all unique x-coordinates and y-coordinates from the rectangles\n2. Sort them to create a grid\n3. For each cell in the grid, check if it's covered by any rectangle\n4. Sum up the areas of all covered cells\n\nLet me trace through the first example:\n- Rectangle 1: (0,1) to (5,3) - area = 5×2 = 10\n- Rectangle 2: (1,0) to (4,5) - area = 3×5 = 15  \n- Rectangle 3: (2,2) to (5,4) - area = 3×2 = 6\n\nBut they overlap, so I can't just sum the areas. Using coordinate compression:\n\n```python\ndef solve():\n    n = int(input())\n    rectangles = []\n    for _ in range(n):\n        a, b, c, d = map(int, input().split())\n        rectangles.append((a, b, c, d))\n    \n    # Get all unique x and y coordinates\n    x_coords = set()\n    y_coords = set()\n    \n    for a, b, c, d in rectangles:\n        x_coords.add(a)\n        x_coords.add(b)\n        y_coords.add(c)\n        y_coords.add(d)\n    \n    x_coords = sorted(x_coords)\n    y_coords = sorted(y_coords)\n    \n    total_area = 0\n    \n    # Check each cell in the grid\n    for i in range(len(x_coords) - 1):\n        for j in range(len(y_coords) - 1):\n            x1, x2 = x_coords[i], x_coords[i + 1]\n            y1, y2 = y_coords[j], y_coords[j + 1]\n            \n            # Check if this cell is covered by any rectangle\n            covered = False\n            for a, b, c, d in rectangles:\n                if a <= x1 and x2 <= b and c <= y1 and y2 <= d:\n                    covered = True\n                    break\n            \n            if covered:\n                total_area += (x2 - x1) * (y2 - y1)\n    \n    print(total_area)\n\nsolve()\n```",
