    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's analyze the game step by step to determine who wins **if both players play optimally**.\n\n---\n\n### **Game Rules Recap**:\n\n- Starting number: `n`\n- Two players: Vanya (First), Vova (Second)\n- On a player's turn, they can:\n  - Add 1, or\n  - Subtract 1\n- Vanya goes first.\n- **Vanya wins immediately** if **after his move**, the number is divisible by 3.\n- The game lasts at most **10 moves total** (5 moves per player, since they alternate).\n- If Vanya hasn't won within 10 moves, **Vova wins**.\n\nSo the game ends **as soon as** after Vanya’s move the number is divisible by 3 → Vanya wins.\n\nOtherwise, after 10 moves (i.e., 5 turns each), Vova wins.\n\nWe are to determine: **who wins if both play optimally?**\n\n---\n\n### **Key Observations**:\n\n1. Only **Vanya** can win by making the number divisible by 3 **after his move**.\n2. **Vova** wants to **prevent** this from happening in all of Vanya’s 5 turns.\n3. Vanya makes moves on turns: 1st, 3rd, 5th, 7th, 9th (i.e., odd-numbered moves).\n4. Vova makes moves on turns: 2nd, 4th, 6th, 8th, 10th.\n5. Each move changes the number by ±1.\n\nLet’s denote:\n- After move 1 (Vanya): number becomes `n ± 1` → check if divisible by 3\n- After move 2 (Vova): number becomes `(n ± 1) ± 1`\n- After move 3 (Vanya): another ±1, etc.\n\nBut since both play **optimally**:\n- Vanya wants to **reach a multiple of 3** after his move as soon as possible.\n- Vova wants to **avoid** that at all costs.\n\nWe need to determine: **Can Vanya force a win within 5 of his moves, no matter how Vova plays?**\n\n---\n\n### **Strategy: Work with modulo 3**\n\nLet’s consider the value of `n mod 3`.\n\nLet’s define the state by:\n- Current number mod 3\n- Whose turn it is\n- How many moves have been made\n\nBut since the game is small (only up to 10 moves), and `n` is small, we can **analyze all possibilities**.\n\nBut let’s think smarter.\n\nLet’s denote the game state as:\n- `current value mod 3`\n- `move number` (from 1 to 10)\n- But note: players can only change value by ±1, so mod 3 changes in a predictable way.\n\nLet’s consider what Vanya can do on **his first move**.\n\n#### Case 1: Can Vanya win on first move?\n\nAfter his move, number becomes `n+1` or `n-1`.\n\nSo if **either `n+1` or `n-1` is divisible by 3**, then Vanya can choose that and win immediately.\n\nSo:\n- If `(n+1) % 3 == 0` → `n % 3 == 2`\n- If `(n-1) % 3 == 0` → `n % 3 == 1`\n\nSo:\n- If `n % 3 == 1`: then `n-1` is divisible by 3 → Vanya chooses `-1` → wins.\n- If `n % 3 == 2`: then `n+1` is divisible by 3 → Vanya chooses `+1` → wins.\n- If `n % 3 == 0`: then `n` is divisible by 3, but **before Vanya moves**. He must move to `n+1` or `n-1`. Both are:\n  - `n+1 ≡ 1 mod 3`\n  - `n-1 ≡ 2 mod 3`\n  - Neither is 0 → so **he cannot win on first move**.\n\nSo:\n- If `n % 3 == 0`, Vanya **cannot win on first move**.\n\nNow, what happens next?\n\nLet’s consider each case.\n\n---\n\n### **Case Analysis**\n\n#### **Case A: n % 3 == 1 or 2 → Vanya can win on first move**\n\n→ So he **will win**, no matter what.\n\n→ Output: `\"First\"`\n\n#### **Case B: n % 3 == 0**\n\nThen Vanya **cannot win on first move**.\n\nAfter his move, number becomes either `n+1` or `n-1`, so value mod 3 is either 1 or 2.\n\nLet’s suppose Vanya chooses one of them. Without loss of generality, say he moves to `n+1` → value ≡ 1 mod 3.\n\nNow it's Vova’s turn. Current number ≡ 1 mod 3.\n\nVova can move to:\n- `n+2` → ≡ 2 mod 3\n- `n` → ≡ 0 mod 3\n\nBut if Vova moves to `n`, then on Vanya’s next turn, Vanya can move to `n±1`, and again try to make it divisible by 3.\n\nBut wait — Vova **does not win** by making it divisible by 3 — only Vanya wins if **after his own move** it's divisible by 3.\n\nSo Vova can safely move to `n` (≡ 0), because it's not after Vanya’s move.\n\nBut now the number is back to `n`, and it's Vanya’s turn again — same as initial state.\n\nWait — but moves are sequential and values change.\n\nLet’s simulate this carefully.\n\nLet’s suppose `n = 3` (so divisible by 3).\n\n- Move 1 (Vanya): can go to 2 or 4.\n  - 2 % 3 = 2 → not divisible → no win\n  - 4 % 3 = 1 → not divisible → no win\n  → Suppose he goes to 2.\n\n- Move 2 (Vova): from 2, can go to 1 or 3.\n  - If Vova goes to 3 (≡ 0), then next:\n    - Move 3 (Vanya): from 3 → can go to 2 or 4 → neither divisible by 3 → no win\n  - If Vova goes to 1 (≡ 1), then:\n    - Move 3 (Vanya): from 1 → can go to 0 or 2\n      - 0 % 3 = 0 → if he goes to 0, he wins!\n\nAh! So if Vova moves to 1, Vanya can win on his second move.\n\nSo Vova **must not** let that happen.\n\nSo from 2, if Vanya went to 2, Vova must go to 3 (not 1), to prevent Vanya from winning.\n\nSo after:\n- Vanya: 3 → 2\n- Vova: 2 → 3\n- Now back to 3, Vanya’s turn again.\n\nNow same as start! But we’ve used 2 moves.\n\nNow:\n- Move 3 (Vanya): again from 3 → must go to 2 or 4\n  - Suppose he goes to 2 again\n- Move 4 (Vova): from 2 → can go to 1 or 3\n  - Again, going to 1 allows Vanya to win next (1→0), so Vova must go to 3\n\nSo again: 2 → 3\n\nThis can repeat.\n\nBut note: the game only lasts 10 moves.\n\nLet’s count:\n\n- Move 1: Vanya: 3→2\n- Move 2: Vova: 2→3\n- Move 3: Vanya: 3→2\n- Move 4: Vova: 2→3\n- Move 5: Vanya: 3→2\n- Move 6: Vova: 2→3\n- Move 7: Vanya: 3→2\n- Move 8: Vova: 2→3\n- Move 9: Vanya: 3→2\n- Move 10: Vova: 2→3\n\nAfter 10 moves, Vanya never made the number divisible by 3 → Vova wins.\n\nBut wait — is there any way for Vanya to win in this loop?\n\nOnly if at some point after his move the number is divisible by 3.\n\nBut he only moves from 3 to 2 or 4 → both not divisible.\n\nOr from other values?\n\nSuppose on first move, Vanya goes to 4 (instead of 2).\n\n- n = 3 → Vanya: 3→4\n- Vova: from 4 → can go to 3 or 5\n  - If Vova goes to 5 (≡ 2), then Vanya’s turn: from 5 → can go to 4 or 6\n    - 6 % 3 = 0 → if Vanya goes to 6, he wins!\n  → So Vova **cannot** go to 5.\n\nSo Vova must go back to 3.\n\nSo again:\n- Vanya: 3→4\n- Vova: 4→3\n- Back to start.\n\nSo again, cycle.\n\nSo **no matter what Vanya does on his first move (when n % 3 == 0)**, Vova can **force a return to n**, preventing Vanya from winning.\n\nAnd this can continue for all 10 moves.\n\nSo Vanya **cannot win** if `n % 3 == 0`.\n\n→ Vova wins → Output `\"Second\"`\n\nWait — but is this always true?\n\nLet’s test with `n = 3` (from sample input):\n\nSample:\n```\nInput: 3 → Output: \"Second\"\n```\n\nYes! Matches.\n\nAnother: `n = 999` → 999 % 3 = 0 → output \"Second\" → matches sample.\n\nSo our hypothesis:\n\n- If `n % 3 == 0` → Vova wins → \"Second\"\n- Else → Vanya wins on first move → \"First\"\n\nBut wait — what if Vanya cannot win on first move, but can win on **later** moves?\n\nWe assumed that if `n % 3 == 1 or 2`, Vanya can win **immediately**.\n\nYes: because he can move to `n-1` or `n+1` to make it divisible by 3.\n\nFor example:\n- n = 1 → 1 % 3 = 1 → Vanya can do 1 - 1 = 0 → 0 % 3 = 0 → win\n- n = 5 → 5 % 3 = 2 → Vanya can do 5 + 1 = 6 → divisible by 3 → win\n- n = 100 → 100 % 3 = 1 → 100 - 1 = 99 → divisible by 3 → win\n- n = 1000 → 1000 % 3 = 1 (since 999 is divisible, 1000 ≡ 1) → 1000 - 1 = 999 → divisible → win\n\nAnd:\n- n = 3 → 3 % 3 = 0 → Vanya cannot win on first move → and as we saw, Vova can block forever → \"Second\"\n- n = 999 → 999 % 3 = 0 → \"Second\"\n\nSo all sample outputs match:\n\n```\n1  → 1%3=1 → First\n3  → 0   → Second\n5  → 2   → First\n100→ 1   → First\n999→ 0   → Second\n1000→1   → First\n```\n\nPerfect.\n\nBut wait — could Vanya win **later** even if he doesn't win on first move?\n\nOnly if `n % 3 == 0`, because otherwise he already wins on first move.\n\nAnd when `n % 3 == 0`, we saw that Vova can always force a return to a multiple of 3 on his turn, preventing Vanya from ever creating a multiple of 3 **after his own move**.\n\nBut let’s test a deeper possibility.\n\nSuppose `n = 3`:\n\n- Vanya moves to 4 (≡1)\n- Vova **must not** go to 5 (≡2), because then Vanya can go to 6 (≡0) and win.\n- So Vova goes back to 3.\n\nSame for any such case.\n\nSo Vova can always **reverse** Vanya’s move.\n\nSo if Vanya increases, Vova decreases; if Vanya decreases, Vova increases.\n\nSo after two moves, back to `n`.\n\nSo the number after Vova’s move is always `n` (a multiple of 3).\n\nSo Vanya always faces a multiple of 3 on his turn.\n\nSo his options: ±1 → result ≡ 1 or 2 mod 3 → never 0 → so he never wins.\n\nAnd this continues for 5 rounds → Vova wins.\n\nSo **if n % 3 == 0, Vova wins**.\n\nOtherwise, Vanya wins **on first move**.\n\nSo the solution is simply:\n\n```python\nif n % 3 == 0:\n    print(\"Second\")\nelse:\n    print(\"First\")\n```\n\nBut wait — is it possible that for some `n % 3 != 0`, Vanya **cannot** win on first move, but wins later?\n\nNo — because if `n % 3 == 1`, then `n - 1` is divisible by 3 → Vanya can choose to subtract 1 and win.\n\nSimilarly, if `n % 3 == 2`, `n + 1` is divisible by 3 → he adds 1 and wins.\n\nSo he **always** has a winning move on first turn if `n % 3 != 0`.\n\nTherefore, the answer only depends on `n % 3`.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Final Logic:\n\nFor each test case:\n- Read `n`\n- If `n % 3 == 0` → print `\"Second\"`\n- Else → print `\"First\"`\n\n---\n\n### Code:\n\n```python\nt = int(input())\nfor _ in range(t):\n    n = int(input())\n    if n % 3 == 0:\n        print(\"Second\")\n    else:\n        print(\"First\")\n```\n\nThis matches the sample.\n\nLet’s verify:\n\nSample Input:\n```\n6\n1\n3\n5\n100\n999\n1000\n```\n\nOutput:\n```\nFirst  (1%3=1)\nSecond (3%3=0)\nFirst  (5%3=2)\nFirst  (100%3=1)\nSecond (999%3=0)\nFirst  (1000%3=1)\n```\n\nPerfect.\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n```python\nt = int(input())\nfor _ in range(t):\n    n = int(input())\n    if n % 3 == 0:\n        print(\"Second\")\n    else:\n        print(\"First\")\n```",
