rdingly.\n\nNote: We only consider starting indices where `nums[i+1] == nums[i] + 1`, then extend.\n\nLet’s code:\n\n```python\ndef findMaximumLengthOfAlternatingSubarray(nums):\n    n = len(nums)\n    max_len = 0\n\n    i = 0\n    while i < n - 1:\n        # Check if we can start an alternating subarray at index i\n        if nums[i + 1] != nums[i] + 1:\n            i += 1\n            continue\n\n        # Start at i, extend as long as pattern holds\n        x = nums[i]\n        j = 0\n        # Traverse while we are in bounds and pattern matches\n        while i + j < n:\n            expected = x if j % 2 == 0 else x + 1\n            if nums[i + j] == expected:\n                j += 1\n            else:\n                break\n\n        # j is the length of the valid subarray starting at i\n        if j >= 2:\n            max_len = max(max_len, j)\n\n        i += 1\n\n    return max_len if max_len >= 2 else -1\n```\n\nWait: we can optimize a bit — once we've processed a long alternating run starting at `i`, the next few indices are already part of it. But given constraints (n <= 100), O(n^2) is fine.\n\nBut let's test with example:\n\n`nums = [3,4,3,4]` starting at i=0:\n- x = 3\n- j=0: i+j=0 → nums[0]=3, expected=3 → ok → j=1\n- j=1: i+j=1 → nums[1]=4, expected=4 → ok → j=2\n- j=2: i+j=2 → nums[2]=3, expected=3 → ok → j=3\n- j=3: i+j=3 → nums[3]=4, expected=4 → ok → j=4\n- then i+j=4 >= n → stop\n- j=4 → length 4 → max_len=4\n\nBut wait: in the example, the array is `[2,3,4,3,4]`, and the longest alternating subarray is `[3,4,3,4]` starting at index 1.\n\nSo in our loop, when i=1, x=3, then:\n- j=0: index1=3 → expected=3 → ok\n- j=1: index2=4 → expected=4 → ok\n- j=2: index3=3 → expected=3 → ok\n- j=3: index4=4 → expected=4 → ok\n- j=4: index5 out of bounds → stop\n- length = 4\n\nSo yes.\n\nNow, return `max_len` if >=2, else -1.\n\nBut note: it's possible that no valid subarray is found, so `max_len` remains 0 → return -1.\n\nBut what if only one valid subarray