t = order[:-1]`\n\n7. Print the result as space-separated numbers\n\nBut wait: is book 1 always last?\n\nYes, because we only start from 1, and we process all its prerequisites before marking it as expanded.\n\nSo when we do:\n- push (1, False)\n- pop → not expanded → mark visited, push (1, True), then push all prereqs of 1\n- then process prereqs\n- only after all prereqs are done, we pop (1, True) and append 1\n\nSo 1 is appended at the end.\n\nThus, `order[-1] == 1`\n\nSo we can safely do `order[:-1]`\n\n---\n\n### **Edge Cases**\n\n- Book 1 has no prerequisites? But constraint says `C_1 >= 1`, so not possible.\n- A book may have no prerequisites → handled\n- A prerequisite may be repeated? Input says: `P_{i,j} != P_{i,k}` → no duplicates in a list\n- But same book may be prerequisite for multiple books → handled by `visited`\n\n---\n\n### **Code**\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not data:\n        return\n    \n    n = int(data[0])\n    prerequisites = [[] for _ in range(n+1)]\n    \n    index = 1\n    for i in range(1, n+1):\n        c = int(data[index]); index += 1\n        prereq_list = []\n        for _ in range(c):\n            prereq_list.append(int(data[index])); index += 1\n        prerequisites[i] = prereq_list\n    \n    # Iterative DFS for post-order traversal\n    visited = set()\n    stack = []\n    order = []\n    \n    stack.append((1, False))  # (node, expanded)\n    \n    while stack:\n        node, expanded = stack.pop()\n        if not expanded:\n            if node in visited:\n                continue\n            visited.add(node)\n            # Push the node back with expanded = True\n            stack.append((node, True))\n            # Push all prerequisites in reverse order\n            # So that the first prerequisite is processed first (since stack is LIFO)\n            # We push in reverse order to maintain the original order of processing\n            for prereq in reversed(prerequisites[node]):\n                if prereq not in visited:\n                    stack.append((prereq, False))\n        else:\n            order.append(node)\n    \n    # Remove book 1 from the order (it's the last one)\n    result = order[:-1]\n    print(\" \".join(map(str, result)))\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Test with Sample 1**\n\nInput: \n```\n6\n3 2 3 4\n2 3 5\n0\n1 5\n0\n0\n```\n\nprerequisites:\n- 1: [2,3,4]\n- 2: [3,5]\n- 3: []\n- 4: [5]\n- 5: []\n- 6: []\n\nStack:\n- Start: [(1, False)]\n- Pop (1,False): not visited → add to visited, push (1,True), then push prereq of 1 in reverse: [4,3,2] → push (4,False), (3,False), (2,False)\n\nNow stack: [(1,True), (4,False), (3,False), (2,False)]\n\nPop (2,False): not visited → mark, push (2,True), push prereq of 2 in reverse: [5,3] → push (5,False), (3,False)\n\nStack: [(1,True), (4,False), (3,False), (2,True), (5,False), (3,False)]\n\nPop (3,False): visited? Not yet → but we already have 3 in visited? No, we haven't processed it yet.\n\nWait: we pushed (3,False) earlier, but haven't processed.\n\nBut when we pop (3,False): \n- not visited → mark visited, push (3,True), prereq of 3: [] → nothing\n- then next\n\nBut we have two (3,False)? But after first one, 3 is in visited, so second one will be skipped.\n\nSo:\n\nAfter pushing (5,False), (3,False), stack: ... (3,False) at top.\n\nPop (3,False): not visited → mark, push (3,True), no children → then later pop (3,True) → append 3\n\nThen pop (5,False): not visited → mark, push (5,True), no children → append 5\n\nThen pop (2,True) → append 2\n\nThen pop (3,False): visited → skip\n\nThen pop (4,False): visited? No → mark, push (4,True), prereq: [5] → 5 is visited → so no push → then pop (4,True) → append 4\n\nThen pop (1,True) → append 1\n\nOrder: [3,5,2,4,1] → output: 3 5 2 4\n\nWhich is valid.\n\nBut sample output is `5 3 4 2` — different, but both valid.\n\nSo it's correct.\n\nWe can change the order by changing the order of prere