      "generated_answer": "I need to solve this step by step.\n\nThe problem asks me to shift the integers on the outer squares of an N×N grid clockwise by one position.\n\nLet me understand what the outer squares are:\n- All squares in the first row (top)\n- All squares in the last row (bottom)\n- All squares in the first column (left)\n- All squares in the last column (right)\n\nWhen we shift clockwise by one position, each value moves to the next position in clockwise order.\n\nLet me trace through the clockwise path:\n1. Start at (1,1) - top-left corner\n2. Move right along the top row: (1,1) → (1,2) → ... → (1,N)\n3. Move down along the rightmost column: (1,N) → (2,N) → ... → (N,N)\n4. Move left along the bottom row: (N,N) → (N,N-1) → ... → (N,1)\n5. Move up along the leftmost column: (N,1) → (N-1,1) → ... → (2,1)\n6. Back to (1,1)\n\nFor a 4×4 grid, this gives us the 12 positions mentioned in the example: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,4),(3,4),(4,4),(4,3),(4,2),(4,1),(3,1),(2,1)\n\nTo implement this:\n1. Read the grid\n2. Extract the values from the outer squares in clockwise order\n3. Shift them by one position (each value moves to the next position)\n4. Place the shifted values back into the outer squares\n5. Print the resulting grid\n\n```python\ndef solve():\n    N = int(input())\n    grid = []\n    for _ in range(N):\n        row = input().strip()\n        grid.append([int(c) for c in row])\n    \n    if N == 1:\n        # Special case, but N >= 2 per constraints\n        print(grid[0][0])\n        return\n    \n    # Extract outer squares in clockwise order\n    outer_values = []\n    \n    # Top row (left to right): (1,1) to (1,N)\n    for j in range(N):\n        outer_values.append(grid[0][j])\n    \n    # Right column (top to bottom, excluding top-right corner): (2,N) to (N,N)\n    for i in range(1, N):\n        outer_values.append(grid[i][N-1])\n    \n    # Bottom row (right to left, excluding bottom-right corner): (N,N-1) to (N,1)\n    for j in range(N