      "generated_answer": "I need to find the repeating number (a) and the missing number (b) in a 2D grid.\n\nLet me think through this step by step:\n\n1. The grid is n×n, so there are n² total elements\n2. The values should be in the range [1, n²], with each number appearing exactly once\n3. But one number (a) appears twice, and one number (b) is missing\n4. I need to return [a, b] where a is the duplicate and b is the missing number\n\nMy approach:\n1. Flatten the 2D grid into a 1D list to make it easier to process\n2. Use a set or dictionary to count occurrences of each number\n3. Find which number appears twice (this is a)\n4. Find which number from 1 to n² is missing (this is b)\n\nAlternatively, I can:\n- Use a set to track seen numbers - when I encounter a number I've already seen, that's my duplicate a\n- Calculate the expected sum of numbers 1 to n² using the formula n²(n²+1)/2\n- Calculate the actual sum of all numbers in the grid\n- The missing number b = expected_sum - (actual_sum - a) = expected_sum - actual_sum + a\n\nLet me implement this approach:\n\n```python\ndef solve(grid):\n    n = len(grid)\n    total_elements = n * n\n    \n    # Calculate expected sum of numbers 1 to n²\n    expected_sum = total_elements * (total_elements + 1) // 2\n    \n    # Find the duplicate and calculate actual sum\n    seen = set()\n    actual_sum = 0\n    duplicate = 0\n    \n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(n):\n            num = grid[i][j]\n            actual_sum += num\n            if num in seen:\n                duplicate = num\n            else:\n                seen.add(num)\n    \n    # Calculate the missing number\n    # expected_sum = actual_sum - duplicate + missing\n    # missing = expected_sum - actual_sum + duplicate\n    missing = expected_sum - actual_sum + duplicate\n    \n    return [duplicate, missing]\n```",
