ces = [pos[a], pos[a+1], ..., pos[a+K-1]]`\n   - Sort these indices → gives us the positions in increasing order\n   - Now, slide a window of size $ K $ over this sorted list (even though list size is $ K $, so only one window) → wait, the list has exactly $ K $ elements!\n     - So we just compute $ \\text{max}(indices) - \\text{min}(indices) = \\text{last} - \\text{first} $\n   - Update the global minimum of $ i_K - i_1 $\n3. Output the minimum value found\n\nWait — is that correct?\n\nYes! Because:\n- The set $ S $ has exactly $ K $ distinct values\n- We collect their positions → exactly $ K $ indices\n- We sort them → $ i_1 < i_2 < \\dots < i_K $\n- Then $ i_K - i_1 = \\text{last} - \\text{first} $\n- And the values are $ a $ to $ a+K-1 $ → so it's a valid good sequence\n\nBut note: Is it possible that **more than $ K $** values belong to some consecutive $ K $-length set?  \nNo — because $ S $ has exactly $ K $ values, and each appears once in the permutation.\n\nSo for each $ a $, we get **exactly one** candidate sequence: the positions of $ a, a+1, ..., a+K-1 $, sorted.\n\nSo we just compute $ \\max(\\text{positions}) - \\min(\\text{positions}) $ for each such $ a $, and take the minimum over all $ a $.\n\n---\n\n### **But wait — could there be a good sequence that uses values from $ \\{a, \\dots, a+K-1\\} $, but not all of them?**\n\nNo! Because:\n- A good sequence of length $ K $ must consist of **exactly $ K $** values that are a rearrangement of $ K $ consecutive integers.\n- So the set must be exactly $ \\{a, a+1, \\dots, a+K-1\\} $ for some $ a $\n- So we are not missing any case.\n\nThus, we can iterate over all $ a \\in [1, N-K+1] $, get the positions of $ a $ to $ a+K-1 $, sort them, compute span = last - first, and minimize.\n\n---\n\n### **Edge Case: K = 1**\n\nThen $ a $ ranges from 1 to $ N $, each set is $ \\{a\\} $, position is `pos[a]`, span = 0 → answer is 0\n\nMatches sample 2.\n\n---\n\n### **Let’s Test on Sample 1**\n\nInput:\n```\n4 2\n2 