      "generated_answer": "I need to find the maximum palindromic cube number not greater than N.\n\nLet me break down the problem:\n1. A palindromic cube number is a number that is both a perfect cube and a palindrome\n2. I need to find the largest such number ≤ N\n\nMy approach:\n1. Start from the cube root of N and work downwards\n2. For each integer x, compute x³ and check if it's a palindrome\n3. Return the first (largest) palindromic cube found\n\nHowever, since N can be up to 10¹⁸, I need to be careful about efficiency. The cube root of 10¹⁸ is 10⁶, so I can iterate from floor(N^(1/3)) downwards.\n\nLet me implement this:\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    N = int(input().strip())\n    \n    # Find the cube root of N\n    x = int(N ** (1/3))\n    \n    # We need to be careful with floating point precision\n    # Make sure we start with a value whose cube is <= N\n    while x ** 3 > N:\n        x -= 1\n    \n    # Check if we need to increment (due to floating point issues)\n    if (x + 1) ** 3 <= N:\n        x += 1\n    \n    # Search downwards from x\n    while x >= 1:\n        cube = x ** 3\n        if cube > N:\n            x -= 1\n            continue\n            \n        # Check if cube is palindrome\n        cube_str = str(cube)\n        if cube_str == cube_str[::-1]:\n            print(cube)\n            return\n        \n        x -= 1\n    \n    # If no palindromic cube found (shouldn't happen since 1^3 = 1 is always valid)\n    print(1)\n\nsolve()\n```\n\nWait, let me reconsider the floating point precision issue. For very large numbers, the cube root calculation might not be accurate. Let me refine this:\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    N = int(input().strip())\n    \n    # Find approximate cube root\n    x = int(N ** (1/3))\n    \n    # Adjust x to make sure x^3 <= N\n    while x ** 3 > N:\n        x -= 1\n    \n    # It's possible we need to go one higher due to floating point truncation\n    if (x + 1) ** 3 <= N:\n        x += 1\n    \n    # Search from x downwards\n    while x >= 1:\n        cube = x ** 3\n        if cube > N:\n            x -= 1\n            continue\n            \n        # Check if cube is palindrome\n        cube_str = str(cube)\n        if cube_str == cube_str[::-1]:\n            print(cube)\n            return\n        \n        x -= 1\n    \n    # 1^3 = 1 is always a palindromic cube\n    print(1)\n\nsolve()\n```",
