imum sum must come from large `a[i]` and large `b[j]`\n\nBut how many top values to consider?\n\nWorst-case: only one good combination is allowed, but we can't iterate all.\n\nAlternative idea:\n\nSince `L ≤ 10^5`, we can do this:\n\n1. Let `forbidden = set()` of tuples `(c_i, d_i)`\n2. We know the maximum sum must be close to `max(a) + max(b)`\n3. So we can:\n   - Sort `a` with indices\n   - Sort `b` with indices\n   - Take the top `K` from `a` and top `K` from `b`, and check all their combinations\n   - But how big should `K` be?\n\nBut worst-case, the best allowed combination could be `a[i] + b[j]` where `a[i]` is not even in top 1000.\n\nWait — better idea:\n\nWe can **iterate over all forbidden pairs**, and **collect all main and side dish indices involved**.\n\nBut that doesn't help directly.\n\nAlternative efficient approach:\n\n- The maximum allowed `a[i] + b[j]` is at most `max(a) + max(b)`\n- If `(i_max, j_max)` is not forbidden, answer is `max(a) + max(b)`\n- Otherwise, we need to check:\n  - `(second max a) + max(b)`\n  - `max(a) + (second max b)`\n  - etc.\n\nBut we can't generate all combinations.\n\nBut note: **only L pairs are forbidden**, so the number of combinations that could block the top sums is limited.\n\nSo we can:\n\n1. Create a set of forbidden pairs: `bad = {(c_i, d_i) for all i}`\n2. Get the maximum possible sum: try top values from `a` and `b`\n3. Generate candidate pairs: take top `k` values from `a` and top `k` from `b`, and check all their combinations in descending order of `a[i] + b[j]`\n4. Return the first (largest) one that is not in `bad`\n\nHow big should `k` be?\n\nNote: worst-case, the best allowed combination may involve a small `a[i]` or `b[j]`, but if `a[i]` is very small, even with large `b[j]`, sum is small.\n\nBut we want the **maximum** sum.\n\nSo the maximum sum must come from **large** `a[i]` and **large** `b[j]`.\n\nIn fact, suppose:\n- `a_sorted` descending: `A1 ≥ A2 ≥ ...`\n- `b_sorted` descending: `B1 ≥ B2 ≥ ...`