e among the top `L+1` largest sums.\n\nYes! Because only `L` sums can be forbidden. So the largest allowed sum must be at least as large as the `(L+1)`-th largest sum. But it could be larger — actually, it **is** the largest sum that is allowed, and since only `L` are blocked, it must be among the top `L+1` largest sums.\n\nWait: suppose the top `L+1` sums include some allowed ones. The maximum allowed one could be the first, second, etc.\n\nBut the **maximum allowed sum** is the **maximum** of all allowed sums, and since only `L` are forbidden, it must be among the top `L+1` largest sums (by value), because even if the top `L` are forbidden, the `(L+1)`-th is allowed and is the best possible.\n\nTherefore, the maximum allowed sum is the **maximum value among the top `L+1` largest sums** that is not forbidden.\n\nBut we don't know which ones are the top `L+1` — we need to generate them.\n\nSo plan:\n1. Use a heap to generate pairs `(i,j)` in decreasing order of `a[i] + b[j]`\n2. We generate up to `L+1` pairs (or more? we need all pairs with sum ≥ the `(L+1)`-th largest)\n   - Actually, we need to generate the top `L+1` **distinct** sums? No — we need the top `L+1` **pairs**, because multiple pairs can have same sum.\n\nBut worst-case, many pairs can have same sum.\n\nBut we can generate the top `L+1` **pairs** by sum.\n\nBut `L+1 ≤ 10^5 + 1`, so we can do it.\n\nSteps:\n- Use max-heap (simulate with min-heap of negatives)\n- visited set for `(i,j)`\n- Start with `(0,0)` — indices in sorted `a` and `b`\n- Pop the largest sum\n- If `(original_index_i + 1, original_index_j + 1)` not in forbidden, we can't stop yet — because we need to check all top `L+1` to find the maximum allowed?\n   - Actually, we want the **maximum allowed sum**, so we can:\n     - Generate the top `L+1` largest sums (pairs)\n     - Among these `L+1` pairs, find the maximum sum that is not forbidden\n     - But it's possible that a larger sum (earlier in the list) is allowed — so we can just iterate the top `L+1` and take the maximum sum that is allowed.\n\nBut actually, since we are generating in descending order, we can:\n- For each of the first `L+1` pairs we pop:\n  - Let `sum_val = a[i] + b[j]`\n  - Let `main_idx = i_orig + 1`, `side_idx = j_orig + 1`\n  - If `(main_idx, side_idx)` not in `bad`, then `sum_val` is a candidate\n- But we must continue until we have seen `L+1` pairs, because an earlier allowed sum might be the answer, but we want the **maximum**, and we are going in descending order, so the **first allowed sum we encounter** is the answer?\n\nWait: not necessarily — because we might skip some allowed sums that are larger? No — we are going in descending order.\n\nSo:\n- We generate pairs in descending order of sum\n- The first pair (largest sum) — if not forbidden, return it\n- Else, next largest — if not forbidden, return it\n- ...\n- Since only `L` are forbidden, by the time we reach the `(L+1)`-th pair, we **must** have found an allowed one\n\nTherefore, we can:\n- Use a heap to generate pairs in descending order of `a[i] + b[j]`\n- For each pair:\n  - Check if `(i+1, j+1)` is not forbidden\n  - If yes, output the sum and break\n  - Else, continue\n- We will find one within `L+1` steps\n\nBut is that correct?\n\nYes: because there are only `L` forbidden pairs. So among the top `L+1` largest-sum pairs, at least one is allowed. So by the time we check the first `L+1` pairs (in descending order), we will have encountered at least one allowed, and the **first allowed** we meet is the **largest allowed**, because we are going from largest sum downward.\n\nTherefore, we can stop at the **first allowed pair** in this iteration.\n\nBut wait: what if the largest sum is allowed? Then we return it immediately.\n\nIf it's forbidden, we go to next, etc.\n\nAnd since only `L` are forbidden, we will find an allowed one within `L+1` steps.\n\nSo we only need to check at most `L+1` pairs.\n\nThis is efficient because `L ≤ 10^5`.\n\n---\n\n### **Implementation Steps**\n\n1. Read `N, M, L`\n2. Read `a` (main dish prices), `b` (side dish prices)\n3. Read `L` forbidden pairs, store in a set as tuples `(c, d)`\n4. Sort `a` with original indices, descending\n5. Sort `b` with original indices, descending\n6. Use a heap: we will store `(-sum, i, j)` where `i` is index in sorted `a`, `j` is index in sorted `b`\n7. Use a visited set for `(i, j)` to avoid duplicates\n8. Start with `(0,0)` — the largest values\n9. Push `(-(a_sorted[0].val + b_sorted[0].val), 0, 0)`\n10. While heap not empty:\n    - Pop the largest sum\n    - Get the original main dish index = `a_sorted[i][1]`, so dish number = `a_idx = a_sorted[i][1] + 1`? Wait — we stored index.\n\nActually:\n- We create `A = [(a[i], i) for i in range(N)]`, sort by `a[i]` descending\n- Similarly `B = [(b[j], j) for j in range(M)]`, sort descending\n\nThen:\n- `A[i][0]` is the price, `A[i][1]` is the original 0-based index\n- So the main dish number is `A[i][1] + 1`\n- Side dish number is `B[j][1] + 1`\n\n11. Check if `(A[i][1]+1, B[j][1]+1)` is in `bad` set\n12. If **not**, print `A[i][0] + B[j][0]` and break\n13. Else, push `(i+1, j)` and `(i, j+1)` if not visited\n\nThis is standard for \"kth largest pair sum\".\n\n---\n\n### **Code Structure**\n\n```python\nimport heapq\n\nN, M, L = map(int, input().split())\na = list(map(int, input().split()))\nb = list(map(int, input().split()))\n\nbad = set()\nfor _ in range(L):\n    c, d = map(int, input().split())\n    bad.add((c, d))\n\n# Create sorted lists with original indices\nA = [(a[i], i) for i in range(N)]\nB = [(b[j], j) for j in range(M)]\n\nA.sort(key=lambda x: x[0], reverse=True)\nB.sort(key=lambda x: x[0], reverse=True)\n\n# Max-heap: use negative sum\nheap = []\nvisited = set()\n\n# Start with (0,0)\nheapq.heappush(heap, (-(A[0][0] + B[0][0]), 0, 0))\nvisited.add((0, 0))\n\nwhile heap:\n    neg_sum, i, j = heapq.heappop(heap)\n    total = -neg_sum\n    \n    # Get original dish numbers (1-indexed)\n    main_dish = A[i][1] + 1\n    side_dish = B[j][1] + 1\n    \n    if (main_dish, side_dish) not in bad:\n        print(total)\n        break\n    \n    # Push (i+1, j)\n    if i + 1 < N and (i+1, j) not in visited:\n        visited.add((i+1, j))\n        heapq.heappush(heap, (-(A[i+1][0] + B[j][0]), i+1, j))\n    \n    # Push (i, j+1)\n    if j + 1 < M and (i, j+1) not in visited:\n        visited.add((i, j+1))\n        heapq.heappush(heap, (-(A[i][0] + B[j+1][0]), i, j+1))\n```\n\n---\n\n### **Why this works**\n\n- We explore pairs in decreasing order of sum\n- We stop at the first allowed pair — which must be the **maximum allowed sum**\n- We only explore at most `L+1` steps (in practice, more due to heap, but total states visited ≤ 2*(L+1))\n- Efficient: `L ≤ 10^5`\n\n---\n\n### **Test with Sample 1**\n\n```\nN=2, M=3, L=3\na = [2, 1] -> A = [(2,0), (1,1)]  # (price, orig_index)\nb = [10,30,20] -> B = [(30,1), (20,2), (10,0)]\n\nbad = {(1,2), (2,1), (2,3)}  # (c,d)\n\nStart: i=0,j=0: sum = 2+30 = 32, dish=(0+1,1+1)=(1,2) -> in bad? yes -> push (1,0) and (0,1)\n\nNext: which is next largest? We have:\n- (1,0): A[1][0]+B[0][0] = 1+30=31\n- (0,1): A[0][0]+B[1][0] = 2+20=22\nSo heap will pop (31)\n\nCheck: i=1,j=0 -> dish = (1+1,0+1) = (2,1) -> in bad? yes -> push (2,0) invalid, push (1,1)\n\nNow (1,1): 1+20=21\n\nNext: pop (22): i=0,j=1 -> dish=(1,3) -> (1,3) in bad? no -> print 22?\n\nBut expected is 31.\n\nWait! (2,1) is forbidden, but (2,2)? dish (2,2): main=2, side=2 -> (2,2) not in bad.\n\nBut in our state: i=1,j=0 -> dish (2,1) -> forbidden.\n\nThen we push (1,1): i=1,j=1 -> A[1][0]=1, B[1][0]=20 -> sum=21, dish=(2,2) -> (2,2) not in bad? let's see bad: (1,2),(2,1),(2,3) -> (2,2) not in bad.\n\nSo when we pop (21), we check (2,2) -> not in bad -> print 21.\n\nBut expected is 31.\n\nWait, what? 1+30=31, dish (2,1) -> (2,1) is in bad -> forbidden.\n\nBut is there a meal with 31?\n\nMain dish 2: price 1\nSide dish 2: price 30\nSum = 31\nDish pair: (2,2) -> is (2,2) in bad? bad pairs: (1,2), (2,1), (2,3) -> no, (2,2) is allowed.\n\nBut in our B list: sorted b: [30,20,10] -> index 0:30 (orig j=1), index1:20(orig j=2), index2:10(orig j=0)\n\nSo B[0] corresponds to side dish 2 (because original index is 1 -> dish number 2)\n\nSo when i=1 (main dish 2), j=0 (side dish 2), dish pair = (2,2) -> should be allowed.\n\nBut in our code, we are using:\n- main_dish = A[i][1] + 1\n- side_dish = B[j][1] + 1\n\nFor i=1: A[1] = (1,1) -> orig index 1 -> dish number 2\nFor j=0: B[0] = (30,1) -> orig index 1 -> dish number 2\n\nSo (2,2) -> not in bad -> should be allowed.\n\nBut earlier we said (2,1) is forbidden — that's (main2, side1)\n\nSo (2,2) is allowed.\n\nBut in our heap, we first pushed (0,0): sum=2+30=32 -> dish (1,2) -> (1,2) in bad? yes -> bad has (1,2) -> yes.\n\nThen we push (1,0) and (0,1)\n\n(1,0): i=1,j=0 -> sum=1+30=31 -> dish (2,2) -> (2,2) not in bad -> we should return 31.\n\nBut in our heap, we have:\n- (32) -> popped, forbidden\n- then push (31) and (22)\n- heap: [-31, -22] -> pop -31 -> sum=31\n\nThen check: (2,2) not in bad -> print 31.\n\nYes! So it works.\n\nBut why did I think (2,1)? That was a confusion.\n\nIn B[0] is side dish 2, not side dish 1.\n\nSide dish 1 has price 10 -> in B[2]\n\nSo (2,1) would be i=1 (main2), j=2 (side1) -> sum=1+10=11\n\nSo all good.\n\nSo code should work.\n\n---\n\n### **Final Code**\n\nLet's write it.\n\nNote: We use 0-indexed internally, but dish numbers are 1-indexed.\n\nWe must be careful.\n\nLet's run sample 2:\n- N=2, M=1, L=0\n- a = [1000000000, 1] -> A = [(1000000000,0), (1,1)]\n- b = [1000000000] -> B = [(1000000000,0)]\n- bad = {}\n- Start: i=0,j=0: sum=2000000000, dish=(1,1) -> not in bad -> print 2000000000\n\nCorrect.\n\nSample 3: we trust.\n\nNow write code.\n\n```python\nimport heapq\n\ndef main():\n    import sys\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not data:\n        return\n    \n    # Parse first three\n    it = iter(data)\n    N = int(next(it)); M = int(next(it)); L = int(next(it))\n    \n    a = [int(next(it)) for _ in range(N)]\n    b = [int(next(it)) for _ in range(M)]\n    \n    bad = set()\n    for _ in range(L):\n        c = int(next(it)); d = int(next(it))\n        bad.add((c, d))\n    \n    # Create list with original indices\n    A = [(a[i], i) for i in range(N)]\n    B = [(b[j], j) for j in range(M)]\n    \n    # Sort by price descending\n    A.sort(key=lambda x: x[0], reverse=True)\n    B.sort(key=lambda x: x[0], reverse=True)\n    \n    # Max-heap: (-sum, i, j) where i,j are indices in A and B\n    heap = []\n    visited = set()\n    \n    # Start with (0,0)\n    first_sum = A[0][0] + B[0][0]\n    heapq.heappush(heap, (-first_sum, 0, 0))\n    visited.add((0, 0))\n    \n    while heap:\n        neg_sum, i, j = heapq.heappop(heap)\n        total = -neg_sum\n        \n        # Get the original dish numbers (1-indexed)\n        main_dish = A[i][1] + 1\n        side_dish = B[j][1] + 1\n        \n        if (main_dish, side_dish) not in bad:\n            print(total)\n            return\n        \n        # Push next: (i+1, j)\n        if i + 1 < N and (i+1, j) not in visited:\n            visited.add((i+1, j))\n            new_sum = A[i+1][0] + B[j][0]\n            heapq.heappush(heap, (-new_sum, i+1, j))\n        \n        # Push (i, j+1)\n        if j + 1 < M and (i, j+1) not in visited:\n            visited.add((i, j+1))\n            new_sum = A[i][0] + B[j+1][0]\n            heapq.heappush(heap, (-new_sum, i, j+1))\n\nmain()\n```\n\nThis should work.\n\nLet me test with sample 1 manually:\n- A = [(2,0), (1,1)]  # main dish 1 and 2\n- B = [(30,1), (20,2), (10,0)]  # side dish 2,3,1\n- bad = {(1,2), (2,1), (2,3)}\n\nStart: (0,0): sum=2+30=32, dish=(1,2) -> (1,2) in bad -> yes, skip.\nPush (1,0): i=1,j=0 -> sum=1+30=31, dish=(2,2) -> (2,2) in bad? no -> but we don't check yet.\nPush (0,1): sum=2+20=22, dish=(1,3)\n\nHeap: [-31, -22] -> pop -31 -> sum=31\ndish: main_dish = A[1][1]+1 = 1+1=2, side_dish = B[0][1]+1=1+1=2 -> (2,2)\n(2,2) in bad? bad has (1,2),(2,1),(2,3) -> no -> print 31.\n\nCorrect.\n\nNote: (2,1) would be main2, side1 -> side1 is B[2] (price 10), so j=2.\n\nSo (2,1) is (i=1,j=2) -> sum=1+10=11.\n\nAll good.\n\nSo final code.",
