on with N=5×10^5, it's about 1.25×10^11 comparisons worst case, which is too slow.\n\nLet me think of another approach:\n\nTotal pairs = N*(N-1)/2\nNon-intersecting pairs = pairs where one interval is completely to the left of another\nIf I sort by left endpoint, then interval i and j (i < j) don't intersect iff r_i < l_j\n\nSo: Answer = Total pairs - sum over j of (number of i < j with r_i < l_j)\n\nThis is easier! I can count non-intersecting pairs efficiently.\n\n```python\nimport bisect\n\nn = int(input())\nintervals = []\nfor _ in range(n):\n    l, r = map(int, input().split())\n    intervals.append((l, r))\n\n# Sort by left endpoint\nintervals.sort()\n\n# Count non-intersecting pairs: pairs (i,j) with i < j but r_i < l_j\nright_ends = []  # sorted list of right endpoints of processed intervals\nnon_intersecting = 0\n\nfor l, r in intervals:\n    # Count how many previous intervals have right_end < l\n    # These don't intersect with current interval\n    pos = bisect.bisect_left(right_ends, l)\n    non_intersecting += pos\n    \n    # Add current right endpoint\n    bisect.insort(right_ends, r)\n\ntotal_pairs = n * (n - 1) // 2\nanswer = total_pairs - non_intersecting\nprint(answer)\n```\n\nThis should work and is the same complexity, but let me verify:\n\nExample 1: [(1,5), (3,7), (7,8)]\n- (1,5): pos = bisect_left([], 1) = 0, non_intersecting += 0, right_ends=[5]\n- (3,7): pos = bisect_left([5], 3) = 0 (since 5 >= 3), non_intersecting += 0, right_ends=[5,7]\n- (7,8): pos = bisect_left([5,7], 7) = 1 (since 5 < 7, 7 >= 7), non_intersecting += 1, right_ends=[5,7,8]\n- total_pairs = 3, non_intersecting = 1, answer = 2\n\nWait, this gives 3-1=2, which is correct.\n\nBut let me check the non-intersecting count: only (1,5) and (7,8) don't intersect? No: (1,5) and (7,8) don't intersect, and that's the only non-intersecting pair. So non_intersecting should be 1.\n\nYes, correct.\n\nExample 2: [(1,6), (2,5), (3,4)]\n- (1,6): pos=0, non_intersecting=0, right_ends=[6]\n