      "generated_answer": "I need to solve this step by step.\n\n**Problem Understanding:**\n- I need to find the maximum distance between indices of two prime numbers in the array\n- The distance is the absolute difference between indices\n- I need to return the maximum possible distance\n- There's at least one prime number in the array\n\n**Steps to solve:**\n\n1. First, I need a helper function to check if a number is prime\n2. Iterate through the array to find all indices that contain prime numbers\n3. Find the maximum distance between any two prime indices\n4. Since we want maximum distance, I only need the first (minimum) and last (maximum) prime indices\n\n**Key insights:**\n- The maximum distance will always be between the leftmost prime and rightmost prime\n- I don't need to check all pairs - just find the first and last occurrence of prime numbers\n- Distance = last_prime_index - first_prime_index\n\n**Prime checking function:**\n- A number is prime if it's greater than 1 and has no divisors other than 1 and itself\n- I only need to check divisors up to sqrt(n)\n\n**Algorithm:**\n1. Create is_prime helper function\n2. Find the first index where nums[i] is prime\n3. Find the last index where nums[i] is prime\n4. Return the difference\n\n```python\ndef maximumPrimeDifference(nums):\n    def is_prime(n):\n        if n < 2:\n            return False\n        if n == 2:\n            return True\n        if n % 2 == 0:\n            return False\n        for i in range(3, int(n**0.5) + 1, 2):\n            if n % i == 0:\n                return False\n        return True\n    \n    first_prime_idx = -1\n    last_prime_idx = -1\n    \n    # Find first prime index\n    for i in range(len(nums)):\n        if is_prime(nums[i]):\n            first_prime_idx = i\n            break\n    \n    # Find last prime index\n    for i in range(len(nums) - 1, -1, -1):\n        if is_prime(nums[i]):\n            last_prime_idx = i\n            break\n    \n    return last_prime_idx - first_prime_idx\n```",
