id.\n\nBut `i` ranges from `0` to `n-1`, so `r = n - i` ranges from `n` down to `1`, but mod `n`, `r=0` when `i=0`.\n\nWait: `i=0` → `r = n` → `r mod n = 0`\n\nYes.\n\nSo:\n- For each `i` in `0 <= i < n` such that `(s+s)[i:i+n] == t`, then `r = (n - i) % n` is a valid rotation.\n\nWe can use **double hashing** or **KMP** to find all such `i`.\n\nBut KMP gives all matches in `O(n)`.\n\nSo:\n- Use KMP to find all starting positions `i` in `[0, n)` where `(s+s)[i:i+n] == t`\n- Then for each such `i`, add `r = (n - i) % n` to `valid_rotations`\n\nBut note: `(n - i) % n` is just `n - i` if `i > 0`, and `0` if `i=0`.\n\n---\n\n### **Let’s code accordingly**\n\nBut note: we only care about whether `r == 0` or not, and count how many valid rotations are 0 and how many are non-zero.\n\nSo we can do:\n\n```python\ntotal_valid = 0\ncount0 = 0\nfor each match at i in [0, n):\n    r = (n - i) % n\n    if r == 0:\n        count0 += 1\n    else:\n        total_valid += 1  # we'll count non-zero separately\n```\n\nActually, just:\n- `num_zero = 1 if there exists i such that (s+s)[i:i+n]==t and (n-i)%n==0, i.e., i%n==0 → i=0\n- So if `(s+s)[0:n] == t`, i.e., `s == t`, then `0` is valid.\n\nBut multiple `i` can give same `r`? No, each `i` gives one `r`, and `i` determines `r` uniquely.\n\nBut different `i` can give same `r`? No, `r = (n - i) % n`, so one-to-one.\n\nSo number of valid rotations = number of `i` in `[0, n)` such that `(s+s)[i:i+n] == t`\n\nLet `matches = number of such i`\n\nThen:\n- `count0 = 1 if i=0 is a match (i.e., s == t) else 0`\n- `count_nonzero = matches - count0`\n\nBut wait: is it possible that `i=0` is not the only one that gives `r=0`? No, only `i=0` gives `r=0`.\n\nSo yes.\n\nAlternatively, we can just compute the list of valid `r`, but we only need:\n- `c0 = 1 if 0 in valid_r else 0`\n- `c1 = len(valid_r) - c0`\n\nBut we can compute `matches` = number of `i` in `[0, n)` such that `(s+s)[i:i+n] == t`\n\nWe can use KMP to count the number of occurrences of `t` in `s+s` in the first `n` starting positions.\n\nBut note: `s+s` has length `2n`, we want all `i` in `[0, n)` such that `s+s[i:i+n] == t`\n\nWe can do:\n\n```python\ntext = s + s\npattern = t\n```\n\nUse KMP to find all matches in `text` with `pattern`, and count how many start at `i < n`.\n\n---\n\n### **Final Plan**\n\n```python\nMOD = 10**9+7\n\ndef kmp_search(text, pattern):\n    # return list of starting indices where pattern appears in text\n    if not pattern:\n        return []\n    # Compute LPS array\n    m = len(pattern)\n    lps = [0] * m\n    length = 0\n    i = 1\n    while i < m:\n        if pattern[i] == pattern[length]:\n            length += 1\n            lps[i] = length\n            i += 1\n        else:\n            if length != 0:\n                length = lps[length-1]\n            else:\n                lps[i] = 0\n                i += 1\n    # Search\n    n = len(text)\n    i = 0  # index for text\n    j = 0  # index for pattern\n    matches = []\n    while i < n:\n        if text[i] == pattern[j]:\n            i += 1\n            j += 1\n        if j == m:\n            matches.append(i - j)\n            j = lps[j-1]\n        elif i < n and text[i] != pattern[j]:\n            if j != 0:\n                j = lps[j-1]\n            else:\n                i += 1\n    return matches\n```\n\nBut we can optimize: we only care about matches in `[0, n)`, and `text = s+s`, `len=2n`, `pattern = t`, `len=n`.\n\nWe can break early if `i - j >= n`? But matches can start at `i < n`.\n\nActually, we only need matches where start index `i < n`.\n\nSo after getting matches, filter `i < n`.\n\n---\n\n### **Matrix Exponentiation**\n\n```python\ndef mat_mult(A, B, mod):\n    return [\n        [(A[0][0]*B[0][0] + A[0][1]*B[1][0]) % mod,\n         (A[0][0]*B[0][1] + A[0][1]*B[1][1]) % mod],\n        [(A[1][0]*B[0][0] + A[1][1]*B[1][0]) % mod,\n         (A[1][0]*B[0][1] + A[1][1]*B[1][1]) % mod]\n    ]\n\ndef mat_pow(matrix, power, mod):\n    # Initialize resul