plement:\n\n1. KMP to find matches of `t` in `s+s` for start indices in `[0, n)`\n2. Count valid rotations: `r = (n - i) % n`, but we only care about whether `r==0` or not.\n3. Use matrix exponentiation to compute `a_k` and `b_k`\n4. Return `(count0 * a_k + count_nonzero * b_k) % MOD`\n\nLet’s write the function.\n\nNote: We can avoid storing all matches, just count.\n\nBut we need to know if `i=0` is a match, and total count.\n\nSo:\n\n```python\nmatches = kmp_search(s+s, t)\nvalid_start = [i for i in matches if i < n]\ntotal_matches = len(valid_start)\ncount0 = 1 if 0 in valid_start else 0\ncount_nonzero = total_matches - count0\n```\n\nBut we can do without storing all:\n\n```python\ncount_matches = 0\nhas_i0 = False\nfor i in matches:\n    if i < n:\n        count_matches += 1\n        if i == 0:\n            has_i0 = True\n```\n\nBut easier to filter.\n\nNow, code.\n\nWe'll write the function.\n\nNote: KMP might be heavy, but `O(n)`.\n\nLet’s code accordingly.\n\nBut note: `s+s` has length `2n`, `t` has length `n`, so KMP is `O(n)`.\n\nGood.\n\n---\n\n### **Final Answer**\n\n```python\ndef numberOfWays(s, t, k):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    n = len(s)\n    \n    # Step 1: Find all starting positions i in [0, n) where (s+s)[i:i+n] == t\n    text = s + s\n    pattern = t\n    \n    def kmp_search(text, pattern):\n        if not pattern:\n            return []\n        m = len(pattern)\n        # Build LPS array\n        lps = [0] * m\n        length = 0\n        i = 1\n        while i < m:\n            if pattern[i] == pattern[length]:\n                length += 1\n                lps[i] = length\n                i += 1\n            else:\n                if length != 0:\n                    length = lps[length - 1]\n                else:\n                    lps[i] = 0\n                    i += 1\n        # Search in text\n        matches = []\n        j = 0  # index for pattern\n        for i in range(len(text)):\n            while j > 0 and text[i] != pattern[j]:\n                j = lps[j - 1]\n            if text[i] == pattern[j]:\n                j += 1\n            if j == m:\n                matches.append(i - m + 1)\n                j = lps[j - 1]\n        return matches\n    \n    matches = kmp_search(text, pattern)\n    valid_starts = [i for i in matches if i < n]\n    total_valid = len(valid_starts)\n    count0 = 1 if 0 in valid_starts else 0\n    count_nonzero = total_valid - count0\n    \n    # Step 2: Matrix exponentiation for recurrence\n    # M = [[0, n-1], [1, n-2]]\n    # We need M^k, then a_k = M^k[0][0], b_k = M^k[1][0]\n    \n    def mat_mult(A, B, mod):\n        return [\n            [ (A[0][0]*B[0][0] + A[0][1]*B[1][0]) % mod,\n              (A[0][0]*B[0][1] + A[0][1]*B[1][1]) % mod ],\n            [ (A[1][0]*B[0][0] + A[1][1]*B[1][0]) % mod,\n              (A[1][0]*B[0][1] + A[1][1]*B[1][1]) % mod ]\n        ]\n    \n    def mat_pow(matrix, power, mod):\n        result = [[1,0],[0,1]]\n        base = matrix\n        while power:\n            if power & 1:\n                result = mat_mult(result, base, mod)\n            base = mat_mult(base, base, mod)\n            power //= 2\n        return result\n    \n    M = [[0, (n-1) % MOD], [1, (n-2) % MOD]]\n    M_k = mat_pow(M, k, MOD)\n    a_k = M_k[0][0]  # ways to be at rotation 0\n    b_k = M_k[1][0]  # ways to be at any specific non-zero rotation\n    \n    result = (count0 * a_k + count_nonzero * b_k) % MOD\n    return result\n```\n\nBut note: in the matrix, we use `n-1` and `n-2` modulo `MOD`, which is fine.\n\nLet’s return this.\n\nNote: We assume KMP is correct.\n\nWe can test with examples.\n\nBut per instructions, we return the function body.\n\n---\n\n### **Final Answer**\n\n```python\ndef numberOfWays(s, t, k):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    n = len(s)\n    \n    text = s + s\n    pattern = t\n    \n    def kmp_search(text, pattern):\n        if not pattern:\n            return []\n        m = len(pattern)\n        lps = [0] * m\n        length = 0\n        i = 1\n        while i < m:\n            if pattern[i] == pattern[length]:\n                length += 1\n                lps[i] = length\n                i += 1\n            else:\n                if length != 0:\n                    length = lps[length - 1]\n                else:\n                    lps[i] = 0\n                    i += 1\n        matches = []\n        j = 0\n        for i in range(len(text)):\n            while j > 0 and text[i] != pattern[j]:\n                j = lps[j - 1]\n            if text[i] == pattern[j]:\n                j += 1\n            if j == m:\n                matches.append(i - m + 1)\n                j = lps[j - 1]\n        return matches\n    \n    matches = kmp_search(text, pattern)\n    valid_starts = [i for i in matches if i < n]\n    total_valid = len(valid_starts)\n    count0 = 1 if 0 in valid_starts else 0\n    count_nonzero = total_valid - count0\n    \n    def mat_mult(A, B, mod):\n        return [\n            [ (A[0][0]*B[0][0] + A[0][1]*B[1][0]) % mod,\n              (A[0][0]*B[0][1] + A[0][1]*B[1][1]) % mod ],\n            [ (A[1][0]*B[0][0] + A[1][1]*B[1][0]) % mod,\n              (A[1][0]*B[0][1] + A[1][1]*B[1][1]) % mod ]\n        ]\n    \n    def mat_pow(matrix, power, mod):\n        result = [[1, 0], [0, 1]]\n        base = matrix\n        while power:\n            if power & 1:\n                result = mat_mult(result, base, mod)\n            base = mat_mult(base, base, mod)\n            power //= 2\n        return result\n    \n    M = [[0, (n-1) % MOD], [1, (n-2) % MOD]]\n    M_k = mat_pow(M, k, MOD)\n    a_k = M_k[0][0]\n    b_k = M_k[1][0]\n    \n    result = (count0 * a_k + count_nonzero * b_k) % MOD\n    return result\n```",
