      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef unmarkedSumArray(nums, queries):\n    \"\"\"\n    To efficiently process the queries:\n    1. Calculate the initial sum of all elements in the array.\n    2. Create a list of (value, index) pairs and sort them. This allows us to\n       access the unmarked elements with the smallest values in order.\n    3. Use a boolean array `is_marked` to keep track of which indices are already marked.\n    4. Maintain a pointer to iterate through the sorted list of elements.\n    5. For each query:\n       - Mark the element at `index_i` if not already marked, update the sum.\n       - Use the pointer to find up to `k_i` smallest unmarked elements, mark them, \n         and update the sum.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(nums)\n    is_marked = [False] * n\n    current_sum = sum(nums)\n    \n    # Store (value, index) and sort by value, then index to handle ties\n    sorted_elements = sorted([(nums[i], i) for i in range(n)])\n    \n    # Pointer to track the next smallest element in the sorted list\n    pointer = 0\n    results = []\n    \n    for index_i, k_i in queries:\n        # Step 1: Mark the element at index_i if it's not marked\n        if not is_marked[index_i]:\n            is_marked[index_i] = True\n            current_sum -= nums[index_i]\n            \n        # Step 2: Mark k_i smallest unmarked elements\n        count = 0\n        while count < k_i and pointer < n:\n            val, idx = sorted_elements[pointer]\n            if not is_marked[idx]:\n                is_marked[idx] = True\n                current_sum -= val\n                count += 1\n            # Move the pointer forward regardless, as this element is now marked\n            pointer += 1\n            \n        results.append(current_sum)\n        \n    return results\n```",
