      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef specialPerm(nums):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given an array of distinct positive integers, this function returns the total \n    number of special permutations where every adjacent pair satisfies the \n    divisibility condition (nums[i] % nums[i+1] == 0 or nums[i+1] % nums[i] == 0).\n    \n    The approach uses dynamic programming with bitmasking to keep track of the \n    subset of elements used and the last placed element.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(nums)\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    \n    # Precompute adjacency matrix to store divisibility relationships\n    # adj[i][j] is True if nums[i] and nums[j] can be adjacent\n    adj = [[False] * n for _ in range(n)]\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(n):\n            if i != j:\n                if nums[i] % nums[j] == 0 or nums[j] % nums[i] == 0:\n                    adj[i][j] = True\n    \n    # memo[mask][last] stores the count of special permutations using \n    # elements indicated by 'mask', ending with the element at index 'last'\n    memo = [[-1] * n for _ in range(1 << n)]\n    \n    def solve(mask, last):\n        # Base case: all elements used\n        if mask == (1 << n) - 1:\n            return 1\n        \n        if memo[mask][last] != -1:\n            return memo[mask][last]\n        \n        res = 0\n        # Try placing every unused number that satisfies the condition\n        for next_idx in range(n):\n            if not (mask & (1 << next_idx)):\n                if adj[last][next_idx]:\n                    res = (res + solve(mask | (1 << next_idx), next_idx)) % MOD\n        \n        memo[mask][last] = res\n        return res\n    \n    # Calculate total special permutations by starting the sequence with each element\n    total = 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        total = (total + solve(1 << i, i)) % MOD\n        \n    return total\n```",
