h 4), so max = 4  \n- 'y': appears as \"y\" (length 1) twice, so max = 1\n- Total = 3 + 4 + 1 = 8 ✓\n\nPerfect! So the solution is:\n1. Read N and S\n2. Find the maximum consecutive length for each character that appears\n3. Sum up these maximum lengths\n\n```python\ndef solve():\n    N = int(input())\n    S = input().strip()\n    \n    # Dictionary to store max consecutive length for each character\n    max_length = {}\n    \n    if N == 0:\n        print(0)\n        return\n    \n    # Traverse the string to find consecutive groups\n    current_char = S[0]\n    current_length = 1\n    \n    # Update max_length for the first character\n    max_length[current_char] = 1\n    \n    for i in range(1, N):\n        if S[i] == current_char:\n            current_length += 1\n        else:\n            # Update max_length for the previous character\n            if current_char in max_length:\n                max_length[current_char] = max(max_length[current_char], current_length)\n            else:\n                max_length[current_char] = current_length\n            \n            # Reset for new character\n            current_char = S[i]\n            current_length = 1\n            \n            # Initialize max_length for new character if not seen\n            if current_char not in max_length:\n                max_length[current_char] = 1\n    \n    # Don't forget the last group\n    if current_char in max_length:\n        max_length[current_char] = max(max_length[current_char], current_length)\n    else:\n        max_length[current_char] = current_length\n    \n    # Sum up all the maximum lengths\n    total = sum(max_length.values())\n    print(total)\n\nsolve()\n```",
