\nBut wait: we start at a land cell, then move — but the move might go to sea — we check that.\n\nSo this is correct.\n\nLet’s test with sample.\n\n---\n\n### **Sample 1**\n\n```\nH=6, W=7, N=5\nT = \"LULDR\"\nGrid:\n#######\n#...#.#\n##...##\n#.#...#\n#...#.#\n#######\n```\n\nLand cells: all '.'.\n\nWe initialize `current_positions` with all (i,j) where grid[i][j]=='.'\n\nThen for each move in \"LULDR\", we update.\n\nBut let's see: after each move, the set shrinks.\n\nBut we don't need to simulate by hand — we trust logic.\n\nBut sample says answer is 2.\n\nWe'll run the code mentally.\n\nBut better to code and test.\n\nBut we are to write the code.\n\nLet’s write it.\n\nNote: we use 0-based indexing.\n\n---\n\n### **Code**\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().splitlines()\n    if not data: \n        return\n    \n    H, W, N = map(int, data[0].split())\n    T = data[1].strip()\n    grid = []\n    for i in range(2, 2+H):\n        grid.append(data[i].strip())\n    \n    # Initial set: all land cells\n    positions = set()\n    for i in range(H):\n        for j in range(W):\n            if grid[i][j] == '.':\n                positions.add((i, j))\n    \n    # Directions\n    di = {'L': 0, 'R': 0, 'U': -1, 'D': 1}\n    dj = {'L': -1, 'R': 1, 'U': 0, 'D': 0}\n    \n    # Simulate each move\n    for move in T:\n        ni_dir = di[move]\n        nj_dir = dj[move]\n        next_positions = set()\n        for (i, j) in positions:\n            ni = i + ni_dir\n            nj = j + nj_dir\n            # Check bounds\n            if ni < 0 or ni >= H or nj < 0 or nj >= W:\n                continue\n            if grid[ni][nj] == '.':\n                next_positions.add((ni, nj))\n        positions = next_positions\n        # Early break if no positions left\n        if not positions:\n            break\n    \n    print(len(positions))\n\nmain()\n```\n\nLet’s test with sample 1.\n\nInitial positions: all '.'.\n\nIn grid:\n\nRow 1 (index 1): \"