  So if we find such a pair at indices `i` and `j` (`i < j`), then:\n   - We keep at least the digits from `i` to `j`, and possibly more digits between `i` and `j` that we don't delete.\n   - But we can delete **all digits after `j`**, and **all digits before `i`**.\n\n   So the number of deletions would be:\n   ```\n   deletions = i + (n - 1 - j)\n   ```\n\n   And we want to **minimize** this.\n\n2. Also consider the case where the final number is `\"0\"` — if there's at least one `'0'`, we can keep one `'0'` and delete all others. That would take `n - 1` deletions.\n\n3. We try all valid two-digit endings, and for each, find the **rightmost possible** valid pair (to minimize deletions after), and **as far to the right as possible**.\n\nBut better: **for each valid two-digit pattern**, we try to find the **last occurrence** of the second digit, and then look for the **last occurrence of the first digit before that**.\n\nWe want to **maximize the position of the second digit**, and **maximize the position of the first digit before it**, so that we delete as few digits as possible.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Algorithm:\n\nLet `n = len(num)`\n\nWe will try each of the valid endings: `['00', '25', '50', '75']`\n\nFor each pattern like `'ab'`:\n- Find the **rightmost** occurrence of `'b'`\n- Then, in the substring before that index, find the **rightmost** occurrence of `'a'`\n- If both exist, compute deletions = `(index_a)` + `(n - 1 - index_b)`\n- Track the minimum deletions over all valid patterns\n\nAlso, consider the case where we just keep a single `'0'`:\n- deletions = `n - 1` (if there's any `'0'`)\n\nBut note: `\"00\"` already gives us a number ending in 00, which is better than just one `'0'`? Not necessarily in deletion count — but if we can get `\"00\"` with fewer deletions than keeping one `'0'`, that's better.\n\nBut also: if the number is `\"10\"`, we can delete `'1'` and get `\"0\"` → 1 deletion.\n\nSo we must consider both.\n\nBut note: `\"0\"` is valid. So if we h