ut of $ N $, the number of combinations per row is $ \\binom{N}{3} \\times 6 = 10 \\times 6 = 60 $ max (for $ N=5 $), and total rows is 5 → worst-case $ 60^5 $ is too big.\n\nBut we can **prune early**.\n\nAlternatively, we can use **constraint satisfaction** with recursion and state tracking.\n\n---\n\n### **Plan**\n\nWe will:\n1. Read input.\n2. Use backtracking (DFS) to assign positions of A, B, C for each row.\n3. Keep track of:\n   - `col_count[j][char]`: how many times `char` appears in column `j` (must not exceed 1).\n   - `col_first[j]`: the first (topmost) character placed in column `j` (if any).\n4. For each row `i`:\n   - Try all combinations of 3 distinct columns.\n   - Try all permutations of A, B, C assigned to those columns.\n   - Check:\n     - The leftmost (smallest column index) character is $ R[i] $.\n     - For each column `j`, if no character has been placed yet, then this character must equal $ C[j] $ (because it will be the topmost).\n     - Placing this character doesn't violate column count (i.e., column `j` doesn't already have this char).\n5. If we reach the last row and all constraints are satisfied, return the grid.\n\nWe'll represent the grid as a list of lists.\n\n---\n\n### **Implementation**\n\nLet’s code accordingly.\n\nWe’ll use recursion over rows.\n\nSteps:\n- Precompute all combinations of 3 columns from $ N $: `itertools.combinations(range(N), 3)`\n- For each combination, try all permutations of ['A','B','C'] → `itertools.permutations(['A','B','C'])`\n- For each such assignment, validate constraints.\n- Recurse to next row.\n- If last row is done, check if all columns have exactly one A, B, C → but since we enforce at most one, and total 3N cells, and each column must have 3 non-empty, if we finish all rows and no column has >1 of any char, and each has exactly 3 non-empty, it's valid.\n\nBut we can just check at the end: for each column, count A, B, C == 1.\n\nBut we can enforce during assignment: never assign a char to a colu