ing one 3 with two 2s (so 2 operations instead of 1), which increases count by 1 → so total is `freq//3 + 1`, which is `ceil(freq/3)`.\n\nSimilarly, remainder 2 → add one 2 → `freq//3 + 1` → same.\n\nSo yes, for `freq >= 2`, minimum operations = `ceil(freq / 3)` = `(freq + 2) // 3`\n\nBut only if `freq != 1`.\n\n---\n\n### Final Plan\n\n1. Count frequencies.\n2. For each frequency:\n   - If freq == 1 → return -1\n3. Else, sum up `(freq + 2) // 3` for all frequencies.\n\nWait — let’s test with example.\n\n---\n\n### Example 1\n\n`nums = [2,3,3,2,2,4,2,3,4]`\n\nCount:\n- 2: 4 times → (4+2)//3 = 6//3 = 2\n- 3: 3 times → (3+2)//3 = 5//3 = 1 → but 3//3=1 → correct\n- 4: 2 times → (2+2)//3 = 4//3 = 1\n\nTotal = 2 + 1 + 1 = 4 → matches ✅\n\n### Example 2\n\n`nums = [2,1,2,2,3,3]`\n\nCount:\n- 1: 1 → return -1 ✅\n\nBut wait — let's count:\n- 2: 3 times\n- 1: 1 time\n- 3: 2 times\n\nYes, 1 appears once → return -1 ✅\n\nAnother test case: `freq=5` → 5//3=1, remainder 2 → 2 operations → (5+2)//3=7//3=2 ✅\n\nAnother: `freq=7` → 7//3=2, rem 1 → need 3 operations → (7+2)//3=9//3=3 ✅\n\nPerfect.\n\n---\n\n### Edge Cases\n\n- All numbers appear at least 2 times → proceed\n- Any number appears once → return -1\n\n---\n\n### Code Implementation\n\nSteps:\n1. Use `Counter` to count frequencies.\n2. For each frequency:\n   - If freq == 1: return -1\n3. Else, sum `(freq + 2) // 3` for all frequencies.\n\nReturn the sum.\n\n---\n\nNow, write the function.\n\nNote: We must return the function body only, without calling it.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef minOperations(nums):\n    freq_map = Counter(nums)\n    total_operations = 0\n    for freq in freq_map.values():\n        if freq == 1:\n            return -1\n        total_operations += (freq + 2) // 3\n    return total_operations\n```\n\nThis should be correct.",
