      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef countInterestingSubarrays(nums: list[int], modulo: int, k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    To find the count of interesting subarrays, we define cnt as the number of elements \n    in a range [l, r] that satisfy nums[i] % modulo == k.\n    The condition is cnt % modulo == k.\n    Let pref[i] be the number of elements in nums[0...i-1] satisfying the condition.\n    Then the number of elements in nums[l...r] satisfying the condition is pref[r+1] - pref[l].\n    The condition becomes (pref[r+1] - pref[l]) % modulo == k.\n    This is equivalent to pref[l] % modulo == (pref[r+1] - k) % modulo.\n    \n    We can iterate through the array while maintaining the prefix sum of matches and \n    using a frequency map to store the occurrence of (pref[l] % modulo) values.\n    \"\"\"\n    from collections import defaultdict\n    \n    count_map = defaultdict(int)\n    count_map[0] = 1\n    \n    ans = 0\n    cnt = 0\n    \n    for x in nums:\n        if x % modulo == k:\n            cnt += 1\n        \n        # We need pref[l] % modulo == (cnt - k) % modulo\n        # Using (cnt - k) % modulo ensures the result is in [0, modulo-1]\n        target = (cnt - k) % modulo\n        ans += count_map[target]\n        \n        # Update the frequency map with current prefix count % modulo\n        count_map[cnt % modulo] += 1\n        \n    return ans\n```",
