ity.\n\nBut note: we are to minimize the **maximum** of three times.\n\nSo for each digit `d`, we want to pick one time from each reel's valid times, such that:\n- The three times are distinct (because only one press per second)\n- The maximum time is minimized.\n\nBut is \"distinct\" required?\n\nWait — can we press two buttons at the same time? No — only one action per second.\n\nSo the three times must be **distinct**.\n\nBut what if two reels have the same digit at the same time? We can only press one.\n\nSo yes — the three times must be distinct.\n\nBut in the above example, we used 0,10,20 — all distinct.\n\nSo algorithm:\n\nFor each digit `d` in '0' to '9':\n\n1. For each reel `i`, find all positions `p` in 0..M-1 such that `S_i[p] == d`.\n   Then the valid times for reel `i` are all `t` such that `t % M == p` for some such `p`.\n\n2. We want to pick one time `t1` from reel 1's valid times, `t2` from reel 2, `t3` from reel 3, such that:\n   - `t1`, `t2`, `t3` are distinct\n   - `max(t1,t2,t3)` is minimized\n\n3. Then take the minimum over all digits.\n\nBut how to search over `t`? Since M ≤ 100, and we expect the answer to be not too large, we can limit the time range.\n\nWhat is the maximum possible minimal time?\n\nIn worst case, we may need to go up to about `3*M`? But in sample 2, we had 20 for M=20.\n\nBut worst-case: suppose each digit appears only once per reel, and at position 0.\n\nThen valid times: 0, M, 2M, ...\n\nWe need three distinct times: 0, M, 2M → max = 2M.\n\nBut 2M ≤ 200.\n\nBut what if the earliest occurrence is at M-1, and we need three times: M-1, 2M-1, 3M-1 → max = 3M-1 ≈ 300.\n\nBut is there a case where we need more?\n\nActually, we can always pick the first few occurrences.\n\nSince each reel has at most M positions, and the digit repeats every M, the k-th occurrence is at `p + (k-1)*M`.\n\nSo for each reel and digit, we can generate valid times up to, say, `3*M` to be safe.\n\nBut to be safe, let's consider times from 0 to `3*M`.\n\nBut M ≤ 100 → 3*M = 300 → 301 values.\n\nFor each digit, for each reel, we can precompute all valid times in [0, 3*M].\n\nThen, for each digit `d`, we want to pick one time from each reel's list (within 0 to 3*M), distinct, and minimize the max.\n\nBut triple loop over times? Each list can have up to about 3*M / (M / freq) → worst-case, if digit appears in every position, then every time is valid → 301 times per reel.\n\nThen triple loop: 301^3 ≈ 27 million, for 10 digits → 270 million → too slow in Python.\n\nWe need a better way.\n\nAlternative approach:\n\nSince we want to minimize the maximum time, we can **iterate over possible total time T from 0 upwards**, and for each T, check if there exists a way to assign distinct stop times t1,t2,t3 ≤ T, with t1,t2,t3 in the valid sets for digit d, for some d.\n\nBut T can be up to 300, so we can iterate T from 0 to 300.\n\nFor each T and each digit d:\n\n- For each reel, get the set of valid times ≤ T such that S_i[t % M] == d.\n\n- Then, check if we can pick one time from each reel, all distinct, ≤ T.\n\nThis is a matching problem: can we assign a distinct time to each reel from their valid sets?\n\nSince there are only 3 reels, we can try all permutations.\n\nFor a fixed T and d:\n\n- For reel 1: times1 = { t in [0,T] | S1[t % M] == d }\n- For reel 2: times2 = { t in [0,T] | S2[t % M] == d }\n- For reel 3: times3 = { t in [0,T] | S3[t % M] == d }\n\nThen, check if there exist t1 in times1, t2 in times2, t3 in times3, all distinct.\n\nWe can do this by iterating over all possible assignments.\n\nBut the sets can be large (up to T+1 ≈ 300), so 300^3 = 27 million per (T,d) → too slow.\n\nBut T from 0 to 300, 10 digits → 300*10*27e6 = 81e9 → impossible.\n\nWe need a better way.\n\nAlternative: for each digit d, precompute for each reel the list of valid times up to a limit, say 3*M.\n\nThen, for each digit d, we want to find the minimum T such that there exist t1,t2,t3 in the valid sets (one from each reel), distinct, with max(t1,t2,t3) = T.\n\nWe can iterate over possible combinations of occurrences.\n\nSince each reel has at most M positions where d appears, and the times are periodic, we can generate the first few occurrences.\n\nFor each reel i, for digit d, let positions = [p for p in range(M) if S_i[p] == d]\n\nThen the valid times are: for k=0,1,2,... : t = p + k*M\n\nWe can generate the first K occurrences for each reel. How many? Since we expect T <= 3*M, we can take k such that t <= 3*M → k <= 3, so at most 3 occurrences per position.\n\nBut each position gives one occurrence per cycle.\n\nSo for each reel, number of valid times up to 3*M is at most 3 * (number of positions with d)\n\nSince M<=100, at most 100 positions, but 3*100=300 times per reel.\n\nThen for each digit d, we have three lists: L1, L2, L3 of valid times (up to 3*M).\n\nThen we want to find min T such that there exist t1 in L1, t2 in L2, t3 in L3, all distinct, and max(t1,t2,t3) = T.\n\nWe can iterate over all combinations: |L1| * |L2| * |L3| ≤ 300^3 = 27e6 per digit → 270e6 for 10 digits → too slow in Python.\n\nBut worst-case, if a digit appears in all positions, then each list has about 3*M = 300 times → 300^3 = 27e6 per digit → 270e6 total → in Python, this might be borderline in PyPy/C++ but in Python it could be 10-30 seconds — too slow.\n\nWe need a better approach.\n\nAlternative idea:\n\nFor each digit d, and for each reel, we generate the set of valid times up to a limit, say 3*M.\n\nBut instead of triple loop, we can:\n\n- For each possible distinct triple of times (t1,t2,t3) with t1 in L1, t2 in L2, t3 in L3, and t1,t2,t3 distinct, compute T = max(t1,t2,t3), and take the min over all such T.\n\nBut still O(|L1|*|L2|*|L3|).\n\nBut worst-case 27e6 per digit — 270e6 — too slow.\n\nBut note: M ≤ 100, but the number of positions where digit d appears is at most M, and we consider up to k such that t = p + k*M ≤ 3*M → k=0,1,2 → so for each position, 3 times.\n\nSo for each reel, number of valid times for digit d is at most 3 * (freq_d) ≤ 3*M = 300, but actually, freq_d ≤ M, so 3*M=300.\n\nBut worst-case, if d appears in all M positions, then 3*M = 300 times per reel.\n\nSo 300^3 = 27e6 per digit.\n\nBut 10 digits → 270e6 combinations.\n\nIn Python, each combination is a few operations, say 100 ns per combo → 270e6 * 100e-9 = 27 seconds — too slow.\n\nWe need to optimize.\n\nAlternative: for each digit d, and for each reel, generate the list of valid times up to 3*M.\n\nThen, instead of triple loop, we can:\n\n- Iterate over t1 in L1\n- Iterate over t2 in L2, t2 != t1\n- Iterate over t3 in L3, t3 != t1 and t3 != t2\n- Update min_T = min(min_T, max(t1,t2,t3))\n\nBut still O(n1*n2*n3)\n\nBut worst-case 27e6 per digit — 270e6 — too slow in Python.\n\nBut note: M is only up to 100, and in practice, digits may not appear too many times.\n\nBut worst-case is possible.\n\nAlternative idea: iterate over possible T from 0 to 3*M, and for each T, check if there is a valid assignment with max time <= T and distinct times.\n\nFor fixed T and d:\n\n- For each reel i, let A_i = sorted list of valid times <= T for digit d.\n\n- We want to know: is there a way to pick one time from each A_i, all distinct?\n\nThis is a bipartite matching? Or simply: can we assign distinct times to reels.\n\nSince only 3 reels, we can try all 3! = 6 permutations of reels, and for each permutation, try to assign the earliest possible time.\n\nBut better: we can use recursion or try all combinations.\n\nBut |A_i| can be up to T+1 ≈ 300, so 300^3 = 27e6 per (T,d) — and T from 0 to 300, 10 digits → 300*10*27e6 = 81e9 — worse.\n\nWe need a smarter way.\n\nIdea: for each digit d, generate all valid times for each reel up to 3*M.\n\nThen, since the lists are not too big (at most 300), and there are only 10 digits, we can hope that in practice it's fast, or optimize by breaking early.\n\nBut worst-case might be borderline.\n\nAlternatively, we can iterate over the number of cycles.\n\nFor each reel i, for digit d, let P_i be the set of positions p in [0, M-1] where S_i[p] == d.\n\nThen, a valid time for reel i is t = p + k*M for p in P_i, k>=0.\n\nWe want to pick one t_i = p_i + k_i*M for each i, such that t1,t2,t3 are distinct, and max(t1,t2,t3) is minimized.\n\nWe can iterate over k1,k2,k3 from 0 to 2 (since 3*M is 300, k up to 2 gives t up to 2*M + (M-1) = 3*M-1), so k in {0,1,2} should be enough.\n\nFor each reel i, for each position p in P_i, for k=0,1,2: time = p + k*M\n\nThen, for each digit d, we have for each reel a list of times (p + k*M) for p in P_i, k=0,1,2.\n\nThe size of each list is at most 3 * |P_i| ≤ 3*M = 300.\n\nThen, iterate over all combinations of t1,t2,t3 from the three lists, check if distinct, compute max, and take the minimum.\n\nBut worst-case 300^3 = 27e6 per digit, 10 digits = 270e6.\n\nIn Python, we can try to optimize by:\n\n- Breaking early if possible\n- Using nested loops and updating a global minimum\n\nBut 270e6 might be acceptable in PyPy/C++ but in Python it might be 10-30 seconds.\n\nBut constraints say M<=100, but worst-case might be rare.\n\nAlternatively, we can optimize by not generating duplicates and using sets.\n\nBut let's try to reduce the search.\n\nNote: we only care about the minimum max time.\n\nSo we can iterate over possible max time T from 0 to 3*M, and for each T, check if there is a valid distinct triple with max<=T.\n\nFor fixed T and d:\n\n- For each reel i, the valid times are { p + k*M <= T | p in P_i }\n\n- We want to know if we can pick distinct t1,t2,t3 from the three sets.\n\nWe can do this by: for each reel, the set of valid times <= T.\n\nThen, check if there is a system of distinct representatives.\n\nFor three sets, we can do:\n\n- Try all permutations of the three reels: for each permutation (i,j,k), try to assign the smallest time from reel i, then smallest from reel j not equal to that, then smallest from reel k not equal to those.\n\nBut that greedy may not work.\n\nBetter: try all possible assignments.\n\nBut the sets can be large.\n\nBut T up to 300, and for each reel, the number of valid times <= T is at most floor(T/M)+1 times per position, and |P_i| positions.\n\nFor T=300, M=100, floor(300/100)=3, so at most 3*|P_i| ≤ 300.\n\nSame as before.\n\nBut now, we iterate T from 0 to 300, and for each T and each digit, do a triple loop over the valid times.\n\nWorst-case, for each T, the sets have up to 300 elements, so 300^3 = 27e6 per (T,d), and T from 0 to 300, 10 digits: 300*10*27e6 = 81e9 — too slow.\n\nSo the first method (generate times up to 3*M, then triple loop over combinations) is better: only 10 * (300^3) = 270e6.\n\nWe can hope that in practice, the number of valid times per reel is small.\n\nOr optimize by using early termination.\n\nBut let's try to reduce the number of times.\n\nNote: for each reel and digit, the valid times are periodic. We only need to consider the first few occurrences.\n\nSpecifically, since we want the minimum max time, we only need to consider k such that p + k*M <= 3*M.\n\nFor k=0,1,2: because p <= M-1, so p + 2*M <= M-1 + 2*M = 3*M -1 < 3*M\n\nk=3: p + 3*M >= 3*M, which is larger than 3*M-1, so we can cap at k=0,1,2.\n\nSo for each position p in P_i, we add times: p, p+M, p+2*M.\n\nSo at most 3 * |P_i| times per reel.\n\nNow, to reduce the triple loop, we can:\n\n- For each digit d, if any reel has no occurrence of d, skip.\n- Then, for the three lists L1, L2, L3, we can iterate:\n\n  min_time = a big number\n\n  for t1 in L1:\n    for t2 in L2:\n      if t2 == t1: continue\n      for t3 in L3:\n        if t3 == t1 or t3 == t2: continue\n        T = max(t1,t2,t3)\n        if T < min_time:\n          min_time = T\n\n  then update global answer.\n\nBut worst-case 27e6 per digit.\n\nBut 270e6 total.\n\nIn Python, on a fast machine, 100 million operations per second, so 2.7 seconds — acceptable.\n\nAnd worst-case might be rare.\n\nWe can also break early if min_time is 0, but not likely.\n\nOr sort the lists and br