      "generated_answer": "In a complete K-ary tree of depth N, the total number of nodes is approximately (K^N - 1)/(K - 1), and the number of nonterminal (internal) nodes is the total number of nodes minus the number of leaves. The number of leaves is K^N, so the internal nodes are roughly (K^N - 1)/(K - 1) - K^N, but more simply, at each level except the last, all nodes are internal, and the fraction of internal nodes approaches (K - 1)/K as N becomes large.\n\nThus, the ratio of nonterminal nodes to total nodes approaches (K - 1)/K for large N, making the best approximation independent of N and dependent only on K.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
