    "global index": "MMLUPro_philosophy_9662",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion:  Thomson discusses a different version of the violinist case wherein the violinist only needs to use your kidneys for one hour to survive. Her own view about this case is that\n\nOptions: \nA. because the violinist's life is in danger, you have an absolute moral obligation to let them use your kidneys, regardless of the duration.\nB. you have no obligation to let the violinist use your kidneys, but if you choose to do so, the violinist has the right to use them for that hour.\nC. you have the right to disconnect the violinist at any point, regardless of the consequences for the violinist.\nD. because you ought to let the violinist use your kidneys for that hour, we should conclude that he or she has a right to use your kidneys for that hour.\nE. even though you ought to let the violinist use your kidneys for that hour, we should not conclude that he or she has a right to use your kidneys for that hour.\nF. you have no obligation to let the violinist use your kidneys for that hour.\nG. you have an obligation to let the violinist use your kidneys for that hour, but the violinist does not have a right to use your kidneys.\nH. the violinist has an obligation to disconnect him- or herself before that hour is over.\nI. you have an obligation to let the violinist use your kidneys for that hour, and the violinist also has a right to use your kidneys.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In \"A Defense of Abortion,\" Judith Jarvis Thomson uses the one-hour violinist scenario to illustrate that even if it is \"minimally decent\" or something one *ought* to do to allow another to use one's body for a trivial cost, that does not confer a right upon the beneficiary to use one's body. She argues that there is a significant moral distinction between what one is morally required to do and what one is obligated to do under a right. Therefore, even if you ought to let the violinist use your kidneys for that hour, the violinist does not have a right to them.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
