    "global index": "MMLUPro_philosophy_9931",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion:  In Baxter's view, the first and foremost step toward a solution of our environmental problems is a clear recognition that our objective is not pure air or water but rather\n\nOptions: \nA. the introduction of new species to balance the ecosystem.\nB. maintaining current levels of pollution.\nC. an overall maximally clean environment.\nD. the total elimination of harmful species.\nE. some maximal state of pollution.\nF. the eradication of all pollution.\nG. an environment free from human interference.\nH. a state of pollution that supports human health and well-being.\nI. some optimal state of pollution.\nJ. the preservation of all living species.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In William F. Baxter’s anthropocentric framework, he argues that the objective of environmental policy should not be the total elimination of pollution, which he deems impossible and inefficient. Instead, he proposes that society should strive for an \"optimal state of pollution\" that balances the benefits of human activities against the costs of environmental degradation. This approach prioritizes human well-being as the primary standard for determining acceptable environmental quality.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
