    "global index": "MMLUPro_philosophy_9705",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion:  The question of whether the killing/letting die distinction is morally relevant can be posed by asking whether we are equally to blame for allowing people in Third World countries to starve as we would be for\n\nOptions: \nA. killing them by blocking access to clean water.\nB. killing them by sending poisoned food.\nC. refusing to share agricultural technology with them.\nD. withholding medicine that they need to survive.\nE. exploiting their resources without fair compensation.\nF. denying them the opportunity to trade fairly in global markets.\nG. depriving them of knowledge they need to produce food.\nH. imposing economic sanctions that lead to food shortages.\nI. depriving them of their land for farming.\nJ. refusing to give them the money they need to purchase food.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The standard philosophical comparison, famously popularized by Peter Singer in his essay *Famine, Affluence, and Morality*, uses the scenario of actively killing children to illustrate the moral weight of failing to prevent their deaths. The most direct comparison to the act of \"letting die\" by starvation is the active act of killing via poisoning, which clarifies whether the mechanism of death (omission vs. commission) alters moral culpability.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
