  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_6392",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: If a Johansen \"trace\" test for a null hypothesis of 2 cointegrating vectors is applied to a system containing 4 variables is conducted, which eigenvalues would be used in the test?\n\nOptions: \nA. The largest and smallest\nB. The smallest and second smallest\nC. The middle two\nD. The smallest 2\nE. The largest 2\nF. The third and fourth largest\nG. The second largest\nH. The second smallest and second largest\nI. All of them\nJ. The largest and second largest\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The Johansen trace test statistic for a null hypothesis of $r$ cointegrating vectors is calculated using the $n-r$ smallest eigenvalues of the system's stochastic matrix ($\\lambda_{r+1}, \\dots, \\lambda_n$). In a system with 4 variables and a null hypothesis of 2 cointegrating vectors ($n=4, r=2$), the test utilizes the remaining $4-2=2$ smallest eigenvalues ($\\lambda_3$ and $\\lambda_4$). Therefore, \"The smallest 2\" is the most standard description of the eigenvalues used in the test statistic.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 193,
        "output_tokens": 5377,
        "total_tokens": 5570
      },
      "provider": "vertex",
      "error": null
    },
    "accuracy": 1.0,
    "cost": 0.00811375
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_6111",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: An ARMA(p,q) (p, q are integers bigger than zero) model will have\n\nOptions: \nA. An acf that declines geometrically and a pacf that is zero after p lags\nB. An acf that declines geometrically and a pacf that is zero after p+q lags\nC. An acf and pacf that are both zero after q lags\nD. An acf and pacf that are both zero after p lags\nE. An acf that is zero after q lags and a pacf that is zero after p lags\nF. An acf and pacf that both decline geometrically\nG. An acf that declines geometrically and a pacf that is zero after q lags\nH. An acf that is zero after q lags and a pacf that declines geometrically\nI. An acf that is zero after p lags and a pacf that declines geometrically\nJ. An acf that is zero after p lags and a pacf that is zero after q lags\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "An ARMA(p,q) model combines AR(p) and MA(q) components. The autocorrelation function (ACF) of an ARMA(p,q) model typically declines geometrically (like an AR process), not cutting off sharply, due to the AR part. The partial autocorrelation function (PACF) also declines geometrically due to the MA part, but neither cuts off after a fixed number of lags as in pure AR or MA models.\n\nTherefore, neither the ACF nor the PACF cuts off sharply after a finite number of lags for a general ARMA(p,q); instead, both decay gradually. The correct description is that both the ACF and PACF decline geometrically.\n\n\\boxed{F}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 307,
        "output_tokens": 151,
        "total_tokens": 458
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "accuracy": 1.0,
    "cost": 3.6896999999999996e-05
  },
