    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5642",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following statements is true?\n\nOptions: \nA. The demand for loanable funds consists of savers.\nB. Real GDP is the sum of the money supply and the velocity of money.\nC. The supply of loanable funds is solely determined by the central bank's policies.\nD. Dollars earned today have more purchasing power than dollars earned a year from today.\nE. The velocity of money is the speed at which the stock market indices change.\nF. The demand for loanable funds is entirely driven by government borrowing.\nG. Purchasing power parity ensures that dollars have the same value across all time periods.\nH. The supply of loanable funds consists of investors.\nI. Inflation rates are inversely proportional to the velocity of money at all times.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's evaluate each option:\n\nA. Incorrect – Savers supply loanable funds; borrowers (investors, governments) demand them.  \nB. Incorrect – Real GDP is not the sum of money supply and velocity; nominal GDP = money supply × velocity.  \nC. Incorrect – The supply of loanable funds comes from private saving, not just central bank policy.  \nD. Correct – Due to inflation, money today typically has more purchasing power than the same amount in the future.  \nE. Incorrect – Velocity of money is the rate at which money changes hands in the economy, not stock market speed.  \nF. Incorrect – Government borrowing is part of demand, but businesses and individuals also borrow.  \nG. Incorrect – Purchasing power parity compares currencies across countries, not over time.  \nH. Incorrect – Investors are typically demanders, not suppliers, of loanable funds.  \nI. Incorrect – There's no universal inverse relationship between inflation and velocity.\n\nThe only correct statement is that dollars today generally have more purchasing power than in the future due to inflation.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 237,
        "output_tokens": 222,
        "total_tokens": 459
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
