    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following criticisms of standard (\"plain vanilla\") GARCH models can be overcome by EGARCH models?\n\ni) Estimated coefficient values from GARCH models may be negative\n\nii) GARCH models cannot account for leverage effects\n\niii) The responsiveness of future volatility to positive and negative shocks is symmetric under a GARCH formulation\n\niv) GARCH models cannot allow for a feedback from the volatility to the returns\n\nOptions: \nA. (iii) and (iv) only\nB. (i), (iii), and (iv) only\nC. (i) and (iii) only\nD. (ii), (iii), and (iv) only\nE. (i), (ii), and (iii) only\nF. (ii) and (iv) only\nG. (ii) and (iii) only\nH. (i), (ii), (iii), and (iv)\nI. (i) and (iv) only\nJ. (i) and (ii) only\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Standard GARCH models impose non-negativity constraints on coefficients and treat positive and negative shocks symmetrically, while EGARCH models log variance to allow negative coefficients and include an asymmetric term to capture leverage effects. However, EGARCH does not inherently incorporate a feedback effect from volatility to returns unless extended to a GARCH-in-Mean specification. Thus, EGARCH overcomes criticisms (i), (ii), and (iii).\n\n\\boxed{E}",
