      "generated_answer": "H.L.A. Hart uses the distinction between \"being obliged\" and \"having an obligation\" to explain the internal aspect of rules, which is essential to understanding the normative nature of law. By showing that an obligation is grounded in the existence of a rule rather than a mere psychological feeling of compulsion, he distinguishes legal systems from situations of simple coercion, such as a gunman's demand. Therefore, this distinction is fundamental to his conceptual analysis of what constitutes a rule.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
