      "generated_answer": "Standard-setting in human rights diplomacy refers to the process of establishing norms and principles that guide state behavior, often through international treaties and declarations. While some outcomes of standard-setting become binding when treaties are ratified, the process itself involves formulating agreed-upon standards of conduct, particularly in treaties, which define expectations for human rights protection. Therefore, the most accurate answer is that standard-setting involves setting certain standards of conduct in human rights treaties.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
