    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_1698",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: American realists argued that formalism devalued the authority of the judge to make law, because:\n\nOptions: \nA. Judicial decisions are less important than legislation.\nB. The structure of legal reasoning is flawed.\nC. Judges are not allowed to interpret the law, only apply it.\nD. Formalism does not allow for moral considerations in the judiciary process.\nE. Formalism treats legal reasoning as syllogistic reasoning.\nF. Formalism undervalues the influence of societal changes on law.\nG. Judges generally base their decisions on formal logic.\nH. It assumes all laws are written with perfect clarity.\nI. Formalism makes the law rigid and inflexible.\nJ. Judges don't have the necessary training to understand formal logic.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "American realists argued that formalism reduced judicial decision-making to a mechanical, syllogistic process where the judge merely deduces conclusions from existing legal premises. By characterizing the judge as an automaton rather than a creative actor, formalism denied the reality of judicial discretion and the judge's role in actively shaping the law. Consequently, this approach devalued the judge's authority by framing their rulings as inevitable logical results rather than policy-driven choices.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
