    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_791",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A state has recently enacted a statute requiring all prospective voters in state elections who wish to write-in a candidate to print the candidate's full name, and designate the office for which the candidate is running. The statute provides that such information must be written on the ballot in ink inan appropriate space. A write-in candidate is a German-American and is of the opinion that he needs a large turnout of German voters in order to win the election. As a result, his campaign manager decides to mail to every registered German voter a tear-off sticker, which bears the candidate's name and office for which he is running. Since many native German people are not proficient in reading and writing English, the campaign manager believes that many of the voters will have difficulty writing the candidate's name and office on the ballot. As a result, the campaign manager has mounted an extensive media campaign to inform voters on how to apply the stickers to the ballot. Five months prior to the election an election official notifies the candidate's campaign committee that the tear-off stickers do not comply with the state statute. In her letter, the official explains that state election officials are of the opinion that it is necessary for potential voters to write the candidate's name in ink. Therefore, she concludes that the stickers do not comply with statutory requirements. Three weeks later, the candidate filed suit in federal district court against state election officials, claiming that their interpretation of the state statute violates the U. S. Constitution. Which of the following sets forth the strongest constitutional argument the candidate could make against the interpretation of the statute by the state officials?\n\nOptions: \nA. It unreasonably discriminates against German voters who lack a proficiency in the English language.\nB. It unreasonably interferes with the exclusive federal election power as embodied in the Fifteenth Amendment.\nC. It goes against the spirit of the Twenty-fourth Amendment by effectively imposing a \"literacy tax.\"\nD. It unreasonably discriminates against non-English speaking voters in general.\nE. It violates the Equal Protection Clause by treating write-in candidates differently.\nF. It unreasonably discriminates against write-in candidates for public office.\nG. It unreasonably limits the means by which a candidate can campaign, infringing on democratic practices.\nH. It unreasonably restricts freedom of speech as guaranteed by the First Amendment.\nI. It unreasonably interferes with the 1965 Voting Rights Act outlawing literacy tests.\nJ. It interferes with the right to vote as guaranteed by the Nineteenth Amendment.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The most likely correct answer is **A**.\n\nThe statute, by requiring voters to manually write the candidate's name in ink, imposes a significant burden on the rights of voters who are not proficient in English, effectively disenfranchising or creating an unequal burden on them compared to English-proficient voters. Under the Equal Protection Clause, when a state regulation creates a substantial burden on the voting rights of a specific class of voters without a sufficiently compelling or rational justification, it is vulnerable to a constitutional challenge. Option A directly addresses this discriminatory effect on the specific group of voters identified in the prompt, making it the strongest constitutional argument based on the Equal Protection Clause.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
