    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The strongest constitutional argument is that the statute violates the Appointments Clause (Article II, Section 2, Clause 2), as established in *Buckley v. Valeo*. Under the principle of separation of powers, Congress (including the Senate) cannot appoint members to an agency that exercises executive authority, such as the power to promulgate federal regulations. Therefore, the provision allowing the Senate to appoint three members is a structural violation of the Constitution.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
