      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is based on the principle that the U.S. Constitution expressly grants Congress the enumerated power to \"establish Post Offices and post Roads\" (Article I, Section 8, Clause 7). Because the Constitution delegates this power to the federal government, the Supreme Court has interpreted this as an exclusive federal authority, prohibiting states from establishing competing postal services. While other arguments like the Commerce Clause or Supremacy Clause are relevant, the structural preemption arising from the specific constitutional delegation to Congress is the most direct and foundational legal barrier.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
